 articles, website content, marketing copy, newsletters, social media posts, etc.).\", \"q_cw_2\": \"Describe the types of content you are most comfortable writing (for example blogs, articles, website content, marketing copy, newsletters, social media posts, etc.). Describe the types of content you are most comfortable writing (for example blogs, articles, website content, marketing copy, newsletters, social media posts, etc.).\", \"q_cw_3\": \"Describe the types of content you are most comfortable writing (for example blogs, articles, website content, marketing copy, newsletters, social media posts, etc.). Describe the types of content you are most comfortable writing (for example blogs, articles, website content, marketing copy, newsletters, social media posts, etc.).\", \"q_cw_4\": \"Describe the types of content you are most comfortable writing (for example blogs, articles, website content, marketing copy, newsletters, social media posts, etc.). Describe the types of content you are most comfortable writing (for example blogs, articles, website content, marketing copy, newsletters, social media posts, etc.).\", \"q_cw_5\": \"Describe the types of content you are most comfortable writing (for example blogs, articles, website content, marketing copy, newsletters, social media posts, etc.). Describe the types of content you are most comfortable writing (for example blogs, articles, website content, marketing copy, newsletters, social media posts, etc.).\", \"q_cw_6\": \"Describe the types of content you are most comfortable writing (for example blogs, articles, website content, marketing copy, newsletters, social media posts, etc.). Describe the types of content you are most comfortable writing (for example blogs, articles, website content, marketing copy, newsletters, social media posts, etc.).\", \"q_cw_7\": \"Describe the types of content you are most comfortable writing (for example blogs, articles, website content, marketing copy, newsletters, social media posts, etc.). Describe the types of content you are most comfortable writing (for example blogs, articles, website content, marketing copy, newsletters, social media posts, etc.).\", \"q_cw_8\": \"Describe the types of content you are most comfortable writing (for example blogs, articles, website content, marketing copy, newsletters, social media posts, etc.). Describe the types of content you are most comfortable writing (for example blogs, articles, website content, marketing copy, newsletters, social media posts, etc.).\", \"q_cw_9\": \"Describe the types of content you are most comfortable writing (for example blogs, articles, website content, marketing copy, newsletters, social media posts, etc.). Describe the types of content you are most comfortable writing (for example blogs, articles, website content, marketing copy, newsletters, social media posts, etc.).\", \"q_cw_10\": \"Describe the types of content you are most comfortable writing (for example blogs, articles, website content, marketing copy, newsletters, social media posts, etc.). Describe the types of content you are most comfortable writing (for example blogs, articles, website content, marketing copy, newsletters, social media posts, etc.).\"}','https://drive.com','not set','{\"pressure\": 3, \"teamwork\": 3, \"adaptability\": 3, \"communication\": 3, \"problemSolving\": 3}',NULL,NULL,NULL),(7,'BIniam Mulugeta','bmulugeta452@gmail.com','939691485','','','','Male',NULL,'www.linkedin.com/in/binachapi','','','','','','','','',NULL,NULL,NULL,'','','','',NULL,'','','Immediate',NULL,NULL,'',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'Yes','Yes',NULL,'No','No',NULL,'[]','[]','[]',NULL,'','No','No','No','No',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'','','','',NULL,NULL,NULL,'No','No',NULL,'No',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'No',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'When I\'m thinking about SWAFRI as a junior frontend developer, I am happy to join and get more and more experience with a working stage of the popular company in Africa.',NULL,'','',1,0,1,0,NULL,'2026-04-18 14:30:12','2026-04-18 14:30:12','[\"full-stack\"]','{\"q_fs_1\": \"Frontend and backend development are the two main parts of web apps. Frontend development is what users see, and backend development is what makes them work. \\nThe frontend is everything that a user sees and does in their web browser. \\nThe backend is the part of the app that runs on a remote server and is not visible to the user.\", \"q_fs_2\": \"An API, or Application Programming Interface, is a set of rules and protocols that lets two software programs talk to each other and share information. In web development, it acts like a digital waiter, taking a user\'s request from the front end to the back end \\\"kitchen\\\" and bringing back the answer.\", \"q_fs_3\": \"SQL is a structural query language for databases like MySQL, and it has strict rules and works mainly with rows and columns of data stored.\\nNoSQL: no structural query language that works mainly file-based structures, key + data-storing combination like mongoDB\", \"q_fs_4\": \"Both are validated in the backend of the web application, and authentication is who you are (username and password). authorization is after a correct credentials get into the system; what data you will access\", \"q_fs_5\": \"Git is one of the version control systems, and it works mainly if the production apps are live. If we add a new functionality to the system, we push after it\'s tested and works properly. We will push it the main git branch that will not affect the production app\", \"q_fs_6\": \"Responsive design is the backbone of web apps with a good user experience on our websites because it runs with different window sizes. If we are not consider the responsiveness of the app, that affect the UX (user experience in the app)\", \"q_fs_7\": \"REST APIs are APIs that work only with the call of the frontend that are not updated live. If we want see updates in frontend call, then again, they are simple to develop and use in web apps; that is the main reason why they are popular nowadays.\", \"q_fs_8\": \"The states in the frontend are to manage and access the same data in different screens; for example, in cart management in e-commerce web apps, we access the same items and prices for more than 2 live pages and state management examples for React frameworks like Zustand.\", \"q_fs_9\": \"To improve application performance, optimize the frontend by implementing lazy loading, code splitting, image compression, and CDN usage to reduce load times. Use database indexing, caching strategies (like Redis), asynchronous processing, and load balancing to handle more traffic and speed up response times on the backend.\", \"q_fs_10\": \"We test it as a user on the production app, then we figure out if the problem is on the front end or the back end. After that, we fix the real problem, test it locally, and then push it as a \\\"this issue fixed\\\" version. Finally, we make it live. \"}','https://github.com/BinaChapi/talent_management_system.git','not set','{\"pressure\": 4, \"teamwork\": 3, \"adaptability\": 4, \"communication\": 3, \"problemSolving\": 4}',NULL,NULL,NULL),(8,'Awoke Guadie Asmamaw','awoke8817@gmail.com','930950562','','','','Male',NULL,'https://www.linkedin.com/in/awoke-guadie','','','','','','','','',NULL,NULL,NULL,'','','','',NULL,'','','1-2 Weeks',NULL,NULL,'',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'Yes','No',NULL,'No','No',NULL,'[]','[]','[]',NULL,'','No','No','No','No',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'','','','',NULL,NULL,NULL,'No','No',NULL,'No',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'No',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'WAFRI appeals to me because it represents a real bridge between scientific talent ',NULL,'','',1,0,1,0,NULL,'2026-04-18 15:44:39','2026-04-18 15:44:39','[\"full-stack\"]','{\"q_fs_1\": \"Frontend development builds the part users see and interact with—UI, layouts, buttons, pages, and client-side logic (typically using HTML/CSS/JavaScript).\\n\\nBackend development builds the server side that handles business logic—APIs, databases, authentication, security, and processing requests (often using languages/frameworks like Node.js, Python, Java, etc.).\", \"q_fs_2\": \"An API (Application Programming Interface) is a set of rules/endpoints that lets one software system talk to another.\\n\\nThe frontend communicates with the backend by making requests to the API (e.g., GET /users, POST /login), usually over HTTP/HTTPS, and the backend returns data (often JSON), which the frontend uses to update the page.\", \"q_fs_3\": \"SQL databases\\n\\nUse structured tables and fixed schemas (rows/columns).\\nUse SQL for queries (joins, transactions, strong consistency).\\nExamples: PostgreSQL, MySQL, SQL Server, Oracle.\\nNoSQL databases\\n\\nStore data in non-tabular formats (documents, key-value, wide-column, graphs).\\nOften use flexible schemas (data shape can vary).\\nTypically scale horizontally and handle large volumes with easier distribution; consistency tradeoffs vary by type.\\nExamples: MongoDB (document\", \"q_fs_4\": \"Authentication answers: “Who are you?”\\nVerifies a user’s identity (e.g., login with username/password, OAuth, tokens).\\n\\nAuthorization answers: “What are you allowed to do?”\\nChecks permissions/roles after login (e.g., whether a user can view, edit, or delete data).\", \"q_fs_5\": \"it and version control are used to track and manage changes to code over time.\\n\\nSave history: Every change is recorded (who changed what and when).\\nCollaboration: Multiple developers can work on the same project and merge changes safely.\\nBranching: Developers can try features or fixes without affecting the main codebase.\\nRevert when needed: You can roll back to an earlier version if something breaks.\\nConsistency & reliability: Helps coordinate releases and keep the project organized.\", \"q_fs_6\": \"Responsive design means a website’s layout and content automatically adapt to different screen sizes and devices (phone, tablet, desktop). It uses flexible grids, images, and CSS rules so the site stays readable and usable.\", \"q_fs_7\": \"REST APIs (Representational State Transfer) are a way for software systems to communicate over the web using HTTP methods (like GET, POST, PUT, DELETE) and resources identified by URLs.\\n\\nThey’re common because:\\n\\nSimple and standard: Fits naturally with the web.\\nStateless: Each request contains what’s needed, making systems easier to scale.\\nWorks with many clients: Browsers, mobile apps, and other services can all use them.\\nClear structure: CRUD-style endpoints map well to typical app data.\\nPopular formats: Usually return JSON, which is easy for frontend code to use.\", \"q_fs_8\": \"State management in a frontend application means how the app stores, updates, and shares data (state) across components over time.\\nComponents need shared data like current user, form inputs, loading/error status, or fetched results.\\nState management ensures changes in one part of the app consistently reflect everywhere that depends on that data.\", \"q_fs_9\": \"Frontend performance techniques\\nMinify and bundle JS/CSS; remove unused code.\\nCode splitting / lazy loading so users download less at first.\\nOptimize images (resize, compress, use modern formats like WebP/AVIF).\\nCaching: use browser caching headers and service workers (where appropriate).\\nReduce re-renders / heavy DOM work; use memoization techniques.\\nUse async/defer for scripts; avoid blocking rendering.\\nCDN for static assets.\\nBackend performance techniques\\nCaching (e.g., Redis/memory) for expensive queries and repeated results.\\nDatabase optimization: proper indexes, query tuning, avoid N+1 queries.\\nUse pagination/limits instead of fetching huge datasets.\\nScale appropriately: horizontal scaling, load balancing.\\nUse background jobs/queues for slow tasks (email sending, report generation).\\nRate limiting & throttling to protect resources.\\nMonitoring and profiling to find bottlenecks (latency, CPU, DB time).\\nOptimize network/IO: compression (gzip/brotli), keep connections efficient.\", \"q_fs_10\": \"Confirm the bug\\n\\nGet exact steps to reproduce, expected vs actual behavior, user info (role/account), and device/browser/version.\\nCheck if it’s 100% reproducible or intermittent.\\nCheck recent changes\\n\\nLook at deployments/commits/feature flags around when it started.\\nIf the issue began after a release, verify by testing that specific version or doing a quick rollback.\\nInspect logs + errors\\n\\nFrontend: browser console errors, failed requests, UI rendering errors.\\nBackend: API logs, stack traces, request IDs, auth/permission failures, DB/cache errors.\\nTrace the request end-to-end\\n\\nVerify what the frontend sends (endpoint, method, payload, headers).\\nConfirm the backend receives it and returns the expected response (status code + body).\\nEnsure the frontend correctly handles the response and updates state.\\nIsolate the layer\\n\\nTest the API directly (with curl/Postman) to see if the problem is backend vs frontend.\\nCheck database/cache state and whether data is missing/wrong.\\nConsider environment differences\"}','https://github.com/Awokegu/Full-Stack-E-Commerce-Website','not set','{\"pressure\": 4, \"teamwork\": 4, \"adaptability\": 4, \"communication\": 5, \"problemSolving\": 5}',NULL,NULL,NULL),(9,'Simon Tamene Lebessa','simontamene.dev@gmail.com','946854382','','','','Male',NULL,'https://www.linkedin.com/in/simon-tamene','','','','','','','','',NULL,NULL,NULL,'','','','',NULL,'','','Immediate',NULL,NULL,'',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'Yes','Yes',NULL,'Yes','No',NULL,'[]','[]','[]',NULL,'','No','No','No','No',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'','','','',NULL,NULL,NULL,'No','No',NULL,'No',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'No',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'I am interested because it gives me global job opportunities. I can use my skills to work with international clients, showcase my abilities, expand my connections, and improve my skills day by day while working hard to meet global standards',NULL,'','',1,0,1,0,NULL,'2026-04-18 16:09:30','2026-04-18 16:09:30','[\"front-end\"]','{\"q_fe_1\": \"HTML: Structures the website (content like text, images, buttons).\\nCSS: Styles the website (colors, layout, fonts, spacing).\\nJavaScript: Makes the website interactive (clicks, forms, animations, logic).\", \"q_fe_2\": \"Responsive design: A way of building websites so they automatically adjust to different screen sizes (mobile, tablet, desktop). It uses flexible layouts, images, and CSS media queries.\\nWhy it’s important: It makes websites look good and work properly on all devices, improves user experience, and is essential because most users browse on mobile phones.\", \"q_fe_3\": \"Client-side rendering (CSR): The browser downloads a mostly empty HTML page and JavaScript builds the content in the browser. It can feel slower on first load but is fast after loading.\\nServer-side rendering (SSR): The server builds the full HTML page and sends it to the browser already ready. It loads faster initially and is better for SEO.\", \"q_fe_4\": \"JavaScript frameworks (like React or Vue): They help developers build user interfaces faster and more efficiently by providing reusable components and better ways to manage state and UI updates.\\nPurpose:\\n- Break UI into reusable components\\n- Make websites more interactive and dynamic\\n- Handle data changes automatically without reloading the page\\n- Improve development speed and code organization\", \"q_fe_5\": \"Component: A reusable building block of a user interface in modern frontend frameworks like React or Vue.\\nWhat it does: Each component represents a part of the UI (like a button, navbar, form, or card) and can be reused in different places\", \"q_fe_6\": \"Local Storage: Stores data in the browser with no expiration (unless manually cleared). It can hold more data and is only accessible by JavaScript.\\nCookies: Small pieces of data sent with every HTTP request. They can have an expiration time and are often used for authentication.\", \"q_fe_7\": \"API (Application Programming Interface): A way for different software systems to communicate with each other.\\nHow frontend communicates with backend:\\nThe frontend sends a request (using HTTP methods like GET, POST)\\nThe backend processes the request and sends back a response (usually data in JSON format)\\nThe frontend displays that data to the user\", \"q_fe_8\": \"State management: The way a frontend application stores, updates, and shares data (state) between components.\\nWhat “state” means: Data that can change, like user input, login status, or a shopping cart.\\nWhy it’s needed: To keep the UI in sync with data changes across different parts of the app.\", \"q_fe_9\": \"Optimize images: Compress and use modern formats like WebP.\\nMinify code: Reduce size of HTML, CSS, and JavaScript files.\\nLazy loading: Load images or components only when needed.\\nCode splitting: Load only required JavaScript for each page.\\nCaching: Store data in the browser to avoid repeated downloads.\\nUse CDN: Deliver files faster from servers closer to users.\\nReduce unnecessary re-renders: Improve React component performance.\", \"q_fe_10\": \"Check event handler: Make sure onClick is properly attached to the button.\\nCheck console errors: Look for JavaScript errors that might stop execution.\\nInspect the element: Use browser DevTools to see if the button is disabled or covered by another element.\\nVerify function logic: Ensure the click function is actually being called and working.\\nCheck state updates: If using React, confirm state is updating correctly.\\nTest in isolation: Remove other code to see if the issue still happens.\"}','https://github.com/simontamenedev/A-simple-web-app-for-collecting-and-displaying-user-information','not set','{\"pressure\": 5, \"teamwork\": 5, \"adaptability\": 5, \"communication\": 5, \"problemSolving\": 5}',NULL,NULL,NULL),(10,'Abdi Megersa','abdimegersa14@gmail.com','900901056','','','','Male',NULL,'https://linkedin.com/in/abdi-frost','','','','','','','','',NULL,NULL,NULL,'','','','',NULL,'','','Immediate',NULL,NULL,'',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'Yes','Yes',NULL,'Yes','No',NULL,'[]','[]','[]',NULL,'','No','No','No','No',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'','','','',NULL,NULL,NULL,'No','No',NULL,'No',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'No',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'SWAFRI stands out to me because it represents a real opportunity to collaborate with people from different backgrounds, share knowledge, and learn from diverse perspectives. The idea of a Swiss-African partnership is especially appealing since it creates space for innovation, growth, and meaningful exchange across regions, which is something I value a lot as a developer.',NULL,'','',1,0,1,0,NULL,'2026-04-19 15:48:47','2026-04-19 15:48:47','[\"full-stack\"]','{\"q_fs_1\": \"Frontend development focuses on the user interface and user experience, dealing with what users see and interact with directly in a web application. It involves HTML, CSS, and JavaScript to build the layout, design, and interactive elements of a website or web app.\\n\\nBackend development, on the other hand, deals with the server-side logic and infrastructure of a web application. It focuses on how the application works behind the scenes, including managing databases, handling server requests, and ensuring data security. Backend developers use languages like Python, Java, and Node.js, along with databases like MySQL, Postgres or MongoDB.\\n\\nIn a web application, the frontend and backend work together seamlessly. The frontend sends requests to the backend for data or actions, and the backend processes these requests and sends back the appropriate response, which the frontend then displays to the user. \", \"q_fs_2\": \"An API (Application Programming Interface) is a set of rules and specifications that software programs can follow to communicate with each other. It allows different applications to exchange data and functionality without needing to know the details of how the other application is implemented.\\n\\nIn the context of frontend and backend communication, the API acts as an intermediary. The frontend sends requests to the backend through the API. These requests typically ask for specific data or to perform certain actions.\\n\\nThe backend processes these requests and sends back responses, usually in a structured format like JSON. The frontend then receives the response and uses the data to update the user interface or perform other necessary tasks.\", \"q_fs_3\": \"SQL databases are relational, using a structured schema to store data in tables with rows and columns, and they use SQL for querying. NoSQL databases are non-relational, offering various data models like document, key-value, and graph, providing flexibility and scalability, and they use different query languages.\", \"q_fs_4\": \"Authentication verifies the identity of a user, confirming that they are who they claim to be. Authorization determines what resources a user has access to after they\'ve been authenticated. In simpler terms, authentication is like showing your ID to enter a building, and authorization is like having a key that only unlocks certain rooms within that building.\", \"q_fs_5\": \"Git and version control are essential in software development for several reasons:\\n\\nTracking Changes - They allow developers to track every modification made to the codebase, including who made the changes and when. This creates a detailed history of the project.\\n\\nCollaboration - Version control systems like Git enable multiple developers to work on the same project simultaneously without overwriting each other\'s changes.\\n\\nBranching and Merging - Git facilitates the creation of branches, which are independent lines of development. This allows developers to work on new features or bug fixes in isolation without affecting the main codebase. Once the changes are complete, they can be merged back into the main branch.\\n\\nReverting to Previous States - If a bug is introduced or a change needs to be undone, version control makes it easy to revert to a previous version of the code.\\n\\nCode Backup and Recovery-  Version control systems provide a backup of the codebase, protecting against data loss due to hardware failures or other unforeseen events.\\n\\nIn summary, Git and version control enhance collaboration, track changes, enable experimentation, and safeguard the codebase, making software development more efficient and reliable.\", \"q_fs_6\": \"Responsive design is an approach to building interfaces where layouts, images, and components automatically adapt to different screen sizes and devices, instead of staying fixed to one rigid format. It relies on flexible grids, scalable media, and CSS techniques like media queries so the same app or website can look and function properly on phones, tablets, and desktops without needing separate versions.\\n\\n\", \"q_fs_7\": \"REST APIs (Representational State Transfer Application Programming Interfaces) are a way for different systems to communicate over the web using standard HTTP methods like GET, POST, PUT, and DELETE, where resources (like users, products, or orders) are identified by URLs and data is usually exchanged in formats like JSON. They follow a stateless structure, meaning each request contains all the information needed, which keeps things simple and scalable.\\n\\nThey’re widely used in modern applications because they’re easy to understand, lightweight, and work seamlessly across different platforms and languages, making them perfect for connecting frontends, backends, and third-party services\", \"q_fs_8\": \"State management in frontend applications is the process of handling and organizing the data (state) that determines how your UI looks and behaves at any given moment, such as user input, API responses, UI toggles, or authentication status. Instead of letting data live chaotically across components, state management provides a structured way to store, update, and share that data so the UI stays consistent and predictable as users interact with the app.\", \"q_fs_9\": \"Improving frontend performance is mostly about reducing how much work the browser has to do and how fast users see something useful, which you can achieve with techniques like code splitting, lazy loading components, optimizing images, minimizing bundle size, caching assets, and avoiding unnecessary re-renders through memoization or efficient state updates. On top of that, using CDNs, compressing files, and prioritizing critical rendering paths helps pages load faster and feel smoother.\\n\\nOn the backend side, performance comes down to handling requests efficiently and scaling well under load, which involves optimizing database queries, adding caching layers (like in-memory stores), using asynchronous processing for heavy tasks, load balancing traffic, and reducing unnecessary API calls. Writing efficient algorithms, indexing databases properly, and monitoring bottlenecks also play a big role, since even small inefficiencies can stack up fast when your app starts getting real traffic\", \"q_fs_10\": \"When users report a broken feature, the first move is to reproduce the issue reliably by using the same steps, environment, and data they’re using, because guessing without reproducing usually wastes time. Once it’s reproducible, I narrow it down where it’s failing by checking logs, browser console errors, network requests, and recent changes, then isolate whether the problem is coming from the frontend, backend, or an external dependency. From there, I test assumptions step by step inspecting state, API responses, and edge cases until you pinpoint the root cause.\\n\\nAfter identifying the issue, I fix it in a controlled way, verify the solution locally and in staging, and make sure it doesn’t break other parts of the system by running tests or manually checking related flows. \"}','https://github.com/abdi-frost/talent-hub','not set','{\"pressure\": 4, \"teamwork\": 5, \"adaptability\": 5, \"communication\": 5, \"problemSolving\": 5}',NULL,NULL,NULL),(11,'Tsedeniya Zewdu','tsedeniyazewdu116@gmail.com','972724131','','','','Female',NULL,'https://www.linkedin.com/in/tsedeniya-zewdu','','','','','','','','',NULL,NULL,NULL,'','','','',NULL,'','','Immediate',NULL,NULL,'',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'Yes','Yes',NULL,'No','No',NULL,'[]','[]','[]',NULL,'','No','No','No','No',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'','','','',NULL,NULL,NULL,'No','No',NULL,'No',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'No',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'I am applying to SWAFRI because I am seeking a rigorous environment that will strengthen my technical competencies and expose me to global standards of practice, particularly in the field of technology and innovation. I am especially interested in opportunities that foster cross-border collaboration, applied learning, and engagement with diverse professional perspectives.\n\nThe Swiss-African partnership is particularly appealing to me because it represents a structured collaboration between two regions with complementary strengths: Switzerland’s excellence in innovation, precision, and research-driven development, and Africa’s rapidly growing technological ecosystem and contextual problem-solving capabilities. I am motivated by the opportunity to learn within such a framework where knowledge exchange is intentional, practical, and globally relevant.\n\nIn the long term, I aim to build a career that enables me to work in international and remote professional environments, contributing to impactful projects while continuously advancing my technical expertise. I view SWAFRI as a strategic stepping stone toward achieving this goal, as it offers the exposure, discipline, and network necessary to develop into a globally competitive professional.\n',NULL,'','',1,0,1,0,NULL,'2026-04-19 19:59:48','2026-04-19 19:59:48','[\"front-end\"]','{\"q_fe_1\": \"HTML (HyperText Markup Language) defines the semantic structure and content hierarchy of a document. It provides the foundational elements such as headings, paragraphs, forms, and media embeds.\\nCSS (Cascading Style Sheets) controls visual presentation—layout, colors, typography, spacing, and responsive behavior. It separates design from structure, enabling consistent theming across pages.\\nJavaScript introduces dynamic behavior and interactivity. It handles user events, manipulates the DOM asynchronously, fetches data from APIs, and manages.\\nIn simple terms:\\n HTML = structure, CSS = design, JavaScript = behavior\\n\\n\", \"q_fe_2\": \"Responsive design is an approach to web development where a website automatically adapts to different screen sizes and devices (mobile, tablet, desktop).\\nIt uses techniques like flexible layouts, media queries, and responsive images to provide a consistent and usable experience on any device.\\nWhy it is important:\\nBetter user experience: Users can easily read, navigate, and interact with the website on any device without zooming or scrolling sideways.\\nMobile usage is dominant: Most users access websites from mobile devices, so responsiveness is essential to reach and retain users.\\nImproves SEO: Search engines like Google rank mobile-friendly websites higher in search results.\\nHigher engagement and conversions: A smooth experience keeps users longer on the site and increases the chances they take action (sign up, buy, etc.).\\nCost and time efficient: One responsive website removes the need to build and maintain separate mobile and desktop versions.\", \"q_fe_3\": \"Server-side rendering (SSR) and client-side rendering (CSR) differ in where and when the HTML document is constructed. SSR builds the HTML on the server for each request, while CSR builds it in the browser after receiving a minimal HTML shell and JavaScript bundle.\\nIn SSR, the server receives a request and executes JavaScript components directly in a Node.js environment to generate a complete HTML string. This HTML is sent to the browser and displayed immediately. After the user sees the content, the browser downloads a JavaScript bundle — which contains the same component code but bundled, minified, and transpiled. The browser then executes this bundle to hydrate the page, attaching event listeners and enabling full interactivity.\\nAdvantages of SSR: Faster Time to First Paint because content appears immediately. Superior SEO since search engine crawlers receive fully populated HTML. Works reliably on slow devices or with JavaScript disabled.\\nDisadvantages of SSR: Higher server load and compute costs. Slower subsequent navigation because each route change requires a full server round-trip. More complex deployment and caching strategies.\\nIn CSR, the server sends a bare-bones HTML document containing little more than a root element (e.g., <div id=\\\"app\\\"></div>) and a reference to one or more JavaScript bundle files. The browser downloads and executes these bundles, which then fetch data from APIs, construct the DOM dynamically, and render the complete page. All rendering happens in the browser. There is no server-side execution of your UI components.\\nAdvantages of CSR: Smooth, fast navigation after the initial load because only data — not full HTML — is fetched between route changes. Reduced server workload since rendering is offloaded to clients. Better suited for highly interactive, app-like experiences.\\nDisadvantages of CSR: Slower initial page load — users see a blank or loading screen until JavaScript downloads and executes. SEO challenges because some search engines may not reliably execute JavaScript. Requires JavaScript to be enabled in the browser.\\n\", \"q_fe_4\": \"\\nJavaScript frameworks like React, Vue, and Angular exist to solve three fundamental problems in frontend development: managing complex user interfaces efficiently, maintaining predictable application state, and enabling reusable, maintainable code at scale. Without frameworks, large applications built with vanilla JavaScript quickly devolve into \\\"spaghetti code\\\" — tightly coupled, difficult to debug, and painful to extend.\\nJavaScript frameworks like React and Vue solve three fundamental problems in frontend development.\\nFirst, imperative DOM manipulation is hard to maintain at scale.** In vanilla JavaScript, developers manually update the UI step by step using methods like `document.getElementById` and `element.innerText`. As applications grow, manually tracking which elements change and when leads to bugs and unmaintainable code. Frameworks introduce declarative programming, where developers describe what the UI should look like for a given state, and the framework handles all DOM updates automatically and efficiently.\\nSecond, state management becomes unmanageable without a framework.** In vanilla JavaScript, data is scattered across DOM elements, global variables, and event handlers. Keeping the same data synchronized across multiple parts of the UI requires manual effort and frequently causes inconsistencies. Frameworks provide a unidirectional data flow and reactive state system, so when state changes, every component that depends on it automatically re-renders with the new value.\\nThird, reusability requires a standard pattern that vanilla JavaScript lacks.** Vanilla JavaScript has no built-in way to encapsulate UI markup, styles, and behavior into a reusable unit. Developers resort to copying and pasting HTML, violating the DRY principle and making global changes difficult. Frameworks introduce components — independent, reusable units that encapsulate their own structure, appearance, and logic. A component written once can be used anywhere, and changes propagate to all instances automatically.\\nAdditional Benefits\\nPerformance optimization: Frameworks use techniques like React\'s virtual DOM or Vue\'s reactive proxy system to batch DOM updates and minimize expensive reflows and repaints. Manually achieving this level of performance is extremely difficult.\\nDeveloper experience: Frameworks provide excellent tooling — hot module replacement (instant feedback without full page reloads), browser devtools extensions, type safety with TypeScript integration, and large ecosystems of third-party libraries.\\nCommunity and standards: Using a popular framework means access to thousands of reusable components, consistent patterns that new team members already understand, and battle-tested solutions for common problems like routing, form handling, and data fetching.\\nJavaScript frameworks like React and Vue solve the core challenges of building interactive web applications at scale by providing declarative rendering, predictable state management, and reusable component architectures — freeing developers from manually manipulating the DOM and synchronizing data across the UI.\\n\", \"q_fe_5\": \"A component is the fundamental building block of modern frontend frameworks like React, Vue, and Angular. It is a self-contained, reusable unit that encapsulates its own structure (HTML-like markup), presentation (CSS styles), and behavior (JavaScript logic) into a single, cohesive piece of code.\\nComponents accept inputs, known as **props** (short for properties), which allow data to be passed from a parent component to a child component. They also manage their own internal **state**, which represents data that can change over time and triggers automatic re-rendering when updated. This combination of props and state makes components dynamic and interactive.\\nThe primary advantages of components are reusability, composability, isolation, and maintainability. A button, modal, form field, or card can be written once as a component and then reused across hundreds of pages. Components can be composed together like building blocks — a larger component such as a user profile card can be built from smaller components like an avatar, a username, and a follow button. Each component is isolated, meaning changes made to one component do not unintentionally affect others. This isolation also makes components easy to test and debug in isolation from the rest of the application.\\nIn practice, a component in React might look like a function that returns JSX, while in Vue it might be a single-file component containing a template, script, and style section. Regardless of the framework, the core concept remains the same: components allow developers to break complex user interfaces into small, manageable, and reusable pieces.\\n\", \"q_fe_6\": \"Local storage and cookies are both browser-based storage mechanisms, but they differ significantly in capacity, scope, automatic transmission, expiration, and security characteristics.\\nLocal storage provides a larger storage capacity of approximately 5 to 10 megabytes per origin, which is significantly more than cookies. Data stored in local storage is never automatically sent to the server with HTTP requests — it remains exclusively on the client and must be accessed manually via JavaScript. Local storage has no built-in expiration mechanism, meaning data persists indefinitely until explicitly cleared by the application or the user. It is accessible only through JavaScript running on the same origin, making it suitable for storing client-side preferences, theme settings, or cached data that does not require server interaction.\\n\\n\\nCookies, by contrast, have a very small storage limit of approximately 4 kilobytes per cookie. They are automatically included in the `Cookie` header of every HTTP request made to the server for the same domain, which can impact network performance if many large cookies are set. Cookies support configurable expiration dates using the `Expires` or `Max-Age` attributes, after which they are automatically deleted. Most importantly, cookies can be marked with the `HttpOnly` flag, which makes them inaccessible to JavaScript and therefore immune to cross-site scripting (XSS) attacks. When combined with the `Secure` flag (requiring HTTPS) and `SameSite` attribute (preventing cross-site request forgery), cookies become a more secure choice for storing sensitive data like session identifiers and authentication tokens.\\nThe practical rule of thumb is to use local storage for non-sensitive, client-only data such as user preferences, theme settings, or offline content. Use cookies — specifically `HttpOnly`, `Secure`, and `SameSite=Strict` or `Lax` — for authentication tokens and session identifiers that must be sent to the server automatically and need protection against XSS attacks. Avoid storing sensitive information like passwords or personal identifiable information in either storage mechanism without proper encryption.\", \"q_fe_7\": \" An API (Application Programming Interface) is a contract that defines how two software systems — such as a frontend browser application and a backend server — exchange data and requests. In web development, the API is typically exposed over HTTP or HTTPS and follows REST (Representational State Transfer) principles or GraphQL conventions.\\nHow frontend communicates with backend: The frontend application initiates an asynchronous HTTP request to a specific API endpoint (a URL). The backend receives the request, authenticates and authorizes the caller if necessary, performs the requested business logic (such as querying a database or calling another service), and returns a structured response — almost always in JSON (JavaScript Object Notation) format. The frontend then parses this response, updates its internal state, and re-renders the user interface accordingly.\\nCommon HTTP methods and their purposes: GET retrieves data from the server without modifying anything. POST creates a new resource on the server. PUT or PATCH updates an existing resource. DELETE removes a resource. Each request includes a URL, an HTTP method, optional headers (such as authentication tokens or content type declarations), and an optional body containing data for POST, PUT, or PATCH requests.\\nImplementation in modern frontends: Developers use the built-in fetch() function or libraries like Axios to make API calls. A typical pattern involves handling three states: loading (while the request is in progress), success (when data is returned), and error (when the request fails). For example, a frontend might call fetch(\'/api/users/123\'), await the JSON response, and then display the user\'s name. The backend API endpoint at that URL would execute the necessary logic and return something like { \\\"id\\\": 123, \\\"name\\\": \\\"Alice\\\" }.\\nAPIs are stateless by convention in REST, meaning each request contains all the information needed for the server to process it without relying on previous requests. Authentication is often handled via tokens (such as JWT) sent in the Authorization header or via HttpOnly cookies. Error handling uses standard HTTP status codes — 200 for success, 400 for bad client requests, 401 for unauthorized, 404 for not found, and 500 for server errors.\", \"q_fe_8\": \"State management in frontend applications focuses on **how data is stored, updated, and shared across the UI in a predictable and efficient way**, ensuring consistency between user interactions, API data, and rendered views. The most effective approaches fall into four categories: local state handling, global state management, server state management, and architectural patterns for data flow.\\n Local state management\\nLocal state refers to data that is confined to a single component and does not need to be shared widely. It is typically managed using built-in React hooks such as `useState` or `useReducer`. Examples include form inputs, toggle states, modals, and UI interactions. Local state is simple, fast, and ideal when data does not need to be accessed outside the component. However, when the same state is required in multiple places, lifting state up or using shared state mechanisms becomes necessary.\\nGlobal state management\\nGlobal state is used when multiple components across different parts of the application need access to the same data. Common examples include authentication status, user profiles, shopping cart data, and application-wide settings like theme or language.\\nReact’s Context API provides a built-in way to manage global state without prop drilling, making it suitable for simple shared data. However, for large-scale applications, dedicated libraries like Redux (especially Redux Toolkit) are more scalable. Redux centralizes state in a single store and uses actions and reducers to ensure predictable state updates. Middleware like Redux-Saga or Redux Thunk handles side effects such as API calls, keeping business logic separated from UI logic.\\n Server state management\\nServer state refers to data that comes from external APIs or backend services. Unlike local or global UI state, server state is asynchronous, cacheable, and often shared across multiple components.\\n\\n\\nInstead of manually managing loading, error, and caching logic, modern applications often use libraries like React Query (TanStack Query) or SWR. These tools automatically handle caching, background refetching, synchronization, and stale data management. This reduces boilerplate and improves performance by avoiding unnecessary network requests and redundant state updates.\\nState architecture and data flow patterns\\nBeyond tools, proper state management depends on how data flows through the application. A common pattern in Redux-based systems is a unidirectional data flow:\\nUser Action → Dispatch Action → Middleware (optional async logic) → Reducer updates store → UI re-renders based on new state\\nThis predictable flow ensures that state changes are traceable and easier to debug. It also prevents inconsistent UI behavior caused by uncontrolled state mutations.\\nFor complex applications, separating concerns (UI, state logic, API services, and side effects) improves maintainability. For example, API calls are often handled in services or sagas, while reducers remain pure and focused only on updating state.\\n Performance considerations in state management\\nEfficient state management also impacts performance. Keeping unnecessary data in global state can cause excessive re-renders, so state should be stored at the lowest appropriate level. Memoization techniques like `React.memo`, `useMemo`, and `useCallback` help prevent unnecessary updates. Normalizing state structures (especially in Redux) reduces duplication and improves update efficiency. Additionally, avoiding frequent global state updates for rapidly changing UI data (like input fields or scroll positions) prevents performance bottlenecks.\\nMeasurement and maintainability\\nEffective state management is not only about structure but also about observability. Debugging tools like Redux DevTools help track state changes over time. Monitoring re-render behavior using React DevTools ensures components are not updating unnecessarily. Performance should be guided by real application behavior rather than assumptions, especially in large-scale frontend systems.\\nIn summary, state management is about controlling complexity in data flow, ensuring that UI stays consistent, scalable, and performant as an application grows.\\n\", \"q_fe_9\": \"Frontend performance optimization focuses on reducing load times, minimizing network requests, and ensuring smooth rendering and interactivity. The most effective techniques fall into four categories: resource optimization, network efficiency, rendering performance, and JavaScript execution.\\nResource optimization: Images are typically the largest assets on a page. Serve modern formats like WebP or AVIF instead of JPEG or PNG. Compress all images without noticeable quality loss and implement lazy loading using the loading=\\\"lazy\\\" attribute so images load only when they enter the viewport. Minify CSS, JavaScript, and HTML by removing whitespace, comments, and unused code. Use CSS sprites for small icons to reduce multiple HTTP requests.\\nNetwork efficiency: Enable compression on the server using Brotli or Gzip to reduce file sizes during transmission. Leverage browser caching by setting appropriate Cache-Control headers so returning visitors do not re-download unchanged assets. Use a Content Delivery Network (CDN) to serve static assets from servers geographically closer to users. Implement code splitting — a technique where JavaScript is broken into smaller chunks loaded only when needed — using dynamic imports. Preload critical resources (like fonts or hero images) with <link rel=\\\"preload\\\"> and prefetch less important resources for future navigation.\\nRendering performance: Reduce layout thrashing by batching DOM reads and writes instead of alternating between them. Avoid expensive CSS selectors (e.g., those that traverse many elements) and minimize the use of JavaScript animations that trigger repaints. Use the will-change CSS property for elements that animate frequently. Virtualize long lists using libraries like react-window so only visible items are rendered in the DOM. Keep the number of DOM elements as low as possible, as each element consumes memory and affects reflow speed.\\nJavaScript execution: Defer or async non-critical JavaScript so it does not block HTML parsing. Remove unused dependencies and polyfills by analyzing bundles with tools like Webpack Bundle Analyzer. Debounce or throttle event handlers attached to scroll, resize, or input events to prevent excessive function calls. Avoid long-running JavaScript tasks that block the main thread — break them into smaller chunks using setTimeout or requestIdleCallback. Use Web Workers for computationally heavy operations to keep the UI responsive.\\nMeasurement and monitoring: Performance work must be data-driven. Use Lighthouse (in Chrome DevTools) to generate performance scores and actionable recommendations. Monitor Core Web Vitals — Largest Contentful Paint (loading), First Input Delay (interactivity), and Cumulative Layout Shift (visual stability). Regularly test on real devices and network conditions, not just fast development environments. Optimize based on metrics, not assumptions.\\n\", \"q_fe_10\": \"Debugging a non-responsive button requires a systematic, hypothesis-driven approach that eliminates potential causes from most likely to least likely.\\nFirst, check the browser console for JavaScript errors. Any uncaught exception in the JavaScript execution thread can halt all subsequent code, including event listeners attached to the button. Fixing syntax errors, reference errors, or network failures often resolves the issue immediately.\\nSecond, verify that the button exists in the DOM and has the expected properties.Use browser DevTools to inspect the element. Confirm that the button does not have a `disabled` attribute, which prevents clicks. Check for CSS properties such as `pointer-events: none`, which makes the element completely unresponsive to mouse interactions. Also verify that no transparent overlay element (such as a modal backdrop or loading spinner) is positioned above the button with a higher `z-index`, capturing the click before it reaches the button.\\nThird, confirm that the event listener is properly attached. In DevTools, select the button and use the `getEventListeners(element)` command to see all registered event handlers. If no click listener appears, the listener was never attached — possibly due to the element being added to the DOM after the listener registration code ran. This commonly occurs in dynamic applications where content loads asynchronously. The solution is to either attach the listener after the element exists or use event delegation, where a parent element listens for events that bubble up from child elements.\\nFourth, check for JavaScript that prevents default behavior or stops propagation.Look for calls to `event.preventDefault()` (which stops the button\'s default action) or `event.stopPropagation()` (which prevents the event from bubbling up to parent listeners). These are often intentional but may be applied incorrectly or conditionally.\\nFifth, test if the issue is environmental or code-specific. Use the DevTools console to manually trigger a click by running `$0.click()` after selecting the button. If this works, the button itself is functional, and the problem lies in how the click event is being blocked or not triggered. If the manual click fails, the button\'s event handler logic may be broken.\\nSixth, isolate the issue by simplifying. Temporarily comment out unrelated JavaScript, CSS, or third-party scripts to rule out conflicts. If the button works in isolation, reintroduce code incrementally until the conflict is identified. Reproduce the issue in a minimal test case — such as CodeSandbox or a local HTML file — to determine whether the bug originates in the application code, a framework behavior, or a browser extension.\\nSeventh, consider framework-specific issues. In React or Vue, a component may not re-render when state changes due to incorrect state updates (e.g., mutating state directly instead of using setter functions). In SSR applications, hydration mismatches between server-generated HTML and client-rendered content can cause event listeners to fail to attach. Use framework DevTools extensions to inspect component state and props.\\nThe key principle is to start with the simplest, most probable cause — a console error or a `disabled` attribute — before moving to complex possibilities like z-index layering or event propagation. Document each step as you go, and change only one variable at a time to clearly identify the root cause.\\n\"}','https://github.com/Tsedeniya-zewdu/TalentProfile','not set','{\"pressure\": 5, \"teamwork\": 5, \"adaptability\": 5, \"communication\": 5, \"problemSolving\": 5}',NULL,NULL,NULL),(12,'Yabets Kifle','yabetskifle9@gmail.com','942750724','','','','Male',NULL,'https://www.linkedin.com/in/yabets-kifle/','','','','','','','','',NULL,NULL,NULL,'','','','',NULL,'','','Immediate',NULL,NULL,'',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'Yes','Yes',NULL,'No','No',NULL,'[]','[]','[]',NULL,'','No','No','No','No',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'','','','',NULL,NULL,NULL,'No','No',NULL,'No',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'No',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'As a frontend developer with two years of experience working with React, Next.js, Tailwind CSS, JavaScript, TypeScript, HTML, and CSS, I am always looking for opportunities to grow and contribute to meaningful projects.\n\nWhat appeals to me most about SWAFRI is the strong Swiss–African partnership that connects African talent with global opportunities. I am excited by the chance to work with international teams, learn from different cultures, and contribute to projects that follow high-quality international standards.\n\nI believe this collaboration is powerful because it combines Swiss precision and professionalism with the creativity and potential of African developers. Through SWAFRI, I hope to further develop my skills, contribute to innovative web applications, and help showcase the strength of African tech talent on the global stage.\n',NULL,'','',1,0,1,0,NULL,'2026-04-20 07:49:11','2026-04-20 07:49:11','[\"front-end\"]','{\"q_fe_1\": \"HTML or Hypertext Markup Language\\nHTML makes up this web page’s skeleton and structure, and almost every other web page you’ll ever visit. It’s a markup language that “marks up” and forms the backbone to online content, putting the site structure in place for the web browser to understand.\\n\\nCSS or Cascading Style Sheets\\nWhereas HTML makes up the structure, or bones, of the site, CSS adds the skin - the aesthetics you see and navigate. The CSS on a webpage designs it and makes it look more attractive for the visitor reading the page.\\nCSS, shape the HTML and affects how it’s displayed to the website visitor. These forms of styling include font color, background color, border-radius (rounded corners on boxes), the position of columns, hue, opacity, and separation. New CSS features even allow for animations, flexbox gaps, and much more responsive design.\\n\\nJavaScript:\\nJavaScript is a versatile, dynamically typed programming language that brings life to web pages by making them interactive. It is used for building interactive web applications, supports both client-side and server-side development, and integrates seamlessly with HTML, CSS, and a rich standard library.\\nJavaScript is a single-threaded language that executes one task at a time. It is an interpreted language which means it executes the code line by line. The data type of the variable is decided at run-time in JavaScript, which is why it is called dynamically typed.\\n\\nThe main three languages used in web page development are HTML, CSS, and JavaScript. In general:\\n    HTML provides the structure, CSS provides the presentation and formatting, and JavaScript makes the elements dynamic and controls their behavior.\", \"q_fe_2\": \"Responsive design is a web development approach that ensures a website automatically adapts to different screen sizes and devices, such as desktops, tablets, and smartphones.\\nResponsive design uses techniques such as:\\nFlexible layouts, CSS media queries, Responsive images, Fluid grids\\nIt is important because:\\nUsers access websites on many different devices.\\nIt improves user experience and accessibility.\\nSearch engines like Google favor mobile-friendly websites.\\nIt reduces the need to create separate mobile and desktop versions.\", \"q_fe_3\": \"Client-side rendering (CSR) refers to the practice of generating HTML content using JavaScript in the browser. CSR is opposed to server-side rendering, where the server generates the HTML content. \\nIt\'s advantage: Highly interactive applications, Faster navigation after initial load\\n\\nServer-side rendering (SSR) refers to the practice of generating HTML content on the server and sending it to the client. SSR is opposed to client-side rendering, where the client generates the HTML content using JavaScript.  \\nIt\'s Advantage: Faster initial page load, Better SEO for search engines.\\n\", \"q_fe_4\": \"JavaScript frameworks like React and Vue help developers build modern web applications more efficiently by providing tools and structured ways to manage UI development.\\nTheir main purposes include:\\nCreating reusable components\\nManaging application state\\nEfficient DOM updates through virtual DOM\\nImproving code organization and maintainability\\nSimplifying development of complex user interfaces\\n\\nFrameworks reduce repetitive coding and allow developers to build scalable and maintainable applications.\", \"q_fe_5\": \"A component is a reusable and independent piece of a user interface that contains its own structure, logic, and styling.\\nExamples of components include: Navigation bars, Buttons, Forms, Cards.\\nComponents allow developers to break large applications into smaller, manageable pieces. They improve maintainability and enable code reuse across the application.\", \"q_fe_6\": \"Local Storage:\\nStores data locally in the user\'s browser\\nStorage capacity is usually around 5–10 MB\\nData does not expire unless manually deleted\\nData is not automatically sent to the server\\n\\nCookies:\\nSmall pieces of data stored in the browser\\nMaximum size around 4 KB\\nCan have expiration dates\\nAutomatically sent to the server with HTTP requests\\n\\nCookies are often used for authentication or session tracking, while local storage is typically used for storing client-side data such as user preferences.\", \"q_fe_7\": \"An API (Application Programming Interface) is a set of rules that allows different software systems to communicate with each other.\\nIn web applications, APIs allow the frontend to request or send data to a backend server.\\nFrontend applications communicate with APIs using HTTP requests such as:\\nGET – retrieve data\\nPOST – send new data\\nPUT/PATCH – update data\\nDELETE – remove data\\n\\nJavaScript methods like fetch() or libraries such as Axios are commonly used to interact with APIs.\", \"q_fe_8\": \"State management refers to the process of handling and controlling application data that can change over time.\\nState may include:\\nUser authentication status\\nForm input values\\nApplication settings\\nData fetched from APIs\\n\\nManaging state properly ensures that the user interface updates correctly when data changes.\", \"q_fe_9\": \"Minifying CSS and JavaScript to reduce file size\\nLazy loading images and components so they load only when needed\\nCode splitting to load only necessary code\\nUsing Content Delivery Networks (CDN) to serve files faster\\nOptimizing images using compressed formats\\nReducing HTTP requests\\nBrowser caching to reuse previously loaded resources\\n\\nThese techniques help reduce load time and improve website performance.\", \"q_fe_10\": \"To debug a button that does not respond when clicked, I would follow these steps:\\nCheck the browser console for JavaScript errors using Developer Tools.\\nInspect the button element to ensure it exists and is not disabled.\\nVerify that the event listener is correctly attached to the button.\\nAdd console.log() inside the click handler to confirm whether the function runs.\\nEnsure the JavaScript file is properly loaded.\\nCheck whether CSS or another element is blocking the button.\\nTest the functionality in different browsers if necessary.\"}','https://github.com/jabez-abija2399/talent-profile','not set','{\"pressure\": 4, \"teamwork\": 4, \"adaptability\": 4, \"communication\": 4, \"problemSolving\": 5}',NULL,NULL,NULL),(13,'Melaku  Azene','melakuazene623@gmail.com','918219856','','','','Male',NULL,'https://www.linkedin.com/in/melaku-azene-1863a23ab/','','','','','','','','',NULL,NULL,NULL,'','','','',NULL,'','','1-2 Weeks',NULL,NULL,'',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'Yes','Yes',NULL,'Yes','No',NULL,'[]','[]','[]',NULL,'','No','No','No','No',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'','','','',NULL,NULL,NULL,'No','No',NULL,'No',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'No',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'I’m especially interested in SWAFRI because it’s not just building software in isolation—it’s working on solutions that can make a real difference in African contexts while maintaining international standards. That kind of cross-cultural and technical environment is where I believe I can grow the most and also contribute meaningfully.',NULL,'','',1,0,1,0,NULL,'2026-04-20 13:09:13','2026-04-20 13:09:13','[\"backend\"]','{\"q_be_1\": \"A backend developer is responsible for designing, building, and maintaining the server-side logic that powers a web or mobile application. Their role is fundamentally about ensuring that the application functions correctly, securely, and efficiently behind the scenes.\\n\\nAt a technical level, this involves several core responsibilities:\\n\\n1. Business Logic Implementation\\nThey translate product requirements into executable logic. This includes handling workflows such as user authentication, data processing, validation, and enforcing rules (e.g., permissions, transactions, or recommendation algorithms).\\n\\n2. API Design and Integration\\nBackend developers expose functionality through APIs (typically REST or GraphQL). These APIs act as the contract between the frontend (React, Flutter, etc.) and the server, enabling data exchange and system interaction.\\n\\n3. Database Design and Management\\nThey design schemas, manage data models, and optimize queries for performance and consistency. This includes working with relational databases (like PostgreSQL, MySQL) or NoSQL systems (like MongoDB).\\n\\n4. Performance and Scalability\\nThey ensure the system handles load efficiently by optimizing queries, implementing caching, and designing scalable architectures (e.g., microservices or distributed systems when needed).\\n\\n5. Security and Data Protection\\nBackend developers implement authentication, authorization, encryption, and safeguards against vulnerabilities (e.g., SQL injection, XSS, CSRF).\\n\\n6. Integration with External Services\\nThey connect the application to third-party services such as payment gateways, notification systems (e.g., push notifications), email/SMS providers, or cloud services.\\n\\n7. System Reliability and Maintenance\\nThey monitor, debug, and maintain the system—handling errors, logging, and ensuring uptime through proper deployment and infrastructure practices.\\n\\nIn short, the backend developer ensures that data flows correctly, logic is enforced, and the system remains secure and scalable, enabling the frontend to deliver a seamless user experience.\", \"q_be_2\": \"An API (Application Programming Interface) is a set of rules that allows the frontend and backend to communicate with each other.\\n\\nIn simple terms, it acts like a bridge: the frontend sends a request (e.g., “get user data”), and the backend processes it and sends back a response.\\n\\nIt’s important because it:\\n\\nSeparates frontend and backend so they can be developed independently\\nStandardizes how data is requested and returned\\nEnables secure and controlled access to the system’s data and logic\\n\\nWithout APIs, the frontend wouldn’t be able to interact with the backend efficiently or reliably.\", \"q_be_3\": \"The main difference is how data is requested and returned.\\n\\nREST API\\n\\nUses multiple endpoints (e.g., /users, /posts)\\nEach endpoint returns a fixed structure of data\\nCan lead to over-fetching (getting extra data) or under-fetching (needing multiple requests)\\n\\nGraphQL\\n\\nUses a single endpoint\\nThe client specifies exactly what data it needs\\nAvoids over-fetching and under-fetching\", \"q_be_4\": \"SQL (Relational databases)\\n\\nUse structured tables (rows and columns)\\nHave a fixed schema (data format must be defined in advance)\\nUse SQL for queries\\nBest for complex queries and strong data consistency\\nExamples: MySQL, PostgreSQL\\n\\nNoSQL (Non-relational databases)\\n\\nUse flexible formats (JSON, key-value, documents, etc.)\\nSchema is dynamic (can change easily)\\nDesigned for scalability and large, unstructured data\\nExamples: MongoDB, Redis\", \"q_be_5\": \"Authentication is the process of verifying who the user is (e.g., login with email/password, OTP, token).\\n\\nAuthorization is about what the user is allowed to do after they are authenticated (e.g., admin vs regular user permissions).\\n\\nWhy they matter:\\n\\nProtect sensitive data\\nPrevent unauthorized access\\nEnsure users only perform actions they’re permitted to\", \"q_be_6\": \"Database indexing is a technique used to speed up data retrieval by creating a separate data structure (an index) that helps the database quickly locate rows without scanning the entire table.\\n\\nThink of it like a book index—you don’t read the whole book to find a topic, you jump directly to the page.\\n\\nHow it improves performance:\\n\\nReduces full table scans\\nSpeeds up SELECT queries, especially on large datasets\\nImproves search, filtering, and sorting operations\", \"q_be_7\": \"Synchronous operations run one after another—each task must finish before the next starts. This can make the system slower if a task takes time (e.g., waiting for a database response).\\n\\nAsynchronous operations allow tasks to run independently, so the system doesn’t wait. While one task is in progress (like an API call), the server can handle other requests.\\n\\nIn short:\\nSynchronous = blocking (waits)\\nAsynchronous = non-blocking (continues working)\\n\\nAsynchronous operations improve performance and scalability, especially in backend systems handling many users.\", \"q_be_8\": \"Common ways to secure a backend API include:\\n\\nAuthentication: verify users with JWT, OAuth, or API keys\\nAuthorization: enforce roles/permissions (e.g., admin vs user)\\nInput validation & sanitization: prevent SQL injection, XSS, etc.\\nHTTPS encryption: protect data in transit\\nRate limiting & throttling: prevent abuse and brute-force attacks\\nCORS configuration: control which clients can access your API\\nSecure storage: hash passwords (e.g., bcrypt) and protect secrets\\nLogging & monitoring: detect suspicious activity early\", \"q_be_9\": \"Caching is the practice of storing frequently accessed data in a faster storage layer (like memory) so it can be returned quickly instead of recomputing or querying the database every time.\\n\\nIt improves performance by:\\n\\nReducing database load\\nDecreasing response time (faster APIs)\\nImproving scalability under high traffic\\n\\nCommon examples include in-memory caches (e.g., Redis) or HTTP caching.\", \"q_be_10\": \"1. Reproduce the issue\\nFirst, confirm the error by calling the API myself (Postman, curl, or logs) to see the exact response and status code.\\n\\n2. Check logs immediately\\nLook at application logs, server logs, and error tracking tools to identify stack traces or failure points.\\n\\n3. Identify the error type\\n\\n4xx → client-side issue (bad request, auth, validation)\\n5xx → server-side issue (code bug, DB failure, crash)\\n\\n4. Trace the request flow\\nFollow the request from controller → service → database/external APIs to see where it breaks.\\n\\n5. Check recent changes\\nLook for recent deployments, config updates, or database changes that may have introduced the issue.\\n\\n6. Inspect dependencies\\nVerify external services (DB, third-party APIs, queues) are healthy and reachable.\\n\\n7. Debug and fix\\nAdd deeper logs if needed, reproduce locally, fix the root cause, and test thoroughly.\\n\\n8. Monitor after fix\\nEnsure errors are resolved and no new issues appear in production.\\n\\nIn short: reproduce → log analysis → isolate failure point → fix → verify.\"}','https://github.com/MelakuAzene21/Talent-Management-System','not set','{\"pressure\": 4, \"teamwork\": 4, \"adaptability\": 5, \"communication\": 5, \"problemSolving\": 5}',NULL,NULL,NULL),(14,'sofoniyas Tekalign','sofoniyastekalegn@gmail.com','978695556','','','','Male',NULL,'https://www.linkedin.com/in/sofoniyas-tekalegn-962868287/','','','','','','','','',NULL,NULL,NULL,'','','','',NULL,'','','Immediate',NULL,NULL,'',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'Yes','Yes',NULL,'No','No',NULL,'[]','[]','[]',NULL,'','No','No','No','No',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'','','','',NULL,NULL,NULL,'No','No',NULL,'No',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'No',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'SWAFRI stands out by combining senior-level talent, strong delivery quality, and smooth outsourcing support. They connect businesses with skilled African developers who bring real experience, modern tech skills, and reliable execution helping companies scale faster without hiring stress.',NULL,'','',1,0,1,0,NULL,'2026-04-20 18:31:22','2026-04-20 18:31:22','[\"front-end\"]','{\"q_fe_1\": \"HTML creates the main structure and content of a website such as text, images, buttons, and forms. CSS controls colors, spacing, layout, and responsiveness. JavaScript adds interactivity like clicks, sliders, animations, validation, and dynamic updates for users.\", \"q_fe_2\": \"Responsive design means a website adapts smoothly to mobile tablet and desktop screens  It is important because users browse on different devices so the site must look clean fast and easy to use everywhere\", \"q_fe_3\": \"client side rendering loads content in the browser using JavaScript while server-side rendering generates the page on the server first SSR improves SEO and first load speed, which is why I enjoy working with Next.js\", \"q_fe_4\": \"Frameworks like React and Vue.js help developers create fast, dynamic, and modern web applications. They provide reusable components, routing, state handling, cleaner code structure, and smoother user experiences while speeding up development.\", \"q_fe_5\": \"A component is a reusable UI block such as a button, navbar, card, modal, or footer. It allows developers to build interfaces faster by reusing code across pages. Components keep projects organized, scalable, easier to update, and more consistent in design and behavior.\", \"q_fe_6\": \"Local storage stores data directly in the browser with larger capacity and usually stays until manually removed. Cookies store smaller pieces of data and can expire automatically. Cookies are often used for sessions, login tokens, preferences, and communication with servers.\", \"q_fe_7\": \"An API is a bridge that allows the frontend and backend to exchange data securely. The frontend sends requests such as GET, POST, PUT, or DELETE. The backend processes the request, interacts with the database, then returns responses like JSON for display in the UI.\", \"q_fe_8\": \"State management means handling dynamic data that changes inside an application, such as form inputs, login status, selected filters, cart items, notifications, or theme mode. It ensures the interface updates correctly, stays organized, and provides a smooth user experience.\", \"q_fe_9\": \"To improve frontend performance, I use image optimization, lazy loading, code splitting, browser caching, minified CSS and JavaScript files, responsive assets, and clean lightweight animations. Server-side rendering also improves first load speed, SEO, and user experience.\", \"q_fe_10\": \"I check the browser console for errors, inspect the element verify click handlers, test CSS overlays blocking it review state logic  and reproduce the issue step by step until I find the root cause and fix it cleanly\"}','https://talent-pool-gamma.vercel.app/','not set','{\"pressure\": 4, \"teamwork\": 4, \"adaptability\": 3, \"communication\": 4, \"problemSolving\": 4}',NULL,NULL,NULL),(15,'Abel Shibabaw','abelshibabaw291@gmail.com','913174185','','','','Male',NULL,'https://www.linkedin.com/in/abel-shibabaw-4b3b6125a/','','','','','','','','',NULL,NULL,NULL,'','','','',NULL,'','','Immediate',NULL,NULL,'',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'Yes','Yes',NULL,'Yes','No',NULL,'[]','[]','[]',NULL,'','No','No','No','No',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'','','','',NULL,NULL,NULL,'No','No',NULL,'No',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'No',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'I applied to SWAFRI because the Swiss-African partnership aligns with my goal of building impactful, globally relevant software solutions. The opportunity to collaborate across continents, access international projects, and contribute to high-quality delivery standards strongly appeals to me. I value environments that emphasize both technical excellence and real-world impact, and SWAFRI represents a platform where I can grow while contributing meaningfully to cross-border innovation.\n',NULL,'','',1,0,1,0,NULL,'2026-04-24 19:03:19','2026-04-24 19:03:19','[\"full-stack\"]','{\"q_fs_1\": \"Frontend development focuses on everything users directly see and interact with in a web application, such as layout, design, and user interface, typically built using technologies like HTML, CSS, and JavaScript. Backend development handles the server-side logic, databases, and application functionality that process data and power the frontend. They work together by having the frontend send requests (like form submissions or data queries) to the backend, which processes them and returns the appropriate data or response, allowing the application to function smoothly as a complete system.\", \"q_fs_2\": \"An API (Application Programming Interface) is a structured way for different parts of a software system to communicate with each other. In a web application, the frontend uses APIs to request data or actions from the backend. This typically happens through HTTP requests (such as GET, POST, PUT, DELETE) sent to specific endpoints. The backend receives the request, processes it (for example, interacting with a database), and returns a response usually in JSON format which the frontend then uses to update the user interface.\\n\", \"q_fs_3\": \"SQL databases are structured and use predefined schemas, meaning data is stored in tables with fixed columns and relationships. They rely on structured query language (SQL) and are well-suited for applications that require strong consistency and complex queries, such as financial systems.\\n\\nNoSQL databases are more flexible and store data in formats like key-value pairs, documents, or graphs, without a fixed schema. This makes them easier to scale and adapt to changing data structures, which is useful for applications handling large volumes of unstructured or rapidly evolving data.\\n\", \"q_fs_4\": \"Authentication and authorization serve distinct but related roles in securing a web application. Authentication verifies the identity of a user confirming they are who they claim to be, typically through credentials like a username and password or tokens. Authorization, on the other hand, determines what that authenticated user is allowed to do, such as accessing specific resources, performing actions, or viewing certain data.\\n\\nThey work together in sequence: first, the system authenticates the user, and once verified, it applies authorization rules to control access and enforce permissions across the application.\\n\", \"q_fs_5\": \"Git is a distributed version control system that tracks changes to code over time, allowing developers to manage and maintain different versions of a project. Its purpose is to make collaboration safer and more organized by enabling multiple developers to work on the same codebase without overwriting each other’s work.\\n\\nVersion control, more broadly, provides a history of changes, making it possible to revert to earlier versions, compare modifications, and understand who made specific changes and why. Together, Git and version control help improve code quality, support teamwork through branching and merging, and reduce the risk of losing or breaking important parts of a project.\\n\", \"q_fs_6\": \"Responsive design is an approach to web development where a website’s layout and elements adapt to different screen sizes and devices, such as phones, tablets, and desktops. This is typically achieved using flexible layouts, fluid images, and CSS media queries.\\n\\nIt is important because users access web applications from a wide range of devices. Responsive design ensures a consistent and usable experience across all of them, improving accessibility, user satisfaction, and overall performance without needing separate versions of the same site.\\n\", \"q_fs_7\": \"REST APIs (Representational State Transfer APIs) are a type of web API that follow a set of architectural principles for building scalable and stateless communication between systems. They use standard HTTP methods such as GET, POST, PUT, and DELETE to perform operations on resources, which are typically identified by URLs and represented in formats like JSON.\\n\\nThey are commonly used in modern applications because they are simple, lightweight, and platform-independent. REST APIs allow frontend and backend systems to communicate efficiently, support scalability due to their stateless nature, and are easy to integrate with different services and technologies, making them a widely adopted standard for building web and mobile applications.\\n\", \"q_fs_8\": \"State management in frontend applications refers to how an application stores, organizes, and updates data (state) that affects what users see and interact with. This state can include things like user input, UI status (e.g., modals or loading states), and data fetched from a backend.\\n\\nIt is important because as applications grow, managing state across multiple components becomes complex. Proper state management ensures that data remains consistent, updates correctly when changes occur, and flows predictably through the application, improving maintainability and user experience.\\n\", \"q_fs_9\": \"Improving application performance involves reducing unnecessary work, optimizing resource usage, and ensuring efficient data flow on both the frontend and backend.\\n\\nOn the frontend, techniques include minimizing bundle size through code splitting and tree shaking, optimizing images and assets, using caching, and reducing unnecessary re-renders with efficient state management. Lazy loading components and using content delivery networks (CDNs) also help improve load times and responsiveness.\\n\\nOn the backend, performance can be improved by optimizing database queries, using indexing, and implementing caching mechanisms like Redis to reduce repeated computations. Efficient API design, load balancing, and asynchronous processing for heavy tasks also help handle higher traffic and reduce response times.\\n\\nOverall, the goal is to deliver data faster, process requests efficiently, and provide a smooth user experience under varying loads.\\n\", \"q_fs_10\": \"Start by clearly reproducing the issue using the same conditions reported by users, noting any error messages or unexpected behavior. This helps confirm whether the problem is consistent and where it occurs.\\n\\nNext, check logs and monitoring tools on both the frontend (browser console, network requests) and backend (server logs, API responses) to identify errors or failed requests. Isolate whether the issue is in the frontend, backend, or communication between them.\\n\\nThen, review the relevant code and recent changes that might have introduced the problem. Use debugging tools, breakpoints, or additional logging to trace the flow of data and pinpoint where it breaks.\\n\\nOnce the root cause is identified, implement a fix and test it thoroughly, including edge cases. Finally, deploy the fix and monitor the application to ensure the issue is resolved and does not recur.\\n\"}','https://github.com/abel-cosmic/swafri-talent','not set','{\"pressure\": 5, \"teamwork\": 5, \"adaptability\": 5, \"communication\": 5, \"problemSolving\": 5}',NULL,NULL,NULL),(16,'Rediet Tedila','rediettedila5@gmail.com','948146464','','','','Female',NULL,'https://www.linkedin.com/in/rediet-tedila-714262347?','','','','','','','','',NULL,NULL,NULL,'','','','',NULL,'','','Immediate',NULL,NULL,'',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'Yes','Yes',NULL,'Yes','No',NULL,'[]','[]','[]',NULL,'','No','No','No','No',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'','','','',NULL,NULL,NULL,'No','No',NULL,'No',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'No',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'What appeals to me about SWAFRI is the opportunity to work with developers and projects across the world, especially through a Swiss-African partnership that connects different tech cultures and ideas. It feels exciting to collaborate in a global environment where you can learn different approaches to building software and gain real international experience while working on meaningful projects.\nThis kind of exposure helps improve skills, communication, and problem-solving by working with diverse teams, and it also gives a chance to grow by contributing to real-world solutions on a wider scale.',NULL,'','',1,0,1,0,NULL,'2026-04-29 12:01:32','2026-04-29 12:01:32','[\"full-stack\"]','{\"q_fs_1\": \"FRONTEND development is everything the user sees and interacts with in the browser. It includes pages, buttons, forms, and layouts. In this project, it’s built with tools like React and Tailwind CSS. The frontend collects user input, shows data, and handles navigation.\\nBACKEND development is what runs on the server behind the scenes. It handles things like storing data, logging users in, applying rules, and managing security. It exposes APIs that the frontend can use to get or send data.\\nHow they work together:\\nThe frontend sends requests to the backend through APIs (like “login” or “get profile”). The backend processes those requests, talks to the database if needed, and sends back a response. The frontend then uses that response to update what the user sees.\", \"q_fs_2\": \"An API (Application Programming Interface) is a set of rules that allows different software systems to communicate and exchange data in a structured way. so it is like a  bridge that allows the frontend to request and receive data from the  backend in a structured and secure way.\\nHow communication happens through an API? Systems communicate mainly through API endpoints:\\nAn endpoint is a specific URL that performs a task (e.g. /auth/login).\\nOne system sends a request to an endpoint.\\nThe other system processes it and sends back a response.\\nso this communication usually includes HTTP methods (GET, POST, PUT, DELETE), Request data (like form inputs or JSON), Headers (like authentication tokens),Response data (like user info or success messages). over all APIs communicate through endpoints, where one system sends a request and another system returns a response. like in the Talent management system there is an endpoints  the React app uses endpoints like /auth/login and /user/profile to interact with the Express backend and exchange data securely.\", \"q_fs_3\": \"SQL database  is use structured data (tables with rows and columns) and use SQL (Structured Query Language) for queries and we must define structure before storing datas example of sql database is MySQL,PostgreSQL. like in this Talent management system i use a postgresql (SQL database)\\nbut NoSQL database is use unstructured or semi-structured data (JSON-like documents, key-value, etc.) and no fixed structure required and Good for large, dynamic data example of this database is Mongodb\", \"q_fs_4\": \"AUTHENTICATION is the process of verifying a user’s identity through login credentials like email and password, where the backend checks the data and confirms who the user is before issuing a token or session, while AUTHORIZATION determines what that authenticated user is allowed to do in the system based on their role or permissions, for example in the Talent Management System the admin can access all users or talents but cannot edit or delete their profiles, while a normal user can view only their own profile and is allowed to edit or delete it but cannot access other users’ data, and together authentication and authorization ensure that only verified users can enter the system and each user can only perform actions that match their permissions.\", \"q_fs_5\": \"Git and version control are used to track, manage, and control changes in source code over time during software development but when we see separately  version control is a system that allow the multiple developers work on the same project without conflicts and Tracks every change made to code etc and git is a popular version control tool that allow and manages different versions of code and save commits on the project and the developers can work collaboratively through branching and merging\", \"q_fs_6\": \"Responsive design means building a website or application so that its layout, content, and elements automatically adjust to different screen sizes such as mobile phones, tablets, and desktops, making sure everything stays readable and easy to use no matter the device.\\n\\nIt is important because it ensures the application works smoothly across all devices, improves user experience, keeps content clear and accessible, and prevents layout issues like overlapping or misaligned elements, which helps users interact with the system comfortably anywhere.\\nIn the Talent Management System that I build responsive design allows the React interface to adapt when an admin or user opens the system on different devices, so the admin dashboard still properly displays all users or talents on a large screen, small screen like a mobile , while a normal user can easily view and manage their own profile on a smaller mobile screen or large screen without losing functionality or clarity.\", \"q_fs_7\": \"REST APIs are a way for different software systems to communicate over the internet using standard HTTP methods like GET, POST, PUT, and DELETE, where everything is treated as a resource (such as users, profiles, or talents) and accessed through clear URLs called endpoints like /auth/login or /user/profile, with data usually sent and received in JSON format.\\n\\nThey are commonly used in modern applications because they are simple, work with any platform (web, mobile, or server), are easy to build and maintain, follow a consistent structure, and are scalable since each request is independent, which makes them ideal for real-world applications that need to serve many users and devices.\\n\\nIn the Talent Management System that i  built REST APIs are  in the backend (talent_management_system_backend) to handle actions such as user registration through POST /auth/signup, login through POST /auth/login, fetching the user profile through GET /user/profile, and allowing the admin to view all users through GET /admin/listUsers, while the React frontend uses these endpoints to send requests with Axios, receive JSON responses like tokens or user data, and display them in the interface, making the whole system work smoothly across different screens and users.\", \"q_fs_8\": \"State management in frontend applications is the process of storing, updating, and sharing data (called “state”) across different parts of the user interface so that the application stays consistent and always shows the correct and updated information.\\nState includes things like user login status, form inputs, and data fetched from the backend, and good state management ensures that when something changes in one part of the app, all related parts of the UI update automatically and stay in sync.\\nIn my Talent Management System, state management is handled using React’s Context API for both authentication and talent data management, where shared state such as login status, access tokens, and fetched talent or user information is stored globally so that components like the navbar, dashboard, profile pages, and admin views can all access the same data; for example, when a user logs in, the auth context updates the state and stores the token, and when talent data is fetched from the backend, it is also stored in context so all components can display updated information without needing to pass data manually or reload the page, making the entire frontend synchronized and responsive.\", \"q_fs_9\": \"Application performance can be improved in the frontend by using techniques like code splitting and lazy loading, memoization to avoid unnecessary re-renders, image optimization, caching, and reducing file sizes to make the app load faster and run smoothly.\\nIn the backend, performance is improved by using database indexing for faster queries, caching frequently used data, asynchronous processing to handle multiple requests efficiently, compression to reduce response size, and connection pooling to reuse database connections. example In my Talent Management System project, the frontend uses Vite for fast builds and code splitting, React 19 and Tailwind CSS for optimized rendering and efficient styling, while Axios interceptors help manage API requests and token handling smoothly, and the backend uses Node.js with Express for asynchronous request handling, PostgreSQL for efficient structured data storage, JWT for secure stateless authentication, and Docker for consistent deployment, all of which together improve the overall performance of the system.\", \"q_fs_10\": \"When users report that a feature is not working, I would start by first trying to reproduce the issue in my own development environment by following the same steps the user took, so I can confirm the problem and understand exactly when it happens.\\n\\nNext, I would collect more information such as error messages, screenshots, browser type, and device details, and also check both frontend and backend logs to see if any errors are being recorded.\\n\\nAfter that, I would inspect the code related to the feature to look for possible issues like logic mistakes, missing validations, or incorrect API usage.\\n\\nThen I would use debugging tools to narrow down the problem: on the frontend I would use the browser console and network tab to check JavaScript errors and API requests, and on the backend I would check server logs, database queries, and test API endpoints using tools like Postman to see if the issue is coming from the server or client side.\\n\\nOnce I identify the cause, I would apply a fix and test it carefully, making sure the feature works correctly and does not break other parts of the system.\\n\\nFinally, I would verify the solution in different cases, including edge cases, and then communicate the fix to users or the team.\\n\\nIn this Talent Management System, for example, if the login feature fails, I would check the React frontend console for Axios errors, inspect backend logs in the Node.js Express server, test the /auth/login endpoint directly using Postman, and verify JWT token generation and validation until the issue is resolved.\"}','https://github.com/YomRTZ/SWAFRI-Talent-Management-System','not set','{\"pressure\": 5, \"teamwork\": 5, \"adaptability\": 5, \"communication\": 5, \"problemSolving\": 5}',NULL,NULL,NULL),(17,'Olana Kelbesa','olanakelbesa@gmail.com','980438627','','','','Male',NULL,'https://www.linkedin.com/in/olana-kelbesa-257148283/','','','','','','','','',NULL,NULL,NULL,'','','','',NULL,'','','Immediate',NULL,NULL,'',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'Yes','Yes',NULL,'Yes','No',NULL,'[]','[]','[]',NULL,'','No','No','No','No',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'','','','',NULL,NULL,NULL,'No','No',NULL,'No',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'No',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'SWAFRI appeals to me because it represents a strong bridge between Swiss innovation and African potential, bringing together two ecosystems with different strengths but shared goals of solving real-world problems.\n\nWhat attracts me most is the focus on collaborative, cross-cultural problem solving. As a software engineering student working on real-world systems, I value environments where I can learn from global best practices while also contributing solutions that are relevant to African contexts.',NULL,'','',1,0,1,0,NULL,'2026-04-29 13:49:19','2026-04-29 13:49:19','[\"full-stack\"]','{\"q_fs_1\": \"Frontend development refers to the client-side of a web application that users interact with directly, including the user interface and experience, built using technologies like HTML, CSS, and JavaScript. \\n\\nBackend development refers to the server-side, where application logic, database management, and APIs are handled. The frontend communicates with the backend through HTTP requests, and the backend processes these requests and returns responses, enabling a complete and dynamic web application.\", \"q_fs_2\": \"An API (Application Programming Interface) is a set of rules and endpoints that allows communication between the frontend and backend. The frontend sends HTTP requests (such as GET, POST, PUT, DELETE) to the API, the backend processes these requests (e.g., retrieving or storing data), and then returns a response (usually in JSON format), which the frontend uses to update the user interface.\", \"q_fs_3\": \"SQL databases store data in structured tables with a fixed schema and use SQL for querying, ensuring strong consistency and support for complex queries. \\nIn contrast, NoSQL databases use flexible, non-tabular data models (such as document, key-value, or graph) with dynamic schemas, making them more scalable and suitable for handling large volumes of unstructured or rapidly changing data.\", \"q_fs_4\": \"Authentication is the process of verifying a user’s identity (e.g., login with username and password), while authorization determines what actions or resources the authenticated user is allowed to access. Together, they secure the application by ensuring only valid users can access the system and only perform permitted actions.\", \"q_fs_5\": \"Git and version control are used to track changes in code, manage different versions, and support collaboration among developers. They allow developers to work on separate branches, merge changes, revert to previous versions, and maintain a complete history of the project, improving code management and team productivity.\", \"q_fs_6\": \"Responsive design is a web design approach that ensures a website adapts to different screen sizes and devices, such as mobile, tablet, and desktop, using techniques like flexible layouts and media queries. It is important because it improves user experience, ensures mobile-friendliness, enhances accessibility, and eliminates the need to build separate versions of a website for different devices.\", \"q_fs_7\": \"REST APIs (Representational State Transfer APIs) are web services that use standard HTTP methods (such as GET, POST, PUT, DELETE) to access and manipulate resources through URLs. They are commonly used because they are simple, stateless, scalable, and flexible, and they work well with modern web and mobile applications by using standard data formats like JSON for communication.\", \"q_fs_8\": \"State management in frontend applications refers to the process of storing, managing, and sharing data (state) across different components so that the user interface stays consistent and updates correctly when the data changes. It helps maintain synchronization between the UI and application data, improving predictability and scalability of the application.\", \"q_fs_9\": \"Application performance can be improved by optimizing both frontend and backend processes. \\nOn the frontend, techniques include code splitting, lazy loading, image optimization, caching, and minimizing unnecessary re-renders. \\nOn the backend, improvements include database indexing, caching, efficient API design, load balancing, pagination, and using asynchronous processing to reduce response time and server load.\", \"q_fs_10\": \"To debug a non working feature, I would first reproduce the issue to understand the problem. Then, I would check the frontend using browser developer tools for errors and failed network requests, and review backend logs or test API endpoints to identify server-side issues. After isolating whether the problem is in the frontend, backend, or communication layer, I would fix the issue, test the solution, and add proper error handling and logging to prevent similar problems in the future.\"}','https://github.com/Olanakelbesa/TMS','not set','{\"pressure\": 4, \"teamwork\": 5, \"adaptability\": 4, \"communication\": 4, \"problemSolving\": 4}',NULL,NULL,NULL),(18,'abigiya habtu ','abigiyahabtu7@gmail.com','965041003','','','','Female',NULL,'','','','','','','','','',NULL,NULL,NULL,'','','','',NULL,'','','Immediate',NULL,NULL,'',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'Yes','Yes',NULL,'No','No',NULL,'[]','[]','[]',NULL,'','No','No','No','No',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'','','','',NULL,NULL,NULL,'No','No',NULL,'No',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'No',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'SWAFRI was suggested to me by friends based on their positive experiences with the platform. I am eager to join because it offers access to global job opportunities, which would allow me to develop my skills further and elevate the standard of my work on an international level. I see it as a valuable opportunity to grow professionally, learn from diverse projects and teams, and strengthen my design practice within a global context.',NULL,'','',1,0,1,0,NULL,'2026-04-29 17:05:36','2026-04-29 17:05:36','[\"graphics\"]','{\"q_gd_1\": \"I am confident in designing social media posts, presentation layouts, brochures, flyers, posters, branding materials, portfolio pages, and promotional graphics. I also enjoy creating visual content for marketing and digital platforms such as website banners and campaign visuals.\\nWith my architectural background, I have developed a strong understanding of composition, visual hierarchy, color balance, and attention to detail. I am comfortable creating clean, modern, and professional designs that communicate ideas clearly while remaining visually engaging.\", \"q_gd_2\": \"I mainly use Adobe Photoshop, Illustrator, InDesign, and Canva for graphic design projects, depending on the requirements of each task. I prefer Photoshop for image editing, social media content, and visual enhancements because it offers strong creative flexibility and detailed control. I use Illustrator for website banners, logos, and branding materials due to its precision and clean vector output, while InDesign is especially effective for brochures and presentation layouts where organization, alignment, and typography are essential. These tools allow me to create clean, professional, and visually engaging designs across both digital and print media.\", \"q_gd_3\": \"First, I try to fully understand the brand’s goals, target audience, existing visual materials, and overall visual direction. Based on this understanding, I develop a design concept that aligns with the brand’s identity and objectives. I then pay close attention to elements such as color palette, typography, logo usage, layout style, and overall tone to ensure consistency and professionalism across all designs.\", \"q_gd_4\": \"When creating a design, I choose colors, fonts, and layouts based on the brand identity, target audience, and the message the design needs to communicate. I select color palettes that match the brand’s tone and create the right visual emotion and balance. For typography, I choose fonts that are clear, professional, and suitable for the design style while maintaining readability and consistency.\\n\\nFor the layout, I focus on visual hierarchy, spacing, alignment, and composition to make the design clean, organized, and visually engaging.\", \"q_gd_5\": \"I try to develop my concepts for products using a simple, modern, clean, bold, and communicative approach that ensures effective design and clear visual communication. I also focus on using creative elements such as color, typography, imagery, and composition in a way that supports the content rather than distracting from it, allowing the message to remain clear, engaging, and visually balanced. When designing marketing or business materials, I prioritize readability, visual hierarchy, and organization so the audience can quickly understand the key message. At the same time, I add creative touches that make the design attractive, memorable, and aligned with the brand identity.\", \"q_gd_6\": \"In my previous experience, the main focus has always been communication and clearly understanding the brand in order to satisfy both my clients and myself. I prioritize learning the client’s goals, target audience, and brand identity so that my design decisions are well-informed and aligned with their expectations. This approach helps me better understand both the client and the targeted audience, allowing me to create designs that are visually effective, meaningful, and aligned with the brand’s message\", \"q_gd_7\": \"When a client does not like my first design concept, I first listen carefully to their feedback to understand what is not working and what they expect instead. I ask clarifying questions if needed to make sure I fully capture their concerns rather than making assumptions. I also try to explain the concept and how I interpreted the brief to check if there is any misunderstanding. Once I clearly understand the issue, I incorporate their feedback and refine the concept and interpretation accordingly to improve the design and better meet their expectations\", \"q_gd_8\": \"I usually optimize and export design files in formats such as JPG or PDF, depending on whether the project is for web or print use. For digital platforms, I make sure the files are optimized for quality and fast loading, while for print materials, I prepare high-resolution PDF files to ensure clear and professional output\", \"q_gd_9\": \"One design project I am especially proud of was creating the visual identity for a startup website development company. The project included designing the logo, website layout, business cards, stamp, and mockups, all developed as part of a unified and consistent brand system.\\nI am proud of this project because it taught me a lot about branding, visual consistency, and communicating a company’s identity through design.\", \"q_gd_10\": \"I usually ask strangers or friends who have no connection to the project what they understand from the design or product I present. Their feedback helps me see the work from an objective perspective and shows whether I have successfully communicated the intended message or not\"}','https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/1Xy1kgWvZTv2jwcImeEjeOV0h3l1wJT7N','not set','{\"pressure\": 3, \"teamwork\": 4, \"adaptability\": 5, \"communication\": 4, \"problemSolving\": 5}',NULL,NULL,NULL),(19,'Solomon Aberra','solomonaberra75@gmail.com','945341787','','','','Male',NULL,'https://www.linkedin.com/in/solomon-aberra-a5506a3a2/','','','','','','','','',NULL,NULL,NULL,'','','','',NULL,'','','1-2 Weeks',NULL,NULL,'',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'Yes','Yes',NULL,'Yes','No',NULL,'[]','[]','[]',NULL,'','No','No','No','No',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'','','','',NULL,NULL,NULL,'No','No',NULL,'No',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'No',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'What appeals to me most about SWAFRI is its focus on building meaningful collaboration between Switzerland and Africa through innovation, research and knowledge exchange. I am particularly interested in opportunities that combine international perspectives with local impact and SWAFRI’s mission reflects that balance very well.',NULL,'','',1,0,1,0,NULL,'2026-05-22 19:34:00','2026-05-22 19:34:00','[\"seo\"]','{\"q_seo_1\": \"SEO (Search Engine Optimization) is the practice of improving a website\'s visibility in organic (unpaid) search engine results. It involves optimizing content, structure and authority so search engines like Google rank the site higher for relevant queries.\", \"q_seo_2\": \"On-page SEO refers to all the optimizations made directly within a website, such as using keywords, improving content quality, adding meta tags, creating internal links, and structuring URLs properly. Its main goal is to make the webpage more relevant, user-friendly, and easy for search engines to understand. In contrast, \\n\\nOff-page SEO involves activities that happen outside the website, such as gaining backlinks, earning brand mentions, encouraging social signals, and writing guest posts. While on-page SEO is fully under the website owner’s control, off-page SEO often depends on third parties and focuses on building the website’s authority, trust, and reputation online.\", \"q_seo_3\": \"Keyword research is the process of finding and analysing the words and phrases that people type into search engines like Google when looking for information, products, or services.\\nIt is important in SEO because it ensures you create content that matches what your target audience is actually searching for. Without it, you risk writing content nobody searches for, wasting time and resources. It also helps you understand search intent whether users want to buy, learn, or compare so you can tailor your content accordingly.\", \"q_seo_4\": \"Meta title: The clickable headline shown in search results. It tells Google and users what the page is about. Should be 50-60 characters, include the primary keyword, and be compelling.\\n\\nMeta description: The short summary below the title in search results. Should be 150–160 characters and act as a \\\"mini ad\\\" for the page.\", \"q_seo_5\": \"Technical SEO is the practice of optimising a website\'s backend and infrastructure so search engines can properly crawl, index, and rank it and common issues include slow page speed, missing HTTPS, broken links, duplicate content, incorrect robots.txt configuration, missing XML sitemaps, poor mobile responsiveness, and lack of structured data markup.\", \"q_seo_6\": \"Page speed refers to how quickly a website loads and becomes usable for a visitor. It matters for two closely connected reasons: search engine rankings and user experience.\\n\\nFor SEO, Google officially confirmed page speed as a ranking factor, and since the Core Web Vitals update, it measures three specific speed-related signals  LCP (how fast the main content loads), INP (how quickly the page responds to interaction), and CLS (how stable the layout is while loading). Pages that score poorly on these metrics are ranked lower, regardless of how good their content is.\", \"q_seo_7\": \"Backlinks (links from other websites pointing to yours) are one of Google\'s most powerful ranking signals. They act as \\\"votes of confidence\\\", the more authoritative and relevant the linking site, the more value passed\", \"q_seo_8\": \"Google Search Console (GSC) and Google Analytics (GA4) are both essential SEO tools, but they serve different purposes. Google Search Console helps you understand how Google views and indexes your website. It provides insights such as impressions, clicks, click-through rate (CTR), average keyword position, index coverage, Core Web Vitals, manual penalties, and sitemap submissions. This makes it useful for monitoring search visibility and identifying technical SEO issues.\", \"q_seo_9\": \"If a website suddenly loses rankings on Google, I would follow a structured diagnostic process to identify and resolve the issue. First, I would confirm the traffic drop using Google Search Console and Google Analytics to ensure it is not caused by a tracking or reporting problem.\\n\\nNext, I would check whether Google released a recent algorithm update around the same date as the ranking decline. I would also review the “Security & Manual Actions” section in Google Search Console to see if the site received any manual penalties.\\n\\nAfter that, I would conduct a technical SEO audit to identify crawl issues, indexing problems, robots.txt errors, accidental noindex tags, broken redirects, or server downtime. I would also review any recent website changes, such as deleted pages, redesigns, content updates, or migration issues that may have negatively affected rankings.\\n\\nAnother important step would be analyzing the backlink profile to determine whether the website lost valuable backlinks or gained toxic links that could harm authority. I would also compare competitor performance to see if competing websites improved significantly or if search intent shifted.\\n\\nAdditionally, I would evaluate the quality and relevance of the content to ensure it still satisfies user intent and aligns with Google’s E-E-A-T principles (Experience, Expertise, Authoritativeness, and Trustworthiness). Based on all findings, I would create an action plan that may include fixing technical problems, improving content quality, optimizing metadata, recovering backlinks, or disavowing harmful links.\\n\\nFinally, I would continuously monitor rankings, impressions, and traffic in Google Search Console and GA4 to track recovery progress over time.\", \"q_seo_10\": \"An SEO strategy is successful when it increases qualified organic traffic and helps achieve business goals such as leads, sales, or conversions. To measure success, I would track both leading indicators, which show early progress, and lagging indicators, which reflect actual business impact.\\n\\nOne of the primary metrics is organic traffic growth, measured through GA4 and Google Search Console, to determine whether more users are discovering the website through search engines. I would also track keyword rankings using tools such as Ahrefs, SEMrush, or GSC to see whether target keywords are improving in search results.\\n\\nClick-through rate (CTR) and impressions in Google Search Console are also important because they show how often pages appear in search results and whether users are choosing to click them. To measure website authority, I would monitor backlinks and Domain Authority using tools like Ahrefs or Moz.\\n\\nUser engagement metrics such as bounce rate and session duration in GA4 help evaluate traffic quality and whether visitors find the content useful. Conversion tracking is especially important because it shows whether SEO traffic is generating meaningful business outcomes, such as purchases, sign-ups, or inquiries.\\n\\nIn addition, I would monitor Core Web Vitals and overall site performance using Google Search Console and PageSpeed Insights to ensure the website maintains a strong technical foundation.\\n\\nUltimately, SEO success is not measured only by rankings, but by the ability to attract the right audience, provide value to users, and generate measurable business results through organic search traffic.\"}','https://github.com/SolomonA17/seo-assessment','not set','{\"pressure\": 4, \"teamwork\": 4, \"adaptability\": 5, \"communication\": 5, \"problemSolving\": 4}',NULL,NULL,NULL),(20,'Amanuel Amare','amnamare07@gmail.com','935973722','','','','Male',NULL,'','','','','','','','','',NULL,NULL,NULL,'','','','',NULL,'','','Immediate',NULL,NULL,'',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'Yes','Yes',NULL,'Yes','No',NULL,'[]','[]','[]',NULL,'','No','No','No','No',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'','','','',NULL,NULL,NULL,'No','No',NULL,'No',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'No',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'What appeals to me most about SWAFRI is how it connects talented African developers with structured Swiss management. This partnership creates a great workspace where clear communication and code quality are highly valued. I want to join a company that helps developers grow while delivering reliable software for international clients, and SWAFRI feels like the perfect place to do that.',NULL,'','',1,0,1,0,NULL,'2026-05-29 17:59:38','2026-05-29 17:59:38','[\"full-stack\"]','{\"q_fs_1\": \"Frontend development focuses entirely on the visual elements and interactive experiences that users see and engage with directly on a website. In contrast, backend development handles the invisible infrastructure, managing the secure data processing, server communication, and core business logic that powers the application behind the scenes.\", \"q_fs_2\": \"API(Application Programming Interface) act as  a middle man b/n the front end and the back end.it takes the request from the front end and also bring the response back to the front end.its doing all about the request and response work.\", \"q_fs_3\": \"SQL(stractured query language) is a relational databse management system that store data in tabular format with rows(fields) and columns(records.)\\nNoSQL database store data in more flexable formats like Graph,Json and documents.\", \"q_fs_4\": \"Authentication and authorization are the dual pillars of modern application security. While authentication strictly verifies the true identity of a user—proving who they are through credentials like passwords or biometrics—authorization determines their specific access rights, dictating exactly what resources or actions that authenticated user is permitted to access within the system.\", \"q_fs_5\": \"Git serves as a widely adopted version control system designed to track modifications in code history over time. By maintaining a detailed record of every change, it enables multiple developers to collaborate on the same project simultaneously without overwriting each other\'s work.\", \"q_fs_6\": \"Responsive Web Design (RWD) is a crucial web development framework that ensures websites automatically adapt and display correctly across various screen sizes, including smartphones, laptops, desktops, and tablets. By utilizing fluid grids, flexible images, and CSS media queries, RWD eliminates the need for separate mobile sites, providing an optimized, cohesive user experience regardless of the device being used.\", \"q_fs_7\": \"REST APIs provide a standardized architectural style that enables disparate applications, such as a frontend user interface and a backend server, to seamlessly communicate over the internet. By leveraging the universal HTTP protocol, this approach allows different systems to exchange data reliably regardless of their underlying programming languages or platforms.\", \"q_fs_8\": \"Using a unit test approach helps isolate bugs in state management, which controls the temporary data users see on screen. By testing components with mock data, you can quickly find exactly where UI rendering or data syncing fails.\", \"q_fs_9\": \"to improve the front end\\n*use code spliting technique(instead of a large long line use smaller version)\\n*compress HTML,CSS and Javascript files to reduce  file size\\n*implement a browser caching service to load static informations from the server.\\nto improve the back end\\n*use a database indexing technique to speed up data retrival\\n*use a server side chaching to store it in  fast memory like Meme chached.\\n*use fast algorithim approach to speed up the System. performance \", \"q_fs_10\": \"Using a unit test approach allows you to systematically isolate specific code behaviors to identify the precise root cause of a software defect. In complex applications, bugs often manifest as vague, high-level system failures, making manual troubleshooting incredibly time-consuming, frustrating, and inefficient. By breaking the software down into its smallest testable components—such as individual functions, classes, or methods—you effectively eliminate external noise and conflicting variables from your diagnosis. When you write targeted tests for a problematic module, you can inject specific inputs and define the exact expected outputs. If a unit test fails under these controlled conditions, it provides immediate, definitive feedback pointing directly to the broken logic, flawed algorithms, or unexpected edge cases within that isolated block of code.\"}','https://github.com/Amanuel088/Talent-Management-System','not set','{\"pressure\": 5, \"teamwork\": 4, \"adaptability\": 4, \"communication\": 4, \"problemSolving\": 5}',NULL,NULL,NULL);
/*!40000 ALTER TABLE `assessments` ENABLE KEYS */;
UNLOCK TABLES;

