iant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Total BNB purchased: </span></span></span><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">1,769</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-left: 95.35pt;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">With the DCA strategy, you have accumulated 1,769 BNB in five months. The average BNB price during this period was ($200 + $250 + $300 + $350 + $400) / 5 = $300. Your average purchase price for BNB was $500 / 1,769 = $282.55. In this example, the DCA strategy resulted in a lower average purchase price than the average total price during the investment period.&nbsp;As the price of BNB rose over time, the DCA strategy allowed you to take advantage of the uptrend by lowering your average purchase price compared to the average market price. DCA can be a useful strategy for investors who want to invest in the market without trying to time it. By investing regularly over time, investors can benefit from compound interest and potentially achieve long-term financial goals.</span></span></span></p><ul style=\"padding-inline-start: 48px;\"><li dir=\"ltr\" style=\"list-style-type: disc; font-size: 9pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre; margin-left: 59.35pt;\" aria-level=\"1\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\" role=\"presentation\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">3. Position Ssizing</span></span></span></p></li></ul><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-left: 95.35pt;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Position size is a risk management technique used to determine the appropriate size of an investment or position based on the risk an investor is willing to take. This technique calculates the maximum potential loss an investor can tolerate and adjusts the size of their investment accordingly. Although position size is often considered a crypto trading strategy, it also applies to other forms of investing and wealth management.&nbsp;For example, if an investor is willing to take a 5% risk on a single trade or position, they should adjust their position size to limit potential losses. This way, investors can avoid taking on excessive risk and potentially incurring losses that are beyond their means.&nbsp;When combined with other risk management strategies, position size can increase an investor\'s chances of success in the markets while effectively managing their risks.</span></span></span></p><ul style=\"padding-inline-start: 48px;\"><li dir=\"ltr\" style=\"list-style-type: disc; font-size: 9pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre; margin-left: 59.35pt;\" aria-level=\"1\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\" role=\"presentation\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">4. Portfolio Rebalancing</span></span></span></p></li></ul><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-left: 95.35pt;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Portfolio rebalancing refers to adjusting the composition of the portfolio to bring it back to its original asset allocation. This ensures that the portfolio\'s risk/reward ratio aligns with the investor\'s goals and risk tolerance.&nbsp;Since cryptocurrency prices fluctuate significantly, rebalancing can help investors maintain their desired level of risk while increasing their returns. For example, if an investor\'s target allocation is to hold 50% of their portfolio in Bitcoin and 50% in Ethereum, but their portfolio is now 60% Bitcoin and 40% Ethereum due to market movements, they may decide to sell some of their Bitcoins and buy more Ethereum to bring their portfolio back to the target allocation.&nbsp;Rebalancing can occur on a regular basis, such as monthly or quarterly, or it can be triggered by specific events, such as a significant change in the value of a particular cryptocurrency or a change in the investor\'s financial goals or risk tolerance.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2449999999999999;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:8pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"width:100px;display:inline-block;position:relative;\"></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-left: 95.35pt;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><br></p><ul style=\"padding-inline-start: 48px;\"><li dir=\"ltr\" style=\"list-style-type: disc; font-size: 9pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre; margin-left: 59.35pt;\" aria-level=\"1\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\" role=\"presentation\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">5. Stop Loss Orders</span></span></span></p></li></ul><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-left: 95.35pt;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">A stop-loss order is a valuable tool that allows investors to automatically sell all or part of their holdings if the price of a particular asset falls below a predetermined level. With a stop-loss order, investors can limit their losses during sudden market downturns and protect their investments from significant price losses. For example, if an investor buys Ethereum at $2,000 and places a stop-loss order at $1,800, their Ethereum holdings will be automatically sold if the price falls to or below $1,800, preventing further losses in a bear market.&nbsp;In addition, price alerts, such as CoinStats Alerts, can help investors manage their crypto investments. These alerts notify investors when a particular cryptocurrency investment reaches a certain price, experiences a significant movement, or when a major news publication publishes groundbreaking news that could affect the market.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-left: 95.35pt;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><br></p><ul style=\"padding-inline-start: 48px;\"><li dir=\"ltr\" style=\"list-style-type: disc; font-size: 9pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre; margin-left: 59.35pt;\" aria-level=\"1\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\" role=\"presentation\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">6. Hedging</span></span></span></p></li></ul><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-left: 95.35pt;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Hedging is a technique used to hedge an investor\'s holdings against adverse market fluctuations. This is achieved by taking a position that offsets the risk of the initial investment. To illustrate, you can hedge your Bitcoin holdings by taking a short position in a Bitcoin futures contract. In the event of a price drop, the losses from the investment would be offset by the gains from the short position. Another example of hedging is the use of stablecoins, which have a stable value compared to other cryptocurrencies, to lock in profits during market instability.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-left: 95.35pt;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><br></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Additional tips for effective risk management</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Now that we\'ve covered the most popular risk management strategies for crypto portfolios, it\'s time to dive into some additional tips to help investors maximize their profits from market movements.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Conduct due diligence</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Investors need to conduct thorough research before investing in any cryptocurrency project. This includes doing your own research and analyzing the team, technology, use cases, and market potential to make informed decisions. For example, before investing in a new DeFi project, be sure to check the backgrounds of the team members, evaluate tokenomics, assess the security and scalability of the technology, assess the platform\'s potential to disrupt the existing financial ecosystem as well as the DeFi ecosystem, and estimate the overall market size.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Assess your risk tolerance</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">An essential aspect of portfolio management is assessing your risk appetite before making investment decisions. Consider important factors such as your personal investment horizon, financial goals, and life circumstances. A conservative investor with a low risk tolerance should favor more stable digital assets such as Bitcoin and Ethereum and choose a more passive investment style. An aggressive investor with a high risk tolerance, on the other hand, could pursue riskier opportunities that have the potential to generate higher profits, such as small-cap altcoins or newly launched crypto projects.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Stay informed</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">If you plan to actively manage your crypto portfolio, you will need to invest a lot of time to keep up to date with the latest news, developments, and market trends in the crypto space. You should stay up to date regularly by following reliable and impartial news channels, social media channels, and community forums about the cryptocurrency market and your entire investment. You can consider following experts and influencers on Twitter or LinkedIn, joining relevant Telegram and Discord groups, and subscribing to newsletters from reputable sources such as CoinStats, CoinDesk, and Cointelegraph.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Only invest what you can afford to lose</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Crypto markets can be highly volatile, and investing in them always carries the risk of losing some or all of your investment. Make sure that you only invest an amount that you can afford to lose without jeopardizing your financial stability. If you are investing in cryptocurrencies for the first time, start with a small amount that you are happy to risk and gradually increase your investments as you gain experience and confidence. Developing an exit plan in advance is an efficient portfolio management strategy and will help minimize losses if things don\'t go your way. This means that you have set an entry point, a point for taking profit, and a point for exiting the position if the trade turns out to be bad.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Be careful with leverage</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Trading with leverage can boost your returns by giving you the opportunity to borrow additional funds for investments, but it can also amplify your losses. It is important that you are careful when using leverage and have a clear understanding of the risks involved. Start with a low leverage and gradually increase it as you become more comfortable with the process and have gained more experience. For example, if you\'re trading on a margin platform, you\'ll want to start with 2x or 3x leverage instead of moving straight to a higher level, which could result in significant losses if the market moves against you. Always remember that the higher the leverage, the greater the potential risks. So use the leverage wisely and only with venture capital that you can afford to lose.</span></span></span></p><div><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><br></span>\n</div></span>\n</div>',1,1,70,'2025-09-10 13:00:13','2025-10-08 09:42:58'),(170,28,'What does due diligence mean?','<div class=\"prose prose-lg max-w-none space-y-4\">\n\n<span id=\"docs-internal-guid-8c28e0fc-7fff-de86-9b2a-34b6fe0bbf89\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:0pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Circumventing regulatory and sanctioning issues in the digital asset space requires careful pre-screening, customized due diligence, and ongoing monitoring.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:0pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">The term \"due diligence\" encompasses a wide range of audit processes that are carried out before contracts are concluded or other business connections are made in order to verify natural or legal persons. Before actually conducting the due diligence process, it is crucial to identify the companies or individuals that will be subject to thorough scrutiny. This process varies greatly depending on the sector, area, or type of business transaction in which it is applied. (Validatis, n.d.)</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2449999999999999;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:8pt;\"><br></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">What is legal due diligence?</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">While due diligence refers to the audit itself, it is referred to as a legal due diligence process or a legal audit when it comes to the preliminary review of a person or a company in order to assess possible risks of establishing an economic relationship with them. The review focuses on establishing the reality of companies or individuals and ascertaining beyond doubt that they have not committed a crime and do not intend to commit one or become delinquents. This is a process of information research carried out by one of the actors in a negotiation situation in order to assess the risks and the situation at the time of negotiation. It is a very common process in the field of B2B (business-to-business) buying or selling, as well as in the acquisition or merger of companies. In some cases, it is also referred to as a procurement audit. You could say that the standard definition is \"research on a person or company before signing a contract or before investing.\"</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:0pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><br></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">What does Know Your Customer (KYC) mean?</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">KYC refers to a process by which banks and other financial institutions collect identification data and contact information from current and potential customers. Its purpose is to prevent fraud, money laundering, and other illegal activities, as well as the misuse of financial accounts. In the US, banks are required by law to use KYC in accordance with the USA Patriot Act of 2001. In 1989, the Financial Action Task Force (FATF) was established to combat money laundering at the international level. FAFT sets standards and makes recommendations to achieve this goal through anti-money laundering (AML). Member countries and jurisdictions enforce various laws, rules, and regulations to work with AML policies. KYC and AML go hand in hand to prevent dishonest and criminal financial activity.</span></span></span></p><div><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><br></span>\n</div></span>\n</div>',1,0,70,'2025-09-10 13:20:06','2025-10-08 09:43:33'),(171,28,'Due diligence in the context of KYC and KYB: understanding of customer due diligence or client due diligence','<div class=\"prose prose-lg max-w-none space-y-4\">\n\n<span id=\"docs-internal-guid-f9ef74c3-7fff-9990-5184-ebbf43e4b1ff\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">In order to capture information about people or companies, initial identification is essential. This step ensures that the specified person or organization is actually who they say they are. As soon as this identification is completed, the basis of trust can be created and the due diligence process can be initiated. The due diligence processes are strongly linked to the concept of Know Your Business (KYB). The preliminary stage in a remote and digital due diligence process is the identification of the natural or legal person to be audited. This process, which involves the digital identification of the responsible person or the legal representative of a company, is known as Know Your Business. The importance of the KYB process as an offshoot of the widespread and globally used Know Your Customer process, or KYC for short, is undeniable. Known as client due diligence or customer due diligence (CDD), it adds a specific dimension. In today\'s world, legal due diligence is essential for companies looking to acquire clients on a remote basis. This process aims to identify users through reliable and secure identity verification systems. The path has evolved from KYC to eKYC (electronic Know Your Customer), bringing due diligence into the digital sphere—a revolution in the contractual relationships between users and companies. This change is taking place in particular in the financial sector, where even existing customers are subject to increased operational due diligence that was not previously part of the process. Organizations use risk models to identify high-risk companies or customers. In the due diligence of a company aimed at individuals or self-employed persons, the KYC process is the initial step. Know Your Customer (KYC) and Know Your Business (KYB) standard due-diligence processes to verify customer identity and business legitimacy. As a result, financial companies are required to consider legal due diligence, financial due diligence, operational due diligence, and technical due diligence in all their processes. (Electronic Identification, 2022)</span></span></span></span>\n\n</div>',1,0,70,'2025-09-10 13:30:21','2025-10-08 09:43:59'),(172,28,'Legal Due Diligence and KYC-Related Regulations','<div class=\"prose prose-lg max-w-none space-y-4\">\n\n<span id=\"docs-internal-guid-ecb42760-7fff-fe53-cf41-925ec9078813\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Like all legal procedures, legal due diligence, more commonly known as legal due diligence, must comply with all applicable regulations that affect it due to the nature of the process. These are the following regulations:</span></span></span></p><ul style=\"padding-inline-start: 48px;\"><li dir=\"ltr\" style=\"list-style-type: disc; font-size: 9pt; font-family: &quot;Noto Sans Symbols&quot;, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre;\" aria-level=\"1\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\" role=\"presentation\"><span style=\"font-size: 10pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">&nbsp;</span></span></span><span style=\"font-size: 10pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">GDPR (GDPR): </span></span></span><span style=\"font-size: 10pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">The General Data Protection Regulation regulates the protection of individuals with regard to the handling and use of their personal data. Every due diligence procedure must strictly comply with the requirements of the GDPR due to the data exploitation of the audited person by the company.</span></span></span></p></li><li dir=\"ltr\" style=\"list-style-type: disc; font-size: 9pt; font-family: &quot;Noto Sans Symbols&quot;, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre;\" aria-level=\"1\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\" role=\"presentation\"><span style=\"font-size: 10pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">KYC Due Diligence/AML Due Diligence:</span></span></span><span style=\"font-size: 10pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\"> The Fifth Anti-Money Laundering Directive (5AMLD) regulates all aspects of anti-money laundering measures in both offline and online processes. It is a mandatory standard that affects all industries, but especially the financial and banking sector, which develops techniques to prevent fraud and money laundering with continuous due diligence focused on AML. 5AMLD remains relevant, but subsequent EU legislative developments, including the Sixth AML Directive (6AMLD) and the 2024 AML legislative package, have further refined AML/CFT obligations for financial institutions and virtual asset service providers (European Commission, Finance European Parliament).</span></span></span></p></li><li dir=\"ltr\" style=\"list-style-type: disc; font-size: 9pt; font-family: &quot;Noto Sans Symbols&quot;, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre;\" aria-level=\"1\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\" role=\"presentation\"><span style=\"font-size: 10pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">eIDAS: </span></span></span><span style=\"font-size: 10pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">The Regulation on Electronic Identification and Trust Services for Electronic Transactions establishes the basis for the identification of companies and users and gives qualified providers a seal of trust. It regulates all aspects related to electronic signatures and online interactions. (BTC-ECHO, n.d.)</span></span></span></p></li></ul></span>\n\n</div>',1,0,70,'2025-09-10 13:31:49','2025-10-08 09:44:23'),(173,28,'Types of Due Diligence Procedures','<div class=\"prose prose-lg max-w-none space-y-4\">\n\n<span id=\"docs-internal-guid-3733dff9-7fff-cd55-5f11-1c8585b4f4c0\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:0pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">The importance of due diligence can also be applied to almost all areas of the company. In addition, there are other types of due diligence:</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:0pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><br></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Operational Due Diligence Importance</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:5pt;padding:5pt 0pt 0pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Operational due diligence refers to the iterative process of formulating and testing an investment thesis with the aim of creating an actionable value creation plan. It uncovers possible potentials and complements the risk-oriented types of due diligence procedures, such as financial due diligence, in order to maximize returns. Operational due diligence can relate to financial, legal, operational, commercial, IT and engineering aspects. The operational due diligence review answers three key questions:</span></span></span></p><ol style=\"padding-inline-start: 48px;\"><li dir=\"ltr\" style=\"list-style-type: decimal; font-size: 10pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre;\" aria-level=\"1\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:0pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\" role=\"presentation\"><span style=\"font-size: 10pt; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Are the processes running smoothly?</span></span></span></p></li><li dir=\"ltr\" style=\"list-style-type: decimal; font-size: 10pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre;\" aria-level=\"1\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:0pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\" role=\"presentation\"><span style=\"font-size: 10pt; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">What are the operational benefits and their full potential?</span></span></span></p></li><li dir=\"ltr\" style=\"list-style-type: decimal; font-size: 10pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre;\" aria-level=\"1\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\" role=\"presentation\"><span style=\"font-size: 10pt; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">What are the forecasts?</span></span></span></p></li></ol><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:0pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Operational due diligence, like any other due diligence review, should be understood as a continuous process, as it does not stop at the end of the due diligence phase. It\'s iterative because it\'s designed to deliver actionable insights. In addition, it is correlative, which means that every part of the company should be involved.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:0pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><br></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Commercial Due Diligence</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:5pt;padding:5pt 0pt 0pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Commercial due diligence is the process by which a company determines the strategic suitability and economic attractiveness of a target company. To know how to conduct commercial due diligence, the company must focus on the following points:</span></span></span></p><ul style=\"padding-inline-start: 48px;\"><li dir=\"ltr\" style=\"list-style-type: disc; font-size: 9pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre;\" aria-level=\"1\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:0pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\" role=\"presentation\"><span style=\"font-size: 10pt; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Comprehensive information about the company.</span></span></span></p></li><li dir=\"ltr\" style=\"list-style-type: disc; font-size: 9pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre;\" aria-level=\"1\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:0pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\" role=\"presentation\"><span style=\"font-size: 10pt; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Conducting an analysis of the company\'s performance.</span></span></span></p></li><li dir=\"ltr\" style=\"list-style-type: disc; font-size: 9pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre;\" aria-level=\"1\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:0pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\" role=\"presentation\"><span style=\"font-size: 10pt; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Analysis of potential risks and problems.</span></span></span></p></li><li dir=\"ltr\" style=\"list-style-type: disc; font-size: 9pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre;\" aria-level=\"1\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\" role=\"presentation\"><span style=\"font-size: 10pt; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Analysis of the company\'s ability to meet the expectations of its shareholders.</span></span></span></p></li></ul><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Financial Due Diligence</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">While commercial due diligence provides a more complete picture of the company as a business entity, financial due diligence focuses on evaluating a company\'s financial stability. As part of the financial due diligence review, the company\'s previous and current business results are examined in detail in order to make a forecast and identify possible risks.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:0pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><br></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Technical Due Diligence</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">The technical due diligence procedure refers to due diligence processes in real estate transactions, i.e., the execution of technical, ecological, and commercial analyses.</span></span></span></p></span>\n\n</div>',1,0,70,'2025-09-10 13:32:50','2025-10-08 09:44:47'),(174,28,'Key features of the due diligence process','<div class=\"prose prose-lg max-w-none space-y-4\">\n\n<span id=\"docs-internal-guid-e79a7241-7fff-895e-f5bf-e2f6e0fa5e70\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:0pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Each type of due diligence process has a set of basic requirements that must be met for the mandatory KYC/KYB process, which standardizes and regulates it in terms of best practices in this type of risk analysis methodology.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:0pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><br></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Due diligence requirements for KYC:</span></span></span></p><ul style=\"padding-inline-start: 48px;\"><li dir=\"ltr\" style=\"list-style-type: disc; font-size: 9pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre;\" aria-level=\"1\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:0pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\" role=\"presentation\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">All information is confidential between both parties and must be used in accordance with the applicable regulations (GDPR, AML5, eIDAS).</span></span></span></p></li><li dir=\"ltr\" style=\"list-style-type: disc; font-size: 9pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre;\" aria-level=\"1\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:0pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\" role=\"presentation\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">In most negotiations, this is a process prescribed by legal due diligence. The good intentions of the person providing the information are always assumed.</span></span></span></p></li><li dir=\"ltr\" style=\"list-style-type: disc; font-size: 9pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre;\" aria-level=\"1\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:0pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\" role=\"presentation\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">It is recommended that this be done by a third-party provider.This should be a company that specializes in due diligence, KYB, or KYC processes.</span></span></span></p></li><li dir=\"ltr\" style=\"list-style-type: disc; font-size: 9pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre;\" aria-level=\"1\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:0pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\" role=\"presentation\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">It must always give a clear professional objective: to identify the contingencies of the party with whom you want to negotiate and not other purposes.</span></span></span></p></li><li dir=\"ltr\" style=\"list-style-type: disc; font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre;\" aria-level=\"1\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\" role=\"presentation\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">The procedure must be carried out within a short period of time and must be up-to-date so that it is reliable and truthful.</span></span></span></p></li></ul><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2449999999999999;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:8pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"width:100px;display:inline-block;position:relative;\"></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-left: 36pt;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\"><br></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Due diligence—what is being investigated?</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:5pt;padding:5pt 0pt 0pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Depending on the purpose for which the due diligence is carried out, the areas to be audited vary. This results in functional forms of due diligence. These include, among others:</span></span></span></p><ul style=\"padding-inline-start: 48px;\"><li dir=\"ltr\" style=\"list-style-type: disc; font-size: 9pt; font-family: &quot;Noto Sans Symbols&quot;, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre;\" aria-level=\"1\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\" role=\"presentation\"><span style=\"font-size: 10pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Customer Due Diligence (CDD): </span></span></span><span style=\"font-size: 10pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Money laundering screening of new and existing customers according to the Know your Customer (KYC) principle</span></span></span></p></li><li dir=\"ltr\" style=\"list-style-type: disc; font-size: 9pt; font-family: &quot;Noto Sans Symbols&quot;, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre;\" aria-level=\"1\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\" role=\"presentation\"><span style=\"font-size: 10pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Financial due diligence:</span></span></span><span style=\"font-size: 10pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\"> Audit of all commercial areas, such as accounting, annual financial statements, controlling, financial relations and cash flows</span></span></span></p></li><li dir=\"ltr\" style=\"list-style-type: disc; font-size: 9pt; font-family: &quot;Noto Sans Symbols&quot;, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre;\" aria-level=\"1\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\" role=\"presentation\"><span style=\"font-size: 10pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Commercial Due Diligence:</span></span></span><span style=\"font-size: 10pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\"> Examination of business activities and relationships with the business environment and market</span></span></span></p></li><li dir=\"ltr\" style=\"list-style-type: disc; font-size: 9pt; font-family: &quot;Noto Sans Symbols&quot;, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre;\" aria-level=\"1\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\" role=\"presentation\"><span style=\"font-size: 10pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Legal due Due diligence: </span></span></span><span style=\"font-size: 10pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Examination of all internal and external legal relationships to identify legal risks for the company\'s results</span></span></span></p></li><li dir=\"ltr\" style=\"list-style-type: disc; font-size: 9pt; font-family: &quot;Noto Sans Symbols&quot;, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre;\" aria-level=\"1\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\" role=\"presentation\"><span style=\"font-size: 10pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">IP Due Diligence:</span></span></span><span style=\"font-size: 10pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\"> Examination of the company\'s intellectual property (IP), such as patents, trademarks and licenses</span></span></span></p></li></ul><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-left: 36pt;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><br></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Different approaches to due diligence</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">If due diligence audits are carried out as a basis for strategic decisions in the area of M&amp;A activities or business initiations, companies usually commission external consultants such as auditors, management consultants, lawyers, appraisers, etc. Customer due diligence audits to meet anti-money laundering requirements are carried out internally within the company. Depending on the group of obligated parties and internal structures, money laundering officers, compliance managers, or those responsible at the management level are responsible for checking new and existing customers. Depending on how complex the corporate structures of the potential new or existing customer are, the research work can be very time-consuming and complex.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:0pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><br></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Who are the obliged entities under the Money Laundering Act?</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:5pt;padding:5pt 0pt 0pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Obliged entities under the Money Laundering Act include, among others:</span></span></span></p><ul style=\"padding-inline-start: 48px;\"><li dir=\"ltr\" style=\"list-style-type: disc; font-size: 9pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre;\" aria-level=\"1\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:0pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\" role=\"presentation\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Credit institutions</span></span></span></p></li><li dir=\"ltr\" style=\"list-style-type: disc; font-size: 9pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre;\" aria-level=\"1\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:0pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\" role=\"presentation\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Financial Services Institutions</span></span></span></p></li><li dir=\"ltr\" style=\"list-style-type: disc; font-size: 9pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre;\" aria-level=\"1\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:0pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\" role=\"presentation\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Financial institutions</span></span></span></p></li><li dir=\"ltr\" style=\"list-style-type: disc; font-size: 9pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre;\" aria-level=\"1\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:0pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\" role=\"presentation\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Certain insurance companies</span></span></span></p></li><li dir=\"ltr\" style=\"list-style-type: disc; font-size: 9pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre;\" aria-level=\"1\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:0pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\" role=\"presentation\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Attorneys</span></span></span></p></li><li dir=\"ltr\" style=\"list-style-type: disc; font-size: 9pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre;\" aria-level=\"1\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:0pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\" role=\"presentation\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Tax advisor</span></span></span></p></li><li dir=\"ltr\" style=\"list-style-type: disc; font-size: 9pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre;\" aria-level=\"1\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:0pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\" role=\"presentation\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Realtor</span></span></span></p></li><li dir=\"ltr\" style=\"list-style-type: disc; font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre;\" aria-level=\"1\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\" role=\"presentation\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Goods dealers</span></span></span></p></li></ul><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2449999999999999;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:8pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"width:100px;display:inline-block;position:relative;\"></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-left: 36pt;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\"><br></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Contents of a Due Diligence Report</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Finally, the due diligence report bundles all findings from the individual audits. Its scope and content vary considerably depending on the objective of the examination. In the context of M&amp;A activities, the report acts as a compilation of the audit results for the specific project and forms the basis for decisions. On the other hand, meticulous documentation, including original documents, is indispensable in money laundering prevention. It serves as legally compliant proof of compliance with due diligence obligations.</span></span></span></p><div><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><br></span>\n</div></span>\n</div>',1,0,70,'2025-09-10 13:34:38','2025-10-08 09:45:08'),(175,28,'Why do you do due diligence?','<div class=\"prose prose-lg max-w-none space-y-4\">\n\n<span id=\"docs-internal-guid-e3dd839f-7fff-5e12-fa41-cf435c69168f\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">As already mentioned above, the due diligence review is complete background research with which companies protect themselves against potential contractual partners on the basis of various sources of information. It helps companies protect their interests and avoid potential risks. Reasons for conducting due diligence reviews can be:</span></span></span></p><ul style=\"padding-inline-start: 48px;\"><li dir=\"ltr\" style=\"list-style-type: disc; font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre;\" aria-level=\"1\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\" role=\"presentation\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Prevention of money laundering and white-collar crime</span></span></span></p></li></ul><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-left: 36pt;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">The verification of the business partner (customer due diligence) is the central component of fulfilling the due diligence obligations under the Money Laundering Act (AMLA). More on this in the chapter \"Due Diligence and the Money Laundering Act.\"</span></span></span></p><ul style=\"padding-inline-start: 48px;\"><li dir=\"ltr\" style=\"list-style-type: disc; font-size: 9pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre;\" aria-level=\"1\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\" role=\"presentation\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Mergers &amp; Acquisitions / M&amp;A Activities</span></span></span></p></li></ul><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-left: 36pt;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Before M&amp;A activities, such as the purchase of companies, company takeovers, or shareholdings, a due diligence review is usually carried out by an external consultant. This makes sense for both the buyer and the seller. In buyer due diligence, the buyer initiates the review with the aim of assessing and evaluating the risks and opportunities of the purchase. The findings are usually incorporated into the purchase price proposal. In vendor due diligence, the seller commissions a systemic analysis in order to eliminate any weaknesses in advance and also to prepare the sales negotiations.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-left: 36pt;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><br></p><ul style=\"padding-inline-start: 48px;\"><li dir=\"ltr\" style=\"list-style-type: disc; font-size: 9pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre;\" aria-level=\"1\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\" role=\"presentation\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Avoidance of reputational damage</span></span></span></p></li></ul><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-left: 36pt;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><br></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-left: 36pt;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Companies often also check business partners for ethical and legal standards in the selection of potential business partners before working together. The aim is to detect incorrect actions in advance in order to avoid reputational damage. The results of due diligence can be used to derive concrete recommendations for further action:For example, the entrepreneurial risk can be reassessed with the customer, or a justification of the business relationship can be generally discouraged.</span></span></span></p><div><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><br></span>\n</div></span>\n</div>',1,0,70,'2025-09-10 13:36:21','2025-10-08 09:45:36'),(176,28,'Procedure of a KYC analysis','<div class=\"prose prose-lg max-w-none space-y-4\">\n\n<span id=\"docs-internal-guid-440b6a65-7fff-e604-0d8b-be17dc1bf883\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:0pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">KYC practices usually start before a person becomes a customer. Financial institutions must first verify the stated identity of a potential customer before opening an account. Since there are no legal verification standards, this process can look different for each bank.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Some identification documents required for KYC processes include:</span></span></span></p><ul style=\"padding-inline-start: 48px;\"><li dir=\"ltr\" style=\"list-style-type: disc; font-size: 9pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre;\" aria-level=\"1\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:0pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\" role=\"presentation\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Driver\'s license / government-issued photo ID</span></span></span></p></li><li dir=\"ltr\" style=\"list-style-type: disc; font-size: 9pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre;\" aria-level=\"1\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:0pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\" role=\"presentation\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Passport</span></span></span></p></li><li dir=\"ltr\" style=\"list-style-type: disc; font-size: 9pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre;\" aria-level=\"1\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\" role=\"presentation\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Social security number</span></span></span></p></li></ul><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:0pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Address verification is also necessary. Depending on the bank\'s practice, applicants may be able to provide different forms of evidence. Some examples include a utility bill, a bank statement from another bank or credit card issuer, or a lease agreement. After providing an account to a customer, banks are required to perform regular record updates. This means that they continue to \"recertify\" their customers with sporadic requests for KYC information throughout their banking relationship. In addition, they assign a risk level to their customers and monitor customer transactions to ensure they are in line with expected behavior.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:0pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><br></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Who has to follow the Know-Your-Customer rules?</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><br></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">KYC practices are not limited to banking institutions. The financial investment industry uses KYC processes in accordance with the Financial Industry Regulatory Authority\'s (FINRA) KYC Rule 2090. But instead of an AML effort, these KYC practices help investment firms better understand customer needs. When it comes to cryptocurrency exchanges, users can often create an account before completing the KYC process. However, these unverified accounts have limited functionalities. Reducing and eliminating money laundering and financial fraud is the clear advantage of KYC practices. Although it makes things more complex and time-consuming—especially for banking and its customers—the benefits likely outweigh the inconvenience. However, standardized practices would likely help streamline the process. In the cryptocurrency space, the use of digital currency to engage in illegal activities has attracted a lot of attention. Reducing these losses is certainly beneficial for the cryptocurrency and its reputation. Compliance is time-consuming, but it should always be encouraged. However, users should not provide sensitive identification data without first ensuring that the company applies the necessary security standards to ensure data security. (Lawpilots, 2023)</span></span></span></p><div><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><br></span>\n</div></span>\n</div>',1,1,70,'2025-09-10 13:36:57','2025-10-08 09:45:57'),(177,29,'Blockchain—A Repetition to Begin','<div class=\"prose prose-lg max-w-none space-y-4\">\n\n<span id=\"docs-internal-guid-b44f336a-7fff-88be-6973-74c60d2e0836\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 14pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">What is a blockchain?</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">A blockchain is a special type of database, also known as a decentralized digital ledger (register), which is managed by numerous computers distributed all over the world. Blockchain data is organized into blocks that are arranged chronologically and secured by cryptography. The first model of a blockchain was developed in the early 1990s, when computer scientist Stuart Haber and physicist W. Scott Stornetta used cryptographic techniques in a chain of blocks to protect digital documents from data tampering. Haber and Stornetta inspired the work of many other computer scientists and cryptography enthusiasts, which eventually led to the creation of the first cryptocurrency, Bitcoin, based on blockchain technology. Since then, the adoption of blockchain technology has gradually expanded, and cryptocurrencies are being used by more and more people around the world. While blockchain technology is often used to record cryptocurrency transactions, it is also suitable for recording many other types of digital data and can be used in a variety of use cases.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\"><br></span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">What is a decentralized blockchain?</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Decentralization in the blockchain refers to the idea that the control and decision-making power of a network is distributed among its users and is not controlled by a single entity, such as a government or a corporation. This can be helpful in situations where people need to coordinate with strangers or want to ensure the security and integrity of their data. In a decentralized blockchain network, there is no central authority or intermediary that controls the flow of data or transactions. Instead, transactions are reviewed and recorded by a distributed network of computers that work together to maintain the integrity of the network. When talking about blockchain technology, it\'s often not just about the database. Blockchain technology powers applications such as cryptocurrencies and non-fungible tokens (NFTs), allowing people to collaborate and trade with each other without relying on a central authority.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\"><br></span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">How does a blockchain work?</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">At its core, a blockchain is a digital ledger that securely records transactions between two parties in a tamper-proof manner. This transaction data is recorded by a globally distributed network of special computers called nodes. When a user initiates a transaction, such as sending a certain amount of cryptocurrency to another user, that transaction is sent to the network. Each node authenticates the transaction by verifying digital signatures and other transaction data. Once the transaction is verified, it is added to a block along with other transactions that have already been verified. The blocks are chained together using cryptographic methods to form the blockchain. The process of verifying transactions and adding them to the blockchain is done through a consensus mechanism, a set of sequences that govern how the nodes in the network agree on the state of the blockchain and the validity of transactions. Cryptography is key for blockchain to ensure secure, transparent, and tamper-proof recording of transactions. For example, an important cryptographic method used in blockchains is hashing. This is a cryptographic process that converts an input of any size into a fixed-size string. The hash functions used in blockchains are usually collision-proof, meaning the probability of finding two pieces of data that give the same result is astronomically low. Another characteristic is the so-called avalanche effect, which refers to the phenomenon that every slight change in the input data results in a drastically different output. Let\'s illustrate this with SHA256, a function used in Bitcoin. As you can see, changing the capitalization of the letters results in a dramatic difference in output. Hash functions are also one-way functions, since it is not computationally feasible to obtain the input data by reversing the hash output. Each block within a blockchain securely contains the hash of the previous block, creating a stable chain of blocks. If you want to change a block, you would have to change all subsequent blocks, which is not only technically demanding but also prohibitively expensive. Another cryptographic method widely used in the blockchain is public-key cryptography. It is also known as asymmetric cryptography and helps to carry out secure and verifiable transactions between users. Here\'s how it works. Each participant has a unique pair of keys: a private key, which they keep secret, and a public key, which is publicly accessible. When a user initiates a transaction, they sign it with their private key, creating a digital signature. Other users of the network can then verify the authenticity of the transaction by applying the sender\'s public key to the digital signature. This approach ensures secure transactions because only the rightful owner of the private key can authorize a transaction, but anyone can verify the signatures against the public key. Another feature of the blockchain is its transparency. Anyone can generally verify a blockchain\'s data, including all transaction and block data, on public blockchain websites.For example, blockchain explorer sites allow you to view every transaction that has ever been recorded on the Bitcoin network, including the sender and recipient identifiers, the transfer amount, and a list of the owners of all Bitcoins. You can also trace the blocks from today (block 788,995, as of 18:52:21 GMT on May 29, 2023) back to the first block, the so-called genesis block.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\"><br></span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">What is a consensus mechanism?</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">A consensus algorithm is a mechanism that allows users or machines to coordinate in a distributed environment. He must ensure that all agents in the system can agree on a single source of truth, even if some agents fail. They ensure that all nodes in the network have the same copy of the ledger, which contains a record of all transactions. Consensus mechanisms are necessary for blockchains because there is no central authority to verify transactions and maintain the integrity of the network. When tens of thousands of nodes keep a copy of blockchain data, issues such as data consistency and malicious nodes can quickly arise. To ensure the integrity of the blockchain, there are various consensus mechanisms that govern how the network nodes reach an agreement. Let\'s now look at the most important of them.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\"><br></span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Types of consensus mechanisms</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">What is a proof of work?</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Proof of Work (PoW) is a consensus mechanism used in many blockchain networks to verify transactions and maintain the integrity of the blockchain. It is the original consensus mechanism used by Bitcoin. In PoW, miners compete to solve a complex mathematical problem in order to add the next block to the blockchain. In this process, known as mining, the first miner to solve the problem is rewarded with cryptocurrency. Miners must use powerful computers to solve mathematical problems in order to mine new coins and secure the network. For this reason, the mining process requires significant amounts of computing power and therefore energy.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\"><br></span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">What is Proof of Stake?</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Proof of Stake (PoS) is a consensus mechanism designed to address some of the drawbacks of Proof of Work (PoW). In a PoS system, miners do not compete to solve complex mathematical problems to validate transactions and add new blocks to the blockchain, but validators are selected based on the amount of cryptocurrency they \"stake\" on the network. Validators hold a certain amount of cryptocurrency as collateral, or \"stake,\" to participate in the consensus process. They are then randomly selected to create new blocks and validate transactions based on the size of their stake. Validators are rewarded with transaction fees for creating new blocks and as an incentive to act in the best interest of the network.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\"><br></span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Other Popular Consensus Mechanisms</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Proof of Work and Proof of Stake are the most common consensus algorithms, but there are others as well. Some are hybrid forms that combine elements from both systems, while others are completely different methods. Delegated Proof of Stake (DPoS), for example, is similar to PoS, but instead of all validators being authorized to create new blocks, token holders elect a smaller group of delegates to do so on their behalf. In Proof of Authority (PoA), on the other hand, validators are identified by their name or identity, rather than the amount of cryptocurrency they own. Validators are selected based on their trustworthiness and can be removed from the network if they act maliciously.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\"><br></span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Advantages of blockchain</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">1. Decentralization:</span></span></span><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\"> The decentralized nature of blockchain means that there is no single point of control or failure, making it more secure and resilient to attacks or data breaches.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">2. Transparency: </span></span></span><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Transactions on a blockchain are visible to all participants, making it easier to track and verify transactions and ensure their accuracy.&nbsp;</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">3. Immutability:</span></span></span><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\"> Once a transaction is recorded on a blockchain, it cannot be changed or deleted. A permanent record of all transactions is created, which can be verified by anyone who has access to the blockchain network. This is a key difference from traditional systems where transactions are reversible.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">4. Efficiency: </span></span></span><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Blockchain can enable faster and more efficient transactions because it does not require intermediaries such as banks.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">5. Lower fees: </span></span></span><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">By eliminating middlemen and automating processes, blockchain can reduce transaction costs and make certain business processes more efficient.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">6. Trustless:</span></span></span><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\"> Blockchain technology allows for transparent transactions that are verified and validated by the participants of the network themselves and without trusted intermediaries.</span></span></span></p><div><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><br></span>\n</div></span>\n</div>',1,0,70,'2025-09-12 05:49:26','2025-10-08 09:46:35'),(178,29,'What are the different blockchain networks?','<div class=\"prose prose-lg max-w-none space-y-4\">\n\n<span id=\"docs-internal-guid-e37e3e66-7fff-3d78-740c-2f45cf2dd0c9\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Public blockchain</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">A public blockchain is a decentralized network that is open to anyone who wants to participate. These networks are typically open-source, transparent, and permission less, which means anyone can access and use them. Bitcoin and Ethereum are examples of public blockchains.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Private Blockchain</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">A private blockchain, as the name suggests, is a blockchain network that is not accessible to the public. Private blockchains are usually operated by a single entity, such as a company, and are used for internal purposes and use cases.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:0pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Private blockchains are environments that require approval with set rules that dictate who can see and write to the chain. They are not decentralized systems because there is a clear hierarchy of control. However, they can be decentralized in that many nodes maintain a copy of the chain on their machines.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Consortium blockchain</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">A consortium blockchain is a mixture of public and private blockchains. In a consortium blockchain, several organizations join forces to create a common blockchain network that is jointly managed and controlled. These networks can be either open or closed, depending on the needs of the consortium members. Instead of an open system where anyone can validate blocks or a closed system where only a single entity determines the block producers, a consortium chain has a handful of equally powerful parties acting as validators. The rules of the system are flexible: the visibility of the chain can be limited to validators, visible to authorized people, or visible to everyone. If validators can reach consensus, changes can be easily implemented. As for how the blockchain works, the system will not run into trouble if a certain number of these parties behave honestly.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2449999999999999;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:8pt;\"><br></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">What is blockchain used for?</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Although blockchain technology is still in its infancy, there are already use cases in many different industries. Some of the most common current applications of blockchain technology include:</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">1. Cryptocurrencies: </span></span></span><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Blockchain technology was developed to support the creation of cryptocurrencies that use the blockchain as a secure and decentralized ledger for recording transactions.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">2. Digital identity: </span></span></span><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Blockchain can be used to create secure and tamper-proof digital identities that can be used to verify personal information and other sensitive data. This could become increasingly important as more personal information and assets become available online.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">3. Voting:</span></span></span><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\"> By providing a decentralized, tamper-proof register of all votes cast, blockchain technology can be used to create a secure and transparent voting system that eliminates the possibility of electoral fraud and ensures the integrity of the electoral process.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">4. Supply chain management: </span></span></span><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Blockchain technology can be used to create a ledger for all transactions within a supply chain. Each transaction can be recorded as a block on the blockchain, creating an immutable and transparent record of the entire supply chain process.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">5. Smart contracts:</span></span></span><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\"> Smart contracts are self-executing contracts that can be programmed to execute automatically when certain conditions are met. Blockchain technology enables the creation and execution of smart contracts in a secure and decentralized manner. One of the most promising applications of smart contracts is decentralized applications (dApps) and decentralized application organizations (DAOs).</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Final Thoughts</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Blockchain technology provides a secure and transparent way to record transactions and store data. It has the potential to revolutionize industries by bringing a new level of trust and security to the digital world. Whether it\'s enabling peer-to-peer transactions, creating new forms of digital assets, or facilitating decentralized applications, blockchain technology opens up a world of possibilities. As the technology continues to develop and spread, it is expected that more innovative and transformative use cases will emerge in the coming years. (Binance Academy, 2023).</span></span></span></p><div><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><br></span>\n</div></span>\n</div>',1,0,70,'2025-09-12 05:51:00','2025-10-08 09:47:04');
INSERT INTO `subsections` VALUES (179,29,'Cryptocurrencies','<div class=\"prose prose-lg max-w-none space-y-4\">\n\n<span id=\"docs-internal-guid-ec239db7-7fff-8e56-81d5-3faa8344912a\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 14pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">What are cryptocurrencies?</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Cryptocurrency is a decentralized digital currency that uses cryptography for security. It can function independently of intermediaries such as banks and payment processors. This decentralized nature facilitates peer-to-peer (P2P) transactions directly between individuals. However, instead of physical wallets and bank accounts, people have access to their cryptocurrency through unique crypto wallets or crypto exchanges. You may have heard that cryptocurrencies are \"stored\" in wallets. However, cryptocurrencies do not exist in crypto wallets or exchanges—in reality, they always remain on the blockchain. In the case of a crypto exchange, it contains the private keys that allow users to access these funds. The first and most well-known cryptocurrency is Bitcoin, which was created in 2009 by a person or group under the pseudonym Satoshi Nakamoto. Since then, thousands of cryptocurrencies have emerged, each with unique characteristics and purposes.&nbsp; traditional fiat currencies, cryptocurrencies can also be used as a medium of exchange. However, the use cases for cryptocurrencies have expanded significantly over the years to include smart contracts, decentralized finance (DeFi), store of value systems, governance, and non-fungible tokens (NFT). We mentioned that cryptocurrencies use cryptography for security purposes, but what does that really mean? Simply put, cryptocurrencies use advanced mathematical algorithms to secure transactions and protect data from unauthorized access or tampering. These algorithms serve two main functions: maintaining the privacy of user identities and verifying the authenticity of transactions. Blockchain transactions are public, and addresses (public keys) are pseudonymous, although not completely anonymous. In other words, while transactions are visible on the blockchain, the users behind them are not easy to identify. Cryptocurrencies achieve this through the use of cryptographic techniques such as hash functions and digital signatures. Cryptocurrencies achieve their autonomy through a distributed computer network called a blockchain. This is essentially a decentralized digital ledger where transaction data is stored on many specialized computers on the network. Each of these computers—also called nodes—maintains a copy of the ledger, and a consensus algorithm preserves the blockchain by ensuring that fake or inconsistent copies are rejected. This distributed architecture increases the security of the network because there is not a single vulnerability that could be exploited by malicious actors, such as a bank vault. Cryptocurrencies allow individuals to transfer money directly to each other. In a typical cryptocurrency transaction, the sender initiates the transfer by creating a digital signature with their private key. The transaction is then sent to the network, where the nodes verify the digital signature and ensure that the sender has sufficient funds. Once the transaction is verified, it is added to a new block, which is then added to the existing blockchain. While this may sound complicated, miners take care of these steps, so the user doesn\'t have to worry about them.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">What makes cryptocurrencies so unique?</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:5pt;padding:5pt 0pt 0pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Cryptocurrencies have influenced various ecosystems, from the financial world to technology, by introducing innovative features that set them apart from traditional protocols and currencies. Some of the unique aspects of cryptocurrencies include:</span></span></span></p><ol style=\"padding-inline-start: 48px;\"><li dir=\"ltr\" style=\"list-style-type: decimal; font-size: 10pt; font-family: Calibri, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre; margin-left: 59.35pt;\" aria-level=\"1\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\" role=\"presentation\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Decentralization:</span></span></span><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\"> The decentralized architecture of cryptocurrencies eliminates the need for a central authority. This allows for greater autonomy as well as less susceptibility to manipulation or control by a single entity.</span></span></span></p></li><li dir=\"ltr\" style=\"list-style-type: decimal; font-size: 10pt; font-family: Calibri, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre; margin-left: 59.35pt;\" aria-level=\"1\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\" role=\"presentation\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Transparency and immutability:</span></span></span><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\"> Blockchain technology records all transactions in a transparent and tamper-proof ledger. Therefore, once a transaction is added to the blockchain, it can be viewed by anyone and cannot be changed or deleted.</span></span></span></p></li><li dir=\"ltr\" style=\"list-style-type: decimal; font-size: 10pt; font-family: Calibri, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre; margin-left: 59.35pt;\" aria-level=\"1\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\" role=\"presentation\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Programmability: </span></span></span><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Many cryptocurrencies, such as ETH, are programmable, allowing developers to deploy smart contracts to create decentralized applications (DApps) and other innovative solutions on blockchains. Since permission less blockchains are also open-source, anyone can start using code on a blockchain and create their own DApps.</span></span></span></p></li><li dir=\"ltr\" style=\"list-style-type: decimal; font-size: 10pt; font-family: Calibri, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre; margin-left: 59.35pt;\" aria-level=\"1\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\" role=\"presentation\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Borderless:</span></span></span><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\"> Cryptocurrencies can be easily transferred and exchanged worldwide, allowing them to be used for international transactions and remittances.</span></span></span></p></li><li dir=\"ltr\" style=\"list-style-type: decimal; font-size: 10pt; font-family: Calibri, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre; margin-left: 59.35pt;\" aria-level=\"1\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\" role=\"presentation\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Predefined supply of coins: </span></span></span><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Many cryptocurrencies have a limited supply of coins, which means that the teams behind them can only produce a finite number of coins at a time. This deflationary aspect of cryptocurrencies can have a positive impact over time, as scarcity drives demand.</span></span></span></p></li></ol><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:0pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">In contrast, fiat currencies are often inflationary, as central banks can print more money. However, with a limited supply, cryptocurrency inflation can be better controlled because the total number of coins is predetermined.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Types of cryptocurrencies</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Among the myriad cryptocurrencies are four notable examples: Bitcoin (BTC) and the popular altcoins Ether (ETH), Binance Coin (BNB), and Tether (USDT).</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Bitcoin (BTC)</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">BTC is the most popular cryptocurrency. It uses a consensus mechanism called Proof-of-Work (PoW), where miners compete to validate transactions and keep the network running. In addition, BTC is relatively scarce due to its limited supply of 21 million coins, helping to maintain its value over time.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Ether (ETH)</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">ETH is the second most popular cryptocurrency and was launched in 2015 by Vitalik Buterin and his team. In addition to value transfers, it enables programmability through smart contracts.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:0pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Like BTC, ETH initially used a PoW consensus mechanism but has since switched to the greener and more energy-efficient proof-of-stake (PoS) model. This switch has allowed users to validate transactions and secure the network by staking their ETH as collateral, rather than through nodes that require computing power.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">BNB</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">BNB (BNB stands for Build and Build) was formerly known as Binance Coin and was launched in 2017 by cryptocurrency exchange Binance as an ERC-20 token on the Ethereum blockchain. In 2019, it migrated to its own blockchain, BNB Chain, as a BEP-2 token. Later, Binance Smart Chain (BSC; now called BNB Smart Chain) was created, and today, the BNB cryptocurrency exists on both BNB Chain as a BEP-2 token and BSC as a BEP-20 token. It\'s also worth noting that BNB Chain consists of two chains: the EVM-compatible BSC, as well as the BNB Beacon Chain (formerly called Binance Chain), which includes governance, staking, and voting. The BNB Chain provides an environment for creating smart contracts and DApps and is characterized by lower transaction fees and faster processing times than many other blockchains. BNB has various use cases, some of which include paying transaction fees on the BNB Chain and trading fees on Binance, participating in token sales, and staking for network validation on the BNB Chain. Binance also uses a periodic token burn mechanism that limits the total supply of BNB.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Tether (USDT)</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">USDT is a USD-pegged stable coin launched in 2014 by Tether Limited Inc. Stable coins are cryptocurrencies that are designed to maintain a consistent value relative to a reserve asset, such as a fiat currency. In the case of USDT, each token is backed by an equivalent amount of assets in the company\'s reserves. Therefore, USDT offers the benefits of a cryptocurrency while minimizing price fluctuations.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">What is crypto market cap?</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">The term \"crypto market capitalization\" is short for \"cryptocurrency market capitalization,\" a metric used to determine the relative size and value of a cryptocurrency. You can easily calculate them by multiplying the current price of a coin by the total number of coins in circulation. However, you may not have to do this at all, as many cryptocurrency platforms charge for you. The cryptocurrency market cap is often used to classify cryptocurrencies, with a higher market cap generally indicating a more stable and widely accepted cryptocurrency. Conversely, a lower market cap usually signals a more speculative or volatile asset. However, keep in mind that this is just one of the many factors to consider when evaluating a cryptocurrency\'s potential. Several other factors, such as technology, team, tokenomics, and use cases, should also be considered when investigating cryptocurrencies.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">How to invest in cryptocurrencies safely</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Like other financial assets, investing in cryptocurrencies can be risky and lead to financial losses. Here are five important tips to make buying and selling cryptocurrencies safer:</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">1. DYOR</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">The acronym DYOR stands for \"do your own research.\" It\'s important to understand the basics of blockchain technology—such as the different types of cryptocurrencies and market dynamics—before investing in any cryptocurrency.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:0pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Books, blogs, podcasts, and online courses are good places to start. You should also learn about the projects, teams, and technologies behind the various cryptocurrencies to make informed decisions.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">2. Start small and diversify</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">The crypto market can be volatile and unpredictable, especially when it comes to less popular coins. Therefore, it is smart to start with small investments that do not weigh on the wallet. In this way, one can gain experience and develop a better understanding of market trends without risking large financial losses. Diversification can also make sense when investing in cryptocurrencies. Rather than focusing on a single cryptocurrency, investing in different cryptocurrencies can reduce your overall risk and increase the chances of long-term growth in your holdings.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">3. Stay involved</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">As the cryptocurrency landscape is constantly changing, it\'s important to stay up-to-date with news, technological advancements, and regulatory updates in order to make timely decisions. Joining a crypto community is an excellent way to do this.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">4. Choose a reputable cryptocurrency exchange</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Choosing a well-known and safe cryptocurrency exchange for your crypto investments should be your top priority in terms of security measures. You can find the right cryptocurrency exchange by researching different options and comparing their fees, customer support, interface, and available cryptocurrencies.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">5. Practice risk management</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Before investing in any cryptocurrency, be sure to use some risk management techniques. For example, investors should only invest as much as they can afford to lose. In addition, stop-loss orders to limit potential losses and take profits at predetermined values can make a big difference to lock in profits.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">What is a crypto white paper?</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">A crypto white paper is a document that explains the details and technical specifications of a blockchain project. It usually includes information such as the goals of the project, how it works, the underlying technology, the team involved, the tokenomics of the project, and the roadmap for development and implementation. Cryptocurrency whitepapers serve as a comprehensive guide to the project, explaining its purpose and potential benefits. Investors and community members often read and review white papers to evaluate the legitimacy and potential of a cryptocurrency project before investing or getting involved. As such, white papers are essential to transparency and accountability in the cryptocurrency industry. However, there are no standards or regulations for white papers, and they could be misleading or inaccurate. Cryptocurrency project creators can write whatever they want in their whitepapers. Therefore, the responsibility for verifying the veracity of the claims in the document lies with the users.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">ResultConclusion</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">The cryptocurrency ecosystem represents a revolutionary approach to finance and technology. However, the future of cryptocurrencies depends on who you ask. Some believe that Bitcoin will replace gold and disrupt the existing financial system, while others argue that cryptocurrencies will always be a secondary system and a niche market. There are also those who believe that Ethereum will become a decentralized computer that will serve as the backbone of a new internet. While there are numerous possible outcomes, it\'s simply too early to determine what will happen in a year. Nevertheless, we cannot deny the already visible influence of cryptocurrency on various industries, which is likely to continue to develop in the coming years. (Binance Academy, 2023)</span></span></span></p><div><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><br></span>\n</div></span>\n</div>',1,0,70,'2025-09-12 05:53:05','2025-10-08 09:47:32'),(180,29,'What is Bitcoin, and how does it work?','<div class=\"prose prose-lg max-w-none space-y-4\">\n\n<span id=\"docs-internal-guid-35e389fb-7fff-d637-3612-140084387014\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:0pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Bitcoin is a digital form of cash. But unlike the government-issued fiat currencies you\'re used to, it\'s not controlled by a central bank. Instead, Bitcoin\'s financial system is powered by thousands of computers around the world. Anyone can participate in this ecosystem by downloading Bitcoin\'s open-source software.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:0pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Bitcoin was the first cryptocurrency to be announced in 2008 (and launched in 2009). It allows users to send and receive digital money called bitcoins (with a lowercase b, or BTC). What makes them so attractive is their inherent resistance to censorship, the impossibility of double-spending funds, and the ability to transact anytime, anywhere.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">What makes Bitcoin so unique?</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:5pt;padding:5pt 0pt 0pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Here are a few of the key features that make Bitcoin unique:</span></span></span></p><ol style=\"padding-inline-start: 48px;\"><li dir=\"ltr\" style=\"list-style-type: decimal; font-size: 10pt; font-family: Calibri, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre; margin-left: 59.35pt;\" aria-level=\"1\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\" role=\"presentation\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Decentralization: </span></span></span><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Bitcoin operates on a decentralized public blockchain, which means that it is not controlled by a central authority. Instead, transactions are verified by a network of computers called nodes. In addition, anyone can join the network and contribute to its security.</span></span></span></p></li><li dir=\"ltr\" style=\"list-style-type: decimal; font-size: 10pt; font-family: Calibri, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre; margin-left: 59.35pt;\" aria-level=\"1\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\" role=\"presentation\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Permission less</span></span></span><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">: Bitcoin\'s permission less nature means that anyone with an internet connection can participate in the Bitcoin network without getting a permit or permission from a central authority. Bitcoin allows users to send and receive payments to anyone on the network, regardless of location or identity. This has made bitcoins especially popular in regions where access to traditional financial systems is limited or nonexistent.</span></span></span></p></li><li dir=\"ltr\" style=\"list-style-type: decimal; font-size: 10pt; font-family: Calibri, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre; margin-left: 59.35pt;\" aria-level=\"1\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\" role=\"presentation\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Limited supply:</span></span></span><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\"> Bitcoin has a limited supply of 21 million coins, which is hardcoded in the protocol. This means that there will never be more than 21 million Bitcoins in circulation, which helps prevent inflation.</span></span></span></p></li><li dir=\"ltr\" style=\"list-style-type: decimal; font-size: 10pt; font-family: Calibri, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre; margin-left: 59.35pt;\" aria-level=\"1\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\" role=\"presentation\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Transparency: </span></span></span><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">All Bitcoin transactions are recorded on a public ledger that is visible to all users. This means that anyone can view the transactions that have taken place, including the Bitcoin amounts involved and the addresses of the sender and receiver. In traditional financial systems, transactions are recorded by banks and other financial institutions, and this information is generally not available to the public. Instead, people rely on these institutions to keep accurate records.</span></span></span></p></li><li dir=\"ltr\" style=\"list-style-type: decimal; font-size: 10pt; font-family: Calibri, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre; margin-left: 59.35pt;\" aria-level=\"1\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\" role=\"presentation\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Divisible: </span></span></span><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Bitcoin can be divided into smaller units called satoshis, which make up one hundred millionth of a Bitcoin. This means that even if the price of a Bitcoin becomes very high, people can still work and transact with very small amounts of the currency. This makes bitcoins more accessible to people with limited financial resources and allows for more accurate transactions.</span></span></span></p></li></ol><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">How does Bitcoin work?</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">When Alice makes a transaction with Bob, she doesn\'t send money as you\'d expect. It\'s not the digital equivalent of handing him a dollar bill. It\'s more like she\'s writing on a piece of paper (for everyone to see) that she\'s giving Bob a dollar. If Bob wants to send Carol the same money, she can see from the note that he has it.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:0pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Transaction examples:</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:0pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">The sheet is a database called Blockchain. All network participants have an identical copy of it stored on their devices. Participants connect to each other to synchronize new information. To ensure the security and integrity of the blockchain, Bitcoin uses a consensus mechanism known as Proof of Work (PoW). When a user makes a payment, they send it to the network, where it is verified by other nodes called \"miners.\" These miners compete to solve a complex mathematical puzzle and have to spend computing power to do so. The first miner to solve the puzzle is allowed to add a new block of transactions to the blockchain. As an incentive, there is a reward for anyone who proposes a valid block. The reward, which is often referred to as a block reward, is made up of two components: transaction fees from the transactions within the block and the block subsidy. The block fee is the only source of \"fresh\" Bitcoins. With each block that is mined, a certain amount of coins is added to the total supply. Bitcoin\'s PoW consensus mechanism is designed in such a way that it is expensive to create a block but cheap to check for validity. Suppose someone tries to cheat with an invalid block. In this case, the network immediately rejects the block, and the miner cannot recoup the cost of mining.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">What is Bitcoin used for?</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Bitcoin is mainly used as a digital currency and store of value. It can be used for online or in-person purchases like traditional currencies. Anyone with an internet connection can send and receive Bitcoin, and since it\'s digital, it can be transferred worldwide. Bitcoin is occasionally used for more private transactions. The transactions are public, and the addresses (public keys) are pseudonymous, although not completely anonymous. In other words, while the transactions are visible on the blockchain, the users behind them are not easy to identify. Some people also buy Bitcoins as a long-term investment in the expectation that their value will increase over time. Like gold or other commodities, Bitcoins are a viable option for investors looking to diversify their portfolio due to their limited supply and decentralized nature.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">The History of Bitcoin</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Bitcoin was first introduced in 2008 when Satoshi Nakamoto published a white paper titled \"Bitcoin: A Peer-to-Peer Electronic Cash System.\" This white paper introduced a new digital currency that is based on a decentralized system and is not dependent on governments or the banking system. In January 2009, the Bitcoin protocol was released, and the first Bitcoin transaction took place between Satoshi Nakamoto and a programmer named Hal Finney. In the transaction, ten Bitcoins were sent from Nakamoto to Finney. After this initial transaction, more and more people discovered Bitcoin and joined the network. The digital currency gained popularity among a small community of tech enthusiasts by showing that Bitcoin can work without a central authority or intermediary. Bitcoin Pizza is another important milestone in the history of Bitcoin, as it was the first time Bitcoins were used as a medium of exchange for a real-world transaction. On May 22, 2010, a programmer named Laszlo Hanyecz made history by using 10,000 bitcoins to buy two pizzas. The transaction became known as \"Bitcoin Pizza Day\" and is now celebrated on May 22 every year.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Who created Bitcoin?</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">The identity of Satoshi Nakamoto remains a mystery. Satoshi could be a person or a group of developers anywhere in the world. The name is of Japanese origin, but Satoshi\'s mastery of the English language has led many to believe that he or she is from an English-speaking country.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Did Satoshi invent blockchain technology?</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Bitcoin combines a number of existing technologies that have been around for a long time, and this includes blockchain technology. The use of such immutable data structures can be traced back to the early 1990s, when Stuart Haber and W. Scott Stornetta proposed a system for timestamping documents. Similar to today\'s blockchains, it relied on cryptographic techniques to secure data and protect it from tampering.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">How many Bitcoins are there?</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">The protocol sets the maximum amount of Bitcoins at 21 million coins. By 2023, just over 90% of it will have been mined, but it will take more than a hundred years to produce the rest. This is due to periodic events known as \"halving,\" which gradually reduce the mining reward.&nbsp;</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">What is Bitcoin halving?</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Bitcoin halving is a process that reduces the rate at which new Bitcoin blocks are created. More specifically, these are the periodic halving events that reduce the block rewards offered to miners. The next Bitcoin halving is expected to take place in 2024, about four years after the last halving, which took place in May 2020. The Bitcoin halving is the core of the economic model because it ensures that the coins are issued at a steady pace and become increasingly difficult at a predictable rate. Such controlled monetary inflation is one of the main differences between cryptocurrencies and traditional fiat currencies, the supply of which is basically infinite.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Is Bitcoin safe?</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">One of the main risks associated with Bitcoin is the potential for hacking and theft. In phishing scams, for example, hackers use social engineering techniques to trick users into revealing their login credentials or private keys. Once the hacker has access to the user\'s account or crypto wallet, they can transfer the victim\'s Bitcoins to their own wallet. Another way for hackers to steal bitcoins is through malware or ransomware attacks. Hackers can infect a user\'s computer or mobile device with malware that allows them to access the user\'s Bitcoin wallet. In some cases, hackers can also use ransomware to encrypt a user\'s files and demand payment in bitcoins to unlock them. Since Bitcoin transactions cannot be reversed and are not insured by a government agency, users must take precautions to protect their Bitcoin holdings. These include using strong passwords, two-factor authentication, and storing bitcoins in a secure crypto wallet that is inaccessible to hackers. It is also important to download Bitcoin-related software only from trusted sources. Another risk associated with Bitcoin is price volatility. The value of bitcoin can fluctuate wildly within short periods of time, making it a risky investment for those who are not prepared for the potential losses.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Result</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Bitcoin is a decentralized digital currency that has garnered a lot of attention in recent years. Created to provide an alternative to traditional financial systems, it operates through a peer-to-peer network that allows users to send and receive payments without intermediaries. Although Bitcoin is still a relatively new technology, it is already revolutionizing the way we think about money. It will be interesting to see how Bitcoin and other cryptocurrencies evolve and whether they become part of our everyday lives. (Binance Academy, 2020)</span></span></span></p><div><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><br></span>\n</div></span>\n</div>',1,0,70,'2025-09-12 05:58:51','2025-10-08 09:48:00'),(181,29,'What is Ethereum?','<div class=\"prose prose-lg max-w-none space-y-4\">\n\n<span id=\"docs-internal-guid-34e6da3d-7fff-a393-e7eb-03768005c996\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:0pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Ethereum is a decentralized computing platform. You can think of it like a laptop or PC, but it doesn\'t run on a single device. Instead, it runs simultaneously on thousands of machines around the world, which means that it has no owner. With Ethereum, you can transfer digital money, just like with Bitcoin and other cryptocurrencies. But it can do much more—you can use your own code and interact with other users\' applications. Because Ethereum is so flexible, all kinds of sophisticated programs can be launched on Ethereum. Simply put, the main idea behind Ethereum is that developers can create and launch code that runs over a distributed network instead of existing on a centralized server. This means that these applications cannot, in theory, be shut down or censored.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">What is the difference between Ethereum and Ether (ETH)?</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">It may be counterintuitive, but the units used in Ethereum are not called Ethereum or Ethereums. Ethereum is the protocol itself, but the currency that powers it is simply known as Ether (or ETH).</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">What makes Ethereum so valuable?</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">We touched on the idea of Ethereum being able to execute code over a distributed system. As such, programs cannot be tampered with by external parties. They are added to the Ethereum database (i.e. the blockchain) and can be programmed so that the code cannot be changed. In addition, the database is visible to everyone, so users can review the code before interacting with it. This means that anyone, anywhere can launch applications that can\'t be taken offline. What\'s even more interesting is that these applications can set conditions for transferring values, as their native unit – Ether – stores values. We call the programs that make up the applications smart contracts. In most cases, they can be set to work without human intervention. Understandably, the idea of \"programmable money\" has captivated users, developers, and businesses around the globe.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">What is the blockchain?</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">The blockchain is at the heart of Ethereum – it is the database where the information used by the protocol is stored. If you read our article What is Bitcoin? , you\'ll have a basic understanding of how a blockchain works. The Ethereum blockchain is similar to Bitcoin\'s, although the data it stores and the way data is it storeds it is different. It\'s helpful to think of the Ethereum blockchain as a book that you keep adding pages to. Each page is called a block and is filled with information about transactions. If we want to add a new page, we need to add a special value at the top of the page. Based on this value, everyone should be able to see that the new page was added after the previous page and was not just randomly inserted into the book. Basically, it\'s something like a page number that points to the previous page. Looking at the new page, we can safely say that it follows on from the previous page. To do this, we use a process called hashing. Hashing involves taking a piece of data – in this case, everything on our side – and returning a unique identifier (our hash). The probability of two pieces of data making the same hash is astronomically low. It\'s also a one-way process: you can easily calculate a hash, but it\'s virtually impossible to reverse the hash to get the information that was used to create it. We will explain why this is important for mining in a later chapter. Now we have a mechanism to link our pages together in the right order. Any attempt to change the order or remove pages makes it clear that our book has been tampered with. &nbsp;Want to learn more about blockchains? Be sure to check out our beginner\'s guide to blockchain technology.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Ethereum vs. Bitcoin - what\'s the difference?</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Bitcoin relies on blockchain technology and financial incentives to create a global digital money system. It has introduced some major innovations that allow the coordination of users around the world without the need for a central party. By running a program on their computer, each participant allows users to communicate about the state of a financial database in a trustless, decentralized environment. Bitcoin is often referred to as a first-generation blockchain. It wasn\'t created as an overly complex system, and that\'s a strength when it comes to security. It was intentionally kept inflexible to prioritize base-layer security. In fact, the smart contract language in Bitcoin is extremely limited, and it doesn\'t lend itself particularly well to applications outside of transactions. The second generation of blockchains, on the other hand, can do more. In addition to financial transactions, these platforms allow for a higher level of programmability. Ethereum offers developers much more freedom to experiment with their own code and create so-called decentralized applications (DApps). Ethereum was the first of the second-generation blockchain wave and remains the most well-known to this day. It shares similarities with Bitcoin and can perform many of the same functions. Under the hood, however, the two are very different and each has its own advantages over the other.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">How does Ethereum work?</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">We could define Ethereum as a state machine. This means that you have a snapshot of all account balances and smart contracts at any given time, as they look right now. Certain actions cause the state to be updated, which means that all nodes update their own snapshot to reflect the change. The smart contracts that run on Ethereum are triggered by transactions (either from users or other contracts). When a user sends a transaction to a contract, each node in the network executes the contract\'s code and records the output. This is done with the help of the Ethereum Virtual Machine (EVM), which converts the smart contracts into instructions that the computer can read. To update the status, a special mechanism called mining is used (for the time being). Mining is done using a proof-of-work algorithm, similar to that of Bitcoin. We will explain this in more detail shortly.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">What is a smart contract?</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">A smart contract is just code. The code is neither intelligent, nor is it a contract in the traditional sense. But we call it intelligent because it executes itself under certain conditions, and it could be considered a contract because it enforces agreements between parties. The idea goes back to computer scientist Nick Szabo, who proposed it in the late 1990s. He explained the concept using the example of a vending machine, which can be seen as a precursor to the modern smart contract. In the case of a vending machine, a simple contract is executed. Users insert coins and in return, the machine dispenses a product of their choice. A smart contract applies this kind of logic in a digital environment. You could specify something simple in the code, such as \"Hello, World!\" when two Ether coins are sent to this contract.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Hello World Contract</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">In Ethereum, the developer would code this so that it can be read by the EVM later. He then publishes it by sending it to a special address that registers the contract. From this point on, anyone can use it. And the contract cannot be deleted unless the developer has set a condition when writing the contract. Now the contract has an address. To interact with it, users only need to send 2 ETH to this address. This triggers the code of the contract - all computers on the network execute it, see that the payment to the contract has been made, and record its output (\"Hello, world!\"). This is perhaps one of the simplest examples of what you can do with Ethereum. More sophisticated applications that connect many contracts can - and have - already been developed.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Who created Ethereum?</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">In 2008, an unknown developer (or group of developers) published the Bitcoin whitepaper under the pseudonym Satoshi Nakamoto. This changed the landscape of digital money permanently. A few years later, a young programmer named Vitalik Buterin had the idea to develop this idea further and apply it to any type of application. The concept was eventually developed into Ethereum. Ethereum was proposed by Buterin in a 2013 blog post titled \"Ethereum: The Ultimate Smart Contract and Decentralized Application Platform.\" In this post, he described the idea of a Turing-complete blockchain – a decentralized computer that could run any application with enough time and resources. Over time, the types of applications that could be deployed on a blockchain would only be limited by the imagination of the developers. Ethereum is intended to find out whether blockchain technology can also be used sensibly outside the intentionally set boundaries of Bitcoin.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">How was Ether spread?</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Ethereum was launched in 2015 with an initial supply of 72 million Ether. More than 50 million of these tokens were distributed in a public token sale called an Initial Coin Offering (ICO), where interested parties could buy Ether tokens in exchange for bitcoins or fiat currency.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">What was \"The DAO\" and what is Ethereum Classic?</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">With Ethereum, completely new ways of open collaboration over the Internet have become possible. Take, for example, DAOs (decentralized autonomous organizations) that are controlled by computer code, much like a computer program. One of the earliest and most ambitious attempts at such an organization was \"The DAO.\" It should consist of complex smart contracts built on Ethereum and act as an autonomous venture fund. The DAO tokens were distributed as part of an ICO, giving token holders an ownership share as well as voting rights. However, not long after launch, malicious actors exploited a vulnerability and drained nearly a third of DAO funds. It\'s important to keep in mind that at that time, 14% of the total Ether volume was locked up in the DAO. Needless to say, this was a devastating event for the fledgling Ethereum network. After some consideration, the chain was split into two chains. In one, the malicious transactions were effectively \"reversed\" in order to recover the funds – this chain is now known as the Ethereum blockchain. The original chain, where these transactions were not reversed and immutability was maintained, is now known as Ethereum Classic. The event was a stark reminder of the risks of this technology and how entrusting large amounts of assets to an autonomous code can backfire. It\'s also an interesting example of how collective decision-making in an open environment can present significant challenges. However, security vulnerabilities aside, The DAO perfectly illustrates the potential of smart contracts that enable trustless collaboration at scale over the internet.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">How is new Ether created?</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">We briefly touched on mining earlier. If you\'re familiar with Bitcoin, you\'ll know that the mining process is an essential part of securing and updating the blockchain. In Ethereum, the same principle applies: to reward users who mine (which is very costly), the protocol rewards them with Ether.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">How many Ether are there?</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">As of February 2020, the total supply of Ether is about 110 million. Unlike Bitcoin, the timeline for issuing tokens on Ethereum was intentionally not set at launch. In the case of Bitcoin, an attempt was made to preserve value by limiting the supply and slowly reducing the number of newly minted coins. Ethereum, on the other hand, aims to create a foundation for decentralized applications (DApps). Since it is unclear what type of token issuance plan best suits this purpose, the question remains open.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2449999999999999;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:8pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"width:100px;display:inline-block;position:relative;\"></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:-3pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><br></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">How does Ethereum mining work?</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Mining is crucial for the security of the network. It ensures that the blockchain can be updated in a fair way and allows the network to function without a single decision-maker. In mining, a subset of nodes (aptly called miners) dedicate their computing power to solving a cryptographic puzzle. What they actually do is hash a bunch of pending transactions along with some other data. For the block to be considered valid, the hash value must fall below a value set by the protocol. If they don\'t succeed, they can change some of the data and try again. Therefore, in order to compete with others, miners need to be able to hack as fast as possible – we measure their performance in hash rate. The higher the hash rate in the network, the more difficult the puzzle becomes to solve. Only the miners have to find the actual solution - once it is known, it is easy for all other participants to check its validity. As you can imagine, continuous hashing at high speed is expensive. To incentivize miners to secure the network, they receive a reward. This is made up of all fees for the transactions in the block. They will also receive freshly generated Ether – 2 ETH at the time of writing.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">What is Ethereum gas?</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Remember our \"Hello, World\" contract earlier? This was an easy program to execute. It\'s not very computationally intensive at all. But you\'re not just running it on your own PC – you\'re asking everyone in the Ethereum ecosystem to run it as well. This leads us to the following question: What happens when tens of thousands of people enter into elaborate contracts? If someone sets up their contract to go through the same code over and over again, each node would have to execute it infinitely. That would put too much strain on resources and the system would probably collapse. Fortunately, Ethereum is introducing the concept of gas to mitigate this risk. Just as your car can\'t run without gas, contracts can\'t run without gas. Contracts stipulate a certain amount of gas that users must pay for it to be successfully executed. If there is not enough gasoline, the contract is stopped. Basically, it is a fee mechanism. The same concept applies to transactions: miners are primarily profit-oriented, which is why they can ignore transactions with a lower fee. Note that ether and gas are not the same thing. The average price of gas fluctuates and is largely determined by miners. When you make a transaction, you pay for the gas in Ether (ETH). In this regard, it\'s like Bitcoin fees – if the network is congested and many users are trying to transact, the average gas price is likely to rise. Conversely, if there is not much activity, it will decrease. While the price of gas changes, a fixed amount of gas is needed for each operation. This means that complex contracts consume much more than a simple transaction. The gas is therefore a measure of computing power. It ensures that the system can pay users a reasonable fee depending on how Ethereum resources are used. Gas generally costs only a fraction of Ether. For this reason, we use a smaller unit (gwei) to denote it. One gwei is equal to one billionth of an ether.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Gas and gas limits</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Suppose Alice is making a transaction on a contract. The contractIt would calculate how much it wants to spend on gasoline (for example with ETH Gas Station). It could set a higher price to incentivize miners to honor their transaction as soon as possible. &nbsp;But it will also set a gas limit for its protection. Something could go wrong with the contract, so it consumes more gas than she planned. The gas limit is designed to ensure that the process is stopped once a certain amount of gas is consumed. The contract won\'t go through, but Alice won\'t end up paying more than she originally agreed. At first glance, this may seem like a confusing concept. Don\'t worry – you can manually set the price you\'re willing to pay for gas (and the gas limit), but most wallets will do that for you. In short, the gas price determines how quickly miners will accept your transaction, and the gas limit defines the maximum amount you pay for it.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">How long does it take to mine an Ethereum block?</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">On average, it takes between 12 and 19 seconds for a new block to be added to the chain. The change we saw on 15</span></span></span><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-size:0.6em;vertical-align:super;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">th</span></span></span></span><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\"> Sept 2022 with shift from Proof of Work to is will most likely change once the network switches to Proof of Stake, did not change this velocity much which is estimated at 14 to 15 seconds these days. which should allow for faster block times, among other things. If you want to learn more about it, read Ethereum Casper Explained.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">What are Ethereum tokens?</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">A big part of Ethereum\'s appeal is that users can create their own assets on-chain, which can be stored and transferred like Ether. The rules for these tokens are set out in smart contracts that allow developers to set specific parameters for their tokens. This includes how many tokens to spend, how to spend them, whether they are divisible, whether they are fungible, and more. The most well-known of the technical standards that allow the creation of tokens on Ethereum is called ERC-20 – and that\'s why the tokens are popularly known as ERC-20 tokens. The token functionality provides innovators with a huge playground for experimenting with state-of-the-art applications in finance and technology. From issuing unified tokens that serve as in-app currency to creating unique tokens backed by physical assets, there\'s a lot of design flexibility. It\'s entirely possible that some of the best use cases for simple and streamlined token creation aren\'t even known yet.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">What can I buy with Ether (ETH)?</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">In contrast to Bitcoin, Ethereum is not only to be used as a cryptocurrency network. It is a platform for building decentralized applications, and as a tradable token, Ether is the fuel of this ecosystem. So, arguably, the primary use case for Ether is the utility it offers within the Ethereum network. However, Ether can also be used like a traditional currency, i.e. You can use ETH to buy goods and services just like any other currency.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">What is Ethereum used for?</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">The Ethereum currency ETH can be used as digital money or collateral. Many also see it as a store of value, similar to Bitcoin. However, unlike Bitcoin, the Ethereum blockchain is more programmable, so you can do a lot more with ETH. It can be used as a lifeblood for decentralized finance applications, decentralized markets, exchanges, games, and much more.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">What if I lose my ETH?</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Since no banks are involved, you are responsible for your own money. You can store your coins on an exchange or in your own wallet. If you\'re using your own wallet, it\'s imperative to take care of your seed phrase. Keep them safe, as you\'ll need them to recover your funds in case you lose access to your wallet.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Can I reverse Ethereum transactions?</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Once data has been added to the Ethereum blockchain, it is almost impossible to change or remove it. This means that when you make a transaction, you can assume that it is set in stone. So, you should always check that you are sending money to the right address. If you\'re sending a large amount, it may make sense to send a small amount at first to be sure you\'re sending to the correct address. Due to a hack in a smart contract, Ethereum was essentially altered (hard forking) in 2016, effectively \"reversing\" the malicious transactions. However, this was an extreme measure for an extraordinary event and not the norm.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Are Ethereum transactions private?</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">No. All transactions added to the Ethereum blockchain are publicly visible. Even if your real name isn\'t on your Ethereum address, an observer could link it to your identity via other methods.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Can I make money on Ethereum?</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Since it is a volatile asset, you can make money with ETH, but you can also lose money. Some people hold Ether for the long term and bet that the network will evolve into a global, programmable billing layer. Others choose to trade Ether for other altcoins. However, both strategies carry their own financial risks. As the main pillar of the DeFi (Decentralized Finance) movement, ETH can also be used for lending, as collateral for borrowing, minting synthetic assets, and – at some point in the future – for staking. Some investors may only hold a long-term position in Bitcoin and may not include other digital assets in their portfolio. In contrast, others may choose to hold ETH and other altcoins in their portfolios or use a certain percentage of them for short-term trading (e.g., day trading or swing trading). There is no one-size-fits-all approach to making money in the markets, and each investor should decide for themselves which strategy might be best suited to their profile and circumstances.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">How can I store my ETH?</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">There are many ways to store coins, each with its own advantages and disadvantages. As with anything that involves risk, it\'s best to diversify between the different options available. In general, you can choose between custodial and non-custodial solutions. With a custody solution, you entrust your coins to a third party (e.g. an exchange). In this case, you will need to log in to the custodian\'s platform to make transactions with your crypto assets. A no-custody solution is the opposite – you remain in control of your own money while using a cryptocurrency wallet. A wallet doesn\'t store your coins like a physical wallet – rather, it contains cryptographic keys that allow you to access your assets on the blockchain. Again, it\'s important to make a backup copy of your seed phrase if you\'re using a non-custodial wallet!</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">How to store your ETH in an Ethereum wallet</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">If you want to store your ETH in your own wallet, you have two options: hot wallets and cold wallets as. we already discussed in the first section of this chapters Hallets can be hot and cold. Wewill briefly relay the wallets summary overview below.&nbsp;</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Hot Wallets</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">A cryptocurrency wallet that is connected to the internet in some way is called a hot wallet. Typically, this is a mobile or desktop application that allows you to check your account balance and send or receive tokens. Because hot wallets are online, they tend to be more vulnerable to attacks, but also more convenient for everyday payments. Trust Wallet is an example of an easy-to-use mobile wallet with a variety of supported coins.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2449999999999999;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:8pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"width:100px;display:inline-block;position:relative;\"></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:-3pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><br></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Cold Wallets</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">A cold wallet is a crypto wallet that is not connected to the internet. Since there is no online attack vector, the likelihood of an attack is lower overall. At the same time, cold wallets tend to be less intuitive to use than hot wallets. Examples of cold wallets include hardware wallets or paper wallets. However, the use of paper wallets is often discouraged as many see them as outdated and risky.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">The Ethereum logo and symbol</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Vitalik Buterin has designed the first Ethereum emblem. It consisted of two twisted sum symbols Σ (sigma from the Greek alphabet). The final design of the logo (based on this emblem) consists of a rhomboid shape, an octahedron, surrounded by four triangles. Similar to other currencies, it could also be useful for Ether to have a standardized Unicode symbol so that applications and websites can easily display Ether values. The most commonly used symbol for Ether is Ξ, although it is not as widely used as, say, the $ for the USD.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">What is scalability?</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Put simply, scalability is a measure of a system\'s ability to expand. For example, in computing, a network or server can be scaled to handle higher demand through various methods.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:0pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">In the case of cryptocurrencies, scalability refers to how well a blockchain can grow to accommodate more users. More users mean more operations and transactions that \"compete\" for inclusion in the blockchain.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Why does Ethereum need to scale?</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Ethereum proponents believe that the next generation of the internet will be built on thise platform. The so-called Web 3.0 would produce a decentralized topology characterized by the absence of intermediaries, a focus on protecting privacy, and a shift toward real ownership of one\'s data. This foundation would be provided by distributed computing in the form of smart contracts and distributed storage/communication protocols. However, to achieve this, Ethereum will need to massively increase the number of transactions it can process without compromising the network\'s decentralization. Currently, Ethereum does not limit transaction volume by limiting the block size like Bitcoin does. Instead, there is a block gas limit - only a certain amount of gas can fit in a block. For example, if you have a block gas limit of 100,000 Gwei and want to include ten transactions with a gas limit of 10,000 Gwei each, that would work. The same applies to two transactions over 50,000 Gwei. All other transactions submitted alongside these transactions would have to wait for the next block. This is not ideal for a system that is used by everyone. If there are more pending transactions than available space in a block, a backlog will quickly occur. The price of gas will rise, and users will have to outbid others to get their transactions first. Depending on how busy the grid is, operation could become too expensive for certain applications. CryptoKitties\' popularity surge has been an excellent example of Ethereum\'s limitations on this front. In 2017, the Ethereum-based game prompted many users to make transactions to participate in breeding their own digital cats (represented as non-fungible tokens). The game became so popular that pending transactions skyrocketed, causing extreme congestion on the network for a while.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">The trilemma of blockchain scalability</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">It seems like simply raising the block gas limit will eliminate all scalability issues. The higher the cap, the more transactions could be processed in a given time frame, right? Unfortunately, this is not feasible without sacrificing important properties of Ethereum. Vitalik Buterin proposed the blockchain trilemma (see below) to explain the delicate balance that blockchains must find.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Blockchain Trilemma</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">The blockchain trilemma: scalability (1), security (2) and decentralization (3).</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:0pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">If one chooses to tweak two of the three features above, the third feature will be missing. Blockchains such as Ethereum and Bitcoin place an emphasis on security and decentralization. Their consensus algorithms ensure the security of their networks, which consist of thousands of nodes, but this results in poor scalability. With so many nodes receiving and validating transactions, the system is much slower than centralized alternatives. In another scenario, the block gas limit could be lifted, allowing the network to achieve security and scalability, but it wouldn\'t be as decentralized. &nbsp;This is because more transactions in a block result in larger blocks. Nevertheless, the nodes in the network have to download and share them regularly. And this process is very hardware-intensive. When the block gas limit is increased, it becomes more difficult for nodes to validate, store, and propagate blocks. As a result, one would expect nodes that can\'t keep up to drop out of the grid. If you continue like this, only a fraction of the powerful nodes can participate, which leads to greater centralization. The end result could be a blockchain that is secure and scalable, but it would not be decentralized. Finally, we can imagine a blockchain that focuses on decentralization and scalability. To be both fast and decentralized, it is necessary to compromise on the consensus algorithm used, resulting in lower security.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">How many transactions can Ethereum process?</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">In recent years, Ethereum has rarely exceeded ten transactions per second (TPS). For a platform that is supposed to become a \"world computer\", this number is surprisingly low. However, scaling solutions have long been part of Ethereum\'s roadmap. Plasma is an example of a scaling solution. It aims to increase the efficiency of Ethereum, but the technique can also be applied to other blockchain networks.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">What is Ethereum 2.0?</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Despite all its potential, early Ethereum currently hads significant limitations. We\'ve already discussed the issue of scalability. In short, if Ethereum was is to be the backbone of the new financial system, it must be able to process many more transactions per second. Given the distributed nature of the network, this wasis an immensely difficult problem to solve that Ethereum developers have been thinking about for years. In order to keep the grid sufficiently decentralized, limits must be set on the one hand. The higher the requirements for running a node, the fewer participants there will be and the more centralized the network will be. So, increasing the number of transactions that Ethereum can process could compromise the integrity of the system, as it would also increase the load on nodes. Another criticism of early Ethereum was that it was (and other Proof of Work cryptocurrency, i.e. it wasies) very is that it is incredibly energy resource-intensive to create it. In order to successfully attach a block to the blockchain, the nodes had to&nbsp; must mine. However, to create a block in this way, they needed&nbsp; to quickly perform calculations that consume large amounts of electricity. To address the above limitations, a series of upgrades known as Ethereum 2.0 (or ETH 2.0) have been proposed. Since its launch on 15</span></span></span><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-size:0.6em;vertical-align:super;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">th</span></span></span></span><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\"> Sept 2022Once fully launched, ETH 2.0 should significantly improved the network\'s performance.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">What is Ethereum sharding?</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">As mentioned earlier, each node stores a copy of the entire blockchain. Every time the blockchain is expanded, each of the nodes has to update it, which consumes their bandwidth and available storage. With a method called sharding, this may no longer be necessary. The name refers to the process of splitting the network into subsets of nodes – these are our shards. Each of these shards processes its own transactions and contracts, but can still communicate with the wider network of shards if needed. Since each shard validates independently, it is no longer necessary for it to store data from other shards. Sharding is one of the most complex approaches to scaling, and it requires a lot of work to design and implement. However, when successfully implemented, it is also one of the most effective, as it increases the throughput capacity of the network by orders of magnitude.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">What is Ethereum Plasma?—that</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Ethereum Plasma is what we call an off-chain scalability solution – that is, it aims to increase transaction throughput by pushing transactions away from the blockchain. In this regard, it shares some similarities with sidechains and payment channels. With Plasma, the sidechains are anchored in Ethereum\'s main blockchain, but they keep communication to a minimum. They operate more or less independently, although users still rely on the main chain to resolve disputes or \"complete\" their activities on the side chains. Reducing the amount of data that needs to be stored by nodes is critical to Ethereum\'s successful scaling. The Plasma approach allows developers to outline how their \"child\" chains work in a smart contract on the main chain. Then they are free to create applications with information or processes that would be too expensive to store/execute on the main chain.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">What are Ethereum rollups?</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Rollups are similar to Plasma in the sense that they aim to scale Ethereum by moving transactions away from the main blockchain. So how do they work? A single contract on the main chain keeps all funds on the side chain and keeps cryptographic proof of the current state of that chain. The operators of this secondary chain, who deposit a bond in the main chain contract, ensure that only valid state transitions are transferred to the main chain contract. The idea behind this is that the data does not need to be stored on the blockchain, as this state is maintained off-chain. However, the main differentiator of rollups from plasma lies in the way transactions are delivered to the main chain. With the help of a special transaction type, a large number of transactions can be \"bundled\" (grouped together) in a special block, called a </span></span></span><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">rollup block</span></span></span><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:0pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">There are two types of rollups: optimistic and ZK-rollups. Both guarantee the correctness of state transitions in different ways. In ZK-Rollups, transactions are submitted using a cryptographic verification method called zero-knowledge proof. More specifically, an approach called ZKzk-SNARK. We won\'t go into detail about how it works here, but here\'s how it can be used for rollups. It is a way for different parties to prove to each other that they have a certain piece of information without revealing what it is. In the case of ZK-Rollups, this information is state transitions that are transmitted to the main chain. A major advantage is that this process can be almost instantaneous, and there is virtually no chance of corrupted state transfers. Optimistic rollups sacrifice some scalability for more flexibility. By using a virtual machine, the Optimistic Virtual Machine (OVM), smart contracts can run on these secondary chains. On the other hand, there is no cryptographic proof that the state transition transmitted to the main chain is correct. To mitigate this issue, there is a slight delay that allows users to challenge and reject invalid blocks submitted to the main chain.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">What is Ethereum Proof of Stake (PoS)?</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Proof of Stake (PoS) is an alternative method to Proof of Work to validate blocks. In a Proof of Stake system, the blocks are not mined, but minted.&nbsp; (sometimes referred to as fake). Instead of miners competing with their hash power, a node (or validator) is randomly selected at regular intervals to validate a candidate block. If they do this correctly, they will receive all transaction fees for that block and, depending on the protocol, possibly a block reward. Since no mining is required, Proof of Stake is considered less harmful to the environment. Validators don\'t consume nearly as much energy as miners and can instead mint blocks on consumer-grade hardware. This shift happened on 15</span></span></span><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-size:0.6em;vertical-align:super;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">th</span></span></span></span><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\"> Sept 2022 as already referenced above. It is planned that Ethereum will move from PoW to PoS as part of Ethereum 2.0 with an upgrade known as Casper. Although an exact date has not yet been set, the first iteration will likely be launched in 2020.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">What is Ethereum Staking?</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">With proof-of-work protocols, the security of the network is ensured by the miners. Miners will not cheat because that would waste electricity and leave them missing out on potential profits. There is no such game theory in Proof of Stake, and various crypto-economic measures are taken to ensure the security of the network. What prevents fraud is not the risk of waste, but the risk of losing money. Validators must provide a stake (i.e., a token holding) to be eligible for validation. This is a certain amount of Ether that is lost if the node tries to cheat, or slowly depleted if the node is unresponsive or offline. However, if the validator runs additional nodes, they can receive more rewards.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">How much ETH do I need to stake for Ethereum?</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">The estimated minimum stake for Ethereum is 32 ETH per validator. This is set so high that the cost of attempting a 51% attack is extremely high.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">How much ETH can I earn staking Ethereum?</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">This question is not easy to answer. Of course, this depends on your stake, but also on the total amount of ETH staked on the network and the inflation rate. As a very rough estimate, current calculations assume an annual quantitative average return of around 6%. We need to kKeep in mind that this is only an estimate and may change in the future.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">How much ETH can I earn staking Ethereum?</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">This question is not easy to answer. Of course, this depends on your stake, but also on the total amount of ETH staked on the network and the inflation rate. As a very rough estimate, current calculations assume an annual return of around 6%. Keep in mind that this is only an estimate and may change in the future.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">How long is my ETH tied up when staking?</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">There will be a queue for withdrawing your ETH from your validator. If there is no queue, the minimum withdrawal time is 18 hours, but it is dynamically adjusted depending on how many validators withdraw at a given time.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Is there any risk involved in staking ETH?</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Since you are a validator on the network responsible for maintaining network security, there are some risks to be aware of. If your validator node goes offline for an extended period of time, you may lose a significant portion of your deposit. If at any point your deposit falls below 16 ETH, you will be removed from the validator set. It is also worth considering a more systemic risk factor. Proof of Stake has never been used to such an extent, so we can\'t be entirely sure that it won\'t somehow fail. Software will always have bugs and vulnerabilities, and that can have devastating effects — especially when billions of dollars are at stake.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">What is Decentralized Finance (DeFi)?</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Decentralized finance (or simply DeFi) is a movement that aims to decentralize financial applications. DeFi is based on public, open-source blockchains that can be accessed freely by anyone with an internet connection (permissionless). This is a crucial element in potentially engaging billions of people in this new, global financial system. In the growing DeFi ecosystem, users interact with smart contracts and each other through peer-to-peer networks (P2P) and decentralized applications (DApps). The big advantage of DeFi is that it allows all of this while users retain ownership of their funds at all times. Simply put, the DeFi (Decentralized Finance) movement aims to create a new financial system that is free from the limitations of the current one. Due to the relatively high level of decentralization and large developer base, DeFi is currently largely built on Ethereum.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">What can decentralized finance (DeFi) be used for?</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">You probably already know it, but one of the great advantages of Bitcoin is that it doesn\'t require a central authority to coordinate the operation of the network. But what if we use this as a core idea and build programmable applications on top of it? This is the potential of DeFi applications. No central coordinators or mediators and no individual sources of error. As mentioned earlier, one of the great advantages of DeFi is open access. There are billions of people in the world who do not have good access to any kind of financial services. Can you imagine how you would go about your everyday life without having certainty about your finances? There are billions of people who live this way, and ultimately, this is the target audience that DeFi wants to serve.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Will decentralized finance (DeFi) ever reach the mainstream?</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">That all sounds great. Why hasn\'t DeFi taken over the world yet? Well, right now, most DeFi applications are hard to use, clunky, break frequently, and are very experimental. As it turned out, even developing frameworks for this ecosystem is extremely difficult, especially in a distributed development environment. Solving all the challenges of building the DeFi ecosystem is a long road for software engineers, game theorists, mechanism designers, and many others. So, it remains to be seen whether DeFi applications will ever be able to catch on in the mainstream.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">What are the applications of decentralized finance (DeFi)?</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">One of the most popular use cases for decentralized finance (DeFi) is stablecoins. These are essentially tokens on a blockchain whose value is pegged to a real-world asset, such as a fiat currency. BUSD, for example, is pegged to the value of the USD. The advantage of these tokens is that they exist on a blockchain and are therefore very easy to store and transfer. Another popular type of application is lending. There are many peer-to-peer (P2P) services that allow you to lend your money to others and earn interest in return. One of the easiest ways to do this is through Binance Lending. All you have to do is transfer your money to your lending wallet, and the very next day you can start earning interest! Probably the most exciting part of DeFi, however, is the multitude of applications, which are difficult to categorize. This can include all kinds of decentralized peer-to-peer marketplaces where users can exchange unique crypto collectibles and other digital items. They can also enable the creation of synthetic assets, where anyone can create a market for pretty much anything that has value. Other possible applications include prediction markets, derivatives and much more.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Decentralized Exchanges (DEXs) on Ethereum</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">A decentralized exchange (DEX) is a place where trading can take place directly between users\' wallets. When you trade on Binance, a centralized exchange, you send your funds to Binance and trade through their internal systems. Decentralized exchanges are different. Through the magic of smart contracts, they allow you to trade directly from your crypto wallet, eliminating the possibility of exchange hacks and other risks.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Centralized vs. decentralized exchanges</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">A good example of a decentralized exchange is Binance DEX. Some other notable examples based on Ethereum include Uniswap, Kyber Network, and IDEX. Many even let you trade from a hardware wallet for maximum security. You will find that there is no third party involved in the transaction. Instead, one userAlice\'s token is exchanged directly for another userBob\'s with the help of a smart contract. In this way, neither party has to trust an intermediary, as the terms of their contract are automatically enforceable. As of February 2020, DEXs are the most used applications on the Ethereum blockchain. However, the trading volume is still low compared to centralized exchanges. However, if the DEX developers and designers improve the user experience, DEXs could compete with centralized exchanges in the future.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">What is an Ethereum node?</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">\"Ethereum node\" is a term that can be used to describe a program that interacts with the Ethereum network in some way. An Ethereum node can be anything from a simple mobile wallet application to a computer that stores a complete copy of the blockchain. &nbsp;All nodes act as a communication point in some way, but there are different types of nodes on the Ethereum network.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">How does an Ethereum node work?</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Unlike Bitcoin, Ethereum does not have a single program as a reference implementation. While the Bitcoin ecosystem has Bitcoin Core as its primary node software, Ethereum has a number of individual (but compatible) programs based on the Yellow Paper. Some of the most popular options include Geth and Parity.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Full Ethereum Nodes</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">To create an interface to the Ethereum network that allows you to validate blockchain data independently, you need to run a full node using software like the one mentioned above. The software downloads blocks from other nodes and checks if the transactions contained are correct. It also executes any smart contracts that have been called to ensure that you receive the same information as other participants. If everything works as intended, we can assume that each node has an identical copy of the blockchain on its machines. Full nodes are essential for Ethereum to function. Without multiple nodes spread across the globe, the network would lose its censorship-resistant and decentralized properties.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:-3pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><br></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Ethereum Light Node</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Running a full node allows you to contribute directly to the health and safety of the network. However, a full node often requires a separate machine for operation and occasional maintenance. Light nodes might be a better option for those users who aren\'t able to run a full node (or just don\'t want to). As the name suggests, light nodes are lightweight - they consume fewer resources and take up little space. Therefore, they can run on less powerful devices such as phones or laptops. However, these low overheads come at a price: light nodes are not completely self-sufficient. They do not fully synchronize the blockchain and therefore need full nodes to provide them with relevant information.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:0pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Light nodes are very popular among merchants, services, and users. They are often used for making and receiving payments in scenarios where full nodes are deemed unnecessary and too costly to operate.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Ethereum mining nodes</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">A mining node can be either a full client or a light client. The term \"mining node\" isn\'t really used the way it is in the Bitcoin ecosystem, but it\'s still worth identifying these participants. To mine Ethereum, users need additional hardware. A common practice is to build a mining rig. It involves connecting multiple GPUs (graphics processors) together to hack data at high speed. Prospectors have two options: they can mine alone or in a pool. Solo mining means that the miner works alone to create blocks. If he is successful, he does not share his profit with others. When they join a mining pool, they combine their hashing power with that of other users. This makes them more likely to find a block, but they also have to share their rewards with the pool members.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">How to run an Ethereum node</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">One of the great aspects of blockchains is open access. This means that anyone can run an Ethereum node and strengthen the network by validating transactions and blocks. Similar to Bitcoin, there are a number of companies that offer plug-and-play Ethereum nodes. This might be the best option if you only want to get one node up and running – but be prepared to pay extra for the convenience. As mentioned earlier, there are a number of different node software implementations for Ethereum, such as Geth or Parity. If you want to run your own node, you\'ll need to familiarize yourself with the setup process for your chosen implementation. Unless you want to run a dedicated node called an archive node, a consumer-grade laptop should be enough to run a full Ethereum node. At the same time, it is best not to use your everyday computer as it could slow it down considerably. Running your own node works best on devices that can always be online. If your node goes offline, it can take a considerable amount of time for it to synchronize with the network once it comes back online. The best solutions are therefore devices that are cheap to build and easy to maintain. For example, you can even run a lightweight node on a Raspberry Pi.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">How to mine on Ethereum</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Since the network will soon switch to Proof of Stake, mining on Ethereum is not the safest solution in the long term. After the switch, Ethereum miners will likely switch their mining equipment to another network or sell it altogether. If you still want to participate in Ethereum mining, you will need special hardware, such as GPUs or ASICs. If you\'re looking for reasonable returns, you\'ll most likely need a dedicated mining facility and access to cheap electricity. You\'ll also need to set up a wallet for Ethereum and configure the mining software to use it. This all requires a significant investment of time and money, so think carefully about whether you\'re up to the challenge.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">What is Ethereum ProgPoW?</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">ProgPoW stands for Programmatic Proof of Work. It is a proposed extension of the Ethereum mining algorithm Ethash that aims to make GPUs more competitive with ASICs. &nbsp;ASIC resistance has been a hotly debated topic in both the Bitcoin and Ethereum communities for years. In the case of Bitcoin, ASICs have become the dominant mining force on the network. On Ethereum, on the other hand, ASICs are present but much less present – a sizable portion of miners still use GPUs. However, this situation could soon change as more and more companies launch Ethereum ASIC miners. But why could ASICs be a problem? On the one hand, ASICs could drastically reduce the decentralization of the network. If GPU miners are not profitable and have to stop their mining operations, the hash rate could be concentrated in the hands of a handful of miners. On top of that, ASIC chip development is costly, and few companies have the skills and resources to do so. This threatens to monopolize on the manufacturing side by centralizing the Ethereum mining industry in the hands of a few companies. The integration of ProgPow has been a controversial topic since 2018. While some think it could be healthy for the Ethereum ecosystem, others are against it as it could lead to a hard fork. With the upcoming transition to Proof of Stake, it remains to be seen if ProgPow will ever be implemented on the network.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Who develops the Ethereum software?</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Like Bitcoin, Ethereum is open-source. Everyone is free to participate in the development of the protocol itself or to develop applications based on it. In fact, Ethereum currently has the largest developer community in the blockchain space. Resources such as the book Mastering Ethereum by Andreas Antonopoulos and Gavin Wood and the developer resources of Ethereum.org are excellent starting points for developers who want to get involved.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">What is Solidity?</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Smart contracts were first described in the 1990s, but their implementation on blockchains presented a whole new set of challenges. Solidity was proposed by Gavin Wood in 2014 and has since become the main programming language for developing smart contracts on Ethereum. Syntactically, it is similar to Java, JavaScript and C++. Essentially, Solidity allows developers to write code that can be broken down into instructions that the Ethereum Virtual Machine (EVM) can understand. If you want to better understand how it works, the Solidity GitHub is a good place to start. It should be noted that Solidity is not the only language available to Ethereum developers. Another popular option is Vyper, which is more similar to Python in its syntax. (Binance Academy, 2020)</span></span></span></p><div><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><br></span>\n</div></span>\n</div>',1,0,70,'2025-09-12 06:01:07','2025-10-08 09:48:32'),(182,29,'What are smart contracts','<div class=\"prose prose-lg max-w-none space-y-4\">\n\n<span id=\"docs-internal-guid-73bc8cb0-7fff-de89-701b-d835e4f72f4f\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:0pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">A smart contract is a digital agreement written in code, stored on a blockchain, and automatically executed without intermediaries. Smart contracts based on blockchain technology benefit from the security and transparency of blockchain, providing users with a way to enforce agreements and streamline various processes. Smart contracts are especially useful when two parties trade directly with each other without the need for a third party to be involved to ensure that the contract is honored by the buyer and seller. Let\'s say you\'re in the market for a digital artwork. As a rule, an art gallery acts as an intermediary in this transaction. Instead of relying on this middleman, a smart contract uses computer code to automatically execute and enforce the terms of the agreement. You can think of it as a virtual \"if-then\" instruction. For example, if the buyer transfers the agreed amount of money, the smart contract transfers ownership of the digital artwork from the seller to the buyer. Because smart contracts are based on blockchain technology, they are decentralized and cannot be altered or manipulated once they have been deployed. This mechanism makes them secure and trustworthy, as other users on the blockchain can verify the transactions and ensure that the contract is executed as intended.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">What are smart contracts used for?</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:5pt;padding:5pt 0pt 0pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">The use cases of smart contracts go beyond buying and selling things. They can increase blockchain\'s potential for widespread adoption by enabling the creation of new use cases that are not possible with traditional systems. Current use cases include:</span></span></span></p><ul style=\"padding-inline-start: 48px;\"><li dir=\"ltr\" style=\"list-style-type: disc; font-size: 10pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre; margin-left: 35.4pt;\" aria-level=\"1\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\" role=\"presentation\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Financial Transactions</span></span></span></p></li></ul><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-left: 35.4pt;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Smart contracts can be used for secure and automated financial transactions, such as transferring digital currencies, making payments, or executing more complex financial agreements such as futures in a direct peer-to-peer environment.</span></span></span></p><ul style=\"padding-inline-start: 48px;\"><li dir=\"ltr\" style=\"list-style-type: disc; font-size: 10pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre; margin-left: 35.4pt;\" aria-level=\"1\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\" role=\"presentation\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Decentralized applications</span></span></span></p></li></ul><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-left: 35.4pt;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Smart contracts form the backbone of many decentralized applications (DApps). These DApps include a wide range of features, including decentralized finance (DeFi) platforms that enable lending, borrowing, and trading, as well as gaming platforms that leverage non-fungible tokens (NFTs) for in-game items and virtual collectibles. NFT platforms with smart contracts enable the creation, ownership, and trading of unique digital assets in the form of tokens by leveraging the programmability and security of smart contracts on blockchain networks.</span></span></span></p><ul style=\"padding-inline-start: 48px;\"><li dir=\"ltr\" style=\"list-style-type: disc; font-size: 10pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre; margin-left: 35.4pt;\" aria-level=\"1\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\" role=\"presentation\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Insurance</span></span></span></p></li></ul><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-left: 35.4pt;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Smart contracts can simplify insurance processes by automating claims processing, verifying eligibility, and enabling payouts based on predefined terms. This can reduce paperwork, improve efficiency, and increase transparency in the insurance industry.</span></span></span></p><ul style=\"padding-inline-start: 48px;\"><li dir=\"ltr\" style=\"list-style-type: disc; font-size: 10pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre; margin-left: 35.4pt;\" aria-level=\"1\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\" role=\"presentation\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Supply Chain Management</span></span></span></p></li></ul><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-left: 35.4pt;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Smart contracts can track and verify the movement of goods throughout the supply chain, providing transparency and traceability and reducing fraud. You can automate supply chain management processes such as order fulfillment, payment processing, and quality control.</span></span></span></p><ul style=\"padding-inline-start: 48px;\"><li dir=\"ltr\" style=\"list-style-type: disc; font-size: 10pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre; margin-left: 35.4pt;\" aria-level=\"1\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\" role=\"presentation\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Intellectual property</span></span></span></p></li></ul><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-left: 35.4pt;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Smart contracts can be used to manage the ownership and distribution of intellectual property such as music, art, or written content. This is often done with the help of NFTs. With smart contracts, creators can set licensing terms, automate royalty payments, and ensure equitable distribution of digital goods.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2449999999999999;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:8pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"width:100px;display:inline-block;position:relative;\"></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-left: 35.4pt;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\"><br></p><ul style=\"padding-inline-start: 48px;\"><li dir=\"ltr\" style=\"list-style-type: disc; font-size: 10pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre; margin-left: 35.4pt;\" aria-level=\"1\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\" role=\"presentation\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Voting systems</span></span></span></p></li></ul><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-left: 35.4pt;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:0pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Smart contracts can facilitate secure and transparent voting systems by ensuring the integrity of votes, preventing fraud, and enabling immediate evaluation of results. This can help to increase trust and transparency in democratic processes.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-left: 35.4pt;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\"><br></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">How do smart contracts work?</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">The smooth operation of smart contracts relies on a combination of blockchain technology, code execution, and decentralized consensus. Here\'s a simplified explanation of the complex inner workings of smart contracts:</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">1. Build and deploy</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">A smart contract developer creates a smart contract using a programming language that is compatible with the blockchain platform (e.g., Solidity for Ethereum). The contract is then deployed on a blockchain network and becomes part of the blockchain\'s decentralized ecosystem. Ethereum smart contracts are very popular, but developers can also use other blockchains with smart contract capabilities, such as Solana and Cardano.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">2. Code and Conditions</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">The smart contract contains code that defines the terms, rules, and conditions of a particular agreement or transaction. These conditions can be as simple as a single payment or as complex as a multi-step process with many participants and data point requirements.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">3. Call for contracts</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Once deployed, anyone with access to the blockchain can access the smart contract by interacting with it. Calling a smart contract typically involves calling certain functions within the contract and providing the necessary inputs.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:0pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">This could mean that regular users are accessing a user interface or application that provides a user-friendly interface for interacting with smart contracts, such as a decentralized application (DApp) used with a crypto wallet. The user can then select the desired action they want to take (such as buying a crypto token), specify the amount, and send a request to complete the trade.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">4. Validation and execution</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">When a smart contract is invoked, the blockchain network validates the transaction and ensures that the conditions set out in the contract are met. This validation is performed by multiple nodes in the network through a consensus mechanism – in case of most coins eithersuch as Proof of Work (PoW) or Proof of Stake (PoS).</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">5. Invariant entry</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Once the terms are validated and consensus is reached, the smart contract code is automatically executed and the transaction is recorded as an immutable entry on the blockchain. Depending on the blockchain, this entry can contain all the relevant details of the transaction, making it transparent and verifiable.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2449999999999999;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:8pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"width:100px;display:inline-block;position:relative;\"></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:-3pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><br></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">6. Finality</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">The execution of a smart contract is final and cannot be reversed because it is stored on a decentralized and tamper-proof blockchain. This ensures the integrity and security of the transaction and prevents fraud or unauthorized changes.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">What are the disadvantages of smart contracts?</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">While smart contracts have numerous advantages, there are also some disadvantages. Here are some of the disadvantages associated with smart contracts:</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;text-indent: 35.4pt;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">1. Dependence on external data</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Smart contracts typically rely on external data sources called oracles to retrieve information from the outside world. While smart contracts themselves are tamper-proof, these oracles can have potential vulnerabilities or inaccuracies because they can be are susceptible manipulated to tampering and tampering.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;text-indent: 35.4pt;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">2. Code vulnerabilities</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Smart contract code, like any software, can contain vulnerabilities or bugs that can be exploited by malicious actors. Errors in code implementation or design can lead to security vulnerabilities that can result in financial loss or other negative consequences. Smart contracts must undergo rigorous testing before they are deployed to avoid exposing users to these dangers.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;text-indent: 35.4pt;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">3. Scalability</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Scalability and performance issues can arise as blockchain networks grow in size and usage. The limitations of blockchain networks can affect the speed and efficiency of smart contract execution, especially in high-demand scenarios.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;text-indent: 35.4pt;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">4. Immutability</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Once a smart contract is deployed and executed on a blockchain, it is immutable, meaning it cannot be modified or reversed. While this is usually seen as an advantage of smart contracts, it can also be problematic if the code contains errors or bugs, or if the contract needs to be updated due to changing circumstances.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">How are the problems of smart contracts solved?</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">The cryptocurrency community is aware of the drawbacks and challenges associated with smart contracts and is actively working to solve them. For example, many crypto platforms have bug bounty programs with high rewards to encourage white hat hackers, developers, and researchers to identify and report vulnerabilities in smart contract code and disclose them responsibly. There are also smart contract audit firms that offer services to conduct thorough security audits to identify and fix vulnerabilities in smart contracts. Their goal is to follow best practices to ensure secure coding standards, including testing, code reviews, and formal smart contract verification techniques. In addition, there is a common goal among developers to create tools, frameworks, and standards to improve smart contract development practices. One way to achieve this is through standardization efforts aimed at creating common interfaces, protocols, and formats for smart contracts. This can improve interoperability and ensure compatibility between different blockchain platforms, facilitating the integration of smart contracts with other protocols and enabling seamless interactions.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2449999999999999;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:8pt;\"><br></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Does Bitcoin have smart contracts?</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Bitcoin\'s scripting language allows for the creation of simple smart contracts, but its capabilities are more limited than those of programmable smart contract blockchains such as Ethereum. Bitcoin\'s programming language, Script, allows users to set rules and conditions for issuing their BTC, but it is not designed for more complex smart contract functions. In addition, Bitcoin can support smart contracts on protocols built on top of the Bitcoin network, such as the Lightning Network. You may be wondering how Bitcoin NFTs and Ordinals use smart contracts. They don\'t use smart contracts. Instead, the files are written directly to individual satoshis, which are then included in blocks on the Bitcoin blockchain.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Final Thoughts Conclusions</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Smart contracts are self-executing digital agreements that enforce the terms of a contract without intermediaries. They use blockchain technology to ensure transparency, security, and efficiency. Smart contracts have the potential to revolutionize traditional business processes by enabling secure and automated transactions, reducing costs, and providing new functionalities. Smart contracts also come with some challenges, but the crypto community and individual protocols are actively working to address these drawbacks through continuous testing, security measures, and ongoing development work. As smart contract technology evolves, we can expect to see more innovation and adoption that will shape the future of decentralized and trustless transactions. (Binance Academy, 2019)</span></span></span></p><div><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><br></span>\n</div></span>\n</div>',1,0,70,'2025-09-12 06:04:29','2025-10-08 09:49:00'),(183,29,'What are Decentralized Applications (DApps)?','<div class=\"prose prose-lg max-w-none space-y-4\">\n\n<span id=\"docs-internal-guid-9a762130-7fff-9470-ba64-df30f882b6d2\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:0pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Since the birth of Bitcoin (BTC) more than a decade ago, blockchains have evolved to unlock a variety of new features and use cases beyond currency. One of these new avenues is the development of decentralized applications (DApps) to use blockchain technology to improve many traditional sectors and services.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">What are decentralized applications (DApps)?</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Decentralized applications (DApps) are smart contract-driven digital applications or programs that run on blockchains rather than centralized servers. They look similar to regular mobile apps on your smartphone and offer a variety of services and features, from gaming to finance to social media and much more. As the name suggests, DApps run on decentralized peer-to-peer networks. According to an initial report, DApps have the following features: Open-source: The source code of DApps is publicly available, meaning anyone can review, use, copy, and modify them. There is no single company that controls the majority of coins or tokens. Users can also propose and vote on changes to the DApp. Decentralized and cryptographically secure: To ensure data security, all DApp information is cryptographically secured and stored on a public, decentralized blockchain maintained by multiple users (or nodes). A tokenized system: DApps can be accessed with a cryptographic token. They can adopt cryptocurrencies such as ETH or create their own token using a consensus algorithm such as Proof of Work (PoW) or Proof of Stake (PoS). The token can also be used to reward contributors such as miners and stakers. Under this broad definition, the Bitcoin blockchain can be defined as a DApp – and is arguably the first DApp ever. It is open-source, all data is on the decentralized blockchain, it is based on a crypto token and uses the PoW consensus algorithm. The same is true for other blockchains that have the above characteristics. However, nowadays, the term \"DApps\" generally refers to all applications that have smart contract capabilities and run on blockchain networks. The Bitcoin blockchain does not support smart contracts, which is why most people would not refer to it as DApps. As of June 2022, most DApps exist on the Ethereum network. It provides a robust infrastructure for DApp developers to extend the existing use cases. However, as DApps have matured, developers have started to develop them on other blockchains, including BNB Smart Chain (BSC), Solana (SOL), Polygon (MATIC), Avalanche (AVAX), EOS, etc.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">How do DApps work?</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">DApps are applications powered by smart contracts. Your backend code runs on distributed peer-to-peer networks. A smart contract consists of a set of predefined rules that are enforced by computer code. If certain conditions are met, all network nodes perform the tasks specified in the contract. Once a smart contract is installed on the blockchain, it is difficult to change the code or destroy it. Therefore, even if the team behind the DApp has disbanded, users will still be able to access the DApp.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><br></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><br></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Benefits of DApps</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">While the interfaces of DApps and traditional applications may look similar, DApps offer several advantages compared to their centralized counterparts. Web apps store data on centralized servers. A single compromised server can bring down the app\'s entire network, rendering it temporarily or permanently unusable. Centralized systems can also experience data loss or theft, putting businesses and individual users at risk. DApps, on the other hand, are based on distributed networks with no central authority. Because there is no single point of failure, DApps are less vulnerable to attacks, making it very difficult for malicious actors to hijack the network. The P2P network can also ensure that the DApps continue to operate with minimal downtime, even if individual computers or parts of the network fail. The decentralized nature of DApps also means that users have more control over the information they share. Since no companies control users\' personal information, they don\'t need to provide a real-world identity to interact with a DApp. Instead, they can use a crypto wallet to connect to DApps and fully control what information they share. Another advantage of DApps is that developers can easily integrate cryptocurrencies into their basic functions by leveraging smart contracts. For example, DApps on Ethereum can use ETH as a form of payment without integrating third-party providers.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Limitations of DApps</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">DApps have the potential to become an important part of a censorship-free future, but there are two sides to every coin. Decentralized applications are still in the early stages of development, and the industry has yet to overcome limitations such as scalability, code changes, and a small user base.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:0pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">DApps require significant computing power to operate, which could overload the networks they run on. For example, in order to achieve the security, integrity, transparency, and reliability that Ethereum is striving for, each validator must execute and store every transaction executed on the network. This could affect the system\'s transaction rate per second (TPS) and lead to network congestion and excessive gas fees. Changes to a DApp are also challenging. To improve the user experience and security, a DApp will likely need to be constantly modified to fix bugs, update the user interface, and add new features. However, once a DApp is deployed on the blockchain, it is difficult to change its backend code. A majority consensus of the nodes of the network would be required to approve changes or improvements, which could take a long time. The abundance of DApps on the market makes it hard for a DApp to stand out and attract a lot of users. For a DApp to work effectively, it needs to have a network effect – the more users a DApp has, the more effective it is in providing services. A larger number of users can also make the DApp more secure and protect it from hackers who interfere with the open-source code.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 15pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Popular DApp use cases</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">DApps offer a new approach for businesses across many industries to reach more users. Popular use cases for DApps include GameFi (game finance), decentralized finance (DeFi), entertainment, and governance.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">GameFi (Game Finance)</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">GameFi DApps are becoming increasingly popular. An example of this is the rise of Axie Infinity, a play-to-earn game on the Ethereum blockchain. According to DappRadar, blockchain gaming activity increased by 2,000% in the first quarter of 2022 compared to 2021. There were also 1.22 million unique active wallets (UAW) in March 2022, with over 50% of activity coming from gaming DApps. Unlike traditional video games, most DApps give players full control over their in-game assets. They also offer players the opportunity to monetize these items outside of the game. Axie Infinity, for example, offers game characters, virtual land, and in-game items in the form of NFTs. Players can store these in crypto wallets, transfer them to other Ethereum addresses, or trade with other players on NFT marketplaces. Within the ecosystem, players can compete against each other to collect ERC-20 tokens that can be traded on exchanges. The longer they play, the more rewards they can earn in the game.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">DeFi and DEXs</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Traditional finance relies on financial institutions acting as middlemen. Through DApps, anyone can use financial services without a central authority and maintain full control over their assets. DeFi can also benefit low-income individuals by providing them with access to a wide range of financial services at a significantly lower cost. Borrowing and lending are the most popular types of financial services that decentralized applications offer. DeFi DApps offer instant transaction processing, minimal to no credit checks, and the ability to use digital assets as collateral. On DApp loan marketplaces, users have more flexibility. For example, lenders have more control over their loans by choosing which token to lend and on which platform. Also, users can potentially earn 100% of the interest generated from the loan because they don\'t have to pay intermediary fees. Decentralized exchanges (DEXs) are another important example of financial DApps. Such platforms facilitate peer-to-peer trading by eliminating the need for middlemen such as centralized crypto exchanges. Users do not have to give up the custody of their funds. Instead of transferring their assets to an exchange, they trade directly with another user using smart contracts. Orders are executed on-chain and directly between users\' wallets. Because DEXs require less maintenance, trading fees tend to be lower compared to centralized exchanges. Some popular DEXs include Uniswap, SushiSwap, and PancakeSwap.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Entertainment</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Entertainment is an essential part of our lives. With DApps, everyday activities that people enjoy are transformed into digital experiences that can also create economic incentives. For example, Audius, a blockchain-based decentralized music streaming platform, eliminates the middlemen that exist in the traditional music industry to connect artists and fans directly. It allows music curators to better monetize their content and create immutable records of their work on the blockchain. DApps also address the problems faced by users of social media platforms. Centralized social media giants like Twitter and Facebook are often criticized for censoring posts and mishandling user data. With decentralized social DApps like Steemit, the community can freely interact and express their opinions with fewer restrictions and censorship while also having greater control over their personal data.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Governance</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">DApps can empower users to play a greater role in the governance of online organizations by introducing a more community-focused decision-making mechanism. With the help of smart contracts, users who own governance tokens of a particular blockchain project can create proposals for the community to vote on and vote anonymously on others\' proposals. One of the decentralized governance models is Decentralized Autonomous Organizations (DAOs). DAOs can be thought of as fully autonomous DApps that use smart contracts to make decisions without a central authority. They have no hierarchy. Instead, it is economic mechanisms that align the interests of the organization with those of individual DAO members.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">How do you connect to DApps?</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">To interact with a DApp, you first need a compatible browser extension such as MetaMask, Trust Wallet, or Binance Chain Wallet. It only takes a few minutes to set up. Some even offer mobile versions for easy access.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Final Thoughts</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">DApps extend the functionality of the web by augmenting traditional applications with blockchain technology. Decentralized applications could bring even more innovative use cases to the market in the future. As reported by DappRadar, DApps had nearly 2.4 million daily active users in Q1 2022, and user interest is expected to continue to grow. However, the developers of DApps and the blockchain networks on which they are built have yet to overcome the current limitations before they reach mass adoption. (Binance Academy, 2022)</span></span></span></p><div><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><br></span>\n</div></span>\n</div>',1,0,70,'2025-09-12 06:07:09','2025-10-08 09:49:28'),(184,29,'What is a crypto wallet?','<div class=\"prose prose-lg max-w-none space-y-4\">\n\n<span id=\"docs-internal-guid-f1982caf-7fff-7d77-c71f-a62af1f8a66d\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:0pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">In a nutshell, a crypto wallet is a tool that you can use to interact with a blockchain network. There are different types of crypto wallets, which can be divided into three groups: software, hardware, and paper wallets. Depending on how they work, they are also known as hot or cold wallets.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:0pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Most crypto wallet providers are software-based, which makes them more convenient to use than hardware wallets. However, hardware wallets are usually the safest alternative. Paper wallets, on the other hand, consist of a \"wallet\" printed on a piece of paper, but their use is now considered outdated and unreliable.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">How do cryptocurrency wallets work?</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Contrary to popular belief, crypto wallets don\'t actually store digital assets. Instead, they provide the tools needed to interact with a blockchain. In other words, these wallets can generate the necessary information to send and receive cryptocurrencies via blockchain transactions. This information consists of, among other things, one or more pairs of public and private keys. The wallet also contains an address. This is an alphanumeric identifier that is generated based on the public and private keys. Such an address is essentially a specific \"place\" on the blockchain where coins can be sent. This means that you can share your address with others to receive funds, but you should never give your private key to anyone. The private key allows access to your cryptocurrencies regardless of which wallet you use. So even if your computer or smartphone is compromised, you can still access your funds with another device – as long as you have the appropriate private key (or seed phrase). Note that the coins never actually leave the blockchain; they are merely transferred from one address to another.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Do I need a crypto wallet to trade cryptocurrencies?</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:5pt;padding:5pt 0pt 0pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">The simple answer is yes. Whether you\'re a frequent trader or a Bitcoin HODLer (popular name of Bitcoin long-term asset holders), you\'ll need a wallet address to store and trade cryptocurrencies. You can use the hot wallet provided by your crypto exchange, a mobile wallet that you install on your phone, a browser extension, a desktop wallet, or a hardware wallet. There are several options. Below are some examples of the different wallet types:</span></span></span></p><ol style=\"padding-inline-start: 48px;\"><li dir=\"ltr\" style=\"list-style-type: decimal; font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre; margin-left: 59.35pt;\" aria-level=\"1\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:0pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\" role=\"presentation\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Hot wallet: Binance exchange.</span></span></span></p></li><li dir=\"ltr\" style=\"list-style-type: decimal; font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre; margin-left: 59.35pt;\" aria-level=\"1\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:0pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\" role=\"presentation\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Mobile crypto wallets: Trust Wallet, MetaMask.</span></span></span></p></li><li dir=\"ltr\" style=\"list-style-type: decimal; font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre; margin-left: 59.35pt;\" aria-level=\"1\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:0pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\" role=\"presentation\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Browser extension crypto wallets: MetaMask, MathWallet, and Binance Chain Wallet.</span></span></span></p></li><li dir=\"ltr\" style=\"list-style-type: decimal; font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre; margin-left: 59.35pt;\" aria-level=\"1\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\" role=\"presentation\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Desktop crypto wallets: Electrum, Exodus.</span></span></span></p></li></ol><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Hot vs. Cold Wallets</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">As mentioned earlier, cryptocurrency wallets can also be defined as \"hot\" or \"cold\" depending on how they operate. A hot wallet is any wallet that is somehow connected to the internet. For example, when you open an account with Binance and send funds to your wallets, you\'re depositing into Binance\'s hot wallet. These wallets are quite easy to set up, and funds are available quickly, making them very convenient for traders and other frequent users. Cold wallets, on the other hand, have no connection to the internet. Instead, they use a physical medium to store the keys offline, making them resistant to online hacking attempts. Therefore, cold wallets are a much safer alternative to \"storing\" your coins. This method is also known as cold storage and is particularly suitable for long-term investors or \"HODLers.\"</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:0pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">To protect users\' funds, Binance only holds a small portion of the coins in its hot wallets. The rest is stored in cold storage that is not connected to the internet. Notably, the DApps ecosystem powered by BNB Chain offers an alternative for users who don\'t want to store their funds in a centralized exchange. It\'s a decentralized trading platform that allows you to have full control over your private keys while trading directly from your cold storage devices (hardware wallets).</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2449999999999999;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:8pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"width:100px;display:inline-block;position:relative;\"></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:-3pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><br></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Software wallets</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">There are many different types of software wallets, each with its own unique characteristics. Most of them are somehow connected to the internet (hot wallets). Below are descriptions of some of the most common and important types: web, desktop, and mobile wallets.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Web wallets</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Web wallets allow you to access blockchains through a browser interface without having to download or install anything. This includes both exchange wallets and other browser-based wallet providers. In most cases, you can create a new wallet and set a personal password to access it. However, some vendors hold and manage the private keys on your behalf. Although this may be more convenient for inexperienced users, it is a dangerous practice. If you don\'t have your private keys, entrust your money to someone else. To solve this problem, many web wallets offer the ability to manage the keys, either completely or through shared control (via multiple signatures). So, it\'s important to check the technical approach of each wallet before choosing the one that works best for you. If you use cryptocurrency exchanges, you should consider the protection tools available. Binance Exchange offers various security features, such as device management, multi-factor authentication, anti-phishing code, and withdrawal address management.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Desktop Wallets</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">As the name suggests, a desktop wallet is software that you download and run locally on your computer. Unlike some web-based versions, desktop wallets give you full control over your keys and funds. When you create a new desktop wallet, a file called \"wallet.dat\" is stored locally on your computer. This file contains the private key information used to access your cryptocurrency addresses, which is why you should encrypt it with a personal password. When you encrypt your desktop wallet, you will need to enter your password every time you launch the software so that it can wallet.dat read the file. If you lose this file or forget your password, you will most likely lose access to your funds. Therefore, it is important that you take a backup of your wallet.dat file and keep it in a safe place. Alternatively, you can export the corresponding private key or seed phrase. This will allow you to access your funds on other devices if your computer stops working or becomes inaccessible for any reason. In general, desktop wallets are considered more secure than most web versions, but it\'s important to make sure your computer is free of viruses and malware before setting up and using a cryptocurrency wallet.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Mobile Wallets</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Mobile wallets work similarly to their desktop counterparts, but are specifically designed as smartphone applications. They are very convenient because they allow you to send and receive cryptocurrencies via QR codes. As such, mobile wallets are particularly well-suited for conducting daily transactions and payments, making them a viable option for spending Bitcoin, BNB, and other cryptocurrencies in the real world. Trust Wallet is a prominent example of a mobile crypto wallet. However, just like computers, mobile devices are also vulnerable to malicious apps and malware infections. We therefore recommend that you encrypt your mobile wallets with a password and secure your private keys (or seed phrase) in case your smartphone is lost or broken.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Hardware wallets</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Hardware wallets are physical, electronic devices that use a random number generator (RNG) to generate public and private keys. The keys are then stored on the device itself, which is not connected to the internet. Hardware wallets are therefore a type of cold wallet and are considered one of the most secure alternatives. While these wallets offer a higher level of security against online attacks, they can also pose risks if the firmware implementation is not done properly. Also, hardware wallets tend to be less user-friendly, and accessing the funds is more difficult compared to hot wallets. You should consider using a hardware wallet if you want to hold your cryptocurrencies for a longer period of time or if you own large amounts of cryptocurrencies. Currently, most hardware wallets allow you to set up a PIN code to protect your device, as well as a recovery phrase that can be used in the event of your wallet being lost.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Paper Wallets</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">A paper wallet is a piece of paper on which a crypto address and its private key are physically printed in the form of QR codes. These codes can then be scanned to make cryptocurrency transactions. Some paper wallet websites allow you to download their code to generate new addresses and keys while you\'re offline. As such, these wallets are highly resistant to online hacking attacks and can be considered an alternative to cold storage. However, due to the numerous vulnerabilities, the use of paper wallets is now considered dangerous and should be discouraged. If you still want to use them, it\'s important to be aware of the risks. A big flaw of paper wallets is that they are not suitable for sending partial amounts of money, but only their entire balance at once. For example, imagine that you created a paper wallet and sent several transactions to fund it, a total of 10 BTC. If you decide to spend 2 BTC, you should first send all 10 coins to another type of wallet (e.g. a desktop wallet) and only then spend part of the funds (2 BTC). You can later return the 8 BTC to a new paper wallet, although a hardware or software wallet would be a better choice. If you import your paper wallet\'s private key into a desktop wallet and spend only a portion of the funds, the remaining coins will be sent to a \"currency exchange\" address that is automatically generated by the Bitcoin protocol. If you don\'t manually set the exchange address to an address you can control, you\'re likely to lose your money.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:0pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Most of today\'s software wallets will do the change for you and send the remaining coins to an address that is part of your wallet. However, it is important to note that your paper wallet is empty after the first transaction is sent—regardless of the amount. So don\'t expect to be able to reuse them later.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">The importance of backups</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Losing access to your cryptocurrency wallets can be very costly. Therefore, it is important to back them up regularly. In many cases, this is achieved by backing up wallet.dat files or seed phrases. A seed phrase basically works like a root key that generates all the keys and addresses in a crypto wallet and grants access to them. If you\'ve opted for password encryption, remember to secure your password as well.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Which crypto wallet should I use?</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">There is no definitive answer to the question of which wallet you should use. If you trade frequently, a web wallet allows you to quickly access your funds and trade conveniently. Provided you\'ve taken extra steps to secure your account using two-factor authentication (2FA) methods, your cryptocurrency is generally safe. However, if you\'re hoarding a large amount of crypto that you don\'t want to sell in the meantime, cold wallets are a better alternative because they\'re not connected to the internet, so they\'re more secure and resistant to online phishing attacks or scams.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Final Thoughts and Conclusions</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Crypto wallets are an essential part of the use of Bitcoin and other cryptocurrencies. They are one of the fundamental elements of infrastructure that allow funds to be sent and received over blockchain networks. Each wallet type has its pros and cons, so it\'s important to understand how they work before moving your money. (Binance Academy, 2019)</span></span></span></p><div><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><br></span>\n</div></span>\n</div>',1,0,70,'2025-09-12 06:08:09','2025-10-08 09:49:55'),(185,29,'What is a stablecoin?','<div class=\"prose prose-lg max-w-none space-y-4\">\n\n\n<span id=\"docs-internal-guid-15ce1b7c-7fff-7051-83ce-6702f2f122da\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:0pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Cryptocurrencies aren\'t just about volatility. In fact, stablecoins are specifically designed to hold a fixed price. In an industry where coins and tokens can crash overnight, there is a huge demand for currencies that combine the benefits of blockchain with the ability to track a more stable asset. If you haven\'t traded or invested in stablecoins before, it\'s worth learning more about them and their pros and cons.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">What is a stablecoin in cryptocurrencies?</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Stablecoins are digital assets that track the value of fiat currencies or other assets. For example, you can buy tokens pegged to the dollar, the euro, the yen, and even gold and oil. A stablecoin allows the holder to lock in profits and losses and transfer value at a stable price across peer-to-peer blockchain networks. Bitcoin (BTC), Ether (ETH), and other altcoins have been highly volatile in the past. While this offers many opportunities for speculation, it also has drawbacks. The volatility makes it difficult to use cryptocurrencies for everyday payments. For example, traders may take $5 in BTC for a coffee one day but find that their BTC is worth 50% less the next day. This makes it difficult to plan and operate a business that accepts crypto payments. Previously, there was no way for crypto investors and traders to lock in profits or avoid volatility without converting cryptocurrencies back to fiat. The creation of stablecoins offered a simple solution to these problems. Today, you can easily get in and out of cryptocurrency volatility with stablecoins like TrueUSD (TUSD).</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">How do stablecoins work?</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Creating a coin that tracks the price or value of another asset requires a pegging mechanism. There are several ways to do this, and most rely on another asset that serves as collateral. Some methods have proven to be more successful than others, but there is no guaranteed bond yet.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Fiat-backed stablecoins</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">A fiat-backed stablecoin holds a fiat currency, such as USD or GBP, as a reserve. For example, each TUSD is backed by $1 held as collateral. Users can then exchange their fiat currency for a stablecoin and vice versa at the fixed rate.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Crypto-backed stablecoins</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Crypto-backed stablecoins work in a similar way to fiat-backed stablecoins. However, instead of using dollars or another currency as a reserve, cryptocurrencies serve as collateral. Since the crypto market is highly volatile, crypto stablecoins usually overcollateralize the reserves as a measure against price fluctuations. Crypto-backed stablecoins use smart contracts. This makes the process more reliable, as users can independently verify the contracts. However, some crypto-backed stablecoins are operated by Decentralized Autonomous Organizations (DAOs), where the community can vote on changes in the project. In this case, you can get involved or trust the DAO to make the best decisions. Let\'s look at an example. To mint 100 DAI pegged to the USD, you need to deposit $150 of cryptocurrencies as 1.5x collateral. Once you have your DAI, you can use it however you want. You can transfer it, invest it, or just keep it as is. If you want your collateral back, you will have to pay back the 100 DAI. However, if your collateral falls below a certain collateral ratio or the value of the loan, it will be liquidated. If the stablecoin is below $1, it incentivizes holders to return their stablecoin in exchange for the pledge. This decreases the supply of stablecoins, so the price rises back to $1. If it is above $1, users will have an incentive to create the token, which will increase the supply and lower the price. DAI is just one example, but all crypto-backed stablecoins rely on a mix of game theory and on-chain algorithms to incentivize price stability.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Algorithmic stablecoins</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Algorithmic stablecoins take a different approach by eliminating the need for reserves. Instead, algorithms and smart contracts manage the supply of issued tokens. This model is much rarer than crypto or fiat-backed stablecoins and more difficult to operate successfully. Essentially, an algorithmic stablecoin system will reduce the token supply if the price falls below the fiat currency it tracks. This could be done via locked staking, burning, or buybacks. When the price exceeds the value of fiat currency, new tokens enter circulation to reduce the value of the stablecoin.</span></span></span></p><span id=\"docs-internal-guid-7bebafbf-7fff-9c1c-e601-86eaa0bcab00\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\">What are the benefits of stablecoins?</span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:5pt;padding:5pt 0pt 0pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Stablecoins are versatile and powerful tools for investors, traders, and cryptocurrency users. Among its key strengths are the following:</span></span></p><ol style=\"padding-inline-start: 48px;\"><li dir=\"ltr\" style=\"list-style-type: decimal; font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre; margin-left: 59.35pt;\" aria-level=\"1\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:0pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\" role=\"presentation\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Stablecoins can be used for everyday payments. Companies and private individuals value stability. Due to their volatility, cryptocurrencies for daily payments have not yet caught on. Major stablecoins have a track record of maintaining their value, making them suitable for everyday use.</span></span></p></li><li dir=\"ltr\" style=\"list-style-type: decimal; font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre; margin-left: 59.35pt;\" aria-level=\"1\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:0pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\" role=\"presentation\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Stablecoins have the advantage of being based on the blockchain. You can send a stablecoin to anyone worldwide who has a compatible crypto wallet (which can be created for free in seconds). Double spending and incorrect transactions are also almost impossible. These characteristics make stablecoins incredibly versatile.</span></span></p></li><li dir=\"ltr\" style=\"list-style-type: decimal; font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre; margin-left: 59.35pt;\" aria-level=\"1\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\" role=\"presentation\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Stablecoins can be used by traders and investors to hedge their portfolios. Allocating a certain percentage of a portfolio of stablecoins is an effective way to reduce overall risk. Your portfolio will be more resilient to market price fluctuations overall, and you\'ll also have funds on hand in case a good opportunity arises. You can also sell cryptocurrencies for stablecoins during a market downturn and buy them back at a lower price (i.e., shorting). Stablecoins allow you to conveniently enter and exit positions without having to take money off-chain.\n</span></span></p></li></ol><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2449999999999999;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:8pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"width:100px;display:inline-block;position:relative;\"></span></span></p></span><span id=\"docs-internal-guid-eb0d9c63-7fff-2eb5-469f-6e1e66905871\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-size: 20px;\">What are the disadvantages of stablecoins?</span></span></p></span><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:5pt;padding:5pt 0pt 0pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Despite their potential to drive widespread adoption of cryptocurrencies, stablecoins still have their limitations:</span></span></span></p><ol style=\"padding-inline-start: 48px;\"><li dir=\"ltr\" style=\"list-style-type: decimal; font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre; margin-left: 59.35pt;\" aria-level=\"1\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:0pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\" role=\"presentation\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Stablecoins are not a guarantee that they will retain their value. While some large projects have a good track record, there have also been many projects that have failed. If a stablecoin is constantly struggling to maintain its peg, it can lose its value dramatically.</span></span></span></p></li><li dir=\"ltr\" style=\"list-style-type: decimal; font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre; margin-left: 59.35pt;\" aria-level=\"1\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:0pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\" role=\"presentation\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Lack of transparency. Not all stablecoins publish a full audit and many only issue regular certificates. Private auditors carry out these on behalf of stablecoin issuers.</span></span></span></p></li><li dir=\"ltr\" style=\"list-style-type: decimal; font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre; margin-left: 59.35pt;\" aria-level=\"1\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:0pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\" role=\"presentation\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Fiat-backed stablecoins tend to be more centralized than other cryptocurrencies. A central body holds the collateral and may also be subject to external financial supervision. This gives it significant control over the coin. Also, you need to trust that the issuer has the reserves it claims.</span></span></span></p></li><li dir=\"ltr\" style=\"list-style-type: decimal; font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre; margin-left: 59.35pt;\" aria-level=\"1\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\" role=\"presentation\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Collateralized and uncollateralized crypto coins rely heavily on their community to function. Open governance mechanisms are common in crypto projects, which means that users have a say in the development and operation of the respective project. So you need to get involved or trust the developers and the community to run the project responsibly.</span></span></span></p></li></ol><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Examples of stablecoins:</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Crypto-backed stablecoin: MakerDAO (DAI)</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">DAI is a crypto-backed stablecoin that tracks USD on Ethereum. The coin is governed by the MakerDAO community, which holds the governance token MKR. MKR allows you to create and vote on proposals to modify the project. DAI is overcollateralized to deal with the volatility of cryptocurrencies, and users take collateralized debt positions (CDPs) that manage their collateral. The entire process is handled via smart contracts.\n\n</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Fiat-backed stablecoin: TrueUSD (TUSD)</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">TUSD is an independently verifiable stablecoin pegged to the dollar. It is the first stablecoin to programmatically control coin production with instant on-chain verification of off-chain USD reserves. TUSD reserves are monitored using Chainlink Proof of Reserve, allowing holders to independently verify that their TUSD is backed by USD reserves.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Are stablecoins regulated?</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Stablecoins have attracted the interest of regulators worldwide due to their unique mix of fiat and cryptocurrencies. Since they are designed to maintain a stable price, they are not only useful for speculation purposes. They can also enable fast international transactions at a low cost. Some countries are even experimenting with creating their own stablecoins. Since a stablecoin is a type of cryptocurrency, it is likely to be subject to the same regulations as cryptocurrencies in your local jurisdiction. The issuance of stablecoins with fiat reserves may also need to be approved by the authorities.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2449999999999999;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:8pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"width:100px;display:inline-block;position:relative;\"></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:-3pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><br></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Final Thoughts and Conclusions</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><br></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">It\'s hard to find an investor or trader these days who hasn\'t held a stablecoin before. Stablecoins are often held on crypto exchanges so that traders can quickly capitalize on new market opportunities. They are also very useful for getting in and out of positions without having to cash out in fiat money. In addition to trading and investing, stablecoins can also be used for payments and international transfers. Even though they are an integral part of cryptocurrency and have enabled the creation of a new financial system, you should not underestimate the risks. We\'ve seen stablecoin projects where pegs failed, reserves were missing, and litigation was running. While stablecoins are an incredibly versatile instrument, keep in mind that they are still cryptocurrencies and carry similar risks. You can mitigate the risks by diversifying your portfolio, but make sure you do your own research before investing or trading and don\'t invest more than you can afford to lose. (Binance Academy, 2023).</span></span></span></p></span>\n\n\n</div>',1,0,70,'2025-09-12 06:09:15','2025-10-09 07:11:44'),(186,29,'What is an NFT?','<div class=\"prose prose-lg max-w-none space-y-4\">\n\n<span id=\"docs-internal-guid-dbc79260-7fff-5773-feb7-306c7a23e7ba\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:0pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">What does \"non-fungible\" mean?</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:0pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">The term \"non-fungible\" refers to the irreplaceability of an object. A non-perishable item cannot be directly exchanged for another item of the same value, as both items have different properties. This means that non-fungible items cannot be traded on a standardized scale, as their value comes from their uniqueness and the subjective value that buyers attach to them. Fungible assets such as money are easily interchangeable due to their uniformity. In contrast, non-fungible assets are distinctive and irreplaceable, which can be interesting for collectors looking to acquire something truly unique.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">What is a non-fungible token (NFT)?</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">An NFT is a cryptographic token hosted on a blockchain that represents a digital asset. She defines the non-fungibility of NFTs as digital assets that represent ownership of one-off items such as artwork, video games, trading cards, virtual real estate, and other digital goods. In recent years, NFTs have gained popularity, as they offer creators the opportunity to monetize their digital creations and allow collectors to own unique digital assets popularity.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">How do NFTs work?</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">NFTs are based on blockchain technology, which provides a decentralized ledger where transactions and ownership details are recorded. Thanks to its transparent and immutable nature, the ownership history of an NFT can be clearly traced. This verifies the authenticity and legitimacy of the NFT as it changes hands over time. Another underlying technology for NFTs is smart contracts, which are essentially self-executing programs. Smart contracts allow NFTs to be created, managed, and transferred without intermediaries by automating and enforcing the corresponding conditions. An important aspect of NFTs is the implementation of token standards. They ensure interoperability and consistency across different platforms by defining rules and functions for creating, managing, and transferring NFTs. For example, the most widely used token standards for NFTs are ERC-721 on Ethereum and BEP-721 on the BNB Chain. The process of creating NFTs is usually referred to as minting. With the help of smart contracts, digital files are converted into digital assets on a blockchain during minting. When you buy an NFT, you essentially acquire ownership of the unique identifier (or token ID) associated with that specific digital asset. As a result, the owner of the code owns the exclusive rights to use, display, and interact with that asset.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">What can NFTs be used for?</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">NFTs have begun to redefine the concept of ownership and value in the digital world, creating new opportunities for creators and consumers. Here are some common NFT applications:</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">NFT art</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">NFT art offers artists a new way to monetize their works. By tokenizing their art, artists can sell unique digital copies, preserving the originality and rarity of each work. NFT art allows collectors to display their works in virtual galleries, trade them, or even lend them to others.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">NFT Games</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">NFT games contain NFTs as digital collectibles, such as in-game items and characters. NFTs can also represent virtual real estate that players can trade. This has the potential to create a gaming ecosystem where players can monetize their achievements and in-game assets and create a secondary market.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">NFT Staking</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">NFT staking allows users to earn rewards by using their NFTs as collateral. This is already possible on certain decentralized finance (DeFi) platforms, allowing NFT holders to earn interest while retaining ownership of their NFTs.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">NFT Tickets</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">NFTs can be useful for ticket management. For example, organizers can issue NFTs as tickets, providing immutable proof of ownership and attendance. In addition, NFT tickets can be transferred and resold without the involvement of third parties. NFT tickets can also come with exclusive benefits, such as access to VIP areas, exclusive merchandise, or special digital content.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 15pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Popular NFT examples:</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">CryptoPunks</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">CryptoPunks is one of the earliest and most iconic NFT projects. It was launched in 2017 and consists of 10,000 unique, algorithmically generated 8-bit pixel art figures. Each CryptoPunk figure has different characteristics, which makes it attractive to collectors. You may even have seen celebrities using these characters as avatars on their social media. The success of the project has ushered in a new era of digital art and collectibles.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Bored Ape Yacht Club</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">The Bored Ape Yacht Club (BAYC) is a collection of 10,000 unique, hand-drawn cartoon monkey characters, each with different characteristics. These digital artworks serve as collectibles and give their owners access to exclusive events and virtual spaces. In this way, these NFTs blur the boundaries between digital art and experiential offerings.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:-3pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><br></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Decentraland</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Decentraland is a virtual reality (VR) platform built on the Ethereum blockchain. It offers a decentralized marketplace for NFTs where users can trade virtual plots of land and various game items. Decentraland is a pioneer in virtual real estate and the metaverse.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 15pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Common misconceptions about NFTs</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">NFTs are completely safe</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">As we have learned, NFTs inherit the security features of their underlying blockchains. However, there is still a risk of scams and rip-offs. This can include phishing attempts or hackers exploiting vulnerabilities in smart contracts. There is also a possibility that NFTs could be counterfeited and copyrighted material could be reproduced without authorization. Another aspect to consider is the long-term value of NFTs. While some NFTs have reached astronomical prices, the market can be volatile and speculative. As with any investment, long-term stability is not guaranteed. At the same time, the security of an NFT can be affected by the blockchain on which it is minted. Because some blockchains have a more developed ecosystem and robust security than others, the security of NFTs tends to vary.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">NFTs and cryptocurrencies are the same thing</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Although both NFTs and cryptocurrencies are digital assets that use blockchain technology, they have different purposes and characteristics. Cryptocurrencies are often intended to facilitate transactions. They are also fungible, meaning each unit is exchangeable for another unit of the same currency. For example, you can swap one Bitcoin for another without any difference in value. NFTs, on the other hand, are unique digital assets. They are non-fungible, meaning each NFT has unique properties and cannot be directly exchanged for another NFT on a one-to-one basis. In short, NFTs derive their value from their uniqueness and scarcity.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Final Thoughts and Conclusion</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">NFTs are unique blockchain-based digital assets that prove ownership of the items they represent and verify their authenticity. They have gained popularity in the form of a variety of applications, providing creators with new ways to monetize their work and collectors the opportunity to own and display unique assets. However, NFTs also carry potential risks such as fraud and market volatility. Although they share some similarities with cryptocurrencies, NFTs are characterized by the fact that they are not counterfeitable, which gives them unique digital opportunities. (Binance Academy, 2023)</span></span></span></p><div><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><br></span>\n</div></span>\n</div>',1,0,70,'2025-09-12 06:11:23','2025-10-08 09:50:53'),(187,29,'What is a Cryptocurrency White Paper?','<div class=\"prose prose-lg max-w-none space-y-4\">\n\n\n\n<span id=\"docs-internal-guid-2145bc11-7fff-4009-9e38-b21a7e1acb9e\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:0pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">A white paper summarizes the most important information about a blockchain or cryptocurrency project in a single document. It\'s a popular way to explain how a particular project works and what problems it\'s trying to solve.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">What is a white paper?</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">In general, a white paper is a report or guide that informs its readers about a specific topic or question. For example, developers can create a white paper about their software to inform users about what they are developing and why. In the field of blockchain, a white paper is a document that describes the key features and technical specifications of a particular cryptocurrency or blockchain project. Although many whitepapers focus on a coin or token, they can also be based on different types of projects, such as a decentralized finance (DeFi) platform or a play-to-earn game. A white paper can provide an overview of the most important data in the form of statistics and charts. Also, a white paper could explain the project\'s leadership structure, people, and current and future development plans (i.e., the roadmap). However, there is no official way to create a white paper. Each project creates a white paper that best suits its terms. Ideally, the white paper should be neutral and informative to clearly present the project and its goals. Users should always be wary of white papers that use persuasive language and projects that overpromise without providing enough information. Cryptocurrency white papers are often seen as business plans for crypto projects. That\'s because they give investors a comprehensive view of the project. But unlike business plans, white papers are usually published before the launch of a cryptocurrency. So, a white paper is often a starting point in which a crypto project lays out the direction and intent of its idea.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">What information can you find in a white paper?</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Founders create white papers to explain the goal of their project. Bitcoin\'s whitepaper, for example, states, \"A pure peer-to-peer version of electronic money would make it possible to send online payments directly from one party to another without going through a financial institution.\" Ethereum\'s whitepaper describes its goal as follows: \"Ethereum\'s intent is to create an alternative protocol for building decentralized applications.\" White papers often give an idea of the real-world benefits of the crypto project. For example, it could describe how it solves a specific problem or how it can improve certain aspects of our lives. Nevertheless, it is important to be careful with the promises. It\'s not hard to create a white paper. For example, the Initial Coin Offering (ICO) boom in 2017 spawned thousands of tokens with \"innovative\" ideas, but most projects didn\'t deliver what they promised. As a rule of thumb, you should remember that simply linking a cryptocurrency to a use case does not mean that it will be adopted and used. So, in addition to goals and promises, white papers can also show how cryptocurrency will really work. For example, it could explain what kind of consensus mechanism is used so that network participants can coordinate in a distributed way. A white paper could also provide a detailed look at the components of the token economy. Finally, a white paper could include a roadmap that informs users of the project\'s timeline so they know when to expect product releases. White papers are often so simple that anyone can read them and at least understand the basic idea of the cryptocurrency or blockchain project. However, a good white paper also includes technical explanations to confirm the competence of the project.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Why are white papers important?</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">White papers are important for the crypto ecosystem. Even though there are no standards for their creation, whitepapers have become a framework for researching crypto projects. It is generally recommended to start crypto research by reading the project\'s whitepaper. White papers can help you identify potential red flags or promising projects. It also allows you to check whether a project is sticking to its original plans and goals. White papers can ensure transparency and equality by making the most important information of the project public. Different parties can benefit from white papers. For example, while investors can use the whitepapers to make better investment decisions, developers can decide on their possible participation in the protocol. Similarly, a person who is interested in the idea will be able to decide more confidently whether to join a particular community after reading the white paper.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 15pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Examples of white papers:</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Bitcoin White Paper</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">The Bitcoin whitepaper was published in 2008 by an anonymous person or group named Satoshi Nakamoto. The Bitcoin whitepaper is titled \"Bitcoin: A Peer-to-Peer Electronic Cash System.\" The white paper describes how people can use Bitcoin as a more efficient form of money outside of the traditional banking model. It includes technical explanations of how the Bitcoin network allows users to send digital currencies over the peer-to-peer network without intermediaries. The white paper also explains how the Bitcoin network is protected against censorship and double-money spending attacks.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Ethereum White Paper</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">A young programmer named Vitalik Buterin published the Ethereum whitepaper in 2014. But even before that, in 2013, Vitalik proposed the idea of the whitepaper in a blog post: \"Ethereum: The Ultimate Smart Contract and Decentralized Application Platform.\" This post introduced the idea of a Turing-complete blockchain, a type of decentralized computer that can run any application if it has enough time and resources. Ethereum\'s whitepaper explains how Ethereum\'s purpose differs from Bitcoin\'s. While Bitcoin has a specific function to enable peer-to-peer digital payments, the Ethereum whitepaper introduces a platform that allows developers to create and deploy all kinds of decentralized applications (DApps). For example, this could be another cryptocurrency or a decentralized lending platform. The whitepaper also explains the technological solutions that have made Ethereum possible, such as smart contracts and the Ethereum Virtual Machine.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Final Thoughts and Conclusions</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Ideally, a white paper should give you the necessary understanding of what the cryptocurrency project intends to do and how. However, white papers are not regulated, and virtually anyone can write one. So, if you are interested in a particular project, it is important to carefully analyze the white paper and consider the possible red flags and risks. (Binance Academy, 2022)\n\n</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><a href=\"https://bitcoin.org/en/bitcoin-paper\" style=\"text-decoration:none;\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt;font-family:Poppins,sans-serif;color:#1155cc;background-color:#f7f7f7;font-weight:700;font-style:normal;font-variant:normal;text-decoration:underline;-webkit-text-decoration-skip:none;text-decoration-skip-ink:none;vertical-align:baseline;white-space:pre;white-space:pre-wrap;\">White Paper BTC</span></a></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><br></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><a href=\"https://ethereum.org/de/whitepaper/\" style=\"text-decoration:none;\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt;font-family:Poppins,sans-serif;color:#1155cc;background-color:transparent;font-weight:700;font-style:normal;font-variant:normal;text-decoration:underline;-webkit-text-decoration-skip:none;text-decoration-skip-ink:none;vertical-align:baseline;white-space:pre;white-space:pre-wrap;\">White Paper Ethereum</span></a></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><br></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span id=\"docs-internal-guid-1a274158-7fff-339d-6595-0f6949ce3b49\"></span><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\"></span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><br></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><br></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><br></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><br></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><br></p></span>\n\n\n\n</div>',1,0,70,'2025-09-12 06:12:17','2025-10-09 07:26:46'),(188,29,'What is a Candlestick Chart?','<div class=\"prose prose-lg max-w-none space-y-4\">\n\n<span id=\"docs-internal-guid-40a786a3-7fff-fb80-d74e-0e6ffbb2c8f7\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 8pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\"><span style=\"font-size: 14px;\"><img src=\"https://ptgr-bucket.s3.us-east-1.amazonaws.com/chapter3/5.jpg\" alt=\"Image\" class=\"max-w-full h-auto rounded-lg my-4 cursor-move hover:ring-2 hover:ring-primary/50 transition-all\" referrerpolicy=\"no-referrer\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto;\"><br></span></span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 8pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\"><span style=\"font-size: 14px;\">Figure 1: Example candlestick chart</span></span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 8pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\"><span style=\"font-size: 14px;\">(Source: Binance Academy)</span></span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Imagine tracking the price of an asset, such as a stock or cryptocurrency, over a period of time, such as a week, a day, or an hour. A candlestick chart is a way to visually represent this price data. The candle has a body and two lines, often referred to as wicks or shadows. The body of the candle represents the spread between the opening and closing prices within this period, while the wicks or shadows represent the highest and lowest prices reached during this period. A green body indicates that the price has risen during this period. A red body, on the other hand, indicates a bearish candle, which means that the price has declined during this period.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">How to read candlestick patterns:</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Candlestick patterns are formed by arranging several candles in a specific order. There are numerous candlestick patterns, each with its own interpretation. While some candlestick patterns provide insight into the balance between buyers and sellers, others may indicate a reversal, continuation, or indecision. It is important to note that candlestick patterns are not buy or sell signals per se. Instead, they are a way to look at current market trends to identify potentially upcoming opportunities. Therefore, it is always helpful to look at the patterns in context. This can be the context of the broader market environment or technical patterns on the chart, including the Wyckoff Method, the Elliott Wave Theory, and the Dow Theory. It can also include technical analysis (TA) indicators such as trend lines, the Relative Strength Index (RSI), the Stochastic RSI, Ichimoku clouds, or the Parabolic SAR. Candlestick patterns can also be used in conjunction with support and resistance levels. Support levels are price levels where strong demand is expected, while resistance levels are price levels where strong supply is expected.</span></span></span></p><div><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><br></span>\n</div></span>\n</div>',1,0,70,'2025-09-12 06:12:53','2025-10-08 09:53:22'),(189,29,'Candlestick Pattern','<div class=\"prose prose-lg max-w-none space-y-4\">\n\n<span id=\"docs-internal-guid-4d052883-7fff-1c23-64d8-e4b3ce8dbbf0\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:0pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Bullish Candlestick Patterns</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">The Hammer</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">A hammer is a candle with a long lower wick at the end of a downtrend, with the lower wick being at least twice the size of the body. A hammer indicates that the bulls have driven the price back up despite the high selling pressure at the opening. A hammer can be either red or green, with green hammers indicating a stronger bullish reaction.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:0pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><br></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:0pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Inverted Bullish Candlestick Patterns – Hammer</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">The inverted hammer</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">This pattern is like a hammer, but with a long wick over the body instead of underneath. Similar to a hammer, the top wick should be at least twice the size of the body. An inverted hammer occurs at the bottom of a downtrend and can indicate upside potential. The upper wick suggests that the price has halted its sustained downward movement, even though sellers eventually managed to push it lower near the open. So, the inverted hammer could indicate that buyers could soon take control of the market.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:0pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><br></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:0pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Inverted Bullish Candlestick Patterns—Inverted Hammer</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Three white soldiers</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">The pattern of the three white soldiers consists of three consecutive green candles, all of which open within the body of the previous candle and close above the high of the previous candle. These candles should not have long lower wicks, suggesting that continued buying pressure is driving the price higher. The size of the candlesticks and the length of the wicks can be interpreted as opportunities for a continuation or a possible pullback.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:0pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><br></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:0pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Reversed Bullish Candlestick Patterns—Three White Soldiers</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Bullish Harami</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">A bullish harami is a long red candle followed by a smaller green candle that is completely contained in the body of the previous candle. The bullish Harami can form over two or more days and is a pattern that indicates that the selling momentum is slowing and may be coming to an end.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:0pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Conversely: Bullish Candlestick Patterns – Bullish Harami</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:0pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">3</span></span></span><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Bearish Candlestick Patterns</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Hanging Man</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">The hanging man is the bearish equivalent of a hammer. It usually forms at the end of an uptrend with a small body and a long lower wick. The lower wick suggests that there was a sharp sell-off, but the bulls regained control and pushed the price higher. In this sense, the sell-off after a long uptrend can serve as a warning that the bulls may soon lose momentum.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:0pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><br></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Shooting Star</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">The Shooting Star consists of a candle with a long upper wick, a low or no lower wick, and a small body, ideally near the bottom. The shooting star has a similar shape to the inverted hammer but is formed at the end of an uptrend. It suggests that the market made a high, but then the sellers took control and pushed the price back down.Some traders prefer to wait for the next candles to develop.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:0pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><br></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">The three black crows</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">The three black crows consist of three consecutive red candles that open within the body of the previous candle and close below the bottom of the last candle. The bearish equivalent of three white soldiers. Ideally, these candlesticks should not have long, tall wicks, indicating that selling pressure is pushing the price further down. The size of the candlesticks and the length of the wicks can be used to assess the chances of continuing the course.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:0pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><br></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><br></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Bearish Harami</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">The bearish harami is a long green candle followed by a small red candle with a body that is completely contained in the body of the previous candle. The bearish Harami can span two or more days, appears at the end of an uptrend, and may indicate that buying pressure is easing.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:0pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><br></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">The dark cloud cover</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">The dark cloud cover pattern consists of a red candle that opens above the closing price of the previous green candle but then closes below the center of that candle. This pattern is often accompanied by high trading volume, suggesting that momentum could shift from bullish to bearish. Traders can wait for a third red bar to confirm the pattern.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:0pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><br></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Three Trend Continuation Patterns</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><br></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Three Rising Candles Method:</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">The candlestick pattern of the rising three methods occurs in an uptrend where three consecutive red candles with small bodies are followed by the continuation of the uptrend. Ideally, the red candles should not break through the area of the previous candle. The continuation is confirmed by a green candle with a large body, indicating that the bulls are back in control of the direction of the trend.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:0pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><br></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Three Falling Candles Method:</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">The three falling methods are the reversal of the three rising methods and instead indicate the continuation of a downtrend.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:0pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><br></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Doji</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">A doji forms when the opening and closing prices are the same (or very close to each other). The price can move above and below the open but will eventually close at or near the open. Thus, a doji can indicate a point of open butting between buying and selling forces. However, the interpretation of a doji is very contextual.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:0pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><br></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:0pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Depending on where the opening and closing lines fall, a doji can be described as follows:</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Gravestone Doji (Gravestone Doji)</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">It is a bearish reversal candle with a long upper wick and an open and close price near the low.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:0pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><br></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Long-legged Doji</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Indecisive candle with upper and lower wicks and the opening and closing price near the midpoint.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:0pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><br></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Dragonfly Doji</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Depending on the context, a bullish or bearish candle with a long lower wick and an opening/closing price near the high. According to the original definition of the doji, the opening and closing prices should be the same. What if the opening and closing prices are not identical, but they are very close to each other? Then we speak of a spinning top. However, because the cryptocurrency markets can be highly volatile, an exact doji is rare. Therefore, the spinning top is often used interchangeably with the term doji.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:0pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><br></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Candlestick patterns based on price gaps</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">A price gap occurs when a financial instrument opens above or below its previous closing price, creating a gap between the two candles. While many candlestick patterns contain price gaps, patterns based on this type of gap are not prevalent in the crypto market because trading takes place around the clock. Price gaps can also occur in illiquid markets, but they are not useful as patterns of action, as they mainly indicate low liquidity and high bid-ask spreads.</span></span></span></p><div><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><br></span>\n</div></span>\n</div>',1,0,70,'2025-09-12 06:14:32','2025-10-08 09:54:16'),(190,29,'How to Use Candlestick Patterns in Crypto Trading','<div class=\"prose prose-lg max-w-none space-y-4\">\n\n<span id=\"docs-internal-guid-aabb0951-7fff-662e-931a-f67fa64971f4\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:0pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Traders should keep the following tips in mind to effectively use candlestick patterns when trading cryptocurrencies:</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">1. Understand the basics</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Crypto traders should have a solid understanding of the basics of candlestick patterns before using them to make trading decisions. This includes understanding how to read candlestick charts and the different patterns that can form.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">2. Combine different indicators</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">While candlestick patterns can provide valuable insights, they should be combined with other technical indicators to create more comprehensive forecasts. Some examples of indicators that can be used in combination with candlestick patterns include moving averages, RSI, and MACD.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">3. Use multiple time periods</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Crypto traders should analyze candlestick patterns over multiple timeframes to gain a more comprehensive understanding of market sentiment. For example, if a trader is analyzing a daily chart, they should also look at the hourly and 15-minute charts to see how the patterns play out on different time frames.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">4. Practice risk management</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">The use of candlestick patterns, like any trading strategy, carries risks. Traders should always use risk management techniques, such as placing stop-loss orders, to protect their capital. It is also important to avoid exaggeration and only enter into trades with a favorable risk/reward ratio.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Final Thoughts and Conclusions</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><br></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Any trader can benefit from being familiar with candlesticks and their patterns, even if they don\'t include them in their trading strategy. While they can be useful in analyzing the markets, it\'s important to remember that they\'re not infallible. They are helpful indicators that convey the buying and selling forces that ultimately drive the markets. (Binance Academy, 2023)</span></span></span></p><div><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><br></span>\n</div></span>\n</div>',1,0,70,'2025-09-12 06:15:30','2025-10-08 09:54:48'),(191,29,'12 Terms Every Crypto Trader Should Know','<div class=\"prose prose-lg max-w-none space-y-4\">\n\n<span id=\"docs-internal-guid-ddb3ab47-7fff-1a89-4b90-8493b8fa38dc\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Whether you\'re in the stock market, day trading forex, or new to cryptocurrency, you\'ll hear a lot of trading terms that you may not be familiar with. FOMO, ROI, ATH, HODL—what do they all mean? Trading and investing have their own language, and it can be daunting to learn all these new terms. However, they can be very useful if you want to stay up-to-date with what\'s going on in the financial markets.</span></span></span></p><ul style=\"padding-inline-start: 48px;\"><li dir=\"ltr\" style=\"list-style-type: disc; font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre; margin-left: 59.35pt;\" aria-level=\"1\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\" role=\"presentation\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">1. Fear, Uncertainty, and Doubt (FUD)</span></span></span></p></li></ul><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-left: 95.35pt;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">While FUD is not exclusively a trading term, it is often used in the context of the financial markets. FUD is a strategy that aims to discredit a particular company, product, or project by spreading misinformation. The goal is to stir up fear and gain an advantage. This can be a competitive advantage, a tactical advantage, or a profit from a stock price decline caused by the potentially damaging news. As you might expect, FUD is quite common in the cryptocurrency space. In many cases, investors take a short position in an asset and then post potentially harmful or misleading news when the position has been established. In this way, large profits can be made by short selling or buying put options. You can also position yourself in advance with over-the-counter (OTC) trades. In many cases, the information turns out to be false or at least misleading. In some cases, however, they turn out to be true. It\'s always good to try to consider all sides of the argument. It can be helpful to think about what incentives people can have when they share certain opinions publicly.</span></span></span></p><ul style=\"padding-inline-start: 48px;\"><li dir=\"ltr\" style=\"list-style-type: disc; font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre; margin-left: 59.35pt;\" aria-level=\"1\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\" role=\"presentation\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">2. Fear Of Missing Out (FOMO)</span></span></span></p></li></ul><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-left: 95.35pt;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">FOMO is the emotion investors feel when they buy an asset in droves for fear of missing out on a profit opportunity. Since there are strong emotions involved, FOMO can lead to parabolic price movements in a large number of people. The \"FOMO\" of investors moving from one asset to the next can often be a sign of the late stages of a bull market. If you\'ve read the article on technical analysis (TA) errors, you know that extreme market conditions can change the usual rules of the markets. When emotions run high, many investors rush into positions for FOMO reasons. This can lead to extended movements in either direction, trapping many traders who are trying to counter the crowd. By intentionally mixing older and newer posts in the timeline, social media platforms want to trigger FOMO among users. In this way, users keep checking back for fear of missing something important.</span></span></span></p><ul style=\"padding-inline-start: 48px;\"><li dir=\"ltr\" style=\"list-style-type: disc; font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre; margin-left: 59.35pt;\" aria-level=\"1\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\" role=\"presentation\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">3. HODL</span></span></span></p></li></ul><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-left: 95.35pt;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">HODL is a term derived from a misspelling of \"Hold\". It is basically the cryptocurrency equivalent of the buy-and-hold strategy. HODL originally appeared in a now-famous post on the BitcoinTalk forum in 2013. The term was a spelling mistake in the title: \"I AM HODLING\". HODLing refers to holding on to investments despite price declines. It is also commonly used in the context of investors (\"HODLers\") who are not good at short-term trading but want to take on price risk in cryptocurrencies. It can also be used for investors who are convinced of a particular coin and intend to hold their investment for a longer period of time. The HODLing strategy is similar to the buy-and-hold investment strategy originating from traditional markets. Buy-and-hold investors try to find undervalued assets and hold them for a long time. Many investors are applying this strategy to Bitcoin. If you\'ve read the article about dollar-cost averaging (DCA), you know that this would have been a highly profitable strategy for Bitcoin. If you had bought BTC for just $10 every week for the past five years, you would have gained more than seven times your initial investment!</span></span></span></p><ul style=\"padding-inline-start: 48px;\"><li dir=\"ltr\" style=\"list-style-type: disc; font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre; margin-left: 59.35pt;\" aria-level=\"1\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\" role=\"presentation\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">4. BUIDL</span></span></span></p></li></ul><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-left: 95.35pt;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">BUIDL is a derivative term of HODL. It usually describes participants in the BUILD cryptocurrency industry who continue to build regardless of price fluctuations. The main idea is that true believers in the crypto industry will continue to build the ecosystem regardless of brutal bear markets. In this sense, the \"BUIDLers\" really care about what blockchain and cryptocurrencies can bring to the world, and they are actively working towards this goal. BUIDL is a mindset designed to illustrate that cryptocurrencies are not just about speculation but about making this technology accessible to the masses. It reminds us to keep our heads down and continue to build the infrastructure that could serve billions of people in the future. In addition, the BUIDLers know that the teams that continue to build with a long-term mindset are likely to do well in the long run.</span></span></span></p><ul style=\"padding-inline-start: 48px;\"><li dir=\"ltr\" style=\"list-style-type: disc; font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre; margin-left: 59.35pt;\" aria-level=\"1\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\" role=\"presentation\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">5. SAFU</span></span></span></p></li></ul><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-left: 95.35pt;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:0pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Secure Asset fund for Users (SAFU)</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-left: 95.35pt;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">SAFU goes back to a meme uploaded by Bizonacci. It included Binance CEO Changpeng Zhao (CZ), who said that \"funds are safe\" during the platform\'s unscheduled maintenance. The video went viral in the cryptocurrency scene. In response, Binance has set up the Secure Asset Fund for Users (SAFU) to provide a fund for potential security breaches. These funds are stored in a separate cold wallet. The idea is that the SAFU can cover the loss of user funds in extreme cases, thus providing additional protection for Binance users. For this reason, you often hear the phrase \"funds are safu.\"</span></span></span></p><ul style=\"padding-inline-start: 48px;\"><li dir=\"ltr\" style=\"list-style-type: disc; font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre; margin-left: 59.35pt;\" aria-level=\"1\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\" role=\"presentation\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">6. Return on investment (ROI)</span></span></span></p></li></ul><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-left: 95.35pt;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:0pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Return on Investment (ROI) is a method of measuring the performance of an investment. ROI measures the return on an investment relative to the original cost. It\'s also a convenient way to compare the performance of different investments. Here\'s how to calculate the return on investment. You take the current value of the investment and subtract the original cost of the investment from it. Then divide that number by the original cost.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-left: 95.35pt;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:0pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">ROI = (Current Value - Original Cost) / Original Cost</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-left: 95.35pt;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:0pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Let\'s say you bought Bitcoin for $6,000. The current market price of Bitcoin is now $8,000.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-left: 95.35pt;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:0pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">ROI = (8000-6000)/6000</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-left: 95.35pt;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:0pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">ROI = 0.33</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-left: 95.35pt;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">This means that you received 33% more than your initial investment. It\'s also worth considering the fees (or interest rate) you\'ll have to pay to get a more accurate picture. However, the pure numbers are not the whole picture. Other factors also play a role when comparing investments. How high are the risks? How long is the time horizon? How liquid is the investment? Can price fluctuations affect your purchase price? While ROI isn\'t the ultimate metric, it\'s a useful tool for measuring the performance of your investments. Calculating position size is crucial when it comes to investment returns. If you want to read a simple formula to help you manage risk effectively, please read How to Calculate Position Size in Trading.</span></span></span></p><ul style=\"padding-inline-start: 48px;\"><li dir=\"ltr\" style=\"list-style-type: disc; font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre; margin-left: 59.35pt;\" aria-level=\"1\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\" role=\"presentation\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">7. All-Time High (ATH)</span></span></span></p></li></ul><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-left: 95.35pt;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">We probably don\'t need to explain this, do we? The all-time high is the highest recorded price of an asset. For example, during the 2017 bull market, Bitcoin\'s ATH was 19,798.86 USDT for the BTC/USDT pair on Binance. This means that this was the highest price that Bitcoin was trading for on this market pair. A compelling aspect of an asset reaching an all-time high is the idea that almost everyone who has ever bought is in profit. If an asset is in a prolonged bear market, many traders with losses will likely want to exit the market when their position breaks even. However, if the asset breaks its ATH, there will be no sellers waiting to exit at break-even. For this reason, some refer to the breaking of the ATH as a \"blue sky breakout\" because there are not necessarily obvious resistance areas. ATH breakthroughs are often accompanied by a spike in trading volume. Why? Day traders can also take the opportunity to make a quick profit with market orders and sell at a higher price. Does crossing the ATH mean that the price will continue to rise forever? No, of course not. Traders and investors will eventually want to take profits and can place limit orders at certain price levels. This is especially true when previous all-time highs are repeatedly broken. Parabolic moves can often end in very sharp price losses, as many investors rush to exit as soon as they realize that the uptrend may be coming to an end. Look at the price drop after Bitcoin\'s parabolic move to $20,000 in December 2017. After reaching an ATH of $19,798.86, Bitcoin fell by almost 45% in a matter of days. For this reason, it is always important to control risk and always use a stop loss.</span></span></span></p><ul style=\"padding-inline-start: 48px;\"><li dir=\"ltr\" style=\"list-style-type: disc; font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre; margin-left: 59.35pt;\" aria-level=\"1\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\" role=\"presentation\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">8. All-time low (ATL)</span></span></span></p></li></ul><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-left: 95.35pt;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">The opposite of ATH, the all-time low (ATL), is the lowest price of an asset. For example, BNB\'s all-time low on the first day of trading was 0.5 USDT for the BNB/USDT market pair. Breaking an all-time low in an asset can have a similar effect to breaking an all-time high—but in the opposite direction. Many stop orders can be triggered when the previous all-time low is broken, resulting in a sharp downward movement. Since there is no price trend below the previous all-time low, the market value can continue to fall and drift further and further down. Since there are not necessarily logical points where the downtrend can be stopped, buying at such times is very risky. Many traders wait for a confirmed trend change by a key moving average or other indicator before even considering entering a long position. Otherwise, they could end up sitting in their laps for a long time, trapped in a position that goes deeper and deeper.</span></span></span></p><ul style=\"padding-inline-start: 48px;\"><li dir=\"ltr\" style=\"list-style-type: disc; font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre; margin-left: 59.35pt;\" aria-level=\"1\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\" role=\"presentation\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">9. Do Your Own Research (DYOR)</span></span></span></p></li></ul><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-left: 95.35pt;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">When it comes to the financial markets, DYOR is a term closely associated with fundamental analysis (FA). It means that investors should do their own research on their investments and not rely on others to do it for them. \"Don\'t trust, verify\" is a commonly used phrase in the cryptocurrency markets with a similar meaning. The most successful investors do their own research and come to their own conclusions. So, anyone who wants to succeed in the financial markets needs to develop their own unique trading strategy. This can also lead to disagreements between different investors, which is a natural part of investing and trading. One investor may be optimistic about an asset, while another may be more pessimistic. Different views can accommodate different strategies, and successful traders and investors will pursue very different strategies. The main idea is that they all did their own research, came to their own conclusions, and made their investment decisions based on those conclusions.</span></span></span></p><ul style=\"padding-inline-start: 48px;\"><li dir=\"ltr\" style=\"list-style-type: disc; font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre; margin-left: 59.35pt;\" aria-level=\"1\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\" role=\"presentation\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">10. Due diligence (DD)</span></span></span></p></li></ul><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-left: 95.35pt;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Due diligence (DD) is related to DYOR in some ways. It refers to the research and diligence expected of a reasonable person or company before entering into an agreement with another party. When reasonable businessmen make an agreement, they are expected to examine each other with due diligence. Why? Any reasonable actor wants to make sure that there are no potential vulnerabilities in the business. Otherwise, how could they compare the potential risks with the expected benefits? The same applies to investments. When investors are looking for potential investments, they need to conduct their own due diligence of the project to ensure they can consider all the risks. Otherwise, they have no control over their investment decisions and could end up making the wrong choice.</span></span></span></p><ul style=\"padding-inline-start: 48px;\"><li dir=\"ltr\" style=\"list-style-type: disc; font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre; margin-left: 59.35pt;\" aria-level=\"1\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\" role=\"presentation\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">11. Anti Money Laundering (AML)</span></span></span></p></li></ul><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-left: 95.35pt;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Anti-money laundering (AML) refers to a set of regulations, laws, and procedures designed to prevent criminals from disguising their illegally acquired money as legitimate income. AML procedures make it much harder for criminals to \"launder\" their money cleanly by hiding it or disguising it as coming from legitimate sources. Criminals will always look for ways to disguise the true source of their funds. Due to the complexity of financial markets, there are many different ways to do this. Derivative products made up of derivative products and other complex market manipulations can make tracing the true origin of funds quite difficult (though not impossible). AML regulations require financial institutions such as banks to monitor their customers\' transactions and report suspicious activity. This way, criminals are less likely to get away with laundering illegally obtained funds.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-left: 95.35pt;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><br></p><ul style=\"padding-inline-start: 48px;\"><li dir=\"ltr\" style=\"list-style-type: disc; font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre; margin-left: 59.35pt;\" aria-level=\"1\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\" role=\"presentation\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">12. Know Your Customer (KYC)</span></span></span></p></li></ul><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-left: 95.35pt;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Exchanges and trading platforms must adhere to national and international guidelines. For example, the New York Stock Exchange (NYSE) and the NASDAQ must comply with regulations set by the U.S. government. Know Your Customer (KYC) or Know Your Client guidelines ensure that institutions that facilitate the trading of financial instruments verify the identity of their clients. Why is this important? The main reason for this is to minimize the risk of money laundering. Moreover, KYC regulations do not only apply to participants in the financial industry. Many other areas must also comply with these guidelines. KYC policies are usually part of a much broader anti-money laundering (AML) policy.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:0pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Final Thoughts and Conclusions</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">The terms of cryptocurrency trading can seem a bit confusing at first. But now you know a good part of them, so you can feel more SAFU with all these abbreviations. &nbsp;Make sure to DYOR on FUD, don\'t blind FOMO in a coin that has reached ATH, and keep HODLing and BUIDLing! (Binance Academy, 2020)</span></span></span></p></span>\n\n</div>',1,0,70,'2025-09-12 06:18:01','2025-10-08 09:55:17'),(192,29,'Analysis Tools in Focus','<div class=\"prose prose-lg max-w-none space-y-4\">\n\n<span id=\"docs-internal-guid-e4ccedc3-7fff-4b97-bac6-7b12ce452a23\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Moving Averages Explained</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Technical analysis (TA) is nothing new in the world of trading and investing. From traditional portfolios to cryptocurrencies like Bitcoin and Ethereum, the use of TA indicators has a simple goal: to use existing data to make more informed decisions that are likely to lead to the desired outcomes. As markets become more complicated, the past few decades have produced hundreds of different types of TA indicators, but few have experienced the popularity and consistent use of moving averages (MA). Although there are different variations of moving averages, their basic purpose is to provide more clarity on the trading charts. This is done by smoothing the graphs to create an easily decipherable trend indicator. Because these moving averages are based on historical data, they are considered lagging or trend-following indicators. Nonetheless, they are able to cut through the noise and determine the direction of a market.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Different types of moving averages:</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">There are several types of moving averages that can be used by traders not only in day trading and swing trading, but also in longer-term setups. Despite the different types, moving averages are most often divided into two different categories: simple moving averages (SMA) and exponential moving averages (EMA). Depending on the market and the desired outcome, the trader can choose which indicator is best suited for their setup.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">The Simple Moving Average (SMA)</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">The SMA takes the data of a specific period of time and forms the average price of the security for the data set. The difference between an SMA and a simple average of past prices is that in the SMA, as soon as a new record is entered, the oldest record is disregarded. So, when the simple moving average calculates the average based on 10 days of data, the entire data set is constantly updated to account for only the last 10 days.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:0pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">It\'s important to note that all data inputs are weighted equally in an SMA, regardless of how recently they were entered. Traders who believe that the latest available data is of greater importance often claim that the weighting of the SMA is detrimental to technical analysis. The Exponential Moving Average (EMA) was created to solve this problem.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">The exponential moving average:</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">EMAs are similar to SMAs in that they provide technical analysis based on past price fluctuations. However, the equation is a bit more complicated, as an EMA assigns more weight and value to recent prices. Although both averages are valuable and commonly used, the EMA is more responsive to sudden price swings and reversals.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:0pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Because EMAs are more able to predict price swings faster than SMAs, they are often preferred by traders involved in short-term trading. It is important that a trader or investor chooses the type of moving average according to their personal strategies and goals and adjusts the settings accordingly.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">How to apply SMA and EMA:</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Because MAs use past prices rather than current prices, they have some lag. The larger the data set, the greater the delay. For example, a moving average that analyzes the last 100 days will be slower to respond to new information than a moving average that only considers the last 10 days. This is simply because a new entry in a larger data set has a smaller impact on the total numbers. Depending on the trading structure, both can be beneficial. Long-term investors benefit from larger data sets because they are less likely to change due to one or two large fluctuations. Short-term traders often prefer a smaller data set that allows for more reactive trading. In traditional markets, the 50-, 100-, and 200-day moving averages are the most commonly used. The 50-day and 200-day moving averages are closely watched by stock traders, and breakouts above or below these lines are usually considered important trading signals, especially when followed by crossovers. The same goes for cryptocurrency trading, but due to the round-the-clock volatile markets, MA settings and trading strategy can vary depending on the trader\'s profile.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Crossover Signals</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Of course, a rising MA indicates an uptrend and a falling MA indicates a downtrend. However, a moving average alone is not a really reliable and strong indicator. Therefore, MAs are always used in combination to detect bullish and bearish crossover signals. A crossover signal occurs when two different MAs intersect on a graph. A bullish crossover (also known as a golden cross) is when the short-term MA crosses over a long-term one, indicating the start of an uptrend. In contrast, a bearish crossover (or death cross) occurs when a short-term MA crosses below a long-term moving average, indicating the start of a downtrend.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Other factors to consider:</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">The examples so far are all related to days, but that\'s not essential when analyzing MAs. Those who are involved in day trading may be much more interested in how an asset has performed in the last two or three hours, not in two or three months. Different timeframes can be used in the equations used to calculate moving averages, and as long as these timeframes align with the trading strategy, the data can be useful. A major disadvantage of the MAs is their delay time. Because MAs are lagging indicators that account for previous price movements, the signals often come too late. For example, a bullish crossover can be a buy signal, but it only occurs after a significant price increase. This means that even if the uptrend continues, potential profit may have been lost in the time between the price surge and the crossover signal. Or even worse, a false golden cross signal can trick a trader into buying the local top just before the price drops. These fake buy signals are commonly referred to as the bull trap.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:0pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Final Thoughts and Conclusions</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Moving averages are powerful technical analysis (TA)&nbsp; indicators and one of the most commonly used. The ability to analyze market trends in a data-driven manner provides a good insight into how a market is evolving. However, keep in mind that moving averages and crossover signals should not be used alone. It is always safer to combine different TA indicators to avoid fake signals. (Binance Academy, 2018)</span></span></span></p></span>\n\n</div>',1,0,70,'2025-09-12 06:18:42','2025-10-08 09:55:43'),(193,29,'What are RSI indicators?','<div class=\"prose prose-lg max-w-none space-y-4\">\n<span id=\"docs-internal-guid-749d28c4-7fff-0196-5a16-2862589fd18a\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:0pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\">The Relative Strength Index Indicator (RSI Indicator)</span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:0pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\">Technical analysis (TA) is basically about studying past market events to try to predict future trends and price movements. From traditional markets to cryptocurrency markets, most traders rely on specialized tools to perform these analyses, and the RSI is one of them. The RSI is a TA indicator that was developed in the late 1970s as a tool that allows traders to examine the performance of a stock over a period of time. It is basically a momentum oscillator that measures both the magnitude of price movements and the speed of those movements. The RSI can be a very helpful tool, depending on the trader\'s profile and their trade setup. The RSI indicator was developed by J. Welles Wilder in 1978. He was featured in his book New Concepts in Technical Trading Systems along with other TA indicators such as the Parabolic SAR, the Average True Range (ATR), and the Average Directional Index (ADX). Before becoming a technical analyst, Wilder worked as a mechanical engineer and real estate developer. He started trading stocks around 1972, but was not very successful. A few years later, Wilder summarized his trading research and experience in mathematical formulas and indicators, which were later adopted by many traders around the world. The book was created in just six months, and although it dates back to the 1970s, it is still a reference for many chartists and traders today.</span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\">How does the RSI indicator work?</span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\">By default, the RSI measures the price changes of an asset over 14 time periods (14 days for daily charts, 14 hours for hourly charts, etc.). The formula divides the average gain the price made over that period by the average loss it suffered and then plots the data on a scale of 0 to 100. As mentioned earlier, the RSI is a momentum indicator, which is a type of technical trading tool that measures the speed at which the price (or data) is changing. If the momentum picks up and the price rises, it indicates that the stock is actively being bought in the market. If the momentum increases to the downside, it is a sign that selling pressure is increasing. The RSI is also an oscillating indicator that makes it easier for traders to identify overbought or oversold market conditions. It evaluates the price of an asset on a scale of 0 to 100, taking into account the 14 periods. While an RSI reading of 30 or less suggests that the asset is likely to be near its bottom (oversold), a reading above 70 suggests that the asset\'s price is likely to be near its peak (overbought) for that period. Although the default setting for the RSI is 14 periods, traders can change it to increase sensitivity (fewer periods) or decrease it (more periods). For example, a 7-day RSI is more sensitive to price movements than an RSI that takes 21 days into account. In addition, for short-term trading plans, the RSI indicator can be adjusted to consider 20 and 80 as overbought and oversold levels (instead of 30 and 70), making it less likely to provide false signals.</span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\">How to use the RSI based on divergences:</span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\">In addition to the RSI values of 30 and 70—which indicate potentially oversold and overbought market conditions—traders also use the RSI to predict trend reversals or to identify support and resistance levels. Such an approach is based on the so-called bullish and bearish divergences. A bullish divergence is a state in which the price and the RSI value move in opposite directions. So, the RSI value rises and creates higher lows while the price falls and creates lower lows. This is known as a \"bullish\" divergence, and it indicates that purchasing power is increasing despite the downward trend in prices. In contrast, bearish divergences can indicate that the market is losing momentum despite a price increase. Therefore, the RSI value decreases and creates lower highs while the asset price rises and creates higher highs. However, keep in mind that RSI divergences are not very reliable during strong market trends. This means that a strong downtrend can have many upward divergences before reaching the actual bottom. For this reason, RSI divergences are better suited to less volatile markets (with sideways movements or subtle trends).</span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\">The following should be noted:</span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\">There are several important factors to consider when using the Relative Strength Index indicator, such as the settings, the score (30 and 70), and the divergences between bull and bear markets. However, it should always be remembered that no technical indicator is 100% efficient—especially when used alone. Therefore, traders should consider using the RSI indicator along with other indicators to avoid false signals. (Binance Academy, 2018).</span></p><div><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><br></span></div></span>\n</div>',1,0,70,'2025-09-12 06:19:41','2025-09-12 06:19:41'),(194,29,'What is the difference between Centralized Exchanges (CEX) and Decentralized Exchanges (DEX)?','<div class=\"prose prose-lg max-w-none space-y-4\">\n\n<span id=\"docs-internal-guid-73bc6056-7fff-1f33-7197-eaad561ac3f7\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:0pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">If you\'re into cryptocurrency, you\'ll probably have come across DEXs. Maybe you\'re already a customer of a CEX like Binance, but you\'re interested in using a DEX, or maybe you\'re looking to buy the latest DeFi coin that isn\'t currently available on CEXs. Regardless of your history, you should know the key differences between a CEX and DEX before choosing one. Depending on what you want to do and how much experience you have, each variant has its advantages and disadvantages.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Why are there different types of exchanges?</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">If you\'re a new trader or just have experience in traditional finance, the difference between a CEX and DEX can be confusing. A CEX works in a similar way to the world of stocks—a single unit works with an order book where market makers and takers place orders. The order book then brings buyers and sellers together and takes a small portion of the transaction as a fee. At the heart of the operation is the order book and the exchange unit, which centralizes the model. To use a CEX like Binance, you\'ll need to set up an account and verify your identity according to local regulations. The exchange also handles the custody of the assets you want to trade on the CEX after you deposit them into your CEX account. However, a DEX is powered by smart contracts, which are self-executing pieces of code on a blockchain. A company or project can create a DEX and help run it, but in theory, it can run itself as long as people provide it with liquidity. Unlike CEXs, DEXs do not typically use order books to facilitate trading. Instead, they mainly use the automated market maker (AMM) model. To use a DEX, all you need is a crypto wallet and some crypto (including enough for any gas/transaction fees). Due to its decentralized nature, there is no need for registration or an account for users. For newcomers, a CEX offers a more user-friendly experience and an easier way to get started. If something goes wrong, you can contact the exchange\'s customer support team. A DEX also has several advantages, but they tend to be more appealing to experienced crypto users who prefer decentralization over ease of use.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:-3pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><br></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Centralized Exchanges (CEX): Advantages</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">A CEX like Binance focuses on providing user-friendly experiences for all types of users. Crypto newbies have access to easy conversion tools like Binance Convert, while experienced traders can use the spot exchange\'s TradingView tools. A CEX will also likely offer detailed guides as part of its services (Binance Academy is an example). The process of registering with a CEX is similar to opening a bank account. Many of us are already familiar with this system, and technical knowledge is not a must. Most CEXs offer payments via credit or debit cards, making your first crypto investment as easy as possible. One of the biggest hurdles for crypto beginners when using DEXs is getting used to decentralized tools. Crypto wallets, addresses, gas fees, and other aspects of the blockchain can be unforgiving if you make mistakes. There are no system administrators to offer help, so you\'re on your own if you send cryptocurrencies to the wrong wallet, for example. You can avoid these mistakes when trading through a CEX. For example, customer support can help you if you lose access to your Binance account, and you won\'t have to pay gas fees when moving your cryptocurrency between Binance products. These measures support users and protect them from making irrevocable mistakes, as is sometimes the case with DEXs. A CEX can offer a wide range of products and integrated services, thus becoming a one-stop crypto shop. On a CEX like Binance, you\'ll have access to trading and staking services, an NFT marketplace, a launchpad, a P2P exchange, and more without leaving the platform. Plus, transferring money between these different areas is easy. You can pay by card and even take advantage of offers from partner merchants such as Travala.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Centralized Exchanges: Disadvantages</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">As large centralized entities, CEXs are often the target of attacks, which means that any user funds stored on a CEX are vulnerable to theft. Although CEXs prioritize security, they can\'t eliminate the risk of hacks and thefts. There have been several high-profile hacks over the years, such as the Mt. Gox hack. Almost every—if not every—service you use through a CEX incurs a transaction fee. Often these are hidden or \"built-in\" to the service. Due to their higher operating costs, CEXs have a hard time competing with the low transaction fees offered by DEXs. However, the additional transaction fees of a CEX must be weighed against the gas fees for using a DEX, which can be substantial on some networks. If you use a CEX, you will need to deposit your cryptocurrency into its wallet. This means that you give up control of your funds by entrusting them to the CEX. Therefore, you may not be able to withdraw them whenever you want. This is problematic if an exchange becomes insolvent and can result in you losing your funds entirely. This situation is similar to storing cash at a bank—during a run on the bank, you may not be able to access your money.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Decentralized Exchanges (DEX): Advantages</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">When you use a DEX, you never lose control of your funds until a trade or interaction is completed. This means that you can move and store them as you wish. If your cryptocurrency is stored in a wallet whose seed phrase only you have access to, you have full control over your assets. For the proponents of decentralization, this is one of the most important factors. When using a DEX, you don\'t have to provide any personal, identifying information and trust it to be stored securely. This means that your personal information is safe from bad actors who want to steal your identity. Keep in mind that when using an exchange, you\'re still bound by local regulations and laws. Using a DEX only requires a wallet and some crypto for trading and paying gas fees. This makes DEXs very accessible to a larger group of people worldwide. For fans of decentralization, this is a huge advantage, and many appreciate the more private nature of a DEX. Of course, this should also be considered in the context of local legislation and relevant debates on cryptocurrency regulation.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Decentralized Exchanges: Disadvantages</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">For a first-time user, it can be challenging to understand how to navigate and use a DEX. Gas fees, liquidity pools, wallets, and slippage are all possible hurdles to overcome. It takes time to understand how a DEX works, and to invest responsibly, you should always understand your tools. In the past, buying the first coin or token has been one of the biggest hurdles to getting started with cryptocurrency. CEXs now offer credit and debit card options for buying cryptocurrencies by default. Although some DEXs are starting to offer this service with third-party providers, most still do not allow the use of credit or debit cards. DEXs have, on average, smaller volumes and less liquidity than large CEXs. Market depth may be less, and large trades on a DEX can have a greater impact on the price than on a CEX. Traders or investors who place orders with large volumes can often get a better price if they trade over-the-counter (OTC) or use a CEX with deep order books, such as Binance.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Should I use a DEX or CEX?</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">By far the easiest experience for a crypto beginner is using a CEX. It eliminates the initial learning curve associated with decentralized blockchain infrastructure. If you encounter any issues while using a CEX, a support team will help you and guide you as much as possible. Overall, the chances of making an irreversible mistake, such as losing access to your money or transferring it to the wrong address, are much lower with a CEX. So, if you\'re new to cryptocurrency or prefer a straightforward, easy-to-understand service, a CEX is for you. A DEX, on the other hand, is an excellent introduction to the world of DeFi. With a single Ethereum Virtual Machine (EVM) wallet, you can explore the vast DeFi universe. A DEX is a versatile option for crypto power users and offers a high degree of freedom. If you\'re looking to invest in a wide range of new small-cap projects, a DEX is also your best bet. While CEXs often launch new tokens, DEXs still facilitate most new token sales and liquidity.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">In light of the above, and to help you decide at a glance, check out the following use cases for each type of exchange:</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><br></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve; font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0);\"><img src=\"https://ptgr-bucket.s3.us-east-1.amazonaws.com/chapter3/6.png\" alt=\"Image\" class=\"max-w-full h-auto rounded-lg my-4 cursor-move hover:ring-2 hover:ring-primary/50 transition-all\" referrerpolicy=\"no-referrer\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto;\"><br></span></p><div><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span id=\"docs-internal-guid-926e67d0-7fff-6ac3-9e7f-0d654a4faa72\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 8pt; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\"><span style=\"font-size: 14px;\">Figure 2: Comparison of CEX and DEX</span></span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 8pt; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\"><span style=\"font-size: 14px;\">(Source: Binance Academy)</span></span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Conclusions: Final Thoughts:</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">If you\'re considering trying out a DEX, you\'ll need to do your own research beyond the points mentioned in this guide. While the benefits may sound tempting to some, we recommend that you do your research on how to use a DEX and the tools that come with it. (Binance Academy, 2022)</span></span></span></p><div><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline;\"><br></span>\n</div></span></span></div></span>\n</div>',1,1,70,'2025-09-12 06:23:22','2025-10-08 09:56:43'),(195,30,'Why do people invest in cryptocurrencies?','<div class=\"prose prose-lg max-w-none space-y-4\">\n\n<span id=\"docs-internal-guid-a43fa8f3-7fff-8ec1-dcdc-6db1f8e7a1c9\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:0pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">People invest in cryptocurrencies for the same reason that everyone invests in anything. They hope that their value will increase and that they will make a profit. For example, if the demand for Bitcoin increases, the interplay of supply and demand could increase the value of Bitcoin. If people start using Bitcoin for payments on a large scale, the demand for Bitcoin would increase, and in turn, the price in dollars would rise. So, if you bought a Bitcoin before the increase in demand, you could theoretically sell that one Bitcoin for more US dollars than you bought it, making a profit. The same principles apply to Ethereum. Ether is the cryptocurrency of the Ethereum blockchain that allows developers to build financial applications without the need for a third-party financial institution. Developers must use Ether to develop and run applications on Ethereum. Theoretically, the demand for Ether increases the more is developed on the Ethereum blockchain. However, it is important to note that for some, cryptocurrencies are not investments at all. Bitcoin enthusiasts, for example, praise Bitcoin as a significantly improved system compared to our current monetary system and would prefer us to spend and accept it as an everyday means of payment.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:0pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\"><br></span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">How do cryptocurrencies work?</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Cryptocurrencies are backed by a technology known as blockchain. It records transactions in a forgery-proof manner and records who owns what. The use of blockchains solved a problem that previous attempts to create purely digital currencies faced: it prevented people from making copies of their holdings and trying to spend them twice. Individual units of cryptocurrencies can be referred to as coins or tokens, depending on how they are used. Some are intended as a medium of exchange for goods and services, others are stores of value, and still others can be used to participate in certain software programs such as games and financial products.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\"><br></span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Are cryptocurrencies financial securities like stocks?</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">The question of whether cryptocurrencies are a security or not is a gray area at the moment. To elaborate a little, in general, a \"security\" in finance is anything that represents value and can be traded. Shares are securities because they represent the ownership of a public company. Bonds are securities because they represent a debt owed to the bondholder. And both securities can be traded on public markets. Regulators are increasingly signaling that cryptocurrencies should be regulated in a similar way to other securities, such as stocks and bonds. However, this view meets with resistance. Academics, law firms, and some of the biggest players in the crypto industry have argued against it, claiming that the rules that apply to stocks and bonds, for example, are not as broadly applicable to cryptocurrencies. The Securities and Exchange Commission has been targeting the sector in general. The agency has raised concerns about activities such as crypto betting and the operations of some major crypto companies. Whether the SEC will treat cryptocurrencies or certain types of cryptocurrencies as securities is at the forefront of crypto regulation and could have a major impact on the asset class in the near future.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\"><br></span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Are NFTs cryptocurrencies?</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">NFTs, or non-fungible tokens, are digital assets that convey ownership of what is known to be an original copy of a digital file. They share many similarities with cryptocurrencies and can be bought and sold on many of the same marketplaces. However, NFTs differ from cryptocurrencies by the unwieldy word in their name: non-fungible. Cryptocurrencies are fungible, meaning that each unit of a particular cryptocurrency is basically the same as any other. My only Bitcoin has the same value as your only Bitcoin.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Pros and Cons of Cryptocurrencies</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Cryptocurrencies arouse passionate opinions across the investor spectrum. Here are some reasons why some people believe it\'s a transformative technology, while others fear it\'s a fad.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Advantages of cryptocurrencies</span></span></span></p><ul style=\"padding-inline-start: 48px;\"><li dir=\"ltr\" style=\"list-style-type: disc; font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre; margin-left: 59.35pt;\" aria-level=\"1\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:0pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\" role=\"presentation\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Some proponents like the fact that cryptocurrencies relieve central banks from managing the money supply, as these banks tend to reduce the value of money through inflation over time.</span></span></span></p></li><li dir=\"ltr\" style=\"list-style-type: disc; font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre; margin-left: 59.35pt;\" aria-level=\"1\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:0pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\" role=\"presentation\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">In communities underserved by the traditional financial system, some people see cryptocurrencies as a promising mainstay. Data from the Pew Research Center in 2021 found that Asians, Blacks, and Hispanics \"are more likely than white adults to say they\'ve ever invested, traded, or used a cryptocurrency.\"</span></span></span></p></li><li dir=\"ltr\" style=\"list-style-type: disc; font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre; margin-left: 59.35pt;\" aria-level=\"1\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:0pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\" role=\"presentation\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Other proponents appreciate the blockchain technology behind cryptocurrencies because it is a decentralized processing and recording system and can be more secure than traditional payment systems.</span></span></span></p></li><li dir=\"ltr\" style=\"list-style-type: disc; font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre; margin-left: 59.35pt;\" aria-level=\"1\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\" role=\"presentation\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Some cryptocurrencies offer their owners the opportunity to earn passive income through a process called staking. Crypto staking involves using your cryptocurrencies to verify transactions on a blockchain protocol. Although staking comes with risks, it allows you to grow your crypto holdings without buying more.</span></span></span></p></li></ul><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Disadvantages of cryptocurrencies</span></span></span></p><ul style=\"padding-inline-start: 48px;\"><li dir=\"ltr\" style=\"list-style-type: disc; font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre; margin-left: 59.35pt;\" aria-level=\"1\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:0pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\" role=\"presentation\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Many cryptocurrency projects are untested and blockchain technology in general has yet to catch on. If the underlying idea of cryptocurrency does not reach its potential, long-term investors may never achieve the returns they were hoping for.</span></span></span></p></li><li dir=\"ltr\" style=\"list-style-type: disc; font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre; margin-left: 59.35pt;\" aria-level=\"1\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:0pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\" role=\"presentation\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">For short-term crypto investors, there are other risks. Prices tend to change quickly. While this means that many people made money quickly by shopping at the right time, many others also lost money by shopping just before a crypto crash.</span></span></span></p></li><li dir=\"ltr\" style=\"list-style-type: disc; font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre; margin-left: 59.35pt;\" aria-level=\"1\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:0pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\" role=\"presentation\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">These wild fluctuations in value can also run counter to the basic ideas of the projects for which cryptocurrencies were created. For example, people are less likely to use Bitcoin as a form of payment if they are not sure what it will be worth the next day.</span></span></span></p></li><li dir=\"ltr\" style=\"list-style-type: disc; font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre; margin-left: 59.35pt;\" aria-level=\"1\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:0pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\" role=\"presentation\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">The environmental impact of Bitcoin and other projects that use similar mining protocols is significant. A comparison by the University of Cambridge, for example, showed that Bitcoin mining consumes more than twice as much electricity worldwide as all the lighting in American households. Some cryptocurrencies use a different technology that uses less energy.</span></span></span></p></li><li dir=\"ltr\" style=\"list-style-type: disc; font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre; margin-left: 59.35pt;\" aria-level=\"1\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\" role=\"presentation\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Governments around the world are not yet fully clear on how to deal with cryptocurrencies, so regulatory changes and a crackdown can affect the market in unpredictable ways. (Rosen, 2023)</span></span></span></p></li></ul></span>\n\n</div>',1,0,70,'2025-09-12 06:24:28','2025-10-08 09:57:20'),(196,30,'What are cryptocurrencies and why is the world paying attention to them?','<div class=\"prose prose-lg max-w-none space-y-4\">\n\n<span id=\"docs-internal-guid-ccd635a2-7fff-223d-d22b-b6bd934a62c8\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:0pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Cyber currencies are digital assets that rely on an encrypted network to execute, verify, and record transactions, independent of a central authority such as a government or bank.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:0pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Key messages:</span></span></span><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\"> Cryptocurrencies were developed as an alternative to the dollar and can be an attractive investment due to their functions. Blockchain, the underlying technology that powers cryptocurrencies, is seen as a technological disruptor. Much like dot-com investing in the 1990s, crypto can be promising, but there will likely be winners and losers. This is a complicated concept, so let\'s break it down:</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:0pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Cryptocurrencies (or \"crypto\" for short) are decentralized currencies, meaning they are neither issued nor managed by a central bank. Some cryptocurrencies are issued by their developers, while others are generated by their respective network algorithms. Cryptocurrencies are digital assets—they don\'t have a tangible form. Cryptocurrencies exist and function on a public ledger, the blockchain, which records all crypto transactions. Blockchain encryption is designed to make all transactions immutable and secure from tampering, counterfeiting, and other forms of fraudulent transactions.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Is cryptocurrency a type of money?</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Although cryptocurrencies are defined as a \"digital currency\"—meaning that they are a type of money—most businesses and consumers have not embraced them as a common medium of exchange. In other words, most stores do not accept cryptocurrencies as payment. Bitcoin may be an exception, as some companies have accepted it as payment for goods and services. So, if cryptocurrencies are not a common form of money, why do people buy them? It is an alternative asset class. Although some crypto investors hope that cryptocurrencies will one day be adopted as a form of money, most see cryptocurrencies as an alternative form of investment that can increase in value. It is a way to invest in blockchain technology. Some people buy cryptocurrencies to invest indirectly in the underlying blockchain.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">What is a blockchain?</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">A blockchain is an encrypted public ledger through which digital assets can be transferred, recorded, and stored. It is essentially a decentralized network, also known as distributed ledger technology (DLT). This means that there is no single authority that serves as a regulator or intermediary for the transactions that take place on the network. Instead, the computers involved in the network are tasked with verifying and facilitating every \"block\" (i.e., every entry or transaction) within the chain. In some cases, all computers work together to verify and enable each block action. In other cases, a group of computers is randomly selected. This makes blockchain transactions secure and nearly impossible to alter. Tens of thousands of computers need to verify a single transaction or entry. If there is a discrepancy between the computers, the transaction will be declared invalid. This verification process is also what can make blockchain transactions slow and energy inefficient. Many computers around the world work to verify every single transaction.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Why is blockchain encrypted?</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Blockchain uses encryption to protect sensitive data from those who are not allowed to receive it. For example, the public can see that a transaction has taken place or that information has been recorded. However, you cannot see the identity of the people involved in the transaction or, in certain cases, the content of the transaction.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Why is blockchain considered a technological disruptor?</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:5pt;padding:5pt 0pt 0pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Blockchain\'s ability to permanently record and store transaction records and information in a highly secure manner makes it an attractive technology for many businesses and governments. Here is a limited list of potential use cases for blockchain:</span></span></span></p><ul style=\"padding-inline-start: 48px;\"><li dir=\"ltr\" style=\"list-style-type: disc; font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre; margin-left: 59.35pt;\" aria-level=\"1\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:0pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\" role=\"presentation\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">National and international payments</span></span></span></p></li><li dir=\"ltr\" style=\"list-style-type: disc; font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre; margin-left: 59.35pt;\" aria-level=\"1\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:0pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\" role=\"presentation\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Contracts</span></span></span></p></li><li dir=\"ltr\" style=\"list-style-type: disc; font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre; margin-left: 59.35pt;\" aria-level=\"1\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:0pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\" role=\"presentation\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Healthcare Files</span></span></span></p></li><li dir=\"ltr\" style=\"list-style-type: disc; font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre; margin-left: 59.35pt;\" aria-level=\"1\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:0pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\" role=\"presentation\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Real estate</span></span></span></p></li><li dir=\"ltr\" style=\"list-style-type: disc; font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre; margin-left: 59.35pt;\" aria-level=\"1\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:0pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\" role=\"presentation\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Energy Transactions</span></span></span></p></li><li dir=\"ltr\" style=\"list-style-type: disc; font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre; margin-left: 59.35pt;\" aria-level=\"1\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:0pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\" role=\"presentation\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Supply chain management</span></span></span></p></li><li dir=\"ltr\" style=\"list-style-type: disc; font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre; margin-left: 59.35pt;\" aria-level=\"1\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:0pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\" role=\"presentation\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Digital art transactions (see non-fungible tokens or NFTs)</span></span></span></p></li><li dir=\"ltr\" style=\"list-style-type: disc; font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre; margin-left: 59.35pt;\" aria-level=\"1\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\" role=\"presentation\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Election Votes</span></span></span></p></li></ul><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:0pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">In addition, the blockchain is an open-source network. This means that developers can work independently on improving or renewing the features. The more efficient a blockchain ecosystem becomes, the easier it is for businesses and governments to adopt it as part of their regular operations.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">What are the biggest cryptocurrencies?</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Among the over 18,000 cryptocurrencies in existence, Bitcoin and Ethereum are the two largest cryptocurrencies by market capitalization. Bitcoin, the original and largest cryptocurrency, was developed in 2009 as an alternative investment. It was intended to be an alternative to the US dollar and other fiat currencies. Although some providers accept Bitcoin as a form of payment, most investors consider it a speculative investment. By market capitalization, Ethereum is the second-largest cryptocurrency. In contrast to Bitcoin, Ethereum was not only designed as an alternative investment. Instead, it was developed as an innovative ledger technology that helps companies securely transport and store data and develop new programs and applications. In a nutshell, Ethereum is a massive digital ecosystem that can be used to transport, store, and even create digital information and computer applications.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">What is to be achieved with cryptocurrencies?</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Some cryptocurrencies, such as Bitcoin and Tether, are designed to serve a monetary function. Others, such as Dogecoin and Shiba Inu Coin, were developed as novelties, the value of which depends on popularity and trade. Many, if not most, cryptocurrencies are designed to solve challenges within the blockchain ecosystem, such as transfer speed, scalability, security, energy efficiency, and cost-effectiveness.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">How can I invest in cryptocurrencies?</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">You can buy cryptocurrencies through a cryptocurrency exchange or financial institution that can broker cryptocurrency transactions. Once you\'ve purchased cryptocurrency, you can secure your crypto coins in a digital wallet, online wallet, or hardware wallet.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">What are the risks of investing in cryptocurrencies?</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:5pt;padding:5pt 0pt 0pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Here are some of the most common risks when investing in cryptocurrencies:</span></span></span></p><ul style=\"padding-inline-start: 48px;\"><li dir=\"ltr\" style=\"list-style-type: disc; font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre; margin-left: 59.35pt;\" aria-level=\"1\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:0pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\" role=\"presentation\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">There is a risk of volatility with some cryptocurrencies. Crypto prices often experience extreme fluctuations under certain economic or market conditions.</span></span></span></p></li><li dir=\"ltr\" style=\"list-style-type: disc; font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre; margin-left: 59.35pt;\" aria-level=\"1\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:0pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\" role=\"presentation\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Liquidity risk: Some cryptocurrencies are traded with low volume and can therefore be easily manipulated by buyers with large capital resources or sellers who have a large stake in a particular currency.</span></span></span></p></li><li dir=\"ltr\" style=\"list-style-type: disc; font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre; margin-left: 59.35pt;\" aria-level=\"1\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:0pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\" role=\"presentation\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Cybersecurity risk: Your cryptocurrency can be stolen if a bad actor has access to your crypto wallet\'s private key.</span></span></span></p></li><li dir=\"ltr\" style=\"list-style-type: disc; font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre; margin-left: 59.35pt;\" aria-level=\"1\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:0pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\" role=\"presentation\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Overnight risk: Because cryptocurrencies are traded around the clock, your holdings are exposed to unfavorable fluctuations overnight.</span></span></span></p></li><li dir=\"ltr\" style=\"list-style-type: disc; font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre; margin-left: 59.35pt;\" aria-level=\"1\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\" role=\"presentation\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Risk of disappearance: There are factors that have caused certain crypto coins to disappear; these cases are rare and limited to certain coins.</span></span></span></p></li></ul><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Although the original idea behind cryptocurrencies was to create an alternative monetary value, many investors do not buy cryptocurrencies as money but as an alternative asset or as a way to invest in the underlying blockchain technology. Cryptocurrencies are an emerging field, not unlike the tech sector in the 1990s. There are many brilliant ideas in the crypto world, but not every blockchain innovation will make its way into the mainstream. So, if you\'re planning to invest in cryptocurrency, you should proceed with a healthy dose of caution. (Montevirgen, 2023)</span></span></span></p></span>\n\n</div>',1,0,70,'2025-09-12 06:25:06','2025-10-08 09:57:39'),(197,30,' Cryptocurrency Market','<div class=\"prose prose-lg max-w-none space-y-4\">\n\n\n<span id=\"docs-internal-guid-d3b575cf-7fff-9a8d-e4cc-14c1411e4524\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\">The global cryptocurrency market was valued at USD 4.67 billion in 2022 and is expected to grow at a compound annual growth rate (CAGR) of 12.5% from 2023 to 2030. The global cryptocurrency market cap was estimated to be over $800 billion in 2022. Cryptocurrency, also known as virtual currency, exists digitally and has no regulatory body. Cryptocurrencies use distributed ledger technology such as blockchain to validate transactions. The increasing adoption of distributed ledger technology is expected to drive the growth of the cryptocurrency market during the forecast period. Moreover, the increasing use of cryptocurrencies for cross-border remittances is expected to drive market expansion as consumer fees and exchange fees decrease.</span></span></span></span></span><div><font color=\"#000000\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><br></span></font><div><font color=\"#000000\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><img src=\"https://ptgr-bucket.s3.us-east-1.amazonaws.com/chapter3/7.jpg\" alt=\"Image\" class=\"max-w-full h-auto rounded-lg my-4 cursor-move hover:ring-2 hover:ring-primary/50 transition-all\" referrerpolicy=\"no-referrer\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto;\"><br></span></font><div><span id=\"docs-internal-guid-01147fc0-7fff-db7d-a765-47014cf82f08\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 14px;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\">Figure 1: Cryptocurrency market in the USA from 2020 to 2030</span></span></span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 14px;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\">(Source: grandviewresearch.com)</span></span></span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\">Recent developments in the field of artificial intelligence (AI) are expected to have a major impact on the cryptocurrency market. The rising popularity of AI-based cryptocurrency platforms has inspired several companies to focus on developing AI technology. For example, in August 2021, researchers at Los Alamos National Laboratory developed an artificial intelligence program to identify illegal cryptocurrency miners who use research computers to mine cryptocurrencies. In addition, the rise of cryptocurrencies as a decentralized asset class has attracted investment from private risk companies in the market. For example, in 2021, the US cryptocurrency market recorded investments of USD 6.1 billion in 106 transactions. The growth is also supported by the increasing demand for cryptocurrencies as an alternative means of payment in emerging markets such as Iran, Argentina, and Zimbabwe, which are plagued by devalued currencies. One of the key elements influencing the growth of digital currency is the growing number of businesses that now accept cryptocurrencies as an official payment method. Also, the adoption of digital currencies by major companies such as Tesla Inc. and MasterCard Inc. is expected to boost the growth of the industry. For example, in November 2021, financial services company MasterCard Inc. allowed its network partners to allow their customers to buy, trade, and hold digital currencies through a digital wallet. The company also offered digital currencies as a reward for customers who participated in loyalty programs. Government agencies around the world are focused on drafting laws to regulate cryptocurrencies. The European Union has developed the Markets in Crypto Assets Act (MiCA) to create a legal framework for the crypto asset market. In addition, in February 2022, the Indian government took a step towards legalizing cryptocurrencies by announcing a 30.0% tax on all revenue from the transfer of digital currencies. Cryptocurrencies like Bitcoin have gained traction in countries with high inflation, including Argentina, Zimbabwe, and Brazil, as their local currency loses value. Although Bitcoin is a cutting-edge financial technology, its growth is expected to be slowed by the lack of regulations and a uniform standard for digital currency exchanges. Regulators around the world are concerned about the use of cryptocurrencies for illicit purposes, which is a significant obstacle to the expansion of the market. However, law enforcement agencies are scrambling to seize illegally acquired cryptocurrencies, which bodes well for the growth of the market. For example, in November 2021, the IRS Criminal Investigation seized cryptocurrencies worth around $3.5 billion.</span></span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\">COVID-19—Impact Analysis</span></span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\">The COVID-19 pandemic has brought global economic activity to a standstill, although it is expected to have a positive impact on the cryptocurrency market. The impact of the COVID-19 outbreak on the global economy has increased uncertainty, which has drawn attention to cryptocurrencies such as Ethereum, Bitcoin, and others. Banks began creating digital currencies powered by blockchain to enable business-to-business (B2B) crypto payments. In October 2020, the first central bank digital currency (CBDC) was launched by the Central Bank of the Bahamas under the name Sand Dollar. This launch of the CBDC aimed to improve financial inclusion and increase safety.</span></span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\">Hardware Insights</span></span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\">The hardware segment dominated the market in 2022, accounting for more than 82.0% of global revenue. Factors such as the higher processing speed and lower power consumption of cryptocurrency miners are expected to drive the growth of the hardware segment. Changing consumer expectations have led many companies to develop hardware for Bitcoin mining. Mining equipment development requires hardware such as GPUs that are fast, effective, and stable. These factors will provide the industry with considerable growth prospects during the forecast period. The software segment is expected to expand at the highest CAGR during the forecast period. The growth can be attributed to the increasing demand for software for cryptocurrency exchange and mining. The development of software to manage the vast amounts of data generated by cryptocurrencies is expected to support the growth of the segment. Moreover, the growing demand for cryptocurrencies across the globe has increased the demand for crypto wallets and crypto exchange software, which is expected to boost the growth of the segment. The application-specific integrated circuits segment dominated the market in 2022, accounting for more than 43.0% of global sales. Application-specific integrated circuits (ASICs) are electronic circuits designed specifically for mining virtual currencies such as Bitcoin, Litecoin, and Ethereum. Several companies are using ASIC miners for cryptocurrency mining as the need for low-energy devices for cryptocurrency mining grows. In addition, ASICs require less maintenance and enable efficient use of energy, which is expected to support the growth of the segment. The graphics processing unit (GPU) segment is expected to grow at a promising CAGR during the forecast period. This growth is due to the increasing demand for GPUs for crypto mining, as they offer high processing speed and consume less power. Several crypto miners are encouraged to consider GPUs for crypto mining. According to Jon Peddie Research Inc., a provider of statistics and trends in the computer industry, 25.0% of GPUs were sold to crypto miners in the first quarter of 2021.</span></span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\">Software Insights</span></span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\">The stock exchange software segment dominated the market in 2022, accounting for more than 32.0% of global revenue. The use of exchange software is essential for conducting cryptocurrency transactions because it facilitates the conversion of cryptocurrency coins to fiat currencies and vice versa. The increasing development of crypto exchange software that enables seamless cryptocurrency trading is expected to boost the growth of the segment. For example, in March 2021, Openware Inc., a fintech software company, launched a crypto exchange software platform with Exchange Liquidity Network (ELN) called OpenDAX3. It aims to offer better exchange services to facilitate the trading of cryptocurrencies, assets, and security tokens. The wallet segment is expected to grow at a higher CAGR during the forecast period. The growth of the segment can be attributed to the increasing use of cryptocurrency wallets, as they provide consumers with online tools to securely manage and store blockchain assets and cryptocurrencies. The ability to store and exchange various cryptocurrencies such as Bitcoin, Litecoin, and Ethereum is made possible by various multi-asset cryptocurrency wallets. In addition, several companies that offer cryptocurrency wallet services are focusing on developing crypto wallets that allow users to send or receive cryptocurrencies through mobile applications. For example, Robinhood, a cryptocurrency wallet provider, unveiled its upcoming beta version of cryptocurrency wallets in January 2022. The wallet is designed to allow its customers to trade, send, and receive cryptocurrencies through the Robinhoodapplication.</span></span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:0pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><br></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\">Process Insights</span></span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><br></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\">The mining segment dominated the market in 2022, accounting for more than 74.0% of global revenue. Blockchain transactions are validated and verified as part of the mining process. New coins are inserted into the current supply chain through mining activities. In order to provide a better mining experience for cryptocurrency miners, many companies around the world are focusing on implementing and building crypto miner farms. These crypto mining farms are in an excellent position to operate their mining activities because they draw electricity from the public grid. For example, in August 2020, Enegix, a mining facility operator, announced the opening of its 180-megawatt Bitcoin mining facility in Kazakhstan, which can accommodate up to 50,000 mining rigs. The transaction segment is expected to grow at the fastest CAGR during the forecast period. The need for high-performance transactional services in businesses is increasing due to the rapid development of technological advancements such as digitalization and automation. As a result, many players worldwide are focusing on the development of Wallet as a Service (WaaS) to provide businesses with secure digital wallets that allow them to conduct their transactions effectively to meet the demand for high-performing transactions. For example, in November 2021, Crypto APIs, a blockchain development company, launched Wallet as a Service (WaaS) in partnership with Sepior, a fintech company. The WaaS is designed to provide small and medium-sized businesses, institutions, and businesses with a secure and reliable multi-currency digital wallet to conduct cryptographic transactions. The Bitcoin segment dominated the market in 2022, accounting for more than 36.0% of global sales. This segment is expected to develop the fastest during the forecast period. Bitcoin is a virtual currency that works as a decentralized alternative payment method. The emergence of Bitcoin exchange-traded funds (ETFs) is expected to drive up the volume of global Bitcoin trading during the forecast period. For example, in May 2022, the Purpose Bitcoin ETF in Canada saw the largest inflow of $207 million. The Ethereum segment is expected to grow significantly during the projection horizon. Ethereum is an open software platform that allows users to develop a range of decentralized applications. Due to the proliferation of new projects using the Ethereum network, network traffic has increased significantly, which is directly related to the expansion of the Ethereum market. Smart contracts were initially used for algorithm-based financial transactions with Ethereum. In the blockchain network, smart contracts are written instructions for conducting transactions. As Ethereum upgrades to Ethereum 2.0, which requires less energy and is less costly to mine, the first-mover advantage is expected to support the cryptocurrency\'s growth during the forecast period. The trading segment dominated the market in 2022, accounting for more than 28.0% of global sales. Cryptocurrency trading allows users to buy, sell, audit, and obtain custodial addresses. Several browser technology providers are focusing on forming alliances with blockchain technology companies to enable their customers to easily trade cryptocurrencies. For example, in March 2020, Brave Software, Inc., a browser technology company, partnered with Binance, a blockchain technology company, to enable its users to seamlessly trade cryptocurrency assets through Binance. Such factors bode well for the growth of the segment.</span></span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><br></span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><img src=\"https://ptgr-bucket.s3.us-east-1.amazonaws.com/chapter3/8.jpg\" alt=\"Image\" class=\"max-w-full h-auto rounded-lg my-4 cursor-move hover:ring-2 hover:ring-primary/50 transition-all\" referrerpolicy=\"no-referrer\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto;\"><br></span></span></span></p><div><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span id=\"docs-internal-guid-fe1369a3-7fff-2394-128d-3a54de8bf0bd\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 8pt; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 14px;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\">Figure 2: Global cryptocurrency market by industry</span></span></span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 8pt; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 14px;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\">(Source: grandviewresearch.com)</span></span></span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\">The Retail &amp; E-Commerce segment is expected to expand at a promising CAGR during the forecast period. The rising number of retail and e-commerce businesses accepting cryptocurrencies as official tender is one of the major factors driving the segment\'s growth. Several cryptocurrency payment processors are entering into strategic partnerships with e-commerce companies to increase the adoption of digital currencies for payments. For example, in May 2020, Coin Payments, a cryptocurrency payment processor, entered into a strategic partnership with Shopify to offer a cryptocurrency payment platform to all Shopify merchants.</span></span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\">Regional insights</span></span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\">Asia-Pacific dominated the cryptocurrency market in 2022, accounting for over 30.0% of global sales. This dominance is due to the adoption of cryptocurrencies in countries such as South Korea, Japan, and India. Also, the presence of cryptocurrency mining companies such as Ebang International Holdings, Inc, Bitmain Technologies Limited, Canaan, Inc., and others is expected to support the market growth in the region.</span></span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><br></span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><img src=\"https://ptgr-bucket.s3.us-east-1.amazonaws.com/chapter3/9.jpg\" alt=\"Image\" class=\"max-w-full h-auto rounded-lg my-4 cursor-move hover:ring-2 hover:ring-primary/50 transition-all\" referrerpolicy=\"no-referrer\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto;\"><br></span></span></span></p><div><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline;\"><span id=\"docs-internal-guid-a70cb81e-7fff-ca00-edc3-324b0a97af71\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 8pt; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 14px;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\">Figure 3: Cryptocurrency market—trends by region</span></span></span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 8pt; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 14px;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\">(Source: grandviewresearch.com)</span></span></span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\">In addition, leading players in the region are partnering to address the competitive market. For example, in February 2020, Canaan Inc., a China-based manufacturer of crypto mining hardware, partnered with Northern Data AG, a blockchain infrastructure company, to pool its technological and operational resources. The North American market is expected to grow significantly during the forecast period. The use of cryptocurrencies in NFTs in the region and the rising adoption of cryptocurrencies as a form of store of value are driving the expansion of the regional market. In addition, the region is investing heavily in companies that are developing blockchain technology and state-of-the-art cryptocurrency mining system solutions that have higher hash rates and better energy efficiency. In February 2022, Intel Corporation announced that it would contribute to the development of blockchain technology with a roadmap for energy-efficient accelerators. The company stated that its blockchain accelerator chip will be launched later this year. Argo Blockchain, Block &amp; Grid Infrastructure is one of the first customers for this upcoming product.</span></span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\">Insights into the most important companies and market shares</span></span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:5pt;padding:5pt 0pt 0pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\">The cryptocurrency market can be described as a moderately fragmented market, characterized by the presence of prominent players. Some of the major providers in the cryptocurrency market include NVIDIA Corporation, Advanced Micro Devices, Inc., Intel Corporation, Binance, and Xilinx, Inc. The market leaders drive competition in the market by adopting various strategies aimed at long-term maintenance, thus making it difficult for new players to enter the market. Strategies pursued by these companies include product innovation, a strong focus on R&amp;D activities, geographic expansions, strategic partnership agreements, and joint ventures. Key players are looking for strategic collaborations and partnerships to expand their offerings. For example, in November 2021, Ripple partnered with the Republic of Palau to explore the country\'s first national digital currency and its potential uses with the XRP ledger. The goal of this partnership was to develop cross-border payments and a USD-backed digital currency for Palau. Strategic partnerships and collaborations enable companies to develop innovative products and increase their sales in different regions. Some of the leading players in the global cryptocurrency market include:</span></span></span></span></p><ul style=\"padding-inline-start: 48px;\"><li dir=\"ltr\" style=\"list-style-type: disc; font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: nowrap; margin-left: 59.35pt;\" aria-level=\"1\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:0pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\" role=\"presentation\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\">Advanced Micro Devices, Inc.</span></span></span></span></p></li><li dir=\"ltr\" style=\"list-style-type: disc; font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: nowrap; margin-left: 59.35pt;\" aria-level=\"1\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:0pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\" role=\"presentation\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; f