--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\">Ripple</span></span></span></span></p></li><li dir=\"ltr\" style=\"list-style-type: disc; font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: nowrap; margin-left: 59.35pt;\" aria-level=\"1\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:0pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\" role=\"presentation\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\">Xapo Holdings Limited</span></span></span></span></p></li><li dir=\"ltr\" style=\"list-style-type: disc; font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: nowrap; margin-left: 59.35pt;\" aria-level=\"1\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\" role=\"presentation\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\">Xilinx, Inc.</span></span></span></span></p></li></ul><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\">Segmentation of the global cryptocurrency market</span></span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\">The report forecasts revenue growth at the global, regional, and country levels and provides an analysis of the latest industry trends in each of the sub-segments from 2017 to 2030. For this study, Grand View Research segmented the global cryptocurrency market by components, hardware, software, process, type, end-use, and region.</span></span></span></span></p><div><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline;\"><br></span><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\">\n</span>\n</div></span></span></div></span></span></div></span></div></div></div>\n</div>',1,0,70,'2025-09-12 06:27:18','2025-10-08 10:01:28'),(198,30,'Why invest in cryptocurrencies?','<div class=\"prose prose-lg max-w-none space-y-4\">\n\n<span id=\"docs-internal-guid-debc82e9-7fff-103b-b5a5-6a33d258ce6f\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:0pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Since Bitcoin, Ether, and the popular altcoins are part of such a volatile asset class, you may be wondering if it\'s worth the investment. Here are the reasons why people do it and why they stay. Everyone comes with their own agenda to crypto investing, whether fast and deliberate or slow over time. Compared to the heavyweights of day trading, who buy and sell during stock market hours (in comparison, there is no time limit to when you can make a profit in the decentralized financial world), investing in cryptocurrencies may seem like a no-brainer. And for younger consumers, cryptocurrencies even seem inevitable in our social media-driven future. \"A lot of people initially thought Dogecoin was a joke,\" Randi Hipper, an 18-year-old influencer known as Miss Teen Crypto, told CoinDesk in late 2021. \"Then people like [Tesla CEO] Elon Musk started tweeting, and it got serious. Then [entrepreneur] Mark Cuban got involved and began accepting DOGE as payment for the [Dallas Mavericks professional basketball team]. DOGE is now being taken seriously. \"But most crypto investors aren\'t just in it because of popular meme coins like Dogecoin. In fact, everyone from hobby traders to institutional investors like JPMorgan Chase is starting to see cryptocurrencies, Web3.0, and the metaverse as a trifecta of opportunity. Below, we\'ll share some common reasons why people get into crypto, but more importantly, why they stick with it.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Speculative investing</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Perhaps the most common reason people invest in cryptocurrencies is to speculate on the price in the hope that the asset will be worth more in the future. Everyone from Twitter thought leaders to institutional asset managers has made assumptions about when Bitcoin will hit the $100,000 mark. Speculatively adding cryptocurrencies to one\'s portfolio—between 1% and 10%—is a common way to gain exposure to volatile but potentially lucrative assets while protecting the rest of your money. Over the past decade, we\'ve seen the meteoric rise in the value of coins like Bitcoin and Ether (the native cryptocurrency of the Ethereum blockchain), as well as many others. Even though returns can\'t keep up with the growth of recent years, many investors are still optimistic that cryptocurrencies will become more and more important in our world. So, if you believe in the crypto industry, calculated speculative investments allow you to invest your money in the future you hope for. Just make sure you have a holistic view:\"If you need 90% of your assets for a comfortable retirement, we manage 90% and you take 10%,\" says Michelle Brownstein, a certified financial planner and senior vice president of the Private Client Group at Personal Capital. Brownstein often recommends that her clients protect the majority of their retirement portfolio. Most qualified fiduciaries would make a similar recommendation to help their clients avoid emotional decisions and taking on too much risk.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Investing in crypto as an inflation hedge</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Known as \"digital gold,\" Bitcoin was known for years as an inflation hedge thanks to its built-in scarcity. Bitcoin will stop minting once the number of coins in circulation reaches the hard cap of 21 million. Due to the rapid adoption by the masses, the value of bitcoin is expected to grow as demand increases. \"It\'s a hedge against inflation because there can\'t be more of it,\" Gemini\'s Dave Abner told CoinDesk in December. \"I think the biggest price driver is probably sentiment around inflation fears and portfolio diversification. It\'s not about it becoming a currency that people use in their daily lives. However, not all cryptocurrencies are treated equally. While scarcity is factored into the algorithm with Bitcoin, other cryptocurrencies are inflationary, i.e., a maximum amount will never be minted. Dogecoin, for example, falls into this category:10,000 new Doge tokens come into circulation every minute, and it will stay that way.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Invest in cryptocurrencies as a store of value</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">While volatile cryptocurrencies like Bitcoin are not the best holders of stable value, there is one type of coin that was designed for this very purpose: stablecoins. Stablecoins are crypto assets that are pegged to a fiat currency or other types of assets. With the help of crypto reward cards and digital DeFi wallets, they are increasingly being used as currency. People all over the world can instantly transfer money to friends, family, or even fellow gamers in a digital world like Splinterlands and Skyweaver. That being said, all cryptocurrencies have some value, even if it\'s hard to predict. Once you\'ve bought, sold, and/or traded your cryptocurrency on an exchange, you can either store it in a hot wallet or cold storage device until you\'re ready to use it. Even if it is offline, its value will continue to rise or fall depending on the state of the market.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">How to participate in decentralized banking</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:5pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">A big reason why people love cryptocurrencies is the decentralized ethos of permissionless peer-to-peer banking. Theoretically, when a new generation of consumers participates in a digital economy with algorithmic money, there is no need for central banking institutions, along with their fees and supervision. Smart contracts, in particular, make such a decentralized world possible. Your instructions are executed according to an encoded, open-source algorithm that anyone can see on the blockchain. However, the U.S. government and governments abroad are keeping a close eye on blockchain developments, leaving some wondering how long cryptocurrencies can actually remain decentralized. President Joe Biden has just announced that he will sign an executive order in March 2022 outlining the federal government\'s potential regulatory changes. Invest Now to Be Part of Future Technology and Infrastructure. Despite cryptocurrency\'s reputation for being the Wild, Wild West, most recognize the potential of blockchain as a new technological infrastructure that will one day have real-world value, just as laptops, iPhones, and cloud storage already do. \"If you think about the technology behind cryptocurrency—blockchain technology—there are a lot of other applications for it,\" says Michelle Brownstein, a certified financial planner and senior vice president of the Private Client Group at Personal Capital. \"Maybe it\'s the future of another industry that no one has thought of yet.\" Blockchain company CasperLabs, for example, was founded in 2018 to drive research and development based on Ethereum\'s smart contract technology. The Casper Network blockchain is based on the original specifications of Ethereum developers and is designed to scale crypto technology for widespread enterprise use, according to Medha Parlikar. \"My co-founders and I realized that there was no blockchain for business,\" Parlikar says. \"Companies and organizations have a duty to regulators, investors, and customers. The Casper protocol positions itself as a great stepping stone to help businesses implement and adopt blockchain. We believe that through this process, consumers will ultimately reap the benefits of this technology. According to Parlikar, blockchain technology has the potential to help businesses keep more secure records, protect customer privacy when handling sensitive information, and verify identity more seamlessly. Blockchain industry sources also claim that the technology can solve problems in various industries and help create better processes for applications such as access to elections, supply chain transparency, reservations, concert tickets and VIP memberships, identity protection, and more. Investors with the right risk appetite and a desire to support burgeoning projects may therefore want to get in on the action by buying tokens of a specific project, company, application, or ecosystem that they believe will be relevant in five to 10 years. But as with any single stock purchase—not to mention an asset as volatile as cryptocurrencies—financial planners like Brownstein still advise caution: \"You don\'t necessarily have to be invested in [crypto] today to achieve your financial goals,\" she says.</span></span></span></p><h3 dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:9pt 0pt 4pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 13pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Investing in Cryptos as a Passive Investment</span></span></span></h3><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:12pt;padding:8pt 0pt 0pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Learning how to buy and hold cryptocurrencies in so-called DeFi positions can lead to passive income. Through borrowing, staking, pooling, and trading, DeFi investors can earn interest by participating in the ecosystem. Typically, you\'ll need to \"lock\" your cryptocurrency for a certain period to earn regular interest on your positions, but DeFi strategies are popular with people who have the time and capital to get involved. Staking yields vary widely by blockchain and provider—for example, ETH staking yields commonly fall in the low single digits (≈2–4% in recent periods), while some altchains or DeFi staking opportunities have offered double-digit yields at materially higher risk. Always check current provider APYs before investing (Coinbase, Staking Rewards).</span></span></span></p><div><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><br></span>\n</div></span>\n</div>',1,0,70,'2025-09-12 06:28:22','2025-10-08 09:59:30'),(199,30,'Challenges','<div class=\"prose prose-lg max-w-none space-y-4\">\n\n<span id=\"docs-internal-guid-b7b1a5ac-7fff-f811-7bc4-99a565a0a009\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">The 7 biggest challenges for crypto traders and how to overcome them.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><br></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">While cryptocurrency trading has the potential for high returns, it also comes with numerous challenges. These obstacles come in various forms, from asset volatility and the complexity of compliance to security threats and the technical aspects of the trading process itself. This article aims to shed light on seven key challenges that crypto traders often face and offer practical solutions to help you navigate this turbulent terrain with more confidence and knowledge.</span></span></span></p><ul style=\"padding-inline-start: 48px;\"><li dir=\"ltr\" style=\"list-style-type: disc; font-size: 9pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre; margin-left: 59.35pt;\" aria-level=\"1\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\" role=\"presentation\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Asset volatility</span></span></span></p></li></ul><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-left: 95.35pt;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:0pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">The extreme price fluctuations in the cryptocurrency market, also known as asset volatility, can be a major challenge for traders. One moment you will reap high profits, the next you may have to accept significant losses. This volatility is often caused by market sentiment and external factors that are beyond the control of the individual trader. To mitigate the risks involved, traders can use a diversified investment strategy and spread their investments across multiple cryptocurrencies. In addition, it can help to keep up to date with market trends and global news with potential implications for cryptocurrencies to successfully manage volatility.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-left: 95.35pt;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">To overcome the challenge of volatile assets in the crypto industry and trading, you need to diversify your portfolio above all else. Instead of focusing all your investments on a single cryptocurrency, you should spread your capital across different cryptocurrencies. This strategy, often referred to as \"not putting all your eggs in one basket,\" can go a long way toward mitigating risk. The key here is to identify cryptocurrencies with potential and invest in them in a balanced way. Even if one cryptocurrency underperforms, the performance of the other can compensate for the loss.</span></span></span></p><ul style=\"padding-inline-start: 48px;\"><li dir=\"ltr\" style=\"list-style-type: disc; font-size: 9pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre; margin-left: 59.35pt;\" aria-level=\"1\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\" role=\"presentation\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Regulatory complexity</span></span></span></p></li></ul><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-left: 95.35pt;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:0pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">With increasing scrutiny from regulators around the world, crypto traders often have to navigate a maze of complex and ever-changing regulations. These complex regulations present a major challenge, as they can significantly affect the trading landscape and the profitability of your investments. Regulations in the cryptocurrency space are far from uniform. They differ significantly from one jurisdiction to another, and the lack of a global regulatory framework adds to the confusion. This lack of consistency can lead to uncertainty and make it difficult for traders to fully understand and comply with applicable laws. Traders could inadvertently find themselves on the wrong side of the law and face penalties or criminal charges.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-left: 95.35pt;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">To overcome the regulatory complexity of crypto trading, you should stay informed about the latest changes in regulations. Regularly follow official sources, centralized exchanges, or trusted news portals to learn about the broader cryptocurrency ecosystem. This helps with compliance and can lead to advantageous trading decisions. Consult with a financial advisor or legal expert who is knowledgeable about cryptocurrencies for valuable insights. He can show you the way through the legal maze and help you navigate within the law in pursuing your trading goals.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-left: 95.35pt;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><br></p><ul style=\"padding-inline-start: 48px;\"><li dir=\"ltr\" style=\"list-style-type: disc; font-size: 9pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre; margin-left: 59.35pt;\" aria-level=\"1\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\" role=\"presentation\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Security threats</span></span></span></p></li></ul><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-left: 95.35pt;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:0pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Security threats pose a major challenge in the digital world of crypto trading. Among these threats, hacking and phishing attacks are the most problematic and often result in significant financial losses for merchants. Hackers exploit vulnerabilities in unsecured digital wallets and exchange platforms, stealing cryptocurrencies without leaving a trace. Phishing attacks, on the other hand, trick merchants into revealing sensitive information such as passwords or private keys. These attacks are often very sophisticated, making it difficult for unsuspecting traders to distinguish legitimate communication from fraudulent ones.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-left: 95.35pt;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">To meet the challenge of security threats, a thorough understanding of cybersecurity practices is required. Traders need to protect their digital wallets and trading accounts with strong, unique passwords and consider enabling multi-factor authentication for trading pairs as an extra layer of security. It\'s also important to stay vigilant against phishing attempts. Always check the source before clicking on a link or downloading attachments. Do not share sensitive information unless you are absolutely sure that the source is legitimate.</span></span></span></p><ul style=\"padding-inline-start: 48px;\"><li dir=\"ltr\" style=\"list-style-type: disc; font-size: 9pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre; margin-left: 59.35pt;\" aria-level=\"1\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\" role=\"presentation\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Technical complexity</span></span></span></p></li></ul><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-left: 95.35pt;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:0pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Crypto trading requires an understanding of complex terms and technologies such as blockchain, smart contracts, etc. This technical complexity can be a significant challenge, especially for inexperienced traders. The cryptocurrency space is teeming with jargon and complicated concepts; understanding them is essential for successful trading. Blockchain, the underlying technology behind cryptocurrencies, for example, is a complex concept that includes decentralized ledgers and cryptography.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-left: 95.35pt;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">To manage the technical complexities of crypto trading, you need to constantly educate yourself and stay up-to-date with new technologies and concepts. Start by understanding the basics of blockchain technology, smart contracts, and how cryptocurrencies work. Expand your knowledge gradually, and don\'t hesitate to use resources such as online tutorials, webinars, or forums to learn. With time and persistent efforts, the technical aspects of crypto trading will be less daunting.</span></span></span></p><ul style=\"padding-inline-start: 48px;\"><li dir=\"ltr\" style=\"list-style-type: disc; font-size: 9pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre; margin-left: 59.35pt;\" aria-level=\"1\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\" role=\"presentation\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Lack of transparency</span></span></span></p></li></ul><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-left: 95.35pt;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:0pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">The lack of transparency in the world of crypto trading can be a significant obstacle for traders. Some crypto exchanges operate in a way that lacks transparency, making it difficult for traders to make informed decisions. The lack of clear and accurate information about trading fees, order books, and trading volume can lead to incorrect trading decisions and potential losses. Opaque procedures and undisclosed policies of certain exchanges can exacerbate this problem, leaving traders in the dark about important aspects of their trading activities. This lack of transparency can undermine trust in the trading platform and affect the overall trading experience.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-left: 95.35pt;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">To address the lack of transparency, you need to choose a trading platform that emphasizes openness and transparency in terms of ethical and transparent operations and ethical business practices. Before choosing an exchange, you should look for detailed information about the fee structure, trading volume, and business policy. Avoid exchanges that don\'t provide clear information or have a history of questionable practices.</span></span></span></p><ul style=\"padding-inline-start: 48px;\"><li dir=\"ltr\" style=\"list-style-type: disc; font-size: 9pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre; margin-left: 59.35pt;\" aria-level=\"1\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\" role=\"presentation\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">High transaction fees on cryptocurrency exchanges</span></span></span></p></li></ul><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-left: 95.35pt;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:0pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">High transaction fees on cryptocurrency exchanges deter many traders. These hefty fees, which are charged for every buy and sell order, can significantly reduce a trader\'s overall profit. Especially for those who engage in high-frequency trading, the cumulative costs can be significant. In addition, traders may not be aware of these fees in advance, as some cryptocurrency exchanges lack transparency on how they deal with them. This could lead to unexpected costs that further affect a trader\'s profitability. In scenarios where exchanges lack transparency regarding their fees, traders often have to deal with hidden fees. Some platforms do not clearly disclose their fee structures, putting traders in an awkward position. This lack of transparency not only leads to unplanned expenses but also undermines trust in the platform.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-left: 95.35pt;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">To overcome this challenge, merchants should look for platforms that are open about their transaction fees. Choose exchanges that disclose their fee structures clearly and in an easy-to-understand format.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-left: 95.35pt;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><br></p><ul style=\"padding-inline-start: 48px;\"><li dir=\"ltr\" style=\"list-style-type: disc; font-size: 9pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre; margin-left: 59.35pt;\" aria-level=\"1\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\" role=\"presentation\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Market manipulation</span></span></span></p></li></ul><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-left: 95.35pt;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:0pt;padding:0pt 0pt 5pt 0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Market manipulation is another major challenge for crypto traders. This problem often arises due to the lack of regulation in crypto trading. Manipulative practices such as pump-and-dump schemes, wash trading, or spoofing can lead to unexpected price fluctuations and create an unstable trading environment. For example, in a pump-and-dump scheme, a group of traders could artificially inflate the price of a cryptocurrency in order to then sell their holdings when the price reaches its peak, resulting in a sudden drop in price. These abrupt price changes can catch unsuspecting traders off guard and lead to significant financial losses. Wash trading and spoofing are other fraudulent practices in which traders fake high trading activity and volume or display false buy or sell orders to influence market sentiment. These manipulative tactics can mislead other traders and distort market prices and trading volumes.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-left: 95.35pt;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">To meet the challenge of market manipulation, you need to stay informed and vigilant. It is important to recognize the signs of market manipulation and be cautious when trading. Avoid making impulsive decisions due to sudden price changes or unusually high or low trading volumes.</span></span></span></p><div><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><br></span>\n</div></span>\n</div>',1,1,70,'2025-09-12 06:29:26','2025-10-08 09:59:57'),(200,31,'Beyond Banks and Brokers: All About Decentralized Finance (DeFi)','<div class=\"prose prose-lg max-w-none space-y-4\">\n\n<span id=\"docs-internal-guid-7bfb0840-7fff-0a94-e8c3-31674397b3c5\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Can you trust it with your assets?</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">If you can imagine sending money, making a payment, or buying a financial asset without enlisting the help of a bank, broker, or other official intermediary, then you\'ve grasped the nature of decentralized finance. Decentralized finance, or DeFi for short, enables peer-to-peer transfer, lending, and trading of assets without intermediaries such as banks or brokers.</span></span></span></p><br><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Key Points: Decentralized finance (DeFi) bypasses the traditional avenues for financial transactions.</span></span></span></p><ul style=\"padding-inline-start: 48px;\"><li dir=\"ltr\" style=\"list-style-type: disc; font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre; margin-left: 59.35pt;\" aria-level=\"1\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\" role=\"presentation\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">A blockchain-based peer-to-peer ecosystem could revolutionize banking as we know it.</span></span></span></p></li><li dir=\"ltr\" style=\"list-style-type: disc; font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre; margin-left: 59.35pt;\" aria-level=\"1\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\" role=\"presentation\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">As of Aug 2025, DeFi protocols hold ~$156B in TVL (DefiLlama). While more mature than in 2020–21, risks remain. The emergence of DeFi could be very significant. Not only does it hint at a new form of financial technology on the horizon, but it promises a whole new financial horizon.</span></span></span></p></li></ul><br><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">As a potential investor, you probably have a lot of questions:</span></span></span></p><ul style=\"padding-inline-start: 48px;\"><li dir=\"ltr\" style=\"list-style-type: disc; font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre; margin-left: 59.35pt;\" aria-level=\"1\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\" role=\"presentation\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">What is the purpose of DeFi?</span></span></span></p></li><li dir=\"ltr\" style=\"list-style-type: disc; font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre; margin-left: 59.35pt;\" aria-level=\"1\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\" role=\"presentation\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">How does it work?</span></span></span></p></li><li dir=\"ltr\" style=\"list-style-type: disc; font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre; margin-left: 59.35pt;\" aria-level=\"1\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\" role=\"presentation\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">How can you invest in DeFi?</span></span></span></p></li><li dir=\"ltr\" style=\"list-style-type: disc; font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre; margin-left: 59.35pt;\" aria-level=\"1\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\" role=\"presentation\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">What are the most important advantages, opportunities, and risks for users and investors?</span></span></span></p></li></ul><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">If you\'re considering getting started with DeFi, you\'ll need a fundamental insight into this emerging space.</span></span></span></p><br><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Why DeFi (or why bypass traditional finance)?</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Decentralized finance may not offer much new in terms of financial products. But if it catches on, it could revolutionize the way we exchange financial products. But why change a system that is already efficient and functionally safe—in other words, why change something that isn\'t broken? Banks and financial institutions can help you transfer money from one place to another, but the path is not direct. Often, there is a chain of third-party providers involved in a single transaction. Not only can this chain slow down a particular transaction, but each provider also charges fees for their services. And since you\'re relying on third-party services (all of which are subject to human error, technical glitches, hardware failures, and security breaches), none of them are 100% secure. Individuals and businesses are always looking for a faster, safer, and more cost-effective way to conduct peer-to-peer (P2P) financial transactions. What DeFi has to offer goes far beyond incremental improvement (as opposed to, say, the introduction of the ATM or direct deposit). It promises innovations that cannot be achieved with conventional systems and technologies.</span></span></span></p><br><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">How does DeFi work?</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">By building a financial system on a blockchain-based network and eliminating intermediaries, transactions can be more direct, service fees can be largely eliminated, and asset transfers and exchanges can be made virtually tamper-proof. Blockchains are digital ledgers that are shared and updated by all participating computers (the so-called nodes). All transactions entering a blockchain are verified by selected nodes participating in the network. Blocks are linked by cryptographic hashes, not encrypted. Transaction data is usually public on blockchains.Any attempt to change the contents of a block alerts all the computers on the network (which can run into the thousands). This makes a blockchain virtually unassailable and secure. Compared to today\'s financial system, even the most efficient, competitively priced, and secure banking processes cannot offer these benefits to the extent that a blockchain network can, according to blockchain proponents. DeFi can take it a step further. Because it uses the blockchain, individuals and businesses can trade other types of assets that are not accessible through traditional financial means, such as smart contracts and non-fungible tokens.</span></span></span></p><br><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">What are the key benefits and risks for DeFi users?</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Overall, DeFi gives users more control over their money. Financial assets can be transferred or purchased within seconds or minutes. Service fees would be largely eliminated, as there are no third-party companies to help with transactions. Your money would be converted into a \"fiat-backed stablecoin\" and made accessible via a digital wallet, so you wouldn\'t have to deposit money in a bank. And since bank accounts are no longer required, almost anyone with an internet connection can have access to the same financial goods and services. The biggest risk is that DeFi is unregulated. There is no support from the FDIC (or any other regulatory body) to protect your funds in the event that a major glitch, bug, or cyber hack causes your funds to be unavailable or disappear. Also, the technology is so new that there is no uniform or comprehensive method to determine whether any part of a DeFi system is functioning optimally or is free of fraud. In theory, every technological component in a DeFi ecosystem should work quickly, efficiently, and securely. In practice, however, this is still untested.</span></span></span></p><br><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">How can you invest in DeFi, and what are the risks?</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">The easiest and safest way would be to invest in shares of companies involved in the development of DeFi. However, many of these companies are new and operate in the cryptocurrency space, making them quite a bit more speculative and volatile than more established companies in mature industries. Investors can also stake cryptocurrencies to invest in a DeFi company\'s blockchain ecosystem. With a stake, crypto holders can support a coin\'s blockchain network by locking coins to validate new blocks for a transaction. If your stake is selected in the validation process, you can earn an income in the form of more cryptocurrency. A more advanced version of this type of investing is called yield farming, where you lend cryptocurrency to a DeFi platform or company in exchange for interest or additional cryptocurrency. Again, the biggest risk in the DeFi space is the lack of regulations to protect your money. This makes you vulnerable to fraud and theft. Since DeFi is an emerging industry, you take the risk of investing in a project that could fail. Also, cryptocurrency markets are highly volatile and complex, making it difficult to assess the market and industry. In addition, technological glitches, high energy consumption, hardware malfunctions, and even system maintenance and upgrades all contribute to DeFi\'s risk factors.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">In this regard, DeFi is similar to the bond market. Bonds with the highest risks offer higher yields to compensate for the additional risk. But if the issuer becomes insolvent, you can lose your money.</span></span></span></p><br><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">The Bottom Line</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">When it comes to emerging industries, early investments can often yield above-average returns. But it\'s important to understand the risks, which can be as big as or bigger than the potential rewards. Decentralized finance could one day turn banking as we know it upside down. But until that happens, the DeFi space will be characterized by uncertainty and speculation. We encourage our readers to proceed with caution.</span></span></span></p><div><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><br></span>\n</div></span>\n</div>',1,0,70,'2025-09-12 06:31:30','2025-10-08 10:02:39'),(201,31,'DeFi-ing the Rules: Five Opportunities and Five Risks of Decentralized Finance','<div class=\"prose prose-lg max-w-none space-y-4\">\n\n<span id=\"docs-internal-guid-d8aa29c1-7fff-ef16-b054-7e8eba38fab0\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">In the Star Wars universe, the Force is a mystical energy field created by living beings that permeates all things and holds the galaxy together. The ability to wield the Force gives the Jedi Knights their superhuman abilities and makes them peacekeepers of the galaxy. But how powerful would the Jedi be if every \"transaction\" involving the Force had to be handled through a centralized system that charges countless fees and is prone to delays? Unfortunately for us, the second scenario describes much of the traditional financial universe. Too much financial friction reduces transaction speed and increases costs. But the age of centralized finance may be coming to an end, and the age of decentralized finance (DeFi) may just begin, as Campbell Harvey explained in his talk \"Rethinking the Global Financial System\" at the CFA Institute\'s Alpha Summit GLOBAL last month. The Duke University finance professor, co-author of DeFi and the Future of Finance, and partner and senior advisor to Research Affiliates believes that DeFi can transform the financial system and unleash a wave of economic energy. For more than a century, Harvey believes that the traditional financial system has operated essentially according to the same model. It all depends on the same central banks, the same commercial banks, the same stock exchanges, the same insurance companies, and so on. Despite technological change and related developments, the basic framework has remained static and centralized. Recently, fintechs have caused some turmoil and helped reduce transaction costs. But fintechs rely on the same centralized financial architecture that sets limits to reducing these costs and increasing efficiency. \"In decentralized finance, there is no such limit,\" Harvey said. \"That\'s why the current fintech wave will only be short-lived.\" But what makes DeFi such an inescapable force of change? Will everything change for the better? And what new risks can arise from this?</span></span></span></p><br><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Connecting the financial galaxy</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Basically, DeFi uses peer-to-peer networks to conduct transactions without intermediary third parties. Digital assets, such as cryptocurrencies, take the form of \"smart contracts,\" which are self-executing algorithms based on blockchain technology. Tokenization is an important aspect of DeFi. Virtual and physical assets can be converted into \"tokens\" that act as a store of value and can be used in financial transactions. They also give the holder a voice in controlling a protocol or platform. Why will DeFi change the financial system? Because it can solve five inherent problems of the traditional financial system that Harvey sees: inefficiency, limited access, opacity, centralized control, and interoperability. Reducing inefficiencies means eliminating fees and middlemen. In the 19th century, a 3% fee was charged for a Western Union transfer, according to Harvey. More than a century later, even simple transactions—using a debit card, for example—often come with significant fees. Buying a stock may seem quite simple, but actually gaining ownership requires an intermediary and can take a lot of time. With DeFi, the execution and settlement of a trade can be done simultaneously. Limited access to the financial system is a serious global problem. Removing these barriers could give billions of people access to the financial services they need. An estimated 1.7 billion people don\'t have banks; the obstacles for many in these groups are financial situation and limited access to financing. For example, excessive costs of capital—banks that restrict access to credit with lower interest rates and instead provide lines of credit at much higher interest rates — discourage many small businesses from pursuing projects that could spur economic growth. According to Harvey, DeFi can directly address the sources of financial friction. That DeFi could reduce opacity in the financial system may come as a surprise to skeptics. For example, in August 2021, U.S. Senator Elizabeth Warren wrote to Gary Gensler, chairman of the U.S. Securities and Exchange Commission (SEC), warning of the need to regulate crypto markets, describing DeFi as \"extremely opaque.\" For Harvey, however, Warren has misunderstood the matter. \"What is opaque is the current financial system,\" he said. Because DeFi is based on open-source technology, there is more transparency, not less. For example, with a decentralized exchange, users can see the code, liquidity, and all other details. The traditional financial system, on the other hand, has numerous blind spots. \"When you go to a bank, you basically don\'t know how healthy that bank is,\" Harvey said. \"And you rely on our institutions like the FDIC to reduce your risk. But our institutions have a dubious track record at best, and I\'m not talking about going back to the 1930s. We can go back to the global financial crisis, where a lot of people had to deal with banks that went under.\" Centralized control and concentration are essential components of the current financial system. Harvey pointed to the \"market power\" of commercial banks as a prime example. \"This means that savings rates are lower than they should be, and loan rates are higher than they should be. Maybe people will be excluded,\" he said. \"And in decentralized finance, by definition, it\'s different. There is strong competition.\" \"There is no difference between the different actors in the decentralized space,\" he added. \"Everyone is equal.\" After all, interoperability is an inevitable structural problem in traditional finance: various obstacles prevent different platforms and systems from connecting to each other. If someone wants to open an account with an online trading platform, they may need to transfer money from a bank account. This process can take days before the new account is ready to trade. \"It\'s very different in decentralized finance,\" Harvey said. \"You have a wallet, you go to an exchange, you plug in your wallet, and you\'re good to go. This is indeed a feature of the so-called Web 3.0. So with Web 3.0, there is no username and password. You plug in your wallet, and you\'re good to go. They are ready to buy. They are willing to receive money. They are ready for use. And Web 3.0 is not possible without decentralized finance.\"</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">The Dark Side</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">So DeFi will defeat crushing centralization and inefficiency and save the financial galaxy, right? Not so fast. DeFi can also create new types of risks, as well as variations of old risks. Harvey has identified five in particular: </span></span></span><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">smart contract risk, oracle risk, custody risk, scaling risk, and regulatory risk.</span></span></span><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">&nbsp;</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Smart contract risk</span></span></span><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">: A smart contract is an automated, self-executing agreement between parties. It is an algorithm on a blockchain platform that enables transactions without centralized mechanisms or intermediaries. Because DeFi is open-source, smart contracts are more vulnerable to cybercriminals than traditional systems, which are protected by layers of security around a proprietary source. The nature of smart contracts makes them vulnerable to various vulnerabilities: logic errors, economic exploits (e.g., exploitation of pricing errors), flash loan attacks, and governance risks (for contracts with changing parameters). The more vulnerabilities are uncovered, the better the security becomes. \"But at the moment it\'s still very risky,\" Harvey concluded.&nbsp;</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Oracle risk</span></span></span><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">: Smart contracts depend on external information, such as a price feed from an exchange. The third-party services that make the connection are called blockchain oracles. If the connection is lost, certain steps required for a transaction cannot be completed, and the contract may fail.&nbsp;</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Scaling risk</span></span></span><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">: Current DeFi platforms use a consensus method with slow transaction speeds. Ethereum—in Harvey\'s words, \"the most important technology for decentralized finance\"—can process 15 transactions per second. Visa can process 65,000 per second. And the scaling problem is much worse with Bitcoin. \"With Bitcoin, you can only make transactions between people,\" he said. \"With the current version of Bitcoin, there is no way to make a smart contract. While some believe that DeFi will never reach the scale of traditional financial networks, Ethereum is already preparing to switch to a new and faster consensus method. Meanwhile, horizontal scaling and other novel approaches are being developed to reduce transaction costs. \"Scaling is a real problem today, and people are complaining about the rising transaction costs of the Ethereum blockchain,\" Harvey said. \"And that\'s true—they\'re high—but all of these initiatives suggest that these costs will drop drastically in the future.&nbsp;</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Custody risk</span></span></span><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">: Self-custody in the form of a digital wallet protects access to crypto assets through a user\'s private key. But a misplaced or stolen key can have catastrophic consequences. \"If you lose your private key, you also lose your cryptocurrency,\" Harvey said. \"And the private key is a long random number, 256 bits.\" Horror stories about missing private keys have made frightening headlines and suffered major financial losses. To alleviate these concerns, third-party services have stepped in to protect private keys and help users secure their access.&nbsp;</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Regulatory risk</span></span></span><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">: \"Regulators are scrambling today over what to do with this new space,\" Harvey said. \"I think they understand the challenge.\" But it will be difficult to find the right regulatory balance. \"If you want to eliminate all risks and put in place very strict regulations, it means that the technology will be moved abroad,\" he explained. \"This technology is not a national technology. It is a global technology. So you can do it just as well from New York as you can from the Cayman Islands.\" In short, regulators face a dilemma. \"If you\'re too strict, you\'re killing innovation,\" Harvey said. \"If you\'re too lenient, it\'s the Wild West and people are taken advantage of.”</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Building a new financial city</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Many observers, including Harvey, believe that DeFi offers an opportunity to gain a first-mover advantage in an emerging new order. \"And these opportunities are rare in history,\" he said. \"This is not a renovation of our current financial system. It\'s a rebuilding from the ground up, and we\'re very early, maybe at 1%, where we see the scaffolding of a new city.\" But ironically, this new financial system may resemble a much older form of financing: barter. \"The barter system was interrupted when money was introduced, and the market exchange became much more efficient,\" Harvey said. \"The decentralized finance system is redefining money so that we can convert any asset into tokens. For example, a digital wallet may have a token pegged to the U.S. dollar, a token pegged to gold, and a token pegged to Apple stock. \"And if I want to pay for something, I choose it,\" he explained. \"Maybe I buy groceries and decide to pay with a portion of my Apple shares. And maybe the dealer doesn\'t want that. He wants something different. That\'s not a problem. The Apple shares are seamlessly sent to a decentralized exchange and exchanged for the product desired by the grocer. This is bartering in a much more efficient way.\" And if anyone believes that exiting DeFi is a realistic option, they should think again. \"Some of the companies in your portfolio of traditional finance have a bullseye painted on them,\" he said. \"And if [DeFi] advances at the same rate as it has in recent years, it could lead to a significant drop in the value of certain names in your portfolio. So think about it. Even if you are not there, you are exposed in a negative way.\" So may the decentralized power be with us (Mitchell, 2022).</span></span></span></p><div><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><br></span>\n</div></span>\n</div>',1,0,70,'2025-09-12 06:32:36','2025-10-08 10:03:00'),(202,31,'Understanding DeFi','<div class=\"prose prose-lg max-w-none space-y-4\">\n\n<span id=\"docs-internal-guid-85deef3d-7fff-b440-74dd-f18f529d8146\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\">Pros and cons of decentralized finance (DeFi)</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\">Before we start discussing the pros and cons of DeFi, it\'s important to have a brief understanding of DeFi. The financial services sector has promoted many of the notable use cases for blockchain technology. Blockchain offers crucial opportunities to transform conventional finance through online payments and the storage and trading of virtual assets. With the advent of DeFi, the applications of blockchain in finance have gained traction. The term \"DeFi\" is actually an acronym for \"decentralized finance.\" It essentially refers to a set of blockchain-based applications that provide peer-to-peer alternatives to traditional financial services and institutions. DeFi\'s notoriety has increased a lot recently, which has led to it attracting a significant amount of capital. So, what exactly should make you want to find out the pros and cons of decentralized finance?</span></span></span></p><br><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\">Why should you focus on the pros and cons of decentralized finance (DeFi)?</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\">The market demand for DeFi is growing day by day. According to estimates by DeFi Pulse, a renowned data aggregator for decentralized finance, the total value in DeFi protocols currently stands at almost $11.5 billion. Just a year ago, the total value of DeFi protocols was only about $1 billion, suggesting promising growth. Even though the monetary value associated with DeFi cannot compete with conventional financial markets, but DeFi has grown strongly. In just one year, DeFi has gained incredible popularity. The faster growth rate also reflects the strength of DeFi\'s value proposition. Decentralized finance has a profound impact on the way banks operate, leading to shifts in the overall financial ecosystem. Currently, there are many conflicting views on decentralized finance, and for good reason. Some see DeFi as a revolution, others as an opportunity, and still others see it as a scam.Therefore, a neutral impression of the world of DeFi could give a clear idea of its role in the financial system as a whole. Let\'s find out more about the pros and cons of decentralized finance in the discussion below. With a comprehensive overview of the pros and cons of DeFi, readers could see the true potential of DeFi.</span></span></span></p><br><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\">Advantages of DeFi</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\">The promising aspects related to the DeFi discussion point to an effective observation of the pros and cons of DeFi. The DeFi movement aims to introduce various benefits for customers and investors. Notable benefits of DeFi include the elimination of middlemen and centralized control.In addition, access to financial markets for institutional investors is to be improved. At the same time, DeFi would also work on creating new investment opportunities that can take the concept to a new level. To achieve the benefits, DeFi solutions rely largely on the fundamental properties of blockchain technology.</span></span></span></p><ul style=\"padding-inline-start: 48px;\"><li dir=\"ltr\" style=\"list-style-type: disc; font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre; margin-left: 59.35pt;\" aria-level=\"1\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\" role=\"presentation\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\">Scalability:</span></span></span></p></li></ul><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-left: 95.35pt;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\">DeFi projects are undoubtedly suitable for enabling the financial inclusion of a broader population. However, DeFi projects face tremendous difficulties when it comes to the scalability of the host blockchain from various points of view. First of all, DeFi transactions take incredibly long periods of time to confirm.&nbsp;At the same time, transactions on DeFi protocols can become very expensive during the period of congestion. Ethereum, for example, could process almost 13 transactions per second at full capacity. In contrast, the centralized counterparts for DeFi could process thousands of transactions in the period in question.</span></span></span></p><ul style=\"padding-inline-start: 48px;\"><li dir=\"ltr\" style=\"list-style-type: disc; font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre; margin-left: 59.35pt;\" aria-level=\"1\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\" role=\"presentation\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\">Uncertainty:</span></span></span></p></li></ul><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-left: 95.35pt;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\">Concerns about uncertainty also play a role in the pros and cons of decentralized finance. In the event of instability of a blockchain hosting a DeFi project, the project could automatically inherit the instability of the host blockchain. Currently, the Ethereum blockchain is undergoing various changes. For example, the mistakes made in the transition from PoW consensus to the new Eth 2.0 PoS system can lead to risks.</span></span></span></p><ul style=\"padding-inline-start: 48px;\"><li dir=\"ltr\" style=\"list-style-type: disc; font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre; margin-left: 59.35pt;\" aria-level=\"1\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\" role=\"presentation\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\">Liquidity bottlenecks:</span></span></span></p></li></ul><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-left: 95.35pt;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\">Liquidity is also undoubtedly a critical factor in DeFi-based projects and blockchain protocols. As of October 2020, the total value tied up in DeFi projects is more than $12.5 billion. So, it is clear that the DeFi market is not as large as traditional financial systems. So, it can be difficult to put your trust in a sector that is not as large as the usual financial sector.</span></span></span></p><ul style=\"padding-inline-start: 48px;\"><li dir=\"ltr\" style=\"list-style-type: disc; font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre; margin-left: 59.35pt;\" aria-level=\"1\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\" role=\"presentation\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\">Shared responsibility:</span></span></span></p></li></ul><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-left: 95.35pt;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\">Among all the pros and cons of DeFi, the </span></span></span><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\">shared responsibility</span></span></span><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"> factor negatively impacts users. The DeFi projects do not take responsibility for your mistakes. Due to the lack of intermediaries, it is the user who has to take responsibility for the funds and assets. That\'s why the DeFi space needs tools that prevent human error and error. At the same time, however, they also offer the security of smart contract integration. The establishment of such functionalities ensures that intermediaries such as banks, which are usually responsible for verifying parties in a transaction, are eliminated. In addition, it also works for verifying the process related to lending and borrowing. As a result, DeFi allows for a faster and easier verification process for loans and loan applications. At the same time, DeFi also ensures the protection of counterparties during a transaction. DeFi applications for lending and borrowing would also enable the benefits of faster transaction processing with better accessibility. Currently, the most well-known DeFi applications can be found in the field of lending and borrowing. One of the most notable examples that shows the pros and cons of DeFi, albeit with a focus on the benefits, is Compound. It is a decentralized lending platform that allows lenders to provide crypto assets to a certain number of loan pools. The loan pools are available for other people to borrow, and lenders receive a share of the interest that borrowers pay back to the pool. The interest rate that a lender receives depends heavily on the contribution they make to the pool. In addition, the liquidity of the crypto assets also plays a crucial role in determining the interest rate on DeFi lending platforms.</span></span></span></p><ul style=\"padding-inline-start: 48px;\"><li dir=\"ltr\" style=\"list-style-type: disc; font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre; margin-left: 59.35pt;\" aria-level=\"1\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\" role=\"presentation\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\">Interest-earning applications</span></span></span></p></li></ul><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-left: 95.35pt;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\">The pros and cons of decentralized finance also shine a spotlight on the growing dominance of DeFi savings products. People could also use DeFi to effectively manage their savings. Users could start earning interest on balances they lock in lending protocols like Compound. Therefore, many DeFi savings applications have emerged recently. Such applications offer the ability to hook into different lending protocols to improve users\' ability to earn interest. At this point, it would make sense to mention \"yield farming.\" Yield farming is one of the positive aspects of the DeFi ecosystem, mainly because it shows how users move their idle crypto assets across various lending protocols to earn better returns.</span></span></span></p><br><ul style=\"padding-inline-start: 48px;\"><li dir=\"ltr\" style=\"list-style-type: disc; font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre; margin-left: 59.35pt;\" aria-level=\"1\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\" role=\"presentation\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\">Tokenization</span></span></span></p></li></ul><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-left: 95.35pt;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\">Any discussion of the pros and cons of DeFi without mentioning the benefits of tokenization is not complete. Tokenization is one of the notable topics that have recently emerged in the blockchain sphere. Ethereum enables robust smart contract capabilities, opening the way for crypto token issuance.Crypto tokens are basically digital assets that exist on a blockchain and have various functions and uses. Notable examples of tokens include utility tokens destined for a specific dApp, real estate tokens, or security tokens. Tokens are able to help you achieve various functions. Real estate tokens allow you to acquire fractional ownership of physical real estate. On the other hand, security tokens can also serve as digital stocks in certain applications. Most importantly, tokenization could also ensure better exposure to other assets, both physical and digital. Assets could include digital currencies, fiat currencies, oil, or gold. The crypto-synthetic assets have collateral in the form of tokens in Ethereum-based smart contracts. Interestingly, one of the largest synthetic asset platforms, Synthetix, currently has approximately $98 million in total value locked (TVL) as of August 2025. This represents a significant decrease from earlier levels, reflecting changes in user engagement, liquidity, and protocol activity.</span></span></span></p><br><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\">Disadvantages of decentralized finance</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\">Many of the prominent discussions surrounding decentralized finance in modern times are mainly focused on the benefits of DeFi. However, it is important to get a neutral impression of the pros and cons of DeFi in order to properly assess its potential. In fact, most of the problems and risks associated with a DeFi project are primarily related to the technologies they are related to. The challenges associated with blockchain are usually responsible for fueling the disadvantages of DeFi. Here are some of the critical setbacks you may experience when adopting DeFi.</span></span></span></p><ul style=\"padding-inline-start: 48px;\"><li dir=\"ltr\" style=\"list-style-type: disc; font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre; margin-left: 59.35pt;\" aria-level=\"1\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\" role=\"presentation\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\">Scalability</span></span></span></p></li></ul><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-left: 95.35pt;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\">DeFi projects are undoubtedly suitable for enabling the financial inclusion of a broader population. However, DeFi projects face tremendous difficulties when it comes to the scalability of the host blockchain from various points of view. First of all, DeFi transactions take incredibly long periods of time to confirm.At the same time, transactions on DeFi protocols can become very expensive during the period of congestion. Ethereum, for example, could process almost 13 transactions per second at full capacity. In contrast, the centralized counterparts for DeFi could process thousands of transactions in the period in question.</span></span></span></p><ul style=\"padding-inline-start: 48px;\"><li dir=\"ltr\" style=\"list-style-type: disc; font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre; margin-left: 59.35pt;\" aria-level=\"1\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\" role=\"presentation\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\">Uncertainty</span></span></span></p></li></ul><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-left: 95.35pt;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\">Concerns about uncertainty also play a role in the pros and cons of decentralized finance. In the event of instability of a blockchain hosting a DeFi project, the project could automatically inherit the instability of the host blockchain. Currently, the Ethereum blockchain is undergoing various changes. For example, the mistakes made in the transition from PoW consensus to the new Eth 2.0 PoS system can lead to risks.</span></span></span></p><br><ul style=\"padding-inline-start: 48px;\"><li dir=\"ltr\" style=\"list-style-type: disc; font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre; margin-left: 59.35pt;\" aria-level=\"1\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\" role=\"presentation\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\">Liquidity bottlenecks</span></span></span></p></li></ul><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-left: 95.35pt;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\">Liquidity is also undoubtedly a critical factor in DeFi-based projects and blockchain protocols. As of October 2020, the total value tied up in DeFi projects is more than $12.5 billion. So, it is clear that the DeFi market is not as large as traditional financial systems. So, it can be difficult to put your trust in a sector that is not as large as the usual financial sector.</span></span></span></p><br><ul style=\"padding-inline-start: 48px;\"><li dir=\"ltr\" style=\"list-style-type: disc; font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre; margin-left: 59.35pt;\" aria-level=\"1\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\" role=\"presentation\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\">Shared responsibility</span></span></span></p></li></ul><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-left: 95.35pt;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\">Among all the pros and cons of DeFi, the shared responsibility factor negatively impacts users. The DeFi projects do not take responsibility for your mistakes. Due to the lack of intermediaries, it is the user who has to take responsibility for the funds and assets. That\'s why the DeFi space needs tools that prevent human error and error.</span></span></span></p><br><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\">Conclusion:</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\">The pros and cons of decentralized finance have been the most valuable input for determining the value of DeFi today. One of the clear observations of the pros and cons of DeFi shows that the pros far outweigh the cons. Decentralized finance has emerged as a promising favorite for reshaping traditional standards for financial services. Most importantly, DeFi could promote the application of blockchain in the financial services sector. Given the benefits of transparency, immutability, and decentralization, DeFi has yet to face obstacles such as scalability. You can enroll in the DeFi training course and expand your knowledge of DeFi. Learn more about the pros and cons of DeFi to draw viable conclusions (Iredale, 2021).</span></span></span></p><div><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><br></span>\n</div></span>\n</div>',1,0,70,'2025-09-12 06:33:33','2025-10-08 10:03:23'),(203,31,'What is Decentralized Finance (DeFi)?','<div class=\"prose prose-lg max-w-none space-y-4\">\n\n\n<span id=\"docs-internal-guid-4d62a5b1-7fff-89e4-8c9b-fb1f486ce782\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">A short guide:</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">The world of cryptocurrencies has always been speculative. However, it is the technological aspect that makes cryptocurrencies so accessible. Blockchain is one of the latest technologies that is changing the way different sectors work. Finance is no different. In this article, we\'ll look at decentralized finance and how it will change the way financial companies operate. Cryptocurrencies are driving financial institutions to take steps for the future. They now see blockchain and crypto as their next technology to solve critical problems in the financial sector.</span></span></span></p><br><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">What is decentralized finance?</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Decentralized finance (DeFi) is about monetary systems that use public blockchains. It\'s a new monetary system, so it\'s one of the hot topics among industrialists, blockchain experts, and learners like you! In essence, the term \"public\" is important here. It can be equated with that of the public Ethereum blockchain. There is no place for a centralized authority in the public blockchain. The need for DeFi stems from the fact that financial services are not available to everyone in the world. Nearly 1.7 billion people around the world have no means and no access to financial services. Nor can financial institutions provide the necessary infrastructure to make it easier for people to access money. The existing infrastructure is huge, but it\'s not enough to reach everyone out there. Decentralization will solve the current infrastructure failures. It eliminates the point of failure and ensures that the records can be stored and shared by different nodes in the network. It can work in a peer-to-peer network without a central authority. For the current infrastructure to work, there is an over-reliance on a central authority. Without their governance, rules, regulations, and scope, it is not possible to implement them in some areas, especially where wealth is less or insufficiently generated and distributed. In addition, a central authority has the power to delete or block accounts if it deems it necessary. Censorship may be necessary in some cases, but in most cases it is restrictive and makes users suffer. Another key element of DeFi is the decentralized apps (dApps). DApps allow financial institutions to build functional apps on the public blockchain, ensuring that anyone can interact with them at a minimal cost per interaction.</span></span></span></p><br><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Decentralized finance approach</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Now that we have an answer to the question \"What is decentralized finance?\", it\'s time to learn how decentralized finance DeFi can be applied. At first glance, DeFi seems promising. It can change many lives and help create an infrastructure that doesn\'t require a centralized approach.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\"><br></span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\"><img src=\"https://ptgr-bucket.s3.us-east-1.amazonaws.com/chapter4/1.jpg\" alt=\"Image\" class=\"max-w-full h-auto rounded-lg my-4 cursor-move hover:ring-2 hover:ring-primary/50 transition-all\" referrerpolicy=\"no-referrer\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto;\"><br></span></span></span></p><div><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 8pt; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\"><span style=\"font-size: 14px;\">Figure 1: Overview of decentralized finance</span></span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 8pt; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\"><span style=\"font-size: 14px;\">(Source: 101blockchains.com)</span></span></span></span></p><br><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Remittance Market Solution</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">The most important impact of DeFi would be on the remittance market. This market is all about workers working abroad sending millions of dollars across borders. They have to deal with many problems when transferring the money. One of the biggest problems is the amount of fees they have to pay for the transfer. If the employee earns less, he has very little to transfer. On top of that, the fee reduction makes it even more difficult for anyone working in extreme conditions to save and ship. With Decentralized Finance (DeFi), it will be possible to reduce the costs associated with sending money by 50% and more. If you work abroad, you no longer have to worry about cross-border payments with DeFi. Ethereum-based decentralized finance applications are more popular here.</span></span></span></p><br><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Loan</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Decentralized finance is also very helpful in managing loans. In general, the lending industry is very dependent on access to a bank. If you don\'t have access to a bank or similar service, you won\'t be able to take out a loan. This means that you must also have a decent credit score or bank account that you can show to prove your eligibility for the loan. With DeFi, all of this will change. The platform can fix the connection issues between lenders and borrowers. It will connect them better and more directly. It will also ensure better credit checks and ensure that digital assets can be transferred quickly. There are many Ethereum-based decentralized finance applications that offer lending opportunities.</span></span></span></p><br><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Stablecoins</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Another very good use case of DeFi is the use of stablecoins. Stablecoins are created in such a way that their value does not change. Decentralized finance coins are mostly stablecoins, and they are an integral part of this technology. They are digital currencies that can be used as digital currency by the general population. They can be used as a currency issued by the government. In fact, it makes the tokenization of real-world assets easy. Real-world assets can be added to the blockchain and thus traded on the blockchain. Storing assets on the blockchain means more security and less vulnerability to cybersecurity. We recommend that you try these decentralized financial coins for yourself to see how they work in the first place.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\"><br></span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\"><img src=\"https://ptgr-bucket.s3.us-east-1.amazonaws.com/chapter4/2.jpg\" alt=\"Image\" class=\"max-w-full h-auto rounded-lg my-4 cursor-move hover:ring-2 hover:ring-primary/50 transition-all\" referrerpolicy=\"no-referrer\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto;\"><br></span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size:8pt;font-family:Poppins,sans-serif;color:#000000;background-color:transparent;font-weight:400;font-style:normal;font-variant:normal;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;white-space:pre;white-space:pre-wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\"><span style=\"font-size: 14px;\">Figure 2: What is DEFI?</span></span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline;\"><span id=\"docs-internal-guid-3ed97c28-7fff-f621-0f65-f039ac46802d\"></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size:8pt;font-family:Poppins,sans-serif;color:#000000;background-color:transparent;font-weight:400;font-style:normal;font-variant:normal;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;white-space:pre;white-space:pre-wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\"><span style=\"font-size: 14px;\">(Source: 101blockchains.com)</span></span></span></span></p></span>\n\n</div></span>\n</div>',1,0,70,'2025-09-12 06:36:31','2025-10-08 10:04:45'),(204,31,'Traditional vs. Decentralized Finance (DeFi)','<div class=\"prose prose-lg max-w-none space-y-4\">\n\n<span id=\"docs-internal-guid-b8ad638a-7fff-36fe-d6af-6cab30d0a18b\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">What is the difference between traditional and decentralized finance (DeFi)? The main difference lies in the way they work. Traditional financial systems operate with centralization, and that brings inefficiency and uncertainty. Security risks are persistent in the current traditional financial system. Cybercrime is also on the rise as the technologies used by financial institutions do not evolve. Most transactions are at risk of being hacked. All of this entails both financial and data-related risks. DeFi, on the other hand, ensures that the problems are fixed to a certain extent. At its core, DeFi uses a public blockchain, which means it doesn\'t rely on a centralized system or entity. It can function without the need for its own infrastructure. It simply decentralizes the global economy and makes economic activity possible for everyone in the world. Public blockchains can effectively replace the traditional financial system, making it transparent, decentralized, and permissionless.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Let\'s talk about the improvements it brings below.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Permissionless</span></span></span><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">: Public blockchains don\'t require permission from others to participate and interact. This makes it an excellent choice for global implementation. It also ensures that the problem of inequality is solved.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Decentralization</span></span></span><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">: Since there is no central authority, the data between the different nodes is stored in a network.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Transparency:</span></span></span><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\"> A public blockchain is also transparent.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">An example of decentralized finance is Rainier AG. This is an independent asset management company that uses the cryptocurrency space to its advantage. It has set up its own STO exchange platform, as well as a cryptocurrency trading platform. They are currently working to improve their ecosystem by offering unique services in the financial sector.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Risks of decentralized finance</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Not everything is positive when it comes to decentralized finance. There are some challenges that need to be overcome to make them workable for the various governments and organizations. One of the biggest challenges DeFi has to overcome is the adoption rate. Although we are connected via the internet, DeFi is hardly known to the public. Not many people know about DeFi and that can affect the usage rate. In fact, there are very few people who know about crypto. Cryptocurrencies gained their popularity during the Bitcoin price surge, yet they are not nearly as well-known as the \"internet\" or other more popular technologies out there. Even though public blockchains are technically capable of integrating any of us out there, they lack the bandwidth to operate efficiently. In comparison, Visa can process a huge number of transactions per second. This limitation is what doesn\'t make Bitcoin such a huge success. Other second-generation blockchain solutions, including Ethereum, also lack the bandwidth to make them interesting for practice. At the moment, blockchain researchers are working hard to make the technology more scalable. Another major concern for DeFi is its connection with cryptocurrencies. Blockchain is an ideal network to transfer digital assets. Stablecoins are the answer, but they can\'t be used for every transaction. That\'s where cryptocurrencies come in. But the volatility of cryptocurrencies doesn\'t make them an ideal solution for the real world. Facebook’s Libra (later Diem) was discontinued in 2022 before its global launch and has received both positive and negative receptions. Overall, it was not well received, and many institutions have withdrawn from cooperation with the Libra project.</span></span></span></p><br><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Why is decentralized finance so popular?</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">You must be wondering why decentralized finance, or DeFi, is so popular among financial institutions. Well, as you can see, it comes with a lot of benefits. For example, it provides you with more security and transparency in the long run. Aside from the adoption work, DeFi also needs to ensure that it has an appropriate regulatory framework. Since it is a public blockchain, it is necessary to put regulations in the room. At the moment, there are several DeFi organizations working on their own solutions. Their solutions are independent and therefore do not follow any guidelines. This creates a fragmented market in which it will be difficult to create a single market in the near future. There are more and more DeFi platforms, but none of them are succeeding, so time and money are wasted, and the idea is also at risk. Also, there are some countries that ban cryptocurrencies or regulate them too much to make their use more difficult. The solution is an open-source regulatory organization that can work together to bring projects together. This way, we can ensure that DeFi can grow in the right direction.</span></span></span></p><br><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Rise to popularity</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Blockchain is more than a decade old. However, we didn\'t see the popularity of DeFi until 2019. That\'s because most of the world isn\'t connected via the internet. At the time, decentralized finance didn\'t make sense because it needed the internet for connectivity. We think that DeFi will become mainstream by 2025. The internet is growing rapidly and is becoming more affordable than ever before. India, one of the third-world countries, has also seen tremendous growth in terms of mobile phones and internet connections.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Conclusion: The Future of Decentralized Finance</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">The future of decentralized finance seems quite bright right now. Many companies are already looking at decentralized finance as an alternative to traditional financing. Also, many companies have already taken the necessary steps to implement decentralized finance projects. So it looks like adoption will continue to grow. So, if you work in a financial institution or similar company that deals with finance, you should definitely take a look at DeFi. Since it is a rising star, you should not miss the chance to benefit from it. (Geroni, 2021)</span></span></span></p><div><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><br></span>\n</div></span>\n</div>',1,1,70,'2025-09-12 06:37:17','2025-10-08 10:05:30'),(205,32,'Introduction to Smart Contracts','<div class=\"prose prose-lg max-w-none space-y-4\">\n\n<span id=\"docs-internal-guid-c4b7068a-7fff-7bec-6cdd-6863e0bebf89\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 14pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">What is a smart contract?</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">A smart contract is code deployed on a blockchain (popularized by Ethereum) that self-executes when predefined conditions are met. It is a collection of code (its functions) and data (its state) that resides at a specific address on the Ethereum blockchain. Smart contracts differ from EOAs: they’re controlled by code, not private keys. This means that they have a balance and can be the target of transactions. However, they are not controlled by a user but are deployed on the network and run as programmed. User accounts can then interact with a smart contract by submitting transactions that perform a function defined in the smart contract. Smart contracts can define rules like a regular contract and automatically enforce them through the code. Smart contracts cannot be deleted by default, and interactions with them are irreversible. Smart contracts are an important part of many platforms and applications that are being developed using blockchain or distributed ledger technology. The following explains the background and functions of smart contracts, discusses whether they can be considered enforceable legal agreements under contract law in the United States, and highlights certain legal and practical considerations that need to be clarified before they can be widely used in commercial contexts.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Historical background</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">The term \"smart contract\" was first introduced by computer scientist and cryptographer Nick Szabo about 20 years ago as a graduate student at the University of Washington. Szabo said, \"New institutions and new ways of formalizing the relationships that make up these institutions are now made possible by the digital revolution.\" I call these new contracts \"smart\" because they are far more functional than their lifeless paper-based ancestors. The use of artificial intelligence is not necessary. A smart contract is a set of promises specified in digital form, including the protocols within which the parties deliver on those promises. It is important that Szabo puts the word \"smart\" in quotation marks when comparing smart contracts to paper-based contracts and that he excludes artificial intelligence. Smart contracts may be \"smarter\" than paper contracts because they can automatically execute certain pre-programmed steps, but they shouldn\'t be thought of as smart tools that can analyze the more subjective requirements of a contract. The classic example of a smart contract that Szabo cites is a vending machine. Once a buyer has fulfilled the terms of the \"contract\" (i.e., inserts money into the machine), the machine automatically abides by the terms of the unwritten agreement and delivers the snack. Smart contracts have their origins in Ricardian contracts, a concept published in 1996 by Ian Grigg and Gary Howland as part of their work on the Ricardo payment system for transferring assets. Grigg saw Ricardian Contracts as a bridge between text contracts and code that had the following parameters: a single document that \"a) is a contract offered by an issuer to holders, b) is for a valuable right held by the holders and managed by the issuer, c) is easily readable by humans (like a contract on paper), d) can be read by programs, e) is digitally signed, f) contains keys and server information, and g) is linked to a unique and secure identifier.\"</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Preconditions.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">If you\'re just starting out or looking for a less technical introduction, we recommend our Introduction to Smart Contracts. Make sure you\'ve read up on Ethereum\'s accounts, transactions, and virtual machine before you jump into the world of smart contracts.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">A digital vending machine</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Perhaps the best metaphor for a smart contract is a vending machine, as described by Nick Szabo. With the right inputs, a certain output is guaranteed.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">To get a snack from a vending machine: 1</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Money + Snack Selection = Snack Spent 2</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">This logic is programmed into the machine.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">A smart contract, like an automaton, has logic programmed into it. Here\'s a simple example of what this machine would look like if it were a smart contract written in Solidity, a prominent blockchain smart contract programming language:</span></span></span></p><br><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 112, 192); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">pragma solidity 0.8.7;</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 112, 192); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">contract VendingMachine {</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 112, 192); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Declare the state variables of the contract</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 112, 192); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">address public owner;</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 112, 192); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">mapping (address =&gt; uint) public cupcakeBalances;</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 112, 192); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">If the \'VendingMachine\' contract is used:</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 112, 192); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">1. Set the provisioning address as the owner of the contract</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 112, 192); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">2. Set the cupcake balance of the staked SMART CONTRACT to 100</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 112, 192); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">constructor() {</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 112, 192); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">owner = msg.sender;</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 112, 192); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">cupcakeBalances[address(this)] = 100;</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 112, 192); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">}</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 112, 192); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Allows the owner to increase the cupcake balance of the smart contract</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 112, 192); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">function refill(uint amount) public {</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 112, 192); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">require(msg.sender == owner, \"Only the owner can populate.\");</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 112, 192); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">cupcakeBalances[address(this)] += amount;</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 112, 192); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">}</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 112, 192); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Allow anyone to buy cupcakes</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 112, 192); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">function purchase(uint amount) public payable {</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 112, 192); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">require(msg.value &gt;= amount * 1 ether, \"You must pay at least 1 ETH per cupcake\");</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 112, 192); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">require(cupcakeBalances[address(this)] &gt;= amount, \"Not enough cupcakes in stock to complete this purchase\");</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 112, 192); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">cupcakeBalances[address(this)] -= amount;</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 112, 192); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">cupcakeBalances[msg.sender] += amount;</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 112, 192); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">}</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 112, 192); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">}</span></span></span></p><br><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Just as a vending machine eliminates the need for a salesperson, smart contracts can replace middlemen in many industries.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Permissionless characteristic.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Anyone can write a smart contract and deploy it on the network. You just need to learn how to program in a smart contract language and have enough ETH to stake your contract. Deploying a smart contract is technically a transaction, so you have to pay for it, just like you have to pay for a simple ETH transfer. However, the cost of providing contracts is much higher. Ethereum has developer-friendly languages for writing smart contracts:</span></span></span></p><ul style=\"padding-inline-start: 48px;\"><li dir=\"ltr\" style=\"list-style-type: disc; font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre; margin-left: 59.35pt;\" aria-level=\"1\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\" role=\"presentation\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Solidity</span></span></span></p></li><li dir=\"ltr\" style=\"list-style-type: disc; font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre; margin-left: 59.35pt;\" aria-level=\"1\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\" role=\"presentation\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Vyper</span></span></span></p></li></ul><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">However, they must be compiled before they can be deployed so that Ethereum\'s virtual machine can interpret and store the contract. Smart contracts are public on Ethereum and can be considered open APIs. This means that you can call other smart contracts in your own smart contract to greatly expand the possibilities. Contracts can even use other contracts.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Application restrictions.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Smart contracts alone cannot obtain information about events in the \"real world\" because they cannot retrieve data from off-chain sources. This means that they cannot react to events in the real world. That is intentional. Relying on external information could jeopardize consensus, which is important for security and decentralization. However, it is important for blockchain applications to be able to leverage data off-chain. The solution is oracles, i.e., tools that take off-chain data and make it available for smart contracts. Another limitation of smart contracts is the maximum contract size. A smart contract can be a maximum of 24 KB in size, as a limit imposed by the Ethereum Virtual Machine (EVM) to ensure the optimal smart contract performance as well as ‘gas cost limitations’ that make smart contracts very sustainable. This can be circumvented by using the diamond pattern, which is a Solidity design pattern used to split large contracts into smaller, modular components. By using this pattern, you can avoid hitting the size limit by dividing a large contract into smaller contracts that each handle a part of the logic.&nbsp;</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">We will explain the ‘EVM’ and ‘gas’ concepts in the next paragraph of this chapter.&nbsp;</span></span></span></p><br><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Multisig Contracts.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Multisig contracts (multi-signature contracts) are smart contract accounts that require multiple valid signatures to complete a transaction. This is very useful for avoiding individual vulnerabilities in contracts that contain large amounts of Ether or other tokens. Multisig contracts also divide the responsibility for contract execution and key management among multiple parties, preventing the loss of a single private key, resulting in irreversible loss of funds. For these reasons, multisig contracts can be used for easy DAO governance. Multisig contracts require N signatures of M possible acceptable signatures (where N ≤ M and M &gt; 1) in order to be executed. N = 3, M = 5 and N = 4, M = 7 are common. A 4/7 multisig requires four out of seven possible valid signatures. This means that the funds can still be accessed even if three signatures are lost. In this case, it also means that the majority of key holders must agree and </span></span></span></p><div><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><br></span>\n</div></span>\n</div>',1,0,70,'2025-09-12 06:40:04','2025-10-08 10:05:57'),(206,32,'How Smart Contracts Work','<div class=\"prose prose-lg max-w-none space-y-4\">\n\n<span id=\"docs-internal-guid-22614848-7fff-84cd-3d17-94b675b4b466\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">The term \"smart contracts\" refers to computer code that automatically executes all or part of an agreement and is stored on a blockchain-based platform. As explained below, the code can either be the only manifestation of the agreement between the parties or complement a traditional text-based contract and execute certain conditions, such as the transfer of funds from party A to party B. The code itself is replicated across multiple nodes of a blockchain and therefore benefits from the security, consistency, and immutability that a blockchain provides. This replication also means that with each new block added to the blockchain, the code is actually executed. If the parties have indicated that certain parameters are met by initiating a transaction, the code will perform the step triggered by those parameters. If no such transaction has been initiated, the code will not execute any steps. Most smart contracts are written in one of the programming languages that are directly suited to such computer programs.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Currently, the input parameters and execution steps for a smart contract must be specific and objective. In other words, if \"x\" occurs, then step \"y\" is executed. Therefore, the actual tasks that smart contracts perform are quite rudimentary, such as automatically moving an amount of cryptocurrency from one party\'s wallet to another when certain criteria are met. With the increasing adoption of blockchain and the increasing tokenization of assets, smart contracts are becoming more complex and capable of performing complex transactions. In fact, developers are already in the process of stringing together several transaction steps to form more complex smart contracts. Still, we are at least many years away from a code being able to determine more subjective legal criteria, such as whether a party has met a standard of commercially reasonable efforts or whether an indemnity clause should be triggered and the compensation paid. Before a created smart contract can actually be executed on certain blockchains, one more step is required, which is to pay a transaction fee so that the contract can be added to the chain and executed. In the case of the Ethereum blockchain, smart contracts are executed on the Ethereum Virtual Machine (EVM), and this payment, which is made through the Ether cryptocurrency, is known as \"gas.\" The more complex the smart contract is (based on the transaction steps to be executed), the more gas will have to be paid to execute the smart contract. Thus, gas currently acts as an important gateway to prevent overly complex or numerous smart contracts from overwhelming the EVM. [2] Smart contracts are currently best suited to automatically execute two types of \"transactions\" that occur in many contracts: (1) ensuring the payment of funds on certain triggering events, and (2) imposing financial penalties when certain objective conditions are not met. In any case, human intervention, including by a trusted trustee or even the judiciary, is no longer necessary once the smart contract is deployed and operational, reducing the cost of executing and enforcing the contract. An example of this is that smart contracts could eliminate the so-called \"procure-to-pay\" gaps. When a product arrives at a warehouse and is scanned, a smart contract could immediately obtain the necessary approvals and, once they are in place, transfer the amount from the buyer to the seller. Sellers would pay faster and would no longer have to dun, and buyers would reduce their costs for liabilities. This could impact working capital needs and simplify financial operations for both parties. In terms of enforcement, a smart contract could be programmed to block access to an internet-connected asset if no payment is received. For example, access to certain content could be automatically blocked if payment has not been received.</span></span></span></p></span>\n\n</div>',1,0,70,'2025-09-12 06:40:48','2025-10-08 10:06:18'),(207,32,'Are Smart Contracts Enforceable?','<div class=\"prose prose-lg max-w-none space-y-4\">\n\n<span id=\"docs-internal-guid-f71922ae-7fff-42eb-96f6-6f23cea7db59\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">In the United States, there is no federal contract law, but rather the enforceability depends on jurisdiction. Courts have started addressing digital contract disputes (see 2022–24 rulings). Even though certain basic principles apply uniformly across state borders, and the National Conference of Commissioners on Uniform State Laws has sought to unify state laws, conclusions regarding smart contracts must be tempered by the fact that states may have different views. A discussion about the enforceability of smart contracts must begin with the basic distinction between an agreement and a \"contract.\" States generally recognize that although two parties can enter into a variety of \"agreements,\" a contract means that the agreement is legally binding and enforceable in a court of law. In order to determine enforceability, the state courts traditionally look at whether the general legal requirements of offer, acceptance, and consideration are met. These basic requirements can certainly be met by complementary smart contracts. For example, an insurer could develop a flight insurance product that automatically grants the insured a payout if a flight is delayed by more than two hours. The most important conditions, such as the calculation of the delay, can be set out in a text-based contract, with the actual formation of the contract (payment of the premium) and the execution (automatic payment in the event of a demonstrable delay) handled via a complementary smart contract. In this case, the insurer has made a concrete offer for a flight insurance product, which is accepted by the insured person in return for payment of the premium. Although certain contracts these days must be in writing, and additional paperwork may be required, such as under the Uniform Commercial Code (UCC) and state fraud laws, agreements do not always have to be in writing to be enforceable. Therefore, many smart contracts that consist of only one code are also enforceable under the laws of the states that govern contracts. The example of a vending machine cited by Szabo is instructive in this regard. There, the buyer has many tacit rights, but a contract was concluded without there being any meaningful written provisions other than a price display for each item. Therefore, the fact that an agreement is only concluded in the form of a code, as is the case with smart contracts, does not constitute a particular obstacle to the formation of a contract that goes beyond the barriers imposed by the UCC and fraud laws. In fact, the role of information technology in the formation of contracts has long been considered in a number of laws and legal constructions. For example, the Uniform Electronic Transactions Act (UETA), which dates back to 1999 and forms the basis for legislation in 47 states, provides that electronic records, including records created by computer programs, and electronic signatures (i.e., digital signatures created using public-key encryption technologies) have the same legal effect as theirs, with limited exceptions. written counterparts. UETA even goes so far as to recognize the validity of electronic agents,\" which it defines as \"a computer program or electronic or other automated means that is used independently to initiate an action or respond to electronic records or services, in whole or in part, without any person performing any verification or action. According to UETA, within the parameters of its programming, an electronic agent is able to activate, react to, or interact with other parties or their electronic representatives once it has been activated by a contracting party, without the latter having to worry about it further, which arguably constitutes a forward-looking recognition of smart contracts. Similarly, the federal Electronic Signature Record Act (E-Sign Act) not only recognizes the validity of electronic signatures and electronic records in interstate commerce but also provides that a contract or other record relating to a transaction may not be denied legal effect, validity, or enforceability solely because it is linked to its formation, creation, or service of one or more electronic representatives, as long as the act of such an electronic representative is legally attributable to the person to be bound. The term \"electronic agent\" means a computer program or electronic automated means that is used independently to trigger an action or to respond in whole or in part to electronic records or services, without any person performing any verification or action at the time of the action or response. Although an understanding of the current legal framework is important to assess the enforceability of smart contracts today, those who use smart contracts in the future may not need to rely on laws that preceded the development of blockchain technology. Arizona and Nevada have already changed their respective state versions of UETA to explicitly include blockchains and smart contracts. The fact that these states have adopted significantly different definitions of these critical terms suggests that as more states follow their example, there could be increasing pressure to adopt uniform definitions that reflect developments in blockchains and smart contracts.</span></span></span></p><div><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><br></span>\n</div></span>\n</div>',1,0,70,'2025-09-12 06:41:31','2025-10-08 10:06:39'),(208,32,'Challenges in the widespread adoption of smart contracts.','<div class=\"prose prose-lg max-w-none space-y-4\">\n\n<span id=\"docs-internal-guid-143180bd-7fff-071b-3ddf-0a8e686fa4cf\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Given the existing legal framework for the recognition of electronic contracts, it is very likely that a court today would recognize the validity of code that executes the provisions of a smart contract—which we have classified as complementary smart contracts. There are also precedents that suggest that a code-only smart contract could enjoy similar legal protections. The challenge standing in the way of widespread adoption of smart contracts may therefore have less to do with the limits of the law than with potential conflicts between how smart contract code works and how contracting parties conduct business. Here are some of these challenges:</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">How can non-technical parties negotiate, design, and evaluate smart contracts?</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">A key challenge in the widespread adoption of smart contracts is that parties must rely on a trusted, technical expert to either record the parties\' agreement in the code or confirm the accuracy of the code written by a third party. Some compare this to hiring a lawyer to explain the \"legal language\" of a traditional text-based contract, but this analogy is not accurate. Non-lawyers can usually understand both simple short contracts and many provisions of longer contracts, especially those that contain terms and conditions. However, a non-programmer would be completely overwhelmed to understand even the simplest smart contract and is therefore much more dependent on an expert to explain to them what is \"written\" in the contract. To some extent, the inability of the parties to the contract to understand the code of the smart contract will not be an obstacle to concluding agreements on additional codes. This is because many basic functions allow text templates to be created and used to specify which parameters to enter and how to execute those parameters. For example, let\'s say a simple smart contract feature deducts a late fee from a counterparty\'s wallet if a particular payment has not been received by a certain date. The text template could ask the parties to record the amount of the expected payment, the due date, and the amount of the late fee. However, a contracting party should ensure that the underlying code actually performs the functions specified in the text and does not contain any additional conditions or parameters, especially if the template excludes any liability for the accuracy of the underlying code. This verification requires a trusted third party with coding skills. In cases where such templates do not exist and new code needs to be developed, the parties must communicate the intent of their agreement to a programmer. Simply handing this programmer a copy of the legal agreement would be inefficient, as the programmer would then have to try to decipher a legal document. Parties relying on complementary smart contracts may therefore need to create a separate \"term sheet\" with the functions that the smart contract is intended to perform and that can be made available to the programmer. The parties may also request written confirmations from the programmer that the code will work as intended. The result is that for customized agreements that are not based on an existing template, parties must enter into a written contract with the programmer of the smart contract, not unlike the contract that parties can enter into today with a provider of EDI (Electronic Data Interchange) transaction services. Insurance companies could also create policies to protect contracting parties from the risk that the smart contract code will not perform the functions set out in the text of the contract. While the contracting parties would also want to review the code (or have it checked by a third party), insurance can provide additional protection, as the contracting parties could overlook errors when reviewing the code. It is also reassuring for the parties that the insurance company probably checked the code itself before agreeing to the insurance policy. Code-only smart contracts used for business-to-consumer transactions could pose a number of additional problems that need to be solved. Courts are cautious when it comes to enforcing contracts where the consumer has not been sufficiently informed about the terms of the contract, and they could be reluctant to enforce a smart contract if the consumer has not also been provided with an underlying text contract with the full terms. As the validity or fulfillment of smart contracts is increasingly being negotiated in court, courts may need a system of court-appointed experts to help them decipher the meaning and intent of the code. Today, parties routinely use their own experts when technical issues are at the heart of litigation. While both federal courts and many state courts have the power to appoint their own experts, they rarely make use of this power. This approach could change as the number of standard contract disputes involving smart contract code interpretation increases.</span></span></span></p><br><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Smart contracts and the dependence on \"off-chain\" resources.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Many smart contract use cases assume that the smart contract receives information or parameters from resources that are not on the blockchain itself—so-called off-chain resources. For example, let\'s say that a crop insurance smart contract is programmed to transmit value to an insured if the temperature drops below 32 degrees at any point. The smart contract must receive this temperature data from an agreed-upon source. This raises two problems. First, smart contracts are not able to retrieve data from off-chain resources; rather, this information must be \"pushed\" to the smart contract. Second, if the data in question is in constant flux, and because the code is replicated across multiple nodes on the network, different nodes can receive different information even if they are only a few seconds apart. In our example, node-1 can get the information that the temperature is 31.9 degrees, while node-2 gets the information that the temperature is actually 32 degrees. Since the consensus of all nodes is required to validate a transaction, such fluctuations can result in the condition being considered \"not met.\" The contracting parties can solve this problem by using a so-called \"oracle.\" Oracles are trusted third parties that retrieve information outside the blockchain and then feed that information into the blockchain at specified times. In the example above, the oracle would monitor the daily temperature, determine that the event of freezing has occurred, and then relay this information to the smart contract. Although oracles are an elegant solution for accessing resources outside of the blockchain, this process adds another party with whom the parties would have to enter into a contract to bring about a smart contract, somewhat diluting the decentralized benefits of smart contracts. It also introduces a potential \"point of failure.\" For example, an oracle might have a system glitch and be unable to transmit the required information, provide erroneous data, or simply shut down operations. Smart contracts need to take these eventualities into account before they can be deployed on a larger scale.</span></span></span></p><br><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">What is the \"final\" agreement between the parties?</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">When analyzing traditional text-based contracts, courts examine the final, written document that the contracting parties have agreed upon to determine whether the contracting parties are complying with or violating the contract. The courts have long emphasized that it is this final agreement that reflects the mutual will of the parties. In the case of code-only smart contracts, the executed code—and the resulting result—represents the only objective evidence of the terms agreed upon by the parties. In these cases, an email exchange between the parties about what functions the smart contract \"should\" perform, or verbal discussions for this purpose, would likely give way to the final lines of code as the authoritative expression of the parties\' will. In terms of complementary smart contracts, a court would likely take a look at the text and code as a single agreement. The matter becomes complicated when the traditional text contract and the code do not match. In the crop insurance example described above, let\'s say that the contract text specifies that an insurance payout will be made when the temperature drops below 32 degrees, while the smart contract code triggers the payment when the temperature is equal to or below 32 degrees. Assuming that the text of the contract does not indicate whether the text or code is relevant in the event of a discrepancy, the courts will have to decide, perhaps on a case-by-case basis, whether the code should be treated as a mutually agreed amendment to the written contract or whether the text of the contract should take precedence. In some respects, the analysis is likely to be no different from a case where the terms of a main agreement differ from those in an annexe or annex. The fact that this is a conflict between text and computer code and not between two text documents should not be decisive, but the courts may take a different view.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Smart contract automation.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">One of the most important features of smart contracts is the automatic and continuous execution of transactions without human intervention. However, this automation, and the fact that smart contracts cannot be easily changed or terminated unless the parties to the contract already include such capabilities when creating the smart contract, pose some of the biggest challenges for the widespread adoption of smart contracts. In traditional text contracts, for example, a party can excuse a breach of contract simply by not enforcing the available sanctions. If a valued customer is one month late with their payment, the seller can decide in real time that maintaining the long-term business relationship is more important than any available termination right or late fee. However, if this relationship had been reduced to a smart contract, the option of not enforcing the agreement on an ad hoc basis would probably not exist. Late payment will result in a late fee being automatically deducted from the customer\'s account or the customer\'s access to a software program or internet-enabled device being blocked if the smart contract has been programmed to do so. Automated execution of smart contracts, therefore, may not be in line with the way many companies operate in the real world. Similarly, a party in a text-based contractual relationship may be willing to accept a partial performance on an ad hoc basis, which is considered a full performance. This can be done out of an interest in maintaining a long-term relationship or because one party finds that partial performance is better than no service at all. Again, the objectivity required for the code of smart contracts may not correspond to the reality of how the contracting parties treat each other.</span></span></span></p><br><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Modifying and Terminating Smart Contracts.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Currently, there is no easy way to change a smart contract, which poses certain challenges for contracting parties. For example, if the parties have agreed in a traditional text-based contract to change the framework of their business, or if there is a change in the law, the parties can quickly draft an amendment to accommodate that change or simply change their behavior. Smart contracts do not currently offer such flexibility. Because blockchains are immutable, modifying a smart contract is far more complicated than modifying standard software code that is not stored on a blockchain. The result is that modifying a smart contract can incur higher transaction costs than modifying a text-based contract and increases the margin of error, making it impossible for contracting parties to accurately reflect the changes they want to be applied. Similar challenges exist when terminating a smart contract. Let\'s say a party discovers a flaw in a contract that gives the other party more rights than intended, or they find that fulfilling their stated obligations will be far more costly than they expected. In a text-based contract, a party may commit or threaten to commit what is known as an \"efficient breach of contract,\" i.e., knowingly breach a contract and pay the resulting damage if it finds that the cost of performance is greater than the damage it would owe. In addition, by failing to perform or threatening to do so, a party can bring the other party back to the negotiating table to negotiate an amicable solution. Smart contracts do not yet offer analog self-help options. Projects are currently underway to develop smart contracts that can be terminated at any time and can be changed more easily. While this is somewhat at odds with the immutable and automated nature of smart contracts, it reflects the fact that smart contracts will only become commercially prevalent if they reflect the business reality of how the contracting parties act.</span></span></span></p><br><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Objectivity and the limits of desired ambiguity in smart contracts.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">The objectivity and automation demanded by smart contracts can be at odds with how business parties actually negotiate agreements. In the course of negotiations, the parties tacitly carry out a cost-benefit analysis, knowing that at some point it is no longer worthwhile to think about and take into account all possible eventualities. The parties may no longer want to spend management\'s time or legal fees on negotiations, or they may come to the conclusion that starting to generate revenue under a concluded contract outweighs clarifying open issues. Instead, they may decide that in the event of an unforeseen event occurring, they will find a solution at that time. Similarly, parties may intentionally leave a provision in an agreement somewhat unclear in order to give themselves the flexibility to argue that the provision should be interpreted in their favor. This approach to contracting is complicated with smart contracts because the computer code requires a level of accuracy that is not given when negotiating text-based contracts. A smart contract cannot contain ambiguous provisions, nor can certain potential scenarios go unaddressed. As a result, parties to smart contracts may find that the transaction costs of negotiating complex smart contracts are higher than those of a traditional text-based contract.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">It will take some time for those adopting smart contracts in a particular industry to determine which provisions are objective enough to lend themselves to smart contract execution. As mentioned earlier, most smart contracts so far largely perform simple tasks where the parameters of the \"if-then\" statements are clear. As smart contracts become more complex, there may be a disagreement about whether a particular contract provision can be captured by the objectivity that a smart contract requires.</span></span></span></p><br><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Do smart contracts really guarantee payment?</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">A frequently touted advantage of smart contracts is that they can automate payment without incurring reminders or other collection costs and without the need to go to court to obtain a payment judgment. While this is indeed true for simpler use cases, it may be less true for complex business relationships. The reality is that parties are constantly moving funds within their organization, rather than \"parking\" the entire amount due for a long-term contract in anticipation of future payment requests. Similarly, a person taking out a loan is unlikely to keep the entire loan amount in a specific wallet connected to the smart contract. Rather, the borrower will use these funds and make the necessary repayments on an ad hoc basis.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">If the party owing the amounts under the smart contract fails to fund the wallet in a timely manner, a smart contract that wants to transfer funds from that wallet after a trigger event may find that the required funds are not available. Implementing another layer in the process, such as the smart contract trying to pull funds from other wallets or the wallet \"funding\" itself from other sources, would not solve the problem if those wallets or sources of funds also do not have the required payment amounts. The parties could try to solve this problem through a text-based requirement that a wallet linked to the smart contract always have a minimum amount, but this solution would merely provide the party with a stronger legal argument in the event of a lawsuit. It would not make the payment process of the smart contract completely automatic. Even though smart contracts will make payments far more efficient, they cannot eliminate the need to resolve payment disputes.</span></span></span></p><br><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Risk distribution in the event of attacks and errors.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Smart contracts carry an additional risk that does not exist in most text-based contractual relationships: the possibility that the contract could be hacked or that the code or protocol simply contains an unintentional programming error. Given the relative security of blockchains, these concepts are closely linked, as most of the \"hacks\" associated with blockchain technology are actually the exploitation of an unintentional programming error. As with many errors in computer code, these errors are not glaring but only become apparent when they have been exploited. In 2017, for example, an attacker managed to relieve several multi-signature wallets offered by the company Parity of $31 million in Ether. Multi-signature wallets provide an extra layer of security because they require more than one private key to access the wallet. However, in the Parity attack, the attacker was able to exploit a vulnerability in the Parity code by reinitializing the smart contract and making himself the sole owner of the multi-signature wallets. The parties to a smart contract need to think about how the risk and liability for unintentional coding errors and resulting attacks will be shared between the parties and possibly with third-party developers or insurers of the smart contract.</span></span></span></p><br><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Governing Law and Jurisdiction.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">One of the most important promises of blockchain technology, and by extension, smart contracts, is the development of robust, decentralized, and global platforms. However, global adoption means that parties can use a smart contract in far more jurisdictions than text-based contracts. Therefore, for the party offering terms under a smart contract, it would be best to determine the applicable law and venue for that smart contract. A provision on applicable law specifies which substantive law applies to the interpretation of the smart contract, while a jurisdiction clause specifies which courts will decide the dispute. In cases where the applicable law or venue is not specified, a plaintiff is largely free to decide where to file a lawsuit or which substantive law should apply, as there are a variety of jurisdictions where a smart contract could be used. Since many early disputes related to smart contracts will be recognizable at first glance, the contracting parties will want to have some certainty about where such disputes will be resolved.</span></span></span></p><br><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Best practice.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Since the adoption of smart contracts is still in its infancy, the best practices for implementing such code are not yet mature. However, the checklist below is designed to help developers design effective smart contracts and serve as a guide for companies that want to use them.</span></span></span></p><ul style=\"padding-inline-start: 48px;\"><li dir=\"ltr\" style=\"list-style-type: disc; font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre; margin-left: 59.35pt;\" aria-level=\"1\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\" role=\"presentation\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">For parties entering into any type of contractual agreement, it would be best to use a hybrid approach that combines text and code at the moment. As mentioned earlier, there is a compelling case that purely coded smart contracts should be enforceable, at least under state contract law in the US. However, until there is greater clarity about their validity and enforceability, purely coded smart contracts should only be used for simpler transactions. Contracting parties will still want text versions of agreements so they can read the agreed terms, record terms that smart contracts are not suitable for, and have a document that they know a court will enforce.</span></span></span></p></li><li dir=\"ltr\" style=\"list-style-type: disc; font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre; margin-left: 59.35pt;\" aria-level=\"1\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\" role=\"presentation\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">In a hybrid contract that uses text and code, the text should clearly specify the smart contract code to which it is linked, and the parties should have full visibility into the variables that will be passed to the smart contract, how they are defined, and which events of the transaction will trigger the execution of the code.</span></span></span></p></li><li dir=\"ltr\" style=\"list-style-type: disc; font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre; margin-left: 59.35pt;\" aria-level=\"1\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\" role=\"presentation\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">If parties rely on oracles for off-chain data, they should clarify what happens if the oracle is unable to issue the required data, provides erroneous data, or simply ceases operations.</span></span></span></p></li><li dir=\"ltr\" style=\"list-style-type: disc; font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre; margin-left: 59.35pt;\" aria-level=\"1\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\" role=\"presentation\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Parties should consider the distribution of risk in the event of a coding error.</span></span></span></p></li><li dir=\"ltr\" style=\"list-style-type: disc; font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre; margin-left: 59.35pt;\" aria-level=\"1\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\" role=\"presentation\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">The text agreement that accompanies the code should specify the applicable law and venue, as well as the order of precedence between the text and the code in the event of a conflict.</span></span></span></p></li><li dir=\"ltr\" style=\"list-style-type: disc; font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre; margin-left: 59.35pt;\" aria-level=\"1\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\" role=\"presentation\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">The text agreement should include a statement from each party that they have reviewed the smart contract code and that it reflects the terms of the text agreement. Although such a statement cannot compel a party to audit the code, it does help the counterparty defend itself against the claim that the code has never been audited. The parties can also insure themselves against the risk that the code contains errors. As mentioned earlier, parties may need to bring in external experts to review the code.</span></span></span></p></li></ul><br><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">The Future of Smart Contracts.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Today, smart contracts are a prototypical example of the \"Amara Law\" formulated by computer scientist Roy Amara of Stanford University, which states that we tend to overestimate new technologies in the short term and underestimate them in the long term. Although smart contracts still need to be further developed before they are widely used for productive use in complex business relationships, they have the potential to revolutionize the reward and incentive structure that will determine the way parties enter into contracts in the future. For this reason, when considering smart contracts, it is important to think not only about how existing concepts and structures can be transferred to this new technology. Rather, the true revolution of smart contracts will come from completely new paradigms that we cannot yet imagine.</span></span></span></p><div><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><br></span>\n</div></span>\n</div>',1,0,70,'2025-09-12 06:43:36','2025-10-08 10:07:01'),(209,32,'Real-world examples of smart contracts and dApps','<div class=\"prose prose-lg max-w-none space-y-4\">\n\n<span id=\"docs-internal-guid-dd32578e-7fff-a92c-48d5-e6c2f59e83f4\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Discover how smart contract use cases are revolutionizing the world around us and how many experts see the utility of smart contracts in the future. Smart contracts are one of the key components of many blockchain-based ecosystems and a particularly important element of many application-oriented blockchains such as Ethereum. These digital contracts are trustless, autonomous, decentralized, and transparent—and usually irreversible and immutable once they are deployed. Among the benefits of smart contracts is that they reduce or even eliminate the need for intermediaries and for the enforcement of contracts in an agreement or transaction. This is because in a smart contract, the code defines the mechanisms of the transaction and makes the final decision on the terms. For this reason, smart contracts have become the building blocks of an entire ecosystem of decentralized applications (dApps) and represent a crucial focus of blockchain development in general. A single smart contract can only be used for one type of transaction: if something happens, something else happens. However, most dApps work by bundling smart contracts to enable a sophisticated, synergistic set of features. There are thousands of dApps on various blockchain networks, including finance, gaming, exchanges, and media—and they can all use smart contracts in different ways and for different purposes. Examples of smart contract applications include financial purposes such as trading, investing, lending, and borrowing. They can be used for gaming, healthcare, and real estate applications, and they can even be used to configure entire enterprise structures. Below, we\'ll share some real-world examples of smart contracts and dApps and explore their potential for shaping the future.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\"><br></span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Smart Contract Use Cases in Finance.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Decentralized finance (DeFi) apps represent an excellent alternative to traditional financial services and are growing in popularity thanks to the trusted, immutable, and transparent nature of blockchain and smart contract technology. DeFi dApps offer parallel services for banks and financial service providers—such as lending, borrowing, trading, and a variety of other financial services—as well as entirely new types of products and decentralized business models that can provide significant benefits and utility to users. With the increased transparency that smart contracts offer (along with 24/7 functionality and reduced costs), dApps have the potential to lower the barriers to entry into the financial services arena for people around the world. DeFi projects have already reached a multi-billion dollar value, and this trend is expected to continue as more and more people become familiar with the unique benefits of the sector. Users will be able to participate in this new generation of financial services without the need for central custody or intermediary fees. Although the DeFi sector is only a few years old, with the number of innovative dApps already providing value and utility to consumers, the impact of smart contract-powered dApps on the financial industry is already being felt.</span></span></span></p><br><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">NFTs and Smart Contracts: Blockchain Technology in Gambling.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">The global gambling industry is a billion-dollar ecosystem that continues to grow rapidly, but the way value is created and distributed in the industry can be unfair. The software developers design and publish games, and players pay to play and interact with those games. This results in a one-way flow of value, where participants spend money to gain access to game values and gameplay configurations. In contrast, blockchain technology in games can allow users to more effectively capture the value and benefits of in-game purchases and asset acquisitions. Blockchain technology in games is typically powered by non-fungible tokens (NFTs)—unique digital assets that represent in-game content. NFTs are based on smart contracts. These tokens are unique, rare, and indivisible, while the blockchain networks that underpin NFTs allow for user ownership, detectable scarcity, interoperability, and immutability. Together, these characteristics of blockchain in gambling have the potential to drive mainstream adoption and a more equitable model of value. Thanks to the implementation of blockchain technology in the gaming industry, you can save in-game purchases, sell them to other players, or transfer them to other supported games. Meanwhile, the scarcity of in-game NFT purchases is demonstrable through the immutable records on the underlying blockchain network—as is the history of ownership of the NFT. Because NFTs are unique and can be designed to retain their value beyond the game in which they were created, blockchain-based games and dApps have the potential to expand the gaming economy, establish new game categories, and encourage the development of new games. Blockchains with significant game development include Ethereum, TRON, EOSIO, and NEO.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\"><br></span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Smart contracts and blockchain in the legal industry.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Perhaps one of the most promising use cases for smart contracts in the real world is their potential to act as legally binding contracts—the kind of contracts that underpin most business relationships today. Technology has driven innovation in the legal industry, most recently with the introduction of electronic signatures for binding legal agreements. Smart contracts represent another new development in this space and could soon be an option for parties to legal contracts, potentially reducing the cost of using lawyers and other intermediaries. The widespread use of customized smart contracts for countless types of transactions, which can reduce costs and increase transaction speed, may be closer than you think. Some U.S. states have begun to allow the use of smart contracts and blockchain in the legal industry in certain contexts. For example, Arizona allows the creation of enforceable legal agreements via smart contracts, and California allows the issuance of marriage licenses via blockchain technology.</span></span></span></p><br><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Blockchain in the real estate industry.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Through tokenization, smart contracts promote fractional ownership of assets, lowering the barrier to entry for many by connecting blockchain and real estate transactions. In particular, there are a number of successful efforts to tokenize real estate assets, including through platforms such as RealT and SolidBlock, which connect blockchain and real estate. Smart contract technology can also overhaul documentation and transaction processes by incorporating blockchain into real estate transactions. For example, the Republic of Georgia (in the Caucasus region) has been developing a blockchain-based land registry since 2016, and similar projects are underway in other countries such as the United Arab Emirates (UAE). Anyone who has ever bought a home or other property is probably aware of the potential hidden costs associated with closing fees, property transfers, and brokerage fees. These costs could be reduced or even eliminated by the automatic execution of smart contracts that operate free of intermediaries. When a property is tokenized, much of the necessary record-keeping can be done through associated smart contracts, saving parties time and money. Some experts even claim that smart contracts can bring benefits to parties by streamlining leases and complex loan or mortgage agreements, as well as guarantees and insurance. The use of smart contracts and blockchain in the real estate industry reduces the need for legal assistance or other advisory services, which can lead to cost savings on a broad scale.</span></span></span></p><br><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Using Smart Contracts in Enterprise Structures: Building </span></span></span><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">decentralized autonomous organizations</span></span></span><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\"> (DAOs).</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">In 2017, Delaware passed Senate Bill 69, which allows companies to be founded and managed using blockchain technology. This law opened the door for the proliferation of DAOs, which operate like companies where ownership and compensation can be built into smart contracts. DAOs that use smart contracts to code corporate structures can enable sophisticated, automatically enforced incentive structures within an enterprise framework. DAOs can also lead to savings in administrative costs, such as office space, hiring, and payroll, through incentive structures that do not include formal employment contracts.</span></span></span></p><br><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Applications of Smart Contracts in Emerging Technologies.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">One of the most exciting applications of blockchain technology and its associated smart contract technology is the ability to facilitate complex computational tasks, such as those involved in machine learning and artificial intelligence (AI). By combining the data-intensive processing of AI with the decentralized security and immutability of blockchain technology, there is the potential to create AI-powered smart contracts. As smart contract applications are implemented in various industries, they must become increasingly complex to fulfill their new role. While rudimentary smart contract use cases can be designed manually, AI-powered smart contracts could enable the construction of highly complex, more responsive, enterprise-grade smart contracts and dApps that have the potential to drastically expand the capabilities of the technology. Many experts believe that AI and blockchain can benefit from each other\'s characteristics. Smart contracts can benefit from the advanced computing capabilities and adaptive systems of AI technology, while AI implementations could leverage smart contract technology to autonomously execute sets of rules and provide a secure environment for sensitive and valuable machine learning data. Zilliqa is one of the many blockchain platforms that is developing advanced computing capabilities with its proprietary smart contract programming language, Scilla, and an advanced parallel processing structure enabled by sharding.</span></span></span></p><br><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Potential benefits of smart contracts in other industries.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">This list of applications of smart contracts in the real world is by no means exhaustive, and smart contract technology will improve numerous other industries in the future. Many researchers and developers are eager to take advantage of smart contract technology to meet the demands of the growing Internet of Things (IoT). While blockchain technology is generally already being used to add security and transparency to IoT devices, the benefits of smart contracts can drive this integration even further. Healthcare is another industry that has begun to integrate blockchain technology for secure, trustless, and transparent data sharing. The relationship between healthcare and blockchain technology could also be strengthened by integrating smart contracts and entire dApps designed to solve key healthcare issues such as interoperability, identification, and authentication. With all these established use cases and the ongoing research and development of many more, smart contracts and dApps are poised to revolutionize the world of digital agreements in a lasting way (Cryptopedia Staff, 2021).</span></span></span></p><div><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><br></span>\n</div></span>\n</div>',1,1,70,'2025-09-12 06:44:41','2025-10-10 11:57:43'),(210,33,'DeFi Scamming – Basics','<div class=\"prose prose-lg max-w-none space-y-4\">\n\n<span id=\"docs-internal-guid-f7f7eeae-7fff-17e4-56b8-6a536e4ec0fb\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">The term \"DeFi,\" in the broadest sense, refers to virtual asset protocols and services that purport to enable some form of automated peer-to-peer (P2P) transactions. This often uses self-executing code known as \"smart contracts,\" which is based on blockchain technology. The term \"DeFi scamming\" is often used imprecisely in the virtual asset industry and often refers to services that are not functionally decentralized. The extent to which an alleged DeFi service is decentralized depends on the facts and circumstances. This risk assessment concludes that DeFi services often have a controlling organization that provides some level of centralized management and control. The decentralized finance industry is one of the fastest-growing areas in the crypto space, with the TVL (Total Value of cryptocurrency Locked) trending to fall from $52.7 billion in Oct \'22 to the lowest level of $38.5 billion in October 2023. (AltIndex, 2023) This also attracts the attention of scammers and bad actors. In 2023, losses from crypto scams reached a staggering $1 billion, reminding us of the importance of vigilance (DeFiLlama, n.d.).</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">What is DeFi Scamming?</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">DeFi scams are hacks or fraudulent schemes aimed at taking possession of someone else\'s digital assets. Crypto scams often aim to obtain private information such as security codes or trick a person into sending cryptocurrency to a digital wallet that may be compromised. Examples of crypto scams include giveaways, new romance scams, phishing, extortion emails, fake company announcements, extortion, \"rug pulls,\" and possibly fake mining apps or networks as well. To protect your crypto assets, it\'s helpful to know when and how you\'ll be targeted by scammers and what to do if you suspect that a cryptocurrency or related communication is a scam (Murphy, 2023).</span></span></span></p><br><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">&nbsp;</span></span></span><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Cryptocurrency scams fall into two main categories:</span></span></span></p><ul style=\"padding-inline-start: 48px;\"><li dir=\"ltr\" style=\"list-style-type: disc; font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre; margin-left: 59.35pt;\" aria-level=\"1\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\" role=\"presentation\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">A. Initiatives aimed at gaining access to the target\'s digital wallet or authentication credentials. This means that fraudsters try to get hold of information that gives them access to a digital wallet or other types of private information, such as security codes. In some cases, this may also include access to physical hardware such as a computer or smartphone.</span></span></span></p></li><li dir=\"ltr\" style=\"list-style-type: disc; font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre; margin-left: 59.35pt;\" aria-level=\"1\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\" role=\"presentation\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">B. &nbsp;Scams that involve transferring your cryptocurrency directly to a scammer triggered by a false identity, fraudulent investment or business opportunities, or other malicious methods are very popular (Murphy, 2023).</span></span></span></p></li></ul><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">&nbsp;</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Why are DeFi platforms vulnerable to fraud?</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Digital assets come in various forms, but most crimes are related to cryptocurrencies. Cryptocurrencies are always portrayed as encrypted, transparent, non-centralized money that does not rely on intermediaries such as brokers, exchanges, or banks and is protected from manipulation by governments and central banks. However, they do not prove to be completely safe from fraud: while it is entirely possible to trace cryptocurrency transactions, you are only given an address. This leaves room for hackers and fraudsters to develop intelligent computer codes to steal money (Miller, 2022). Digital assets are not a scam per se, but they can attract scammers due to their complexity and high profit potential. You can reduce your risk of becoming a victim of a scam by following the most important best practices.</span></span></span></p><br><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Types of cryptocurrency DeFi scams:</span></span></span></p><ul style=\"padding-inline-start: 48px;\"><li dir=\"ltr\" style=\"list-style-type: disc; font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre; margin-left: 59.35pt;\" aria-level=\"1\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\" role=\"presentation\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Social engineering scams: In social engineering scams, the perpetrators use psychological manipulation and deception to gain control of important information about user accounts. Successful scammers trick people into thinking they\'re dealing with a trusted entity, such as a government agency, a well-known company, tech support, a member of the community, a work colleague, or a friend.&nbsp;Scammers take as much time as they need to gain the trust of a potential victim. Then, they can eventually ask the person to reveal private keys that allow them to authorize transactions and prove ownership of a blockchain asset in order to send money to their digital wallet.&nbsp;If one of these \"trustworthy\" people asks for cryptocurrency for some reason, it is a sign of something unusual—a scam.</span></span></span></p></li></ul><br><ul style=\"padding-inline-start: 48px;\"><li dir=\"ltr\" style=\"list-style-type: disc; font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre; margin-left: 59.35pt;\" aria-level=\"1\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\" role=\"presentation\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Scammers who promise romance: Scammers often use dating websites to trick unsuspecting people into believing they\'re in a real relationship, whether it\'s a new one or a long-term one. Once the person trusts the scammer, conversations often shift to supposedly lucrative cryptocurrency opportunities and the possible transfer of coins or account authentication credentials. According to the FTC, scams that promise romance are number 1 of all money scams. The average amount swindled amounts to a total of 1.3 billion dollars. (Fletcher, 2023)</span></span></span></p></li></ul><br><ul style=\"padding-inline-start: 48px;\"><li dir=\"ltr\" style=\"list-style-type: disc; font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre; margin-left: 59.35pt;\" aria-level=\"1\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\" role=\"presentation\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Scammers and giveaways: Scammers also try to impersonate celebrities, businessmen, or cryptocurrency influencers. To attract the attention of potential victims, many scammers promise to double or multiply the cryptocurrency sent to them, which is known as a \"giveaway.\" Well-crafted messages, which often look like an existing social media account, can create and evoke a sense of validity and urgency. This mythical \"once-in-a-lifetime\" opportunity can entice people to transfer money quickly in the hope of getting an immediate repayment. Scammers posing as members of the cryptocurrency exchange\'s support or security team also contact crypto owners to rob them of their money. (Federal Bureau of Investigation, 2022)</span></span></span></p></li></ul><br><ul style=\"padding-inline-start: 48px;\"><li dir=\"ltr\" style=\"list-style-type: disc; font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre; margin-left: 59.35pt;\" aria-level=\"1\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\" role=\"presentation\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Phishing: In the cryptocurrency industry, phishing scams target information related to online wallets. The scammers send an email with links that lead to a specially set-up website and ask them to enter private keys. Once the hackers have this information, they steal the victim\'s cryptocurrencies. \"Phishing scams are among the most common attacks on consumers. According to the FBI, more than 300,000 people fell victim to phishing scams in 2022. Combined, these people handed over $52 million to the fraudsters.\" (Federal Bureau of Investigation, 2022)</span></span></span></p></li></ul><div><font color=\"#000000\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><img src=\"https://ptgr-bucket.s3.us-east-1.amazonaws.com/chapter4/3.jpg\" alt=\"Image\" class=\"max-w-full h-auto rounded-lg my-4 cursor-move hover:ring-2 hover:ring-primary/50 transition-all\" referrerpolicy=\"no-referrer\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto;\"></span></font></div><div><span id=\"docs-internal-guid-9091a885-7fff-3ac2-42c1-82dc9f710a11\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 8pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\"><span style=\"font-size: 14px;\">Figure 1: Pishing scams</span></span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 8pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\"><span style=\"font-size: 14px;\">(Source: tabtrader.com)</span></span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><br></p><ul style=\"padding-inline-start: 48px;\"><li dir=\"ltr\" style=\"list-style-type: disc; font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre; margin-left: 59.35pt;\" aria-level=\"1\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\" role=\"presentation\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Blackmail and extortion schemes: Blackmail is another popular social engineering method used by scammers. Extortionists claim to potential victims that they have a record of adult websites or other illegal websites that the user visits. The extortionists then threaten to expose the people if they do not give them their private keys or cryptocurrencies.</span></span></span></p></li></ul><br><ul style=\"padding-inline-start: 48px;\"><li dir=\"ltr\" style=\"list-style-type: disc; font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre; margin-left: 59.35pt;\" aria-level=\"1\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\" role=\"presentation\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Scam Airdrops -&gt; New Crypto-Based Opportunities: ICOs and NFTs Crypto-based investments, such as Initial Coin Offerings (ICOs) and non-fungible tokens (NFTs), offer scammers even more ways to get their hands on your money. Keep in mind that while the crypto-based investments or business opportunities may sound lucrative, they don\'t always correspond to reality. For example, some scammers create fake websites for ICOs and instruct users to deposit cryptocurrency into a compromised digital wallet. In other cases, the ICO itself may be to blame. The founders could distribute tokens that are not regulated or mislead investors through false advertising about their products.</span></span></span></p></li></ul><div><font color=\"#000000\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><img src=\"https://ptgr-bucket.s3.us-east-1.amazonaws.com/chapter4/4.jpg\" alt=\"Image\" class=\"max-w-full h-auto rounded-lg my-4 cursor-move hover:ring-2 hover:ring-primary/50 transition-all\" referrerpolicy=\"no-referrer\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto;\"><br></span></font></div><div><span id=\"docs-internal-guid-cf5713ca-7fff-6601-a2e9-73a2000b3728\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 8pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\"><span style=\"font-size: 14px;\">Figure 2: Scam Airdrops</span></span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;background-color:#ffffff;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 8pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\"><span style=\"font-size: 14px;\">(Source: tabtrader.com)</span></span></span></span></p><br><ul style=\"padding-inline-start: 48px;\"><li dir=\"ltr\" style=\"list-style-type: disc; font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre; margin-left: 59.35pt;\" aria-level=\"1\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\" role=\"presentation\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Rug Pulls: A rug pull occurs when project members raise capital or crypto to fund a project and then suddenly withdraw all liquidity—and they themselves disappear and become unreachable. The scammers abandon the project, and the investors lose everything they have contributed (TabTrader Team, 2022).</span></span></span></p></li></ul><div><font color=\"#000000\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><img src=\"https://ptgr-bucket.s3.us-east-1.amazonaws.com/chapter4/5.jpg\" alt=\"Image\" class=\"max-w-full h-auto rounded-lg my-4 cursor-move hover:ring-2 hover:ring-primary/50 transition-all\" referrerpolicy=\"no-referrer\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto;\"><br></span></font></div><div><span id=\"docs-internal-guid-b55b6906-7fff-8e68-bcc9-0b760261cec8\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 8pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\"><span style=\"font-size: 14px;\">Figure 3: Rug Pull Scams</span></span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 8pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\"><span style=\"font-size: 14px;\">(Source: </span></span></span></span><a href=\"http://tabtrader.com/\" style=\"text-decoration:none;\"><span style=\"font-size: 8pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 255); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; text-decoration-line: underline; text-decoration-skip-ink: none; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\"><span style=\"font-size: 14px;\">tabtrader.com(opens in a new tab)</span></span></span></span></a><span style=\"font-size: 8pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\"><span style=\"font-size: 14px;\">)</span></span></span></span></p><br><ul style=\"padding-inline-start: 48px;\"><li dir=\"ltr\" style=\"list-style-type: disc; font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre; margin-left: 59.35pt;\" aria-level=\"1\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\" role=\"presentation\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Cloud mining: Platforms solicit an upfront payment from retail investors and investors to secure a continuous stream of mining power and rewards. These platforms do not own the hash rate they claim they do and do not deliver the rewards after receiving your deposit. While cloud mining is not necessarily a scam, in order to keep your money, you need to thoroughly vet the platform before investing.</span></span></span></p></li></ul><br><ul style=\"padding-inline-start: 48px;\"><li dir=\"ltr\" style=\"list-style-type: disc; font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre; margin-left: 59.35pt;\" aria-level=\"1\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\" role=\"presentation\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Pump and dump: To avoid falling for a pump-and-dump scam, Defi users should carefully consider the feasibility of the promises of a DeFi project they want to invest in.</span></span></span></p></li></ul><div><font color=\"#000000\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><img src=\"https://ptgr-bucket.s3.us-east-1.amazonaws.com/chapter4/6.jpg\" alt=\"Image\" class=\"max-w-full h-auto rounded-lg my-4 cursor-move hover:ring-2 hover:ring-primary/50 transition-all\" referrerpolicy=\"no-referrer\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto;\"><br></span></font></div><div><span id=\"docs-internal-guid-1e507f04-7fff-c1eb-bf06-1898413c3a35\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 8pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\"><span style=\"font-size: 14px;\">Figure 4: Pump and Dump</span></span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 8pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\"><span style=\"font-size: 14px;\">(Source: tabtrader.com)</span></span></span></span></p><br><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Protective measures and risk avoidance</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Here are some steps investors can take to protect themselves from DeFi scams:</span></span></span></p><ul style=\"padding-inline-start: 48px;\"><li dir=\"ltr\" style=\"list-style-type: disc; font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre; margin-left: 59.35pt;\" aria-level=\"1\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\" role=\"presentation\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Don\'t share your private keys</span></span></span></p></li><li dir=\"ltr\" style=\"list-style-type: disc; font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre; margin-left: 59.35pt;\" aria-level=\"1\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\" role=\"presentation\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Don\'t invest in projects that lack background information</span></span></span></p></li><li dir=\"ltr\" style=\"list-style-type: disc; font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre; margin-left: 59.35pt;\" aria-level=\"1\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\" role=\"presentation\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Download only apps from official platforms</span></span></span></p></li><li dir=\"ltr\" style=\"list-style-type: disc; font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre; margin-left: 59.35pt;\" aria-level=\"1\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\" role=\"presentation\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Do not open links or attachments in emails or SMS messages from unknown sources</span></span></span></p></li><li dir=\"ltr\" style=\"list-style-type: disc; font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre; margin-left: 59.35pt;\" aria-level=\"1\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\" role=\"presentation\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Double-check the URLs of crypto-related websites.</span></span></span></p></li><li dir=\"ltr\" style=\"list-style-type: disc; font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre; margin-left: 59.35pt;\" aria-level=\"1\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\" role=\"presentation\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Use multi-factor authentication in your crypto wallet</span></span></span></p></li></ul><div><font color=\"#000000\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><img src=\"https://ptgr-bucket.s3.us-east-1.amazonaws.com/chapter4/7.jpg\" alt=\"Image\" class=\"max-w-full h-auto rounded-lg my-4 cursor-move hover:ring-2 hover:ring-primary/50 transition-all\" referrerpolicy=\"no-referrer\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto;\"><br></span></font></div><div><span id=\"docs-internal-guid-362660fd-7fff-eca5-7cc2-a8a13ee3ac5e\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 8pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\"><span style=\"font-size: 14px;\">Figure 5: How to protect yourself from Defi scams</span></span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 8pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\"><span style=\"font-size: 14px;\">(Source: tabtrader.com)</span></span></span></span></p><div><span style=\"font-size: 8pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><br></span>\n</div></span></div></span></div></span></div></span></div></span></div></span>\n</div>',1,0,70,'2025-09-12 06:46:46','2025-10-08 10:10:35'),(211,33,'Experiences and case studies','<div class=\"prose prose-lg max-w-none space-y-4\">\n\n<span id=\"docs-internal-guid-81bcf576-7fff-d56a-389e-bae690276cbf\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\">Case 1 Example:</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\">In January 2023, the&nbsp;</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\">The Commodity Futures Trading Commission (CFTC) filed a civil enforcement action accusing Avraham Eisenberg of unlawfully acquiring over $110 million in digital assets from Mango Markets, an alleged decentralized digital asset exchange, through price inflation using \"oracle manipulation\" (CFTC, 2023). The CFTC alleges that Eisenberg set up two anonymous accounts with Mango Markets, which he used to build large leveraged positions in a swap contract based on the relative value of MNGO, Mango Markets\' \"native\" token, and USDC, a stablecoin. Eisenberg then allegedly artificially inflated the price of MNGO by rapidly buying significant amounts of MNGO on three digital asset exchanges that served as input to the \"oracle,\" or data feed, used to determine the value of Eisenberg\'s swap positions, resulting in a temporary, artificial increase in the value of Eisenberg\'s swap positions on Mango Markets. The CFTC alleges that Eisenberg then cashed out his illicit profits by using the artificially inflated value of his swaps as collateral to withdraw over $110 million in digital assets from Mango Markets. In a separate indictment filed in January 2023, the U.S. The The The Attorney\'s Office for the Southern District of New York charged Eisenberg with merchandise fraud, merchandise market manipulation, and fraud (DOJ, 2023).&nbsp;</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\">You can learn more about the Eisenberg’s case here:</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\">Alleged Perpetrator of $100 Million Crypto Market Manipulation Scheme to Make Initial Appearance in the Southern District of New York. https://www.justice.gov/usao-sdny/pr/alleged-perpetrator-100-million-cryptomarket-manipulation-scheme-make-initial Eisenberg has also been indicted by the SEC in connection with his alleged manipulation of the MNGO token (SEC, 2023).</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><br></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\">Case 2 Example:</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\">In November 2022, the Department of Justice (DOJ) announced that James Zhong had pleaded guilty to committing wire fraud in September 2012 when he unlawfully obtained more than 50,000 Bitcoin from the Silk Road internet marketplace. In September 2012, Zhong executed a plan to defraud Silk Road of its money and property by (a) setting up a series of about nine Silk Road accounts in a manner designed to disguise his identity; (b) triggering over 140 transactions in rapid succession to trick Silk Road\'s withdrawal system into releasing approximately 50,000 Bitcoin from its Bitcoin-based payment system into Zhong\'s accounts; and (c) transferring this Bitcoin to a variety of separate addresses, which are also under Zhong\'s control, in a way that aims to prevent detection, disguise his identity and property, and disguise the origin of the Bitcoin. (DOJ, 2022) As part of this process, Zhong allegedly pushed about 750 Bitcoin of Silk Road proceeds through a decentralized Bitcoin mixer. (DOJ, 2022) In November 2021, law enforcement seized more than 50,000 Bitcoin from Zhong\'s home. This was the largest cryptocurrency seizure in the history of the DOJ at the time and the second-largest financial seizure ever by the department in November 2022 (DOJ, 2022).</span></span></span></p><br><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\">Examples of scams include:</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:12pt;margin-bottom:12pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\">Several U.S. government agencies are tracking and publishing complaints from the public related to virtual assets, indicating a sharp increase in losses related to virtual assets. Federal agencies have also issued warnings regarding their findings, including noting a significant increase in virtual assets as a payment method for all types of fraud (United States Secret Service, n.d.). In fact, the Federal Bureau of Investigation\'s (FBI) Internet Crime Complaint Center (IC3) reported that while the number of virtual asset-related complaints decreased by about 3 percent in 2021, the amount of losses reported in IC3 virtual asset-related complaints increased by nearly 600 percent—from $246 million in 2020 to more than $1.6 billion in 2021 (FBI, 2021). These scams often result in significant losses for victims. These scams are committed by a variety of actors who use a range of techniques, including \"rug pulls\" and \"pig butchering\" schemes.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:12pt;margin-bottom:12pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\">Updated figures:</span></span></span><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"> IC3/FBI reports show cryptocurrency-related investment fraud losses of ≈$2.57 billion in 2022 and ≈$3.96 billion in 2023, with further large totals reported in 2024. Phishing, investment scams, and impersonation scams remain major loss drivers. Always consult the latest IC3/FBI annual and cryptocurrency fraud reports for the most recent numbers (ic3.gov, Federal Bureau of Investigation).</span></span></span></p><br><ul style=\"padding-inline-start: 48px;\"><li dir=\"ltr\" style=\"list-style-type: disc; font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre; margin-left: 59.35pt;\" aria-level=\"1\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\" role=\"presentation\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\">In a \"rug pull,\" a scammer collects investment funds for a seemingly legitimate project before terminating the project and stealing the invested funds. Rug pulls can involve scammers creating and contributing to a liquidity pool in a DeFi service in the form of a new virtual asset, often a stablecoin. A form of rug pull is executed when a creator promotes the asset to investors to increase demand and thereby increase the price of the virtual asset and then abruptly withdraws its contributions from the liquidity pool, causing the price of the new virtual asset to crash. Rug pulls can also involve restrictions on the sale of assets by investors or the coding of an explicit, malicious backdoor into a new virtual asset smart contract that allows the developer to withdraw assets from a liquidity pool at once. (Financial Stability Oversight Council, 2022)</span></span></span></p></li><li dir=\"ltr\" style=\"list-style-type: disc; font-size: 9pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre; margin-left: 59.35pt;\" aria-level=\"1\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\" role=\"presentation\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\">In \"pig butchering,\" scammers build relationships with their victims and pressure them to invest in fake investment platforms that allow the scammers to steal the invested funds. (DOJ, 2022); (FINRA, 2022) These scammers meet their victims through dating apps, social media sites, or even text messages that seem to have been sent to the wrong number by mistake. After building trust with their target over a period of weeks or months, the scammers introduce the idea of investing in virtual assets and use confidence-building techniques to convince victims that they are investing in a legitimate virtual asset opportunity. A common variant of the scam is that users access fraudulent investment websites through virtual wallet applications, where vulnerabilities are exploited to give the scammers full access and control over victims\' wallets.</span></span></span></p></li></ul><br><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\">Case 3 Examples:</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\">In June 2022, the DOJ and law enforcement partners announced that Le Anh Tuan, a 26-year-old Vietnamese citizen, had been charged in the Central District of California with conspiracy to commit fraud via cable and conspiracy to commit international money laundering in connection with a scheme involving the non-fungible token (NFT) \"Baller Ape\" (DOJ, 2022). Tuan was allegedly involved in the Baller Ape Club, an NFT investment project that allegedly sold NFTs in the form of various cartoon characters, often including the figure of a monkey. According to the indictment, Tuan and his co-conspirators performed a \"rug pull\" shortly after the first day that Baller Ape Club\'s NFTs were publicly sold, ending the alleged investment project, deleting the website, and stealing investors\' money. Based on blockchain analysis, Tuan and his co-conspirators reportedly laundered investors\' funds through \"chain-hopping\" shortly after the \"rug pull,\" a form of money laundering in which one type of coin is converted into another type of coin and funds are moved across multiple cryptocurrency blockchains, and they used decentralized cryptocurrency exchange services to cover the trail of Baller Ape Club investors\' stolen funds. In total, Tuan and his co-conspirators are said to have received about $2.6 million from investors.</span></span></span></p><br><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\">Case 4 Examples:</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\">In March 2022, the DOJ, the Internal Revenue Service-Criminal Investigation, the Department of Homeland Security, and the U.S. Postal Inspection Service announced that Ethan Nguyen and Andre Llacuna had been charged with conspiracy to commit wire fraud and conspiracy to commit money laundering in a criminal complaint related to a $1.1 million scheme to defraud NFT buyers, which were advertised as \"Frosties.\" Instead of giving the advertised benefits to buyers of the Frosties NFTs, Nguyen and Llacuna reportedly transferred the plan\'s cryptocurrency proceeds to various cryptocurrency wallets under their control (DOJ, 2022). As alleged in the criminal complaint, after the sale of Frostie\'s NFT was publicly denounced as a scam on social media, significant amounts of Ether (ETH) were sent to Tornado Cash smart contracts from the wallets associated with the defendants. These smart contracts later transferred ETH to a wallet address, which eventually deposited the funds into wallets of the defendants.</span></span></span></p><div><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><br></span>\n</div></span>\n</div>',1,0,70,'2025-09-12 06:48:06','2025-10-08 10:11:04'),(212,33,'DeFi Services and AML/CFT Regulation Obligations','<div class=\"prose prose-lg max-w-none space-y-4\">\n\n<span id=\"docs-internal-guid-5d384e8e-7fff-d4e8-22dc-aacca2c3ccff\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\">Despite its promising opportunities, DeFi is in a legal and regulatory gray area. Traditional regulatory frameworks created for centralized financial systems are challenged by the decentralized and borderless nature of DeFi protocols. As a result, many features of DeFi are beyond the reach of existing regulations, leaving participants and regulators perplexed. Efforts are being made to address the legal and regulatory ambiguities of DeFi. Regulators around the world are beginning to understand the need to foster innovation while protecting investors and ensuring financial stability. They are looking for methods to change existing —inations or develop new frameworks to provide clarity and control in the DeFi space. DeFi operates on a decentralized infrastructure, often based on blockchain technology, that enables trustless transactions and eliminates intermediaries. This unique framework poses challenges for regulators as traditional supervisory mechanisms struggle to adapt. Regulators are faced with issues such as establishing case law, consumer protection, anti-money laundering (AML), and privacy. Tackling these complexities requires collaboration and dialogue between regulators and DeFi-related companies to develop an appropriate regulatory framework. The AML (Anti-Money Laundering) / CFT (Countering the Financing of Terrorism) obligations in the United States are based on the activities that a person engages in. The BSA (Bank Secrecy Act) and its implementing regulations state that \"financial institutions\" such as banks, broker-dealers, mutual funds, money services companies (MSBs), Futures Commission Merchants (FCMs), and Introducing Brokers have AML/CFT obligations. These AML/CFT obligations include the obligation to establish and implement an effective anti-money laundering (AML) program, as well as record-keeping and reporting obligations, including the obligation to report suspicious activity (SAR). The type of activities an individual engages in is the key factor in deciding whether and how that individual must register with the Commodities Future Trading Commission (CFTC) (for FCMs and Introducing Brokers), the Financial Crimes Enforcement Network (FinCEN) (for MSBs), or the Securities and Exchange Commission (SEC) (for broker-dealers and mutual funds). While the degree of centralization of a person could affect the services he provides, persons carrying out the activities of financial institutions as defined by the BSA will have these obligations, whether they are centralized or decentralised. For example, if a DeFi service operates wholly or substantially in the United States and accepts and transfers virtual assets from one person to another person or location, then it would most likely be considered a money transmitter and have the same AML/CFT obligations as a money transmitter offering services in fiat currency. (FinCen, 2019) The degree of decentralization of a service does not affect these obligations, as long as the service meets this definition. The industry claims that there is insufficient regulatory clarity in this area. The industry often states that there is a lack of clarity on what counts as a security, which regulators they need to register with, and whether their DeFi services meet the definition of a financial institution under the BSA or other regulatory frameworks. The industry has also publicly approached the Treasury Department for more clarity on when the provision of software becomes an activity covered by the BSA. The CFTC, FinCEN, and SEC dispute the view that there is insufficient regulatory clarity, citing guidance they have issued over the past 10 years (SEC, 2019) and—in the case of the CFTC and SEC—enforcement actions against alleged DeFi services that have failed to meet regulatory obligations. (SEC, n.d.) Through public statements, guidance, and enforcement actions, these authorities have made it clear that the automation of certain functions through smart contracts or computers does not affect the obligations of financial institutions providing covered services. (SEC, 2017) In addition, DeFi services that are U.S. individuals, like all other U.S. persons, regardless of their location, must comply with economic sanctions programs administered and enforced by the U.S. Treasury Department\'s Office of Foreign Assets Control (OFAC), while non-U.S. persons must also comply with OFAC sanctions in certain circumstances. The obligations to comply with sanctions are the same whether a transaction is made in virtual assets or traditional fiat currency. (OFAC, n.d.) In addition, in 2021, OFAC issued the \"Sanctions Compliance Guidance for the Virtual Currency Industry,\" which outlines sanctions compliance obligations, reporting requirements, and best practices. OFAC has subsequently published several FAQs (Treasury, 2021) on sanctions compliance obligations and virtual assets.</span></span></span></p><br><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\">Conclusions.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\">Due to the rapid expansion of DeFi, legal and regulatory questions have arisen. To navigate the gray areas, regulators, industry participants, and legal experts need to work together. Stakeholders can strike a balance between supporting innovation and ensuring compliance by adapting existing laws, adopting new frameworks, and seeking self-regulation. While there are limits, it\'s important to acknowledge the transformative potential of DeFi. DeFi has the potential to transform the traditional financial system and promote global financial inclusion by enabling open and permissionless access to financial services. The legal and regulatory issues related to DeFi can be solved through smart regulation and collaboration, allowing this innovative sector to develop while protecting the interests of participants and maintaining financial stability. The decentralized finance sector is thriving, with new, innovative projects emerging every day—this is great news for anyone who believes in the future of DeFi and Web 3.0 innovation in general. However, with great innovation comes with the risk of being scammed—DeFi scams are no exception. Now that you know what a crypto scam is and how to spot crypto scams, you should be able to trade with much greater confidence and invest in different projects. Of course, you should always do your own research—this cannot be emphasized enough! Be critical of opportunities that seem too good to be true, and only trust apps and software from official sources!</span></span></span></p><div><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><br></span>\n</div></span>\n</div>',1,1,70,'2025-09-12 06:49:21','2025-10-08 10:11:31'),(213,34,'Applications of Blockchain and Tokenization in Energy Markets.','<div class=\"prose prose-lg max-w-none space-y-4\">\n\n<span id=\"docs-internal-guid-d3942e94-7fff-5078-551a-5f93687e58fd\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\">All functions of society are based on the use of energy. The basic laws of thermodynamics state that any activity requires energy in some form. Cooking, moving, heating, and cleaning—even the simple act of thinking requires energy, whether you are an animal or a computer. Energy has long been a limited resource for societies that are constantly under pressure to increase their supply to support an expansion of human society and its activities. As energy consumption increases, so does the buying, selling, and tracking of data related to energy resources, a significant portion of commercial transactions in the global economy. In recent years, energy companies have made record profits, highlighting their importance. The Industrial Revolution began with the development of energy sources on an industrial scale, first with the fast-flowing waterways in England for mass production by mills and then with the shift to coal-powered steam engines. The heavy reliance on coal and fossil fuels led to a search for cheap and widely available sources of energy, and nuclear power at one point aroused great interest and the idea of unlimited energy. But in the last century, nuclear research programs proved insufficient to meet expectations—and so dependence on oil remained. The ensuing oil crisis in the mid-1970s, which led to a shortage of oil and skyrocketing prices in the Western world as a result of an embargo imposed by members of the Organization of Arab Petroleum Exporting Countries (OAPEC), and today\'s greenhouse gas crisis have shown how energy resources must be properly managed. Global energy consumption amounts to about 580 million terajoules annually—that is 580 million trillion joules, which would require 13,865 million tons of oil equivalents. Energy consumption is also increasing. Since 2000, it has increased by a third and is expected to reach 740 million terajoules by 2040, a further increase of 30%. More than 80% of the energy consumed today still comes from fossil fuels, which could mean enormous amounts of greenhouse gas emissions that will exacerbate global warming if we don\'t switch the energy supply mix to renewables. In this context, our shared responsibility for our environment, as stewards of our planet, arises to avoid the depletion of natural resources and to maintain the ecological balance that ensures our long-term coexistence as a society. According to a global survey led by the University of Bath, the threat of climate change and dissatisfaction with government responses are causing anxiety among young people up to the age of 25 that is impacting their daily lives. 59% of respondents said they were very or extremely concerned about climate change, while 84% said they were at least moderately concerned. Accountability in the management and distribution of clean energy resources is facilitated by technology and open data, especially as demand for renewable energy and decarbonization initiatives comes to the fore. Blockchain technology can reduce costs and improve efficiency, thus increasing access to low-carbon energy sources at a low cost for individuals and communities. Blockchain technology also ensures transparency in supply chains, ensuring the ethical sourcing and consumption of clean energy, as well as the tracking of greenhouse gas emissions. This will encourage the adoption of clean energy and help resolve the conflict between heating our homes and doing so in a sustainable way. The advent of blockchain in the electricity industry can be a crucial step in meeting the commitments of the Paris Agreement. Like the accident insurance industry described above, the energy sector includes a trio of elements that make it a likely beneficiary of smart contracts: AI, blockchain, and cryptocurrency. According to a report by the International Council on Large Electric Systems (CIGRE), \"Blockchain technology has spawned many technological frameworks, of which Powerledger and Ethereum are the most important.\" To date, these two primary frameworks have largely set the stage for blockchain use cases to be used in the energy sector. A transparent and enhanced ledger based on blockchain technology can facilitate a wider range of transactions in support of energy distribution and energy trading, thus improving access to clean energy and accountability in the process. Democratization can also put individuals at the center of the energy transition by incentivizing and providing economic benefits for individuals and underrepresented communities by enabling fractional ownership of energy assets, empowering individuals in their energy consumption choices, encouraging peer-to-peer trading, and enabling energy investment at the retail level.</span></span></span></p></span>\n\n</div>',1,0,70,'2025-09-12 06:50:23','2025-10-08 10:11:58'),(214,34,'Peer-to-peer (P2P) trading with decentralized energy.','<div class=\"prose prose-lg max-w-none space-y-4\">\n\n<span id=\"docs-internal-guid-748bccb2-7fff-043d-b92a-26082a73156c\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\">Fossil fuels, nuclear energy, and hydropower, which provide very stable electricity production, fit well into the classic centralized grid model, which provides for fixed energy prices. Renewable energy sources (RES), such as solar and wind power, on the other hand, provide highly variable electricity that is supplied in batches that are often remote and can have devastating consequences in a centralized system. When wind and solar energy emerged as energy sources capable of producing electricity for the grid, it became clear that these energy sources can be found in numerous locations that are generally not close to large power plants. Wind and solar farms are usually built in places where there is no major connection to the grid, so their energy production is essentially stranded. The states could then invest in strengthening the network, but this could make these projects uneconomical. Adding to this geographical problem was the time challenge that the volatile nature of solar and wind energy required significant additional investment in battery storage systems (BESS). This would also be uneconomical. The two problems of place and time thus reflect the uniqueness of renewable energies compared to traditional energies and show why promoting their scale requires a different approach. In this context, the energy market came to a new realization: that the owners of even relatively small energy sources can trade electricity with each other. They could agree on a price and sell a certain amount of electricity at a time and place of their choosing. As the development of these new local markets leads to efficiency gains, more battery solutions will be integrated into the system, allowing demand peaks and throttling periods to be better managed at better prices, accelerating the green energy transition. This formed the basis for peer-to-peer electricity trading, which inherently lends itself to a decentralized energy model that uses blockchain technology to track all underlying financial transactions. If the paradigm shifts from centralized to decentralized distribution, the contributions of renewable energies can fit much better into the energy grids. Localized and flexible pricing works well with a decentralized approach, and the flexibility of pricing helps balance a system characterized by variability in supply and intermittent bottlenecks in the grid to enable efficient energy distribution. The role of blockchain in decentralized systems: In the new peer-to-peer paradigm, where numerous actors can trade energy with each other, there are also numerous transactions that need to be tracked. Energy customers now interact as equals and no longer as customers or dependents of a centralized organization. This approach opens up the market to smaller energy producers who can contribute to the energy supply. What is the best approach to billing in a distributed environment where transactions are frequent and low-margin and where there is a flat hierarchy that requires security? Blockchain, as already described, offers an immutable and trustworthy way to manage a database (ledger). It also pairs well with smart contracts that are suitable for peer-to-peer trading. Smart contracts use computerized embedded code that sets out the terms, rules, and conditions of bilateral trade agreements. Once the peer-to-peer trading contracts are written on the blockchain platform, with the tokens on the blockchain representing the amounts of energy and the currency used to buy and sell, the transactions are permanently stored and generally cannot be changed or removed. Billing is seamless between peer-to-peer traders based on real-time data even before the billing cycle is reconciled, so energy traders\' and grid operators\' margins are not affected. Confidentiality and data protection are also ensured by symmetric and asymmetric encryption and anonymous signatures. At the end of each billing cycle, the peer-to-peer transaction data stored on the blockchain is sent to the energy traders and grid operators to complete the transactions. Since the entire process is handled through smart contracts, the model of energy traders as we know it is changing significantly.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\">Here\'s an example transaction: Customer A produces 150 kWh in September and, after settling the peer-to-peer trade of 100 kWh, sells the excess 50 kWh to an energy retailer in exchange for cryptocurrency tokens or their equivalent in fiat currency. A certain amount is transferred to customer A\'s account, which he can then spend on charging an electric vehicle at any location in the energy trader\'s network. The energy retailer can then charge transaction fees and benefit from this process.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><br></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><br></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><img src=\"https://ptgr-bucket.s3.us-east-1.amazonaws.com/chapter4/8.png\" alt=\"Image\" class=\"max-w-full h-auto rounded-lg my-4 cursor-move hover:ring-2 hover:ring-primary/50 transition-all\" referrerpolicy=\"no-referrer\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto;\"><br></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size:8pt;font-family:Poppins,sans-serif;color:#000000;background-color:transparent;font-weight:400;font-style:normal;font-variant:normal;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;white-space:pre;white-space:pre-wrap;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 14px;\">Figure 1: Customer A trades excess energy to the energy retailer in exchange for crypto or fiat currency</span></span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size:8pt;font-family:Poppins,sans-serif;color:#000000;background-color:transparent;font-weight:400;font-style:normal;font-variant:normal;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;white-space:pre;white-space:pre-wrap;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 14px;\">(Source: GBBC)</span></span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><br></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt;font-family:Poppins,sans-serif;color:#000000;background-color:transparent;font-weight:400;font-style:normal;font-variant:normal;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;white-space:pre;white-space:pre-wrap;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\">In the current model, as shown in the diagram above, individuals consume energy directly from power sources. In a decentralized model, each individual can both consume and produce energy by owning subsets of energy-producing equipment such as solar panels, thus becoming a \"prosumer\"—i.e.,a producer and consumer. They can sell their surplus energy for a profit to other private individuals or even to the electricity grid. This flexibility and monetary incentives would encourage individual energy consumers operating under a traditional centralized model to participate in a decentralized model. At scale, peer-to-peer energy trading enables functions such as dynamic pricing, preferential trading, and giving/donating or managing surplus energy in a way that would not have been possible without blockchain applications. This new role, which consumers can play, will become a market-driven incentive to accelerate the deployment of decentralized energy resources. In this way, communities around the world can be freed from the need to rely on other financial incentives and government subsidies to promote renewable energy (e.g., feed-in tariffs or net metering to meet expansion targets). Ultimately, both blockchain and cryptocurrencies can offer significant benefits to peer-to-peer trading. Working with energy traders, who are key players in the process described above, is key to driving adoption.</span></span></span></p><div><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><br></span>\n</div></span>\n</div>',1,0,70,'2025-09-12 06:51:31','2025-10-08 10:12:43'),(215,34,'Local Energy Markets (LEM)','<div class=\"prose prose-lg max-w-none space-y-4\">\n\n<span id=\"docs-internal-guid-d79717c7-7fff-f0f5-5b24-db2aa32b6455\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span id=\"docs-internal-guid-2da95420-7fff-018a-e2b1-597a111f753b\"></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt;font-family:Poppins,sans-serif;color:#000000;background-color:transparent;font-weight:400;font-style:normal;font-variant:normal;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;white-space:pre;white-space:pre-wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">LEM is essentially a community of people who create a sub-market for electricity for each other. This can be any region or district where electricity is traded between players, allowing energy consumers to negotiate and decide on energy volumes and prices for each transaction. This flexibility facilitates clean energy integration by helping to manage shortages and surpluses of an electricity market when they occur. Similar to seasonal vegetables, where prices adjust to supply throughout the year, energy prices at the local level also adjust to seasonal changes in supply, which are natural with renewable energy. In each LEM, clean energy supply is matched with energy demand at a reasonable price by applying advanced optimization techniques and restriction management. Any mismatch can be traded with the electricity grid on a business-as-usual (BAU) basis, i.e., surplus local energy is fed back into the grid at the feed-in tariff (FiT), while unmet demand is purchased at the time-of-use (ToU) prices. Distributed energy models using the underlying blockchain technology for data sets are best suited to manage energy distribution and underlying transactions, as opposed to centralized energy models. Centralized energy sources are usually constant, unlike decentralized energy sources, which are intermittent, as the following figures show.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt;font-family:Poppins,sans-serif;color:#000000;background-color:transparent;font-weight:400;font-style:normal;font-variant:normal;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;white-space:pre;white-space:pre-wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\"><br></span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt;font-family:Poppins,sans-serif;color:#000000;background-color:transparent;font-weight:400;font-style:normal;font-variant:normal;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;white-space:pre;white-space:pre-wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\"><br></span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt;font-family:Poppins,sans-serif;color:#000000;background-color:transparent;font-weight:400;font-style:normal;font-variant:normal;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;white-space:pre;white-space:pre-wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\"><img src=\"https://ptgr-bucket.s3.us-east-1.amazonaws.com/chapter4/9.png\" alt=\"Image\" class=\"max-w-full h-auto rounded-lg my-4 cursor-move hover:ring-2 hover:ring-primary/50 transition-all\" referrerpolicy=\"no-referrer\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto;\"><br></span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size:8pt;font-family:Poppins,sans-serif;color:#000000;background-color:transparent;font-weight:400;font-style:normal;font-variant:normal;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;white-space:pre;white-space:pre-wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\"><span style=\"font-size: 16px;\">Figure 2: Centralized energy (left): The ability to maintain a constant power supply supports a centralized structure and fixed prices. Distributed energy (DE) (right): Intermittent energy works well with a local energy market and dynamic, flexible pricing.</span></span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size:8pt;font-family:Poppins,sans-serif;color:#000000;background-color:transparent;font-weight:400;font-style:normal;font-variant:normal;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;white-space:pre;white-space:pre-wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\"><span style=\"font-size: 16px;\">(Source: GBBC)</span></span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt;font-family:Poppins,sans-serif;color:#000000;background-color:transparent;font-weight:400;font-style:normal;font-variant:normal;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;white-space:pre;white-space:pre-wrap;\"><br></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt;font-family:Poppins,sans-serif;color:#000000;background-color:transparent;font-weight:400;font-style:normal;font-variant:normal;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;white-space:pre;white-space:pre-wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Just as the physical topology of a city like Los Angeles is determined by the use of cars and is different from that of a small medieval village in the south of France, so is the energy grid. The differences in the physical structure of a grid reflect the differences in the technology behind it, as shown in the physical representations of energy flows below. The ideal grid for a future blockchain-based system will be designed for a decentralized flow of variable energy.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt;font-family:Poppins,sans-serif;color:#000000;background-color:transparent;font-weight:400;font-style:normal;font-variant:normal;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;white-space:pre;white-space:pre-wrap;\"><br></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size:8pt;font-family:Poppins,sans-serif;color:#000000;background-color:transparent;font-weight:400;font-style:normal;font-variant:normal;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;white-space:pre;white-space:pre-wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\"><img src=\"https://ptgr-bucket.s3.us-east-1.amazonaws.com/chapter4/10.jpg\" alt=\"Image\" class=\"max-w-full h-auto rounded-lg my-4 cursor-move hover:ring-2 hover:ring-primary/50 transition-all\" referrerpolicy=\"no-referrer\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto;\"><br></span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size:8pt;font-family:Poppins,sans-serif;color:#000000;background-color:transparent;font-weight:400;font-style:normal;font-variant:normal;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;white-space:pre;white-space:pre-wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">(Source: GBBC)</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt;font-family:Poppins,sans-serif;color:#000000;background-color:transparent;font-weight:400;font-style:normal;font-variant:normal;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;white-space:pre;white-space:pre-wrap;\"><b style=\"font-weight:normal;\" id=\"docs-internal-guid-965c5a37-7fff-5c8f-04c1-fb3535e6d118\"><br></b></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt;font-family:Poppins,sans-serif;color:#000000;background-color:transparent;font-weight:400;font-style:normal;font-variant:normal;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;white-space:pre;white-space:pre-wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">A number of studies have tested local energy markets, with promising results. Probably the most remarkable example is Pebbles, a demonstration and research project by Allgäu Netz, Allgäu Überlandwerk, Siemens, Kempten University of Applied Sciences, and Fraunhofer FIT. Pebbles led to increased self-consumption and less congestion of the power grid in the area. The project also enabled day-ahead and intraday trading, allowing the local market to provide flexibility for the larger network—a significant benefit.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt;font-family:Poppins,sans-serif;color:#000000;background-color:transparent;font-weight:400;font-style:normal;font-variant:normal;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;white-space:pre;white-space:pre-wrap;\"></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt;font-family:Poppins,sans-serif;color:#000000;background-color:transparent;font-weight:400;font-style:normal;font-variant:normal;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;white-space:pre;white-space:pre-wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">While Pebbles itself does not use blockchain technology, blockchain would be a suitable technology to help scale by transparently coding market rules using smart contracts. This would allow for real-time, peer-to-peer energy trading that is secure and verifiable and allows untrusted actors to collaborate and interact with each other securely, rather than relying on a centralized institution to maintain these markets without manipulation. As the chart below shows, local energy markets smooth out the troughs in electricity availability, thus promoting a balanced and efficient market. This will be further supported by battery storage systems to promote energy trading.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt;font-family:Poppins,sans-serif;color:#000000;background-color:transparent;font-weight:400;font-style:normal;font-variant:normal;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;white-space:pre;white-space:pre-wrap;\"><br></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size:11pt;font-family:Poppins,sans-serif;color:#000000;background-color:transparent;font-weight:400;font-style:normal;font-variant:normal;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;white-space:pre;white-space:pre-wrap;\"><img src=\"https://ptgr-bucket.s3.us-east-1.amazonaws.com/chapter4/11.jpg\" alt=\"Image\" class=\"max-w-full h-auto rounded-lg my-4 cursor-move hover:ring-2 hover:ring-primary/50 transition-all\" referrerpolicy=\"no-referrer\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto;\"><br></span></p><div><span style=\"font-size:11pt;font-family:Poppins,sans-serif;color:#000000;background-color:transparent;font-weight:400;font-style:normal;font-variant:normal;text-decoration:none;vertical-align:baseline;white-space:pre;white-space:pre-wrap;\"><span id=\"docs-internal-guid-88b67dd2-7fff-8c92-b5c9-597941128669\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 8pt; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\"><span style=\"font-size: 14px;\">Figure 4: Simulation of the impact of LEM on the grid</span></span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 8pt; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\"><span style=\"font-size: 14px;\">(Source: GBBC)</span></span></span></span></p><div><span style=\"font-size: 8pt; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline;\"><br></span>\n</div></span></span></div></span>\n</div>',1,0,70,'2025-09-12 06:54:13','2025-10-08 10:21:59'),(216,34,'Demand Response (DR) and Wholesale Energy Market (WEM)','<div class=\"prose prose-lg max-w-none space-y-4\">\n\n<span id=\"docs-internal-guid-07aae0f9-7fff-5c3b-2de1-17aeb235153e\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">DR is about managing energy demand, not supply. This often means a rescheduling of the activities of energy consumers, which is carried out exclusively by the electricity grid operators—either retailers, grid operators, or both. A classic candidate for demand management would be an aluminum smelter, which can save megawatts by shutting it down for a few hours, leaving the material hot enough for further processing without affecting the final product. Aluminum production consumes large amounts of electricity, which has led to major strategic challenges in view of rising energy prices. In Australia, which is one of the world\'s top 10 aluminium producers, energy and cost savings at smelters may be key to navigating the current environment. There are several other examples of smaller-scale demand reduction initiatives. Refrigeration in supermarkets, for example, can be lowered by a few degrees at certain controlled times without compromising the quality of the food. In Texas, an energy company ran a program where it could control the thermostats of the households. At the household level, individuals can optimize their own energy consumption by performing tasks that use more energy during periods of high supply and low prices (such as using washing machines and other appliances). In the future, developments in batteries and connected smart devices can also be programmed to optimize energy consumption in households, with blockchain providing the decentralized computing power, resulting in lower grid consumption and supported by additional battery storage capacity.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">As artificial intelligence plays a huge role in drawing patterns of energy demand to make informed predictions, it will be possible to predict the response of demand to these predictions in order to balance the grid. This is made possible by algorithms that analyze current data. The Wholesale Energy Market (WEM) is a pool-based electricity market that regulates the supply and trading of electricity at a wholesale price between energy retailers and upstream producers. In Western Australia, it is operated by the Australian Energy Market Operator (AEMO) under the rules of the WEM.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">The main objectives are:</span></span></span></p><ol style=\"padding-inline-start: 48px;\"><li dir=\"ltr\" style=\"list-style-type: decimal; font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre; margin-left: 59.35pt;\" aria-level=\"1\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\" role=\"presentation\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Reduce electricity costs in the long term</span></span></span></p></li><li dir=\"ltr\" style=\"list-style-type: decimal; font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre; margin-left: 59.35pt;\" aria-level=\"1\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\" role=\"presentation\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Control the efficient use of electricity</span></span></span></p></li><li dir=\"ltr\" style=\"list-style-type: decimal; font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre; margin-left: 59.35pt;\" aria-level=\"1\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\" role=\"presentation\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">promote price competition between energy retailers and upstream producers</span></span></span></p></li><li dir=\"ltr\" style=\"list-style-type: decimal; font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre; margin-left: 59.35pt;\" aria-level=\"1\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\" role=\"presentation\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Strengthen reliable and safe power generation and distribution</span></span></span></p></li></ol><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">In general, WEM works with three processes. The first process includes inputs for pre-market clearing, such as contracts, trade executions, regulations, and logistics. The second process consists of optimization, economic dispatch, and contingencies (all or some of them, depending on the jurisdiction). The third process includes processing, billing, and reporting. Blockchain technology can also be used to store WEM rules, regulations, and analytics using smart contracts. Financial trading to clear the WEM via the market clearing price (MCP) can also be conducted on a blockchain platform using digital assets. Executed trade information and subsequent settlements can also be permanently recorded on the blockchain ledger.</span></span></span></p></span>\n\n</div>',1,0,70,'2025-09-12 06:54:48','2025-10-08 10:13:40'),(217,34,'Renewable Energy Certificates (RECs)','<div class=\"prose prose-lg max-w-none space-y-4\">\n\n<span id=\"docs-internal-guid-6ca2914e-7fff-ef59-4664-873d41a3439f\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">RECs are market-based instruments that certify that the holder owns one megawatt-hour (MWh) of electricity generated from a renewable energy (RE) source. They are not utilities and act as a kind of tax break. An REC can be sold profitably as an energy commodity, such as a carbon credit, to other companies that want to offset their Scope 2 emissions, or to REC brokers or marketers, who then resell the energy credits on a secondary market at a higher price or commission. In various markets, this certificate can also be referred to as a Green Tag, Tradable Renewable Energy Certificate, Renewable Electricity Certificate, or Renewable Energy Loan. These credits allow the renewable part of the certificate to be traded independently of the underlying electricity, effectively supporting the financing of new renewable energy sources by increasing the price of the electricity then sold. This is a fundamental part of a book-and-claim system and forms the cornerstone of Energy Attribute Certificates (EACs) worldwide, which provide proof of electricity generated from renewable sources, of which RECs are a subgroup. The REC life cycle begins with the registration of the renewable energy generation plant. During this time, a third party checks the validity of the plant\'s renewable energy production capacity, fuel source, and location. Once reviewed and approved, the renewable energy producer can submit monthly generation data and request the output of the corresponding value in RECs for each megawatt hour (MWh) of renewable energy generated. To make this possible, the local REC issuer would have checked its generation data and then issued the corresponding amount of RECs directly to the corresponding register account. These registries, or tracking systems as they may also be called, are designed to establish, manage, and oversee the process of device registration, issuance of RECs, transfer of ownership, and decommissioning of RECs. Until the RECs are redeemed, canceled, or retired, REC marketers or brokers may act on behalf of renewable energy producers to sell their RECs to energy retailers or large enterprise customers. Energy retailers can buy RECs and decommission them on behalf of their customers under a \"green energy tariff\" or a \"green pricing program.\" These are optional programs offered by public utilities for regulated electricity markets, with government-approved electricity pricing structures that allow customers to source their electricity from renewables. Energy retailers can also use RECs to meet state or national regulatory requirements. Large companies can also buy and retire RECs to meet their voluntary environmental, social, and governance (ESG) goals. As both statutory and voluntary targets are set and measured annually, there is a final annual audit period for all decommissioned, redeemed and deleted RECs to verify that the voluntary or statutory targets have been met. Traditionally, all this accounting work comes with a number of potential difficulties that can arise from time to time. These include double counting or selling, errors in manual operations, system inefficiencies, market inefficiencies, transaction costs, and lack of visibility. RECs are therefore another promising use case for blockchain technology, and many REC markets around the world are already experimenting with it. The blockchain-based issuance of RECs also facilitates the instant transfer and settlement of ownership of RECs, minimizing counterparty risks. Blockchain also creates an immutable audit trail for ownership of RECs, from issuance to decommissioning. This can effectively solve the confidence problem of carbon credit markets, as cases of double counting have affected their credibility as reliable markets. Blockchain-based accounting systems can make the supply chain transparent, thus restoring the reputation and integrity of these markets.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">A blockchain-based REC marketplace can work as follows:</span></span></span></p><ol style=\"padding-inline-start: 48px;\"><li dir=\"ltr\" style=\"list-style-type: decimal; font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre; margin-left: 59.35pt;\" aria-level=\"1\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\" role=\"presentation\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Smart meters transmit generation data to the smart contract, which mints an REC for each MWh or in a granular time frame and allocates it to the asset owner\'s wallet. Alternatively, smart meters can send this data to a registry that controls the minting of RECs on the blockchain via a smart contract.</span></span></span></p></li><li dir=\"ltr\" style=\"list-style-type: decimal; font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre; margin-left: 59.35pt;\" aria-level=\"1\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\" role=\"presentation\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">All trading operations are recorded on the blockchain, creating a strict audit trail.</span></span></span></p></li><li dir=\"ltr\" style=\"list-style-type: decimal; font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre; margin-left: 59.35pt;\" aria-level=\"1\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\" role=\"presentation\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Settlement can be instantaneous, through the use of cryptocurrencies or through traditional means (bank transfers) that can be recorded and then added to the chain.</span></span></span></p></li><li dir=\"ltr\" style=\"list-style-type: decimal; font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre; margin-left: 59.35pt;\" aria-level=\"1\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\" role=\"presentation\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">In the event of a decommissioning, REC tokens are burned on the blockchain and thus permanently destroyed. If a new REC is minted on the chain that contains the generation data of a decommissioned REC, the registry can be alerted to investigate possible double spending.</span></span></span></p></li><li dir=\"ltr\" style=\"list-style-type: decimal; font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre; margin-left: 59.35pt;\" aria-level=\"1\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\" role=\"presentation\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Beneficiaries of the retirement can also be provided with a non-forgeable token (NFT) that certifies their consumption of RECs and adds value.</span></span></span></p></li></ol></span>\n\n</div>',1,0,70,'2025-09-12 06:55:37','2025-10-08 10:14:05'),(218,34,'24/7 CO²-free energy','<div class=\"prose prose-lg max-w-none space-y-4\">\n\n<span id=\"docs-internal-guid-cf527a0d-7fff-0279-d1e9-c8fdb2603da9\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">The concept of 24/7 carbon-free energy (CFE) is different from 100% carbon-free energy or 100% renewable energy. The term \"100% renewable energy\" refers to a company or organization buying renewable energy, which is not always an incentive for the most environmentally sustainable form of renewable energy. The energy that the company buys can be energy from fossil fuels, backed by an REC certificate. The incentive structures for 100% renewable energy can lead to renewable energy being wasted. On the other hand, 24/7 CFE is a more environmentally sustainable concept. Here, the use and generation of electricity are closely linked, i.e., down to 15-minute periods, these two are coordinated. This also allows the premium for the purchase of renewable energy to go to sources that are relevant to a reasonable energy demand. This approach therefore reduces the amount of fossil energy that is consumed. Compared to other processes, 24/7 CFE is a more accounting-intensive activity. It requires linking certificates to schedules and causes significant back-office work, all of which can be done by computers. Blockchain technology coupled with smart contracts can both provide the decentralized computing power to enable the massive computations and provide assurance that the back-office processes are carried out correctly, further reducing the susceptibility to fraud.</span></span></span></p></span>\n\n</div>',1,0,70,'2025-09-12 06:56:46','2025-10-08 10:14:39'),(219,34,'Metering, Billing and Security','<div class=\"prose prose-lg max-w-none space-y-4\">\n\n<span id=\"docs-internal-guid-7e3b0c85-7fff-b828-eb99-d2156332ecee\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Traditionally, energy measurement has been carried out manually and often on an estimated basis. However, as the need for finer-grained energy monitoring has increased, smart energy meters are increasingly being used to track energy consumption in real-time. By combining smart energy meters with the immutable data storage enabled by the blockchain, users will not only be able to have more confidence in real-time electricity billing, but it will also open doors for them to adjust their consumption based on current energy prices and interact with local energy markets in a transparent and secure way. Blockchain technology in this process greatly facilitates electronic settlement systems through seamless payment processing and trusted records, allowing all transactions to be carried out automatically without a centralized party in control. The producers of the meter data can finally own their data and share it with their counterparty for billing. These transactions are also easy to track and monitor. Also, the entire exchange history can be downloaded from the blockchain platform and used for regular settlement.</span></span></span></span>\n\n</div>',1,0,70,'2025-09-12 06:57:18','2025-10-08 10:15:10'),(220,34,'Network Management','<div class=\"prose prose-lg max-w-none space-y-4\">\n\n<span id=\"docs-internal-guid-9da9678c-7fff-3405-ddfd-e304b94a27ff\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Grid management sets the rules for managing various services of the electricity grid, such as capacity, flow direction, flexibility services, and security. The goal is to provide an effective solution for an increasingly complex distribution environment. The network management system features a flexible, interoperable, cyber-secure, and highly resilient design, providing the opportunity to upgrade to the best possible solution in the future. The grid management system replaces the existing Outage Management System (OMS) and the outdated Distributed Management System (DMS). It consists of an Advanced Distribution Management System (ADMS) and a Distributed Energy Resource Management System (DERMS). An ADMS retrieves the model of the power grid, processes it, and updates it down to the power subdistribution level. It helps you self-heal circuits, conduct real-time power grid studies, optimize power grid, generate reports, switch over planned and unplanned outages, and control supervisory control and data acquisition (SCADA) systems. It has another important feature, mobile grid operation, which allows on-site personnel to access the grid\'s data and update the information as needed. A DERMS manages and distributes distributed energy systems (DEs) optimally to provide the services of the power grid, makes it easier for DEs to participate in markets, cost-effectively manages distribution shift resources, and provides alternatives without cables. In addition, it improves power grid reliability, DE constraint management, DE usage through communication and forecasting, and situational awareness as DE penetration increases. Blockchain technology can provide additional insights into the network management system as it breaks down the centralized architecture and operates on a decentralized platform, resulting in the absence of a single point of failure and providing trusted data to increase value in the market. It can enable consensus-based negotiations to procure network management services using smart contracts. In particular, blockchain technology can help track generation, consumption, and grid data faster, as well as proper real-time coordination between these factors to stabilize the power grid. This can significantly contribute to avoiding the flexibility services required for electricity grid management. In other words, reliability is increased, and thus the fees for flexibility services can be reduced. Another advantage of blockchain technology for grid management is the open and verified data sets, which allow authorized persons to access the data of the electricity grid at any time in order to perform grid operation in real time. This technology offers new opportunities to review, secure, and improve energy flows between energy producers and consumers. The security and privacy of such a blockchain-powered power grid can also be confirmed by ensuring encrypted and public/private key cryptography and anonymous signatures. The security of power grid systems is of paramount importance today, especially given the increasing cybersecurity risks that come with increasing digitalization. In a world of centralized data storage, the risk of public utility data systems being compromised or hacked can have catastrophic consequences that can jeopardize the availability of essential services for entire communities. Blockchain\'s security features can ensure information system resilience, trusted data, and transparency.</span></span></span></p><div><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><br></span>\n</div></span>\n</div>',1,0,70,'2025-09-12 06:57:59','2025-10-08 10:15:36'),(221,34,'Electric Vehicles (EVs)','<div class=\"prose prose-lg max-w-none space-y-4\">\n\n<span id=\"docs-internal-guid-d4f6ac72-7fff-0319-24f3-eca39a3a9dfe\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: Arial;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">EVs contain battery units and have the potential to balance the energy gap between peaks and troughs in the supply of renewable energies, both spatially and temporally. In practice, this means that energy resources in the form of batteries and vehicles can participate in the energy market by buying and storing energy when and where it is abundant and cheap and then selling that energy back to the grid when the opposite is the case. This also provides a faster return on investment for the individual owner of these energy resources. With the expansion of the charging infrastructure and the introduction of cheaper electric models, the number of electric cars is expected to increase significantly. This is where blockchain offers a promising application: the organization of EV charging transactions carried out by energy traders. This technology is able to permanently store financial information on its ledger, while privacy and security are ensured by advanced algorithms. EVs can be charged/discharged at home or at public stations, either from the grid or from guaranteed green sources. Blockchain can manage the underlying energy transactions, ensuring transparent and accurate billing of all energy produced and sold in local energy markets or peer-to-peer trading.</span></span></span></span></p><div><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><br></span>\n</div></span>\n</div>',1,0,70,'2025-09-12 06:58:47','2025-10-08 10:16:08'),(222,34,'Conclusion','<div class=\"prose prose-lg max-w-none space-y-4\">\n\n<span id=\"docs-internal-guid-c0f28e37-7fff-76b0-e113-6097b56795be\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\">The commitments to comply with the Paris Agreement highlight the need for new business models in the energy market. Blockchain technology can greatly facilitate real-time data management and verify the authenticity of decarbonization claims. Once verified, blockchain technology also ensures that no unauthorized changes are made. To meet the increasing demand for green energy from residential and business customers, utilities are also under pressure to prove how and where the electricity was generated. Against the backdrop of rising energy prices, innovations that benefit users are fundamental to gaining fair access to basic resources, especially at the bottom of the economic pyramid. The green energy transition will benefit from a predictable source of clean energy that is billed transparently and easy to manage. A transparent platform is key to managing the network securely and effectively. Precise data from IoT sensors on devices such as solar panels, recorded on a blockchain and fed into AI algorithms to make predictions and informed decisions, can change the current energy landscape towards more sustainable models that better meet the needs of users. An interesting question that arises with decentralized financial models around energy consumption is the responsibility of individuals to take care of their own housekeeping. If a customer of a centralized bank loses their credit card, the bank can reissue it upon request. If unauthorized activity is detected on a customer\'s balance, a bank employee with appropriate authorizations can be contacted to repair the damage. [1] In decentralized finance and cryptocurrencies, the responsibility for the custody and secure storage of virtual wallets lies with the individual. If a rightful owner loses access to a non-fungible token, there is no third party to make amends, and it is possible to lose access to the represented object entirely. Ensuring security will likely shift to individual customers and the digital wallets they use for their transactions—likely in the form of apps on their phones connected to sensors on solar panels or other physical devices. This poses a new problem for phone companies and other major infrastructure providers, as the architecture of these devices is not designed for blockchain technology. While companies can temporarily create fixes and workarounds, it is likely that new hardware and software will be developed around the security and architecture necessary to prevent hacking and vulnerabilities specific to distributed energy models. It could be that blockchain companies will try to develop their own hardware. Google, for example, has been very successful in creating a phone with its Samsung operating system that is tailored to the company\'s needs. According to Powerledger\'s John Bulich, the future may have already begun with the upcoming release of Solana\'s Android Web3-enabled SAGA mobile device, including Solana blockchain integration. As the trend towards virtualization continues, individuals will become the owners of their own data. Electrical energy and water data, as well as all other facets of human life (e.g. medical data), could be decentralized in the coming years. This trend will require both software and hardware (e.g., phones) with the highest levels of security and reliability. In this context, a race to develop phones and other necessary devices to support Web 3.0 will be inevitable. Finally, blockchain technology can also help scale and bundle small projects to collectively achieve the scale needed to attract large energy investments. This can democratize access to project finance and other renewable energy investment models at the retail level. Decentralized energy systems enable energy investments at the local level in cities and villages and at the individual level. This can be a game-changer for restricted grids, remote areas, low-income communities, and developing countries. New financing networks can support these new energy networks and enable new forms of bankability based on trusted networks and transparency.</span></span></span></p></span>\n\n</div>',1,1,70,'2025-09-12 06:59:53','2025-10-08 10:16:28'),(223,35,'Web:Definition of the main term','<div class=\"prose prose-lg max-w-none space-y-4\">\n\n<span id=\"docs-internal-guid-6f9c88ac-7fff-24df-7a24-e08bc0962750\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">First, let\'s understand what the difference is between the web and the internet. The web, defined as the World Wide Web, is the pages you see when you log in online. The Internet is a set of interconnected computer systems on which the Web works, as well as the medium that allows the transfer of files and emails (Terra, 2023). As an analogy, the internet is the highway system that connects many cities, and the web is the collection of rest stops, gas stations, supermarkets, and other stops. All versions of the web have used and continue to use the internet to connect users to websites and to each other. By the way, no one uses the term \"World Wide Web\" anymore. However, a remnant of the term still exists, as most URLs begin with the letters \"www,\" which unsurprisingly stand for \"World Wide Web.\" It\'s an indelible part of our internet culture!</span></span></span></p><div><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><br></span>\n</div></span>\n</div>',1,0,70,'2025-09-12 07:05:43','2025-10-08 10:24:15'),(224,35,'What is Web 1.0?','<div class=\"prose prose-lg max-w-none space-y-4\">\n\n<span id=\"docs-internal-guid-1f152d42-7fff-3469-5242-bd671c080c54\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Web 1.0 refers to the first stage of the development of the World Wide Web. In the past, Web 1.0 had few content creators and a large majority of users who consumed content. Web 1.0 was largely static, read-only pages (early 1990s). Banner advertising emerged in the mid-1990s and became a common monetization model. (</span></span></span><a href=\"https://www.wired.com/2010/10/1027hotwired-banner-ads?utm_source=chatgpt.com\" style=\"text-decoration:none;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(17, 85, 204); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; text-decoration-line: underline; text-decoration-skip-ink: none; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">WIRED</span></span></span></a><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">). In Web 1.0, advertising on websites is prohibited when surfing the Internet. (Sharma, 2023) Web 1.0: It is designed to make it easier for people to search for information. This web version was designed for users looking for data. This web version is sometimes referred to as the \"read-only web\" because it lacks the necessary forms, visuals, controls, and interactivity that we enjoy on today\'s internet. The term \"Web 1.0\" is used to describe the earliest form of the Internet. Users saw the first example of a worldwide network that hinted at the future potential of digital communication and information sharing.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Here are some features of Web 1.0:</span></span></span></p><ul style=\"padding-inline-start: 48px;\"><li dir=\"ltr\" style=\"list-style-type: disc; font-size: 9pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre; margin-left: 77.35pt;\" aria-level=\"1\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\" role=\"presentation\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">It consists of static pages that are connected to a system via hyperlinks.</span></span></span></p></li><li dir=\"ltr\" style=\"list-style-type: disc; font-size: 9pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre; margin-left: 77.35pt;\" aria-level=\"1\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\" role=\"presentation\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">The content is served from the server\'s file system.</span></span></span></p></li><li dir=\"ltr\" style=\"list-style-type: disc; font-size: 9pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre; margin-left: 77.35pt;\" aria-level=\"1\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\" role=\"presentation\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">The pages were created using Server Side Includes or Common Gateway Interface (CGI).</span></span></span></p></li><li dir=\"ltr\" style=\"list-style-type: disc; font-size: 9pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre; margin-left: 77.35pt;\" aria-level=\"1\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\" role=\"presentation\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">It contains HTML 3.2 elements such as frames and tables.</span></span></span></p></li><li dir=\"ltr\" style=\"list-style-type: disc; font-size: 9pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre; margin-left: 77.35pt;\" aria-level=\"1\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\" role=\"presentation\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">HTML forms are sent via email.</span></span></span></p></li><li dir=\"ltr\" style=\"list-style-type: disc; font-size: 9pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre; margin-left: 77.35pt;\" aria-level=\"1\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\" role=\"presentation\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">The content comes from the server\'s file system, not from a relational database management system.</span></span></span></p></li><li dir=\"ltr\" style=\"list-style-type: disc; font-size: 9pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre; margin-left: 77.35pt;\" aria-level=\"1\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\" role=\"presentation\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">It includes GIF buttons and graphics.</span></span></span></p></li><li dir=\"ltr\" style=\"list-style-type: disc; font-size: 9pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre; margin-left: 77.35pt;\" aria-level=\"1\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\" role=\"presentation\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Users can only read the content but not react to it.</span></span></span></p></li></ul><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Take a real-world dictionary, digitize everything in it, and make it available to people online so they can look at it (but not act on it). This is Web 1.0. The era of Web 1.0 lasted roughly from 1991 to 2004.</span></span></span></p><div><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><br></span>\n</div></span>\n</div>',1,0,70,'2025-09-12 07:08:04','2025-10-08 10:24:33'),(225,35,'What is Web 2.0?','<div class=\"prose prose-lg max-w-none space-y-4\">\n\n<span id=\"docs-internal-guid-b0e7880e-7fff-94b6-5271-6daa1fce5046\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">If Web 1.0 consisted of a small number of people creating content for a larger audience, then Web 2.0 consists of many people creating even more content for a larger audience. Web 1.0 focused on reading; Web 2.0 focused on content participation and contribution. This form of internet focuses on user-generated content (UGC), ease of use, interactivity, and improved compatibility with other systems and devices. Web 2.0 is all about the end-user experience. Consequently, this form of weaving has been responsible for creating communities, collaborations, dialogues, and social media. As a result, Web 2.0 is considered the most important form of web interaction for most users today (Terra, 2023). While Web 1.0 has been referred to as the \"read-only web,\" Web 2.0 is known as the \"participatory social web.\" Web 2.0 is a better, more advanced version of its predecessor that incorporates web browser technologies such as JavaScript frameworks.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Typical Web 2.0 features:</span></span></span></p><ul style=\"padding-inline-start: 48px;\"><li dir=\"ltr\" style=\"list-style-type: disc; font-size: 9pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre; margin-left: 77.35pt;\" aria-level=\"1\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\" role=\"presentation\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">It provides free information sorting that allows users to retrieve and classify data together.</span></span></span></p></li><li dir=\"ltr\" style=\"list-style-type: disc; font-size: 9pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre; margin-left: 77.35pt;\" aria-level=\"1\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\" role=\"presentation\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">It contains dynamic content that responds to the user\'s input.</span></span></span></p></li><li dir=\"ltr\" style=\"list-style-type: disc; font-size: 9pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre; margin-left: 77.35pt;\" aria-level=\"1\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\" role=\"presentation\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">It uses developed application programming interfaces (APIs) that enable self-use, e.g., by a software application.</span></span></span></p></li><li dir=\"ltr\" style=\"list-style-type: disc; font-size: 9pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre; margin-left: 77.35pt;\" aria-level=\"1\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\" role=\"presentation\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">It encourages self-use and enables forms of interaction such as:</span></span></span></p></li><li dir=\"ltr\" style=\"list-style-type: disc; font-size: 9pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre; margin-left: 77.35pt;\" aria-level=\"1\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\" role=\"presentation\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Podcasting</span></span></span></p></li><li dir=\"ltr\" style=\"list-style-type: disc; font-size: 9pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre; margin-left: 77.35pt;\" aria-level=\"1\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\" role=\"presentation\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Social media</span></span></span></p></li><li dir=\"ltr\" style=\"list-style-type: disc; font-size: 9pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre; margin-left: 77.35pt;\" aria-level=\"1\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\" role=\"presentation\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Tagging</span></span></span></p></li><li dir=\"ltr\" style=\"list-style-type: disc; font-size: 9pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre; margin-left: 77.35pt;\" aria-level=\"1\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\" role=\"presentation\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Blog</span></span></span></p></li><li dir=\"ltr\" style=\"list-style-type: disc; font-size: 9pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre; margin-left: 77.35pt;\" aria-level=\"1\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\" role=\"presentation\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Comment</span></span></span></p></li><li dir=\"ltr\" style=\"list-style-type: disc; font-size: 9pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre; margin-left: 77.35pt;\" aria-level=\"1\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\" role=\"presentation\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Curating with RSS</span></span></span></p></li><li dir=\"ltr\" style=\"list-style-type: disc; font-size: 9pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre; margin-left: 77.35pt;\" aria-level=\"1\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\" role=\"presentation\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Social networks</span></span></span></p></li><li dir=\"ltr\" style=\"list-style-type: disc; font-size: 9pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre; margin-left: 77.35pt;\" aria-level=\"1\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\" role=\"presentation\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Voting on web content</span></span></span></p></li><li dir=\"ltr\" style=\"list-style-type: disc; font-size: 9pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre; margin-left: 77.35pt;\" aria-level=\"1\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\" role=\"presentation\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">It is used by society in general and is not limited to specific communities.</span></span></span></p></li></ul><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Mobile Internet access and the rise of social networks have contributed to a dramatic upswing of Web 2.0.This explosion is also fueled by the huge popularity of mobile devices such as Android devices and iPhones. In addition, the growth of Web 2.0 has allowed apps like TikTok, Twitter, and YouTube to spread and dominate the online landscape.</span></span></span></p><div><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><br></span>\n</div></span>\n</div>',1,0,70,'2025-09-12 07:12:01','2025-10-08 10:25:12'),(226,35,'What is Web 3.0?','<div class=\"prose prose-lg max-w-none space-y-4\">\n\n<span id=\"docs-internal-guid-752ff8c3-7fff-0976-7c50-430bc3c7271e\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:12pt;margin-bottom:12pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">It refers to the evolution of web usage and interaction, which involves turning the web into a database with the integration of DLT (Distributed Ledger Technology). This allows data to help create smart contracts based on the needs of individuals and enables upgrading the backend of the web after a long focus on the frontend (Web 2.0 was mostly about AJAX, tagging, and other frontend innovations for user experience). In this case, the data is no longer owned but shared, with services showing different views for the same web/data. Web 3.0 is a term that describes many developments in web use and interaction in different ways, including decentralized identity and peer-to-peer transactions.</span></span></span></p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:12pt;margin-bottom:12pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">The Semantic Web (Web 3.0) promises to capture \"the world\'s information\" in a more rational way than Google can achieve with its existing machine schema. This is especially true from the perspective of machine conception as opposed to human understanding. The Semantic Web requires the use of a declarative ontological language such as OWL (Web Ontology Language) to create domain-specific ontologies that machines can use to think about information, apply metadata, leverage natural language processing (NLP) and draw new conclusions rather than just matching keywords (W3C; Terra, 2023).</span></span></span></p><br><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">The most important features of Web 3.0:</span></span></span></p><ul style=\"padding-inline-start: 48px;\"><li dir=\"ltr\" style=\"list-style-type: disc; font-size: 9pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre; margin-left: 77.35pt;\" aria-level=\"1\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\" role=\"presentation\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Semantic Web: The subsequent evolution of the Web includes the Semantic Web. The Semantic Web improves the web technologies needed to create, share, and link content through search and analysis based on the ability to grasp the meaning of words, rather than keywords or numbers.</span></span></span></p></li><li dir=\"ltr\" style=\"list-style-type: disc; font-size: 9pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre; margin-left: 77.35pt;\" aria-level=\"1\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\" role=\"presentation\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Artificial intelligence: By combining this capability with natural language processing, Web 3.0 computers can distinguish information like humans to deliver faster and more relevant results. They are becoming smarter to meet users\' demands.</span></span></span></p></li><li dir=\"ltr\" style=\"list-style-type: disc; font-size: 9pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre; margin-left: 77.35pt;\" aria-level=\"1\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\" role=\"presentation\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">3D graphics: Three-dimensional design is widely used in Web 3.0 websites and services. Museum guides, computer games, e-commerce, geographical contexts, etc. are all examples of the use of 3D graphics.</span></span></span></p></li><li dir=\"ltr\" style=\"list-style-type: disc; font-size: 9pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre; margin-left: 77.35pt;\" aria-level=\"1\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\" role=\"presentation\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Connectivity: With Web 3.0, information is more connected thanks to semantic metadata. As a result, the user experience evolves to another level of connectivity that takes advantage of all the information available.</span></span></span></p></li><li dir=\"ltr\" style=\"list-style-type: disc; font-size: 9pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre; margin-left: 77.35pt;\" aria-level=\"1\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\" role=\"presentation\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Omnipresence: Content is accessible through multiple applications, every device is connected to the web, and the services can be used anywhere.</span></span></span></p></li><li dir=\"ltr\" style=\"list-style-type: disc; font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre; margin-left: 77.35pt;\" aria-level=\"1\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\" role=\"presentation\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">DLT and smart contracts: With the help of DLT, we can have a virtually impossible-to-hack database from which to derive the value of your content and things that you can own virtually. This is the technology that enables a trustless society through the integration of smart contracts that don\'t need a middleman as a guarantor for the contract to come about on certain terms because it\'s based on data from DLT. It is a powerful tool that can make the world a far better place and create more opportunities for everyone on the internet.</span></span></span></p></li></ul></span>\n\n</div>',1,0,70,'2025-09-12 07:16:47','2025-10-08 10:26:15');
INSERT INTO `subsections` VALUES (227,35,'Pros and Cons of the Web','<div class=\"prose prose-lg max-w-none space-y-4\">\n\n<span id=\"docs-internal-guid-b497b358-7fff-6ca3-490d-48db7913eb82\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Advantages and disadvantages of Web 1.0</span></span></span></p><div dir=\"ltr\" style=\"margin-left:0.35pt;\" align=\"left\"><table style=\"border-width: initial; border-style: none; border-color: initial;\"><colgroup><col width=\"320\"><col width=\"320\"></colgroup><tbody><tr style=\"height:15.6pt\"><td style=\"border-width: 0.75pt; border-color: rgb(0, 0, 0); vertical-align: middle; overflow: hidden; overflow-wrap: break-word;\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;text-align: center;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Advantages of Web 1.0</span></span></span></p></td><td style=\"border-width: 0.75pt; border-color: rgb(0, 0, 0); vertical-align: middle; overflow: hidden; overflow-wrap: break-word;\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;text-align: center;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Disadvantages of Web 1.0</span></span></span></p></td></tr><tr style=\"height:132.7pt\"><td style=\"border-width: 0.75pt; border-color: rgb(0, 0, 0); vertical-align: middle; overflow: hidden; overflow-wrap: break-word;\"><ul style=\"padding-inline-start: 48px;\"><li dir=\"ltr\" style=\"list-style-type: disc; font-size: 9pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre;\" aria-level=\"1\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\" role=\"presentation\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">the simplicity and accessibility of the technology</span></span></span></p></li><li dir=\"ltr\" style=\"list-style-type: disc; font-size: 9pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre;\" aria-level=\"1\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\" role=\"presentation\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">easy to use and access the internet from home or office.</span></span></span></p></li><li dir=\"ltr\" style=\"list-style-type: disc; font-size: 9pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre;\" aria-level=\"1\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\" role=\"presentation\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">allows users to easily share information through websites.</span></span></span></p></li><li dir=\"ltr\" style=\"list-style-type: disc; font-size: 9pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre;\" aria-level=\"1\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\" role=\"presentation\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Collaborate and share information quickly and efficiently</span></span></span></p></li></ul></td><td style=\"border-width: 0.75pt; border-color: rgb(0, 0, 0); vertical-align: middle; overflow: hidden; overflow-wrap: break-word;\"><ul style=\"padding-inline-start: 48px;\"><li dir=\"ltr\" style=\"list-style-type: disc; font-size: 9pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre;\" aria-level=\"1\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\" role=\"presentation\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Lack of security and privacy features</span></span></span></p></li><li dir=\"ltr\" style=\"list-style-type: disc; font-size: 9pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre;\" aria-level=\"1\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\" role=\"presentation\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Online identities can be easily compromised by hackers.</span></span></span></p></li><li dir=\"ltr\" style=\"list-style-type: disc; font-size: 9pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre;\" aria-level=\"1\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\" role=\"presentation\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">static version of the Internet</span></span></span></p></li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table><br><br><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:8pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 12pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Advantages and disadvantages of Web 2.0</span></span></span></p><div dir=\"ltr\" style=\"margin-left:0.35pt;\" align=\"left\"><table style=\"border-width: initial; border-style: none; border-color: initial;\"><colgroup><col width=\"319\"><col width=\"319\"></colgroup><tbody><tr style=\"height:15.3pt\"><td style=\"border-width: 0.75pt; border-color: rgb(0, 0, 0); vertical-align: middle; overflow: hidden; overflow-wrap: break-word;\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;text-align: center;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Advantages of Web 2.0</span></span></span></p></td><td style=\"border-width: 0.75pt; border-color: rgb(0, 0, 0); vertical-align: middle; overflow: hidden; overflow-wrap: break-word;\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;text-align: center;margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space-collapse: preserve;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Disadvantages of Web 2.0</span></span></span></p></td></tr><tr style=\"height:129.9pt\"><td style=\"border-width: 0.75pt; border-color: rgb(0, 0, 0); vertical-align: middle; overflow: hidden; overflow-wrap: break-word;\"><ul style=\"padding-inline-start: 48px;\"><li dir=\"ltr\" style=\"list-style-type: disc; font-size: 9pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre;\" aria-level=\"1\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\" role=\"presentation\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">Improved user interface and functionality</span></span></span></p></li><li dir=\"ltr\" style=\"list-style-type: disc; font-size: 9pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre;\" aria-level=\"1\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-height:1.2;margin-top:5pt;margin-bottom:5pt;\" role=\"presentation\"><span style=\"font-size: 11pt; background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; text-wrap-mode: wrap;\"><span style=\"font-family: var(--font-inter), Inter, sans-serif;\"><span style=\"font-size: 18px;\">allows users to share content and interact with each other.</span></span></span></p></li><li dir=\"ltr\" style=\"list-style-type: disc; font-size: 9pt; font-family: Poppins, sans-serif; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; font-variant-alternates: normal; font-variant-position: normal; font-variant-emoji: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre;\" aria-level=\"1\"><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"line-heig